les that report the same matter.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/acceptance-reader-prerun.md:38: \"repeat_groups\": [{\"members\": [{\"matter\": \"...\", \"kind\": \"first|repeat|revision\"}]}],\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/acceptance-reader-settle.md:25: \"repeat_groups\": [{\"members\": [{\"matter\": \"...\", \"kind\": \"first|repeat|revision\"}], \"settled_by\": \"both|third\"}],\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/di-codex.jsonl:9:[Omitted long matching line]\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ap-codex.jsonl:13:[Omitted long matching line]\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ap-codex.jsonl:40:[Omitted long matching line]\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ap-codex.jsonl:41:[Omitted long matching line]\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ap-codex.jsonl:42:[Omitted long matching line]\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ap-codex.jsonl:43:[Omitted long matching line]\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ap-codex.jsonl:44:[Omitted long matching line]\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ap-codex.jsonl:45:[Omitted long matching line]\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/acceptance-reader-settle-groups.md:24:{\"repeat_groups\": [{\"members\": [{\"matter\": \"<settled id>\", \"kind\": \"first|repeat|revision\"}], \"settled_by\": \"both|third\",","numLines":12,"totalLines":12},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NQ5q3okCkiqAAsB7QTwhgk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/claude-challenge.jsonl:21:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N9pChX3D2io4NMRMaKRhdm","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t## D202: the writer retires nothing on a matching label (2026-10-27 23:10–23:20)\n101\t\n102\tCensus before the change: all 47 retirements of the run read pair by pair (`replacements-47-labels.json`): 22 right (the owner's\n103\tlater value or status of the same claim), 20 wrong (10 a contradiction or qualification retired, 10 a different matter), 5\n104\trestatements mislabelled. Crossed with Jev: 17 of the 22 right and 12 of the 20 wrong carry `supersedes`; the split criterion moves\n105\t4 wrong to `contradicts` and 3 right with them. No label the matcher gives separates right from wrong well enough to delete on.\n106\t\n107\tChange (`store`, `writing`, `judging`): a `replace` decision stores the piece, links it where the target is linked, and records the\n108\tpair in `revisions`; both stay in force; `replacement_refused` is `revision_refused` with the same conditions, deciding only whether\n109\tthe relation is recorded; `replacements` is history (read as before, never written); `evidence_in_force` carries `revises`; the\n110\tjudge's request prints \"It revises eX (both in force)\". Old ledgers without the table read as before (guarded).\n111\t\n112\tTests: `test_replacement_rule.py` rewritten to the D202 spec after the decision was recorded (9 → 11 tests), including the G09 shape\n113\twith its real texts: the denial arrives with a `replace` decision, both pieces stay in force, the judge sees both and the revision\n114\tnote. Mutant: a scratch copy that calls `store.replace` again fails both decisive tests (`is_replaced('e1')` is True); the unmutated\n115\tcode passes. Suite 208 pass; preflight exit 0 (B judged 4,035, C judged 618). Week regression replay through the whole path (`english-v34b-nvda-week-2026-10-27`, 2026-07-01..07, 98 items, 342 calls, none failed,\n116\t$0.23): `replacements` 0 rows; evidence 129 / theses 12 / judgements 23 against the v18 week's 176 / 9 / 24. The count difference is\n117\textraction sampling (fresh model answers; the first divergent answer changes every later request), so the replay proves that the path\n118\truns with the new writer and retires nothing, not a row-level equality. The v18 week's four retirements, read: three right (NVIDIA's\n119\tannouncement over The Information's rumour ×2, the Palantir fact over its rumour) and one wrong (NVIDIA's \"roadmap intact\" retired\n120\tSemiAnalysis's Kyber setbacks, the G09 class again). The regression week's single `replace` decision exposed a second silent loss in\n121\tthe same path: the incoming SemiAnalysis NVL576 delay note carried `original_day` \"2026-07-05 to 2026-07-06\", which the writer read as\n122\tolder than the 07-06 denial, logged in `late_statements` and linked nowhere. Census: one such drop per run (the three-month run lost\n123\tthe SharonAI $4.88bn backstop figure; the v18 week lost the Kyber delay report). Fixed in the slice: an older statement is stored and\n124\tlinked as one more piece with no revision; the log row stays. 34 of the run's 1,530 pieces carry a non-ISO `original_day`: an\n125\textraction-contract item, noted for the next pass, not changed here.\n126\t\n127\t## Rigour re-check (Alex, 2026-10-27 late: \"你看下你的 learning 是不是又犯了一样的错误\"), quantified at $0\n128\t\n129\t**1. The stick moved after the send; the score splits in two.** `gold-v1.json` is the gold as frozen before the send\n130\t(reconstructed by reverting the post-send corrections); the same stored answers, the same harness fixes, scored under both:\n131\t\n132\t| field (development 21) | gold v1, pre-send | gold v2, corrected after reading the answers |\n133\t|---|---|---|\n134\t| action | 10 | 20 |\n135\t| same_as | 13 | 20 |\n136\t| revises | 20 | 21 |\n137\t| opposes | 20 | 21 |\n138\t| nothing wrongly retired | 21 | 21 |\n139\t| question ids | 21 | 21 |\n140\t| held-out action | 2 of 3 | 3 of 3 |\n141\t\n142\tTen of the twenty `action` points come from one post-send contract change (a pickup may be `background_only`); seven `same_as`\n143\tpoints from ids verified in the ledger after the model named them. Both were made with the answers in view, so the 20/21 is not a\n144\tmeasurement. What stands under either gold: nothing wrongly retired 21/21, revises 20/21, opposes 20/21, question ids 21/21.\n145\tThe only clean number for the action field is a fresh case set, drawn the same way, written from the complete expansion, sent once\n146\tand scored once.\n147\t\n148\t**2. The harness contradiction was avoidable before the send.** A synthetic answer written to the prompt's own rule (a pickup →\n149\t`background_only` with `same_as`) fails the original `code_checks`; running that check before the send costs nothing and would have\n150\tremoved 11 of the 18 refusals. The other 7 (the record's own id) likewise.\n151\t\n152\t**3. The regression replay was the wrong instrument; the right one ran afterwards at $0.** `d202-writer-replay-47.json`: all 47\n153\tstored `replace` decisions of the three-month run written through the new writer on fresh ledgers with the real pieces: 47 of 47\n154\tkeep both pieces in force, 0 rows in `replacements`. The relation recorded: 37 `revisions` rows (18 right, 4 restatements, 15 wrong\n155\tby the pair reading), 9 refused by the D167 conditions (4 right ones refused, 4 wrong ones caught, 1 restatement), 1 older\n156\tstatement stored as new. So the judge's \"It revises eX\" note is wrong in 19 of 37 cases: it inherits the matcher's label noise.\n157\tNothing is lost any more, but the annotation is an opinion and must be labelled as one (or dropped) until step 4 decides it.\n158\t\n159\t**4. The reader side of D202, quantified, not judged.** Of the 52 retired (piece × question) rows, 30 were in the judge's basis\n160\twhen retired (cited in one of the ten previous judgements): 15 right revisions (under D202 the stale value stays visible for a\n161\twhile), 11 wrong retirements (the judge lost a piece it had been citing), 4 restatements. Median 13 judgements followed each\n162\tretirement. Whether a visible stale value with a dated \"revises\" note moves a judgement is not measured; it needs the stored\n163\tjudging requests re-sent with and without the old piece (about 30 calls), not done.\n164\t\n165\t**5. Variance: one sample per case, none.** The design said three sends on disagreements; not run. Every per-case result above is a\n166\tsingle draw.\n167\t\n168\t**6. The late-older drop: fixed downstream, cause upstream.** Stored pieces: 1,530, `original_day` non-ISO 34 (26 month-only, 6\n169\ttext, 2 year-only), empty 0; raw handover pieces: 2,369, non-ISO 54, empty 1,445, which means the writer fills an unknown day with\n170\tthe publication day (06-views' rule; D245's source contract says keep unknown). Of the 47 `replace` decisions 1 had a non-ISO\n171\t`said_day`. The extraction schema types `original_day` as a free string. The owner of both facts is extraction's contract.\n172\t\n173\t**Repeated lessons, named:** (1) a stick built from an excerpt and corrected after output (retrospective #7/#8 and §7); (2) the\n174\tharness not checked against its own prompt before the send (#1); (3) a sampler run bought as an acceptance of a code change\n175\t(#1, §5); (4) one sample, no control variance (§6). Not repeated: no long run, no edits during a live run, cases drawn by seed,\n176\tdecision before test change, mutants run.\n177\t\n178\t## The fresh set, scored once against a frozen gold (fresh1, 2026-10-28 00:20, $0.0907)\n179\t\n180\t16 new cases (`cases-fresh.json`, seed 20261028; one redraw after an incoming record turned out to be shared with the used set),\n181\treference answers written from the complete expansion of every linked question before any send (`gold-fresh.json`, frozen), the\n182\tharness checked with three prompt-conformant synthetic answers (a pickup, an unrelated record, a new question) through every code\n183\tcheck, then the arm1 prompt sent once per case: 16 of 16 accepted, 89 calls, 1.34M input tokens. No gold row was edited after the\n184\tsend; the one encoding mistake found is reported, not fixed in the score.\n185\t\n186\t| field | development (12) | held out (4) |\n187\t|---|---|---|\n188\t| action | 11 | 4 |\n189\t| question ids | 11 | 2 (one of the two is a gold-encoding error, K04: an empty list was meant to be allowed) |\n190\t| same_as | 8 | 4 |\n191\t| revises | 11 | 3 |\n192\t| opposes | 12 | 4 |\n193\t| nothing wrongly retired | 12 | 4 |\n194\t| independent (where scorable) | 6 of 6 | 1 of 1 |\n195\t| Jev question relation | 11 | 3 |\n196\t| Jev piece relation | 6 | 0 of 2 |\n197\t\n198\tEvery miss read against its answer:\n199\t- **F02, F10 (same_as): a second newspaper's own reporting of the same talks is treated as a distinct, independent claim.** The\n200\t  gold says same claim (one rumour of one deal); the model says \"distinct original outlet, independent corroboration\". Together\n201\t  with G11 in arm1 (a bank note repeating a customer's remark) this is one class, and its root is in my contract, not only in the\n202\t  model: `same_as` means \"same claim from the same original source\" and `independent` is defined against the `same_as` pieces, so\n203\t  \"the same claim, independently sourced\" cannot be expressed at all. The analyst's standard needs both: claim identity (what the\n204\t  argument counts once) and source independence (what raises confidence). Arm 2 splits them.\n205\t- **F09 (action, questions):** NVIDIA's six-bank $500bn financing platforms, at a sequence where no financing question exists yet,\n206\t  judged `background_only` with no question: \"a first-party announcement that states no investment judgement\". The gold wanted an\n207\t  update to the revenue questions as a demand/financing driver, or a new question on backstop exposure. A real miss of rule F/G: a\n208\t  large new fact that opens a judgement was waved through as context.\n209\t- **F03 (same_as):** an author's valuation argument citing the Q1 figures; the model called it background (accepted) but did not"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78ccad56-ed8d-4e29-91b7-f576c6d2916d","uuid":"83387e17-c948-4a04-aa8a-901b297f88d3","timestamp":"2026-11-09T21:16:08.259Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/results.md","content":"## D202: the writer retires nothing on a matching label (2026-10-27 23:10–23:20)\n\nCensus before the change: all 47 retirements of the run read pair by pair (`replacements-47-labels.json`): 22 right (the owner's\nlater value or status of the same claim), 20 wrong (10 a contradiction or qualification retired, 10 a different matter), 5\nrestatements mislabelled. Crossed with Jev: 17 of the 22 right and 12 of the 20 wrong carry `supersedes`; the split criterion moves\n4 wrong to `contradicts` and 3 right with them. No label the matcher gives separates right from wrong well enough to delete on.\n\nChange (`store`, `writing`, `judging`): a `replace` decision stores the piece, links it where the target is linked, and records the\npair in `revisions`; both stay in force; `replacement_refused` is `revision_refused` with the same conditions, deciding only whether\nthe relation is recorded; `replacements` is history (read as before, never written); `evidence_in_force` carries `revises`; the\njudge's request prints \"It revises eX (both in force)\". Old ledgers without the table read as before (guarded).\n\nTests: `test_replacement_rule.py` rewritten to the D202 spec after the decision was recorded (9 → 11 tests), including the G09 shape\nwith its real texts: the denial arrives with a `replace` decision, both pieces stay in force, the judge sees both and the revision\nnote. Mutant: a scratch copy that calls `store.replace` again fails both decisive tests (`is_replaced('e1')` is True); the unmutated\ncode passes. Suite 208 pass; preflight exit 0 (B judged 4,035, C judged 618). Week regression replay through the whole path (`english-v34b-nvda-week-2026-10-27`, 2026-07-01..07, 98 items, 342 calls, none failed,\n$0.23): `replacements` 0 rows; evidence 129 / theses 12 / judgements 23 against the v18 week's 176 / 9 / 24. The count difference is\nextraction sampling (fresh model answers; the first divergent answer changes every later request), so the replay proves that the path\nruns with the new writer and retires nothing, not a row-level equality. The v18 week's four retirements, read: three right (NVIDIA's\nannouncement over The Information's rumour ×2, the Palantir fact over its rumour) and one wrong (NVIDIA's \"roadmap intact\" retired\nSemiAnalysis's Kyber setbacks, the G09 class again). The regression week's single `replace` decision exposed a second silent loss in\nthe same path: the incoming SemiAnalysis NVL576 delay note carried `original_day` \"2026-07-05 to 2026-07-06\", which the writer read as\nolder than the 07-06 denial, logged in `late_statements` and linked nowhere. Census: one such drop per run (the three-month run lost\nthe SharonAI $4.88bn backstop figure; the v18 week lost the Kyber delay report). Fixed in the slice: an older statement is stored and\nlinked as one more piece with no revision; the log row stays. 34 of the run's 1,530 pieces carry a non-ISO `original_day`: an\nextraction-contract item, noted for the next pass, not changed here.\n\n## Rigour re-check (Alex, 2026-10-27 late: \"你看下你的 learning 是不是又犯了一样的错误\"), quantified at $0\n\n**1. The stick moved after the send; the score splits in two.** `gold-v1.json` is the gold as frozen before the send\n(reconstructed by reverting the post-send corrections); the same stored answers, the same harness fixes, scored under both:\n\n| field (development 21) | gold v1, pre-send | gold v2, corrected after reading the answers |\n|---|---|---|\n| action | 10 | 20 |\n| same_as | 13 | 20 |\n| revises | 20 | 21 |\n| opposes | 20 | 21 |\n| nothing wrongly retired | 21 | 21 |\n| question ids | 21 | 21 |\n| held-out action | 2 of 3 | 3 of 3 |\n\nTen of the twenty `action` points come from one post-send contract change (a pickup may be `background_only`); seven `same_as`\npoints from ids verified in the ledger after the model named them. Both were made with the answers in view, so the 20/21 is not a\nmeasurement. What stands under either gold: nothing wrongly retired 21/21, revises 20/21, opposes 20/21, question ids 21/21.\nThe only clean number for the action field is a fresh case set, drawn the same way, written from the complete expansion, sent once\nand scored once.\n\n**2. The harness contradiction was avoidable before the send.** A synthetic answer written to the prompt's own rule (a pickup →\n`background_only` with `same_as`) fails the original `code_checks`; running that check before the send costs nothing and would have\nremoved 11 of the 18 refusals. The other 7 (the record's own id) likewise.\n\n**3. The regression replay was the wrong instrument; the right one ran afterwards at $0.** `d202-writer-replay-47.json`: all 47\nstored `replace` decisions of the three-month run written through the new writer on fresh ledgers with the real pieces: 47 of 47\nkeep both pieces in force, 0 rows in `replacements`. The relation recorded: 37 `revisions` rows (18 right, 4 restatements, 15 wrong\nby the pair reading), 9 refused by the D167 conditions (4 right ones refused, 4 wrong ones caught, 1 restatement), 1 older\nstatement stored as new. So the judge's \"It revises eX\" note is wrong in 19 of 37 cases: it inherits the matcher's label noise.\nNothing is lost any more, but the annotation is an opinion and must be labelled as one (or dropped) until step 4 decides it.\n\n**4. The reader side of D202, quantified, not judged.** Of the 52 retired (piece × question) rows, 30 were in the judge's basis\nwhen retired (cited in one of the ten previous judgements): 15 right revisions (under D202 the stale value stays visible for a\nwhile), 11 wrong retirements (the judge lost a piece it had been citing), 4 restatements. Median 13 judgements followed each\nretirement. Whether a visible stale value with a dated \"revises\" note moves a judgement is not measured; it needs the stored\njudging requests re-sent with and without the old piece (about 30 calls), not done.\n\n**5. Variance: one sample per case, none.** The design said three sends on disagreements; not run. Every per-case result above is a\nsingle draw.\n\n**6. The late-older drop: fixed downstream, cause upstream.** Stored pieces: 1,530, `original_day` non-ISO 34 (26 month-only, 6\ntext, 2 year-only), empty 0; raw handover pieces: 2,369, non-ISO 54, empty 1,445, which means the writer fills an unknown day with\nthe publication day (06-views' rule; D245's source contract says keep unknown). Of the 47 `replace` decisions 1 had a non-ISO\n`said_day`. The extraction schema types `original_day` as a free string. The owner of both facts is extraction's contract.\n\n**Repeated lessons, named:** (1) a stick built from an excerpt and corrected after output (retrospective #7/#8 and §7); (2) the\nharness not checked against its own prompt before the send (#1); (3) a sampler run bought as an acceptance of a code change\n(#1, §5); (4) one sample, no control variance (§6). Not repeated: no long run, no edits during a live run, cases drawn by seed,\ndecision before test change, mutants run.\n\n## The fresh set, scored once against a frozen gold (fresh1, 2026-10-28 00:20, $0.0907)\n\n16 new cases (`cases-fresh.json`, seed 20261028; one redraw after an incoming record turned out to be shared with the used set),\nreference answers written from the complete expansion of every linked question before any send (`gold-fresh.json`, frozen), the\nharness checked with three prompt-conformant synthetic answers (a pickup, an unrelated record, a new question) through every code\ncheck, then the arm1 prompt sent once per case: 16 of 16 accepted, 89 calls, 1.34M input tokens. No gold row was edited after the\nsend; the one encoding mistake found is reported, not fixed in the score.\n\n| field | development (12) | held out (4) |\n|---|---|---|\n| action | 11 | 4 |\n| question ids | 11 | 2 (one of the two is a gold-encoding error, K04: an empty list was meant to be allowed) |\n| same_as | 8 | 4 |\n| revises | 11 | 3 |\n| opposes | 12 | 4 |\n| nothing wrongly retired | 12 | 4 |\n| independent (where scorable) | 6 of 6 | 1 of 1 |\n| Jev question relation | 11 | 3 |\n| Jev piece relation | 6 | 0 of 2 |\n\nEvery miss read against its answer:\n- **F02, F10 (same_as): a second newspaper's own reporting of the same talks is treated as a distinct, independent claim.** The\n  gold says same claim (one rumour of one deal); the model says \"distinct original outlet, independent corroboration\". Together\n  with G11 in arm1 (a bank note repeating a customer's remark) this is one class, and its root is in my contract, not only in the\n  model: `same_as` means \"same claim from the same original source\" and `independent` is defined against the `same_as` pieces, so\n  \"the same claim, independently sourced\" cannot be expressed at all. The analyst's standard needs both: claim identity (what the\n  argument counts once) and source independence (what raises confidence). Arm 2 splits them.\n- **F09 (action, questions):** NVIDIA's six-bank $500bn financing platforms, at a sequence where no financing question exists yet,\n  judged `background_only` with no question: \"a first-party announcement that states no investment judgement\". The gold wanted an\n  update to the revenue questions as a demand/financing driver, or a new question on backstop exposure. A real miss of rule F/G: a\n  large new fact that opens a judgement was waved through as context.\n- **F03 (same_as):** an author's valuation argument citing the Q1 figures; the model called it background (accepted) but did not","numLines":110,"startLine":100,"totalLines":323}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01N9pChX3D2io4NMRMaKRhdm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/claude-challenge.jsonl:23:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012B5MpxqYBVzqddDDEPW1wc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Acceptance protocol for the clean Steps 1–2 (draft for council, 2026-11-09)\n2\t\n3\tGoverning decisions: D380–D386 (`docs/DECISIONS-log.md`); Alex's thesis line \"50 篇里最多 2 个\" (AskUserQuestion, 2026-11-08);\n4\tthe agreed protocol in `LOG.md` §40. This page fixes everything before any article of the fresh set is sent.\n5\t\n6\t## 1. What is accepted\n7\t\n8\tThe configuration c8, frozen as these files:\n9\t- `arms/c8-system.txt`, `arms/c8-closing.txt` and `arms/c8-schema.json`;\n10\t- DeepSeek Flash at high effort, a 64,000-token output cap, and one resend for a format failure or a cut answer (D380);\n11\t- Step 2 and Step 3 as in `clean-arch/step2/compare.py` and `clean-arch/step3/library.py`.\n12\t\n13\tTheir sha256 values are written to `SHA256SUMS` in each run folder before launch.\n14\t\n15\t## 2. The fresh set\n16\t\n17\tD386 asks for \"NVDA articles no run has used\".\n18\t\n19\t- **Eligible:** an item of `poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite` that passes the runner's own selection rules. That means:\n20\t  - the NVDA ticker tag is present;\n21\t  - the text is present;\n22\t  - the feed flag is 1;\n23\t  - the item is neither a copy nor superseded.\n24\t- **Order:** by publication day; then by `published_at` in UTC when its date equals the publication day, or else 23:59:59Z of that\n25\t  day; then by input id.\n26\t- **Choice:** the first 50 unused items from 2026-08-30. The procedure is `select_fresh.py`.\n27\t- **Used:** the item's full id appears in any file under `poc/data`, `clean-arch`, `docs`, `reports`, `research_notes` or\n28\t  `../tn-hivemind/docs`. The census scanned 10,871 files. It found every dev id (115 of 115) and every test id (26 of 26). The match\n29\t  takes whole ids, including ids with a second colon.\n30\t- **Not counted as uses:** these files hold the source articles themselves, and no model read an article in them. The list is in\n31\t  `selection.json`.\n32\t  - the source databases and their copies;\n33\t  - five corpus-wide page-cleaning inventories (HTML extractor output and character counts).\n34\t\n35\t  **Every pipeline run counts as a use**, old or new. The old pipeline's whole-window runs processed every article up to 2026-08-29.\n36\t  The 2026-11-05 seven-day run listed every article from 2026-08-29 to 2026-09-04.\n37\t- **Two earlier drafts were withdrawn before any reader saw them.** Both were July sets.\n38\t  1. The first was ordered by (day, id).\n39\t  2. The second excluded the old whole-window runs from the use census. Council B blocked it because those runs used the articles\n40\t     (`council/am-codex.md`).\n41\t\n42\t  The withdrawn July manifest is kept outside the repository.\n43\t- **Result:** 50 news articles from 2026-09-05 (18), 2026-09-06 (22) and 2026-09-07 (10). Every item has a stated time. The text has\n44\t  a median of 4,040 characters and a maximum of 17,829. Inside these three days, 8 used items were skipped; each is listed in\n45\t  `selection.json` with its files, which come from the source-cleaning checks of 2026-11-07. These days are a weekend and the US\n46\t  Labor Day holiday: they hold no NVIDIA blog or newsroom post. The topics are:\n47\t  - the Hugging Face acquisition, covered by several outlets;\n48\t  - DLSS 5;\n49\t  - RTX Spark;\n50\t  - Groq racks;\n51\t  - the Wistron share sale;\n52\t  - the Nscale funding talks;\n53\t  - China competitors;\n54\t  - many stock-commentary pieces.\n55\t- **Frozen:** `poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/manifest.json`, sha256 `81f3f579…bd688`. The runner accepts it.\n56\t- **What a result supports:** a real three-day stream of about 50 articles, with its repeats. It says nothing about other months.\n57\t  The ordering inside a day is approximate: stored times are coarse, and ties are broken by id.\n58\t\n59\t## 3. Runs\n60\t\n61\t- **Fresh set.** Step 1 forward from an empty library, then Steps 2–3 forward on its trace. One draw.\n62\t- **Dev stability.** Step 1 on the 115 dev articles from an empty library, three draws, each followed by Steps 2–3.\n63\t- **Cost estimate.** About $0.0135 per article at published rates (from the c8 check sends), so about $0.7 for the fresh set and $4.7\n64\t  for dev ×3, plus embeddings and Step 2 comparisons. The limits are scaled to each run: `max_calls` is 2.2 × the number of articles,\n65\t  and `max_usd` is $20.\n66\t\n67\t## 4. Readers\n68\t\n69\tFresh Codex sessions, read-only; none wrote c8.\n70\t\n71\t- **Before the run, frozen with sha256:**\n72\t  - Two readers each read all 50 sources and write three lists:\n73\t    1. **Matters.** Per article, the matters an analyst would note under the standard's evidence test (§5.1 and the §5.3 rows; D385:\n74\t       target prices and valuation conclusions are not kept), each with its central claim. One matter is counted once per article\n75\t       (D250).\n76\t    2. **Repeat groups.** Articles that report the same matter, each article marked as a repeat or as a revision (a changed number,\n77\t       a denial, a later step).\n78\t    3. **Dossiers.** For each repeat group, the details each article adds.\n79\t  - A third reader settles every difference between the two readers against the source.\n80\t  - The settled lists are frozen.\n81\t- **After the run:** the readers judge the output against the frozen lists and the source. They see:\n82\t  - each kept item: helpful, noise or wrong;\n83\t  - each opened thesis, against the standard's opening tests;\n84\t  - each link;\n85\t  - each Step 2 merge;\n86\t  - each repeat or revision that search missed;\n87\t  - retention for each dossier.\n88\t\n89\t  A third reader settles disputes.\n90\t- **Calibration.** Before the readers' counts are read, I hand-check two articles whose answers I traced myself.\n91\t- **Batches.** Every reader call covers at most ten articles. A single 50-article call stopped after 5 (LOG §42).\n92\t\n93\t## 5. Lines, declared before the run\n94\t\n95\t| Line | Pass |\n96\t|---|---|\n97\t| Helpful matters kept | ≥ 81.2% of the settled matter list. A matter counts as kept when a kept item states its central claim; a missing secondary detail is a field note, not a miss; several items for one matter count once |\n98\t| Wrongly opened theses | ≤ 2 in 50 (Alex). A thesis is wrong when it does not meet the standard's four reasons, when it is small, when it duplicates an open thesis, or when it is a fragment of one bet |\n99\t| Search | ≥ 90% of the settled repeat pairs (a later member and an earlier member of the same group; a matter may belong to two pairs) reach the earlier member's group among the search candidates |\n100\t| Wrong merges | ≤ 2% of merges, none unjudged |\n101\t| Detail retention | Grouped libraries keep no fewer settled dossier details than ungrouped records |\n102\t\n103\tEvery line reports its denominator.\n104\t- A line with no cases is reported as **unexercised**, not passed.\n105\t- A case still disputed after the third reader counts against the system for the verdict, and is reported both ways.\n106\t- A failed article (a format failure after one resend) counts as missing every matter it carries, and is listed.\n107\t- A pending later link counts as unlinked, and is listed.\n108\t\n109\t**Reported without a line:**\n110\t- noise, wrong statements, and items kept without links (with their materiality);\n111\t- later-link precision and sampled recall;\n112\t- the fields party, date and tier;\n113\t- cost and time.\n114\t\n115\t**Excluded in advance:**\n116\t- class A financial-measure theses (D382);\n117\t- valuation conclusions (D385; the conflict with the frozen gold's conditional-valuation row is disclosed).\n118\t\n119\t**Limits** are set per run:\n120\t- `max_calls` is 2.2 × the number of articles;\n121\t- `max_usd` is $20;\n122\t- `max_seconds` is 21,600. The c7 dev run of 115 articles took less than half of that.\n123\t\n124\t**Dev ×3** decides one thing, declared now: whether c8 clears the earlier line in every draw, rather than in one lucky draw. The line\n125\tis helpful ≥ 190 of 234 on the frozen golden-v5 dev gold. The scorer is a lead, and I read the traces myself. Each draw is reported,\n126\tnever only the best, together with:\n127\t- the question measure against must_open, may_open and must_not;\n128\t- Step 2 lines 1 and 2.\n129\t\n130\t## 6. Known items carried in, disclosed\n131\t\n132\t- **The lidar boundary.** In one c8 check send, the model opened a lidar phase-out policy question that the gold lists as must_not.\n133\t  This is recorded and watched; no rule was added.\n134\t- **The supplier statement.** It is now kept without links (D383). Alex's earlier ruling \"丢掉\" came before D383, so the overlap goes\n135\t  to him as a report.\n136\t- **Later linking (c6 Step 2 run).**\n137\t  - 0 of 40 sampled rejected pairs should have been linked: both readers agreed.\n138\t  - 2 of the 3 accepted pairs were judged wrong by both readers. Both link the company's overall revenue estimates to the thesis\n139\t    \"will NVIDIA's $119 billion in supply commitments be matched by enough AI demand\"; the comparison scored one of them 0.55.\n140\t  - Traced: in the same run, Step 1 filed the company's reported revenue and its next-quarter guidance under that same thesis at\n141\t    extraction time (2 of its 7 direct links). Later linking therefore applies the boundary Step 1 already applies; the readers drew\n142\t    it more strictly.\n143\t  - The same class in earlier runs: the c4 run had 5 such links before the \"informs\" question gained its company-wide sentence, and\n144\t    its two reruns had none. c5 and the test run had none of this class.\n145\t  - The boundary (do company-wide results inform a thesis about demand against commitments?) is reported, and no rule is added. It is\n146\t    measured again in acceptance.\n147\t- **Quotes.** 2 of 288 kept items carry a passage that was not verified: one extra quotation mark, and one added word. This is the\n148\t  known one-word rewrite class; the item is kept and the passage is marked unverified.\n149\t- **The test set is exposed.** Golden-v5 test was used across configurations (D386). Its scores are not acceptance.\n150\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78ccad56-ed8d-4e29-91b7-f576c6d2916d","uuid":"9b685e74-9d3d-496a-949f-d9f90621394c","timestamp":"2026-11-09T21:16:08.908Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/ACCEPTANCE-PROTOCOL.md","content":"# Acceptance protocol for the clean Steps 1–2 (draft for council, 2026-11-09)\n\nGoverning decisions: D380–D386 (`docs/DECISIONS-log.md`); Alex's thesis line \"50 篇里最多 2 个\" (AskUserQuestion, 2026-11-08);\nthe agreed protocol in `LOG.md` §40. This page fixes everything before any article of the fresh set is sent.\n\n## 1. What is accepted\n\nThe configuration c8, frozen as these files:\n- `arms/c8-system.txt`, `arms/c8-closing.txt` and `arms/c8-schema.json`;\n- DeepSeek Flash at high effort, a 64,000-token output cap, and one resend for a format failure or a cut answer (D380);\n- Step 2 and Step 3 as in `clean-arch/step2/compare.py` and `clean-arch/step3/library.py`.\n\nTheir sha256 values are written to `SHA256SUMS` in each run folder before launch.\n\n## 2. The fresh set\n\nD386 asks for \"NVDA articles no run has used\".\n\n- **Eligible:** an item of `poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite` that passes the runner's own selection rules. That means:\n  - the NVDA ticker tag is present;\n  - the text is present;\n  - the feed flag is 1;\n  - the item is neither a copy nor superseded.\n- **Order:** by publication day; then by `published_at` in UTC when its date equals the publication day, or else 23:59:59Z of that\n  day; then by input id.\n- **Choice:** the first 50 unused items from 2026-08-30. The procedure is `select_fresh.py`.\n- **Used:** the item's full id appears in any file under `poc/data`, `clean-arch`, `docs`, `reports`, `research_notes` or\n  `../tn-hivemind/docs`. The census scanned 10,871 files. It found every dev id (115 of 115) and every test id (26 of 26). The match\n  takes whole ids, including ids with a second colon.\n- **Not counted as uses:** these files hold the source articles themselves, and no model read an article in them. The list is in\n  `selection.json`.\n  - the source databases and their copies;\n  - five corpus-wide page-cleaning inventories (HTML extractor output and character counts).\n\n  **Every pipeline run counts as a use**, old or new. The old pipeline's whole-window runs processed every article up to 2026-08-29.\n  The 2026-11-05 seven-day run listed every article from 2026-08-29 to 2026-09-04.\n- **Two earlier drafts were withdrawn before any reader saw them.** Both were July sets.\n  1. The first was ordered by (day, id).\n  2. The second excluded the old whole-window runs from the use census. Council B blocked it because those runs used the articles\n     (`council/am-codex.md`).\n\n  The withdrawn July manifest is kept outside the repository.\n- **Result:** 50 news articles from 2026-09-05 (18), 2026-09-06 (22) and 2026-09-07 (10). Every item has a stated time. The text has\n  a median of 4,040 characters and a maximum of 17,829. Inside these three days, 8 used items were skipped; each is listed in\n  `selection.json` with its files, which come from the source-cleaning checks of 2026-11-07. These days are a weekend and the US\n  Labor Day holiday: they hold no NVIDIA blog or newsroom post. The topics are:\n  - the Hugging Face acquisition, covered by several outlets;\n  - DLSS 5;\n  - RTX Spark;\n  - Groq racks;\n  - the Wistron share sale;\n  - the Nscale funding talks;\n  - China competitors;\n  - many stock-commentary pieces.\n- **Frozen:** `poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/manifest.json`, sha256 `81f3f579…bd688`. The runner accepts it.\n- **What a result supports:** a real three-day stream of about 50 articles, with its repeats. It says nothing about other months.\n  The ordering inside a day is approximate: stored times are coarse, and ties are broken by id.\n\n## 3. Runs\n\n- **Fresh set.** Step 1 forward from an empty library, then Steps 2–3 forward on its trace. One draw.\n- **Dev stability.** Step 1 on the 115 dev articles from an empty library, three draws, each followed by Steps 2–3.\n- **Cost estimate.** About $0.0135 per article at published rates (from the c8 check sends), so about $0.7 for the fresh set and $4.7\n  for dev ×3, plus embeddings and Step 2 comparisons. The limits are scaled to each run: `max_calls` is 2.2 × the number of articles,\n  and `max_usd` is $20.\n\n## 4. Readers\n\nFresh Codex sessions, read-only; none wrote c8.\n\n- **Before the run, frozen with sha256:**\n  - Two readers each read all 50 sources and write three lists:\n    1. **Matters.** Per article, the matters an analyst would note under the standard's evidence test (§5.1 and the §5.3 rows; D385:\n       target prices and valuation conclusions are not kept), each with its central claim. One matter is counted once per article\n       (D250).\n    2. **Repeat groups.** Articles that report the same matter, each article marked as a repeat or as a revision (a changed number,\n       a denial, a later step).\n    3. **Dossiers.** For each repeat group, the details each article adds.\n  - A third reader settles every difference between the two readers against the source.\n  - The settled lists are frozen.\n- **After the run:** the readers judge the output against the frozen lists and the source. They see:\n  - each kept item: helpful, noise or wrong;\n  - each opened thesis, against the standard's opening tests;\n  - each link;\n  - each Step 2 merge;\n  - each repeat or revision that search missed;\n  - retention for each dossier.\n\n  A third reader settles disputes.\n- **Calibration.** Before the readers' counts are read, I hand-check two articles whose answers I traced myself.\n- **Batches.** Every reader call covers at most ten articles. A single 50-article call stopped after 5 (LOG §42).\n\n## 5. Lines, declared before the run\n\n| Line | Pass |\n|---|---|\n| Helpful matters kept | ≥ 81.2% of the settled matter list. A matter counts as kept when a kept item states its central claim; a missing secondary detail is a field note, not a miss; several items for one matter count once |\n| Wrongly opened theses | ≤ 2 in 50 (Alex). A thesis is wrong when it does not meet the standard's four reasons, when it is small, when it duplicates an open thesis, or when it is a fragment of one bet |\n| Search | ≥ 90% of the settled repeat pairs (a later member and an earlier member of the same group; a matter may belong to two pairs) reach the earlier member's group among the search candidates |\n| Wrong merges | ≤ 2% of merges, none unjudged |\n| Detail retention | Grouped libraries keep no fewer settled dossier details than ungrouped records |\n\nEvery line reports its denominator.\n- A line with no cases is reported as **unexercised**, not passed.\n- A case still disputed after the third reader counts against the system for the verdict, and is reported both ways.\n- A failed article (a format failure after one resend) counts as missing every matter it carries, and is listed.\n- A pending later link counts as unlinked, and is listed.\n\n**Reported without a line:**\n- noise, wrong statements, and items kept without links (with their materiality);\n- later-link precision and sampled recall;\n- the fields party, date and tier;\n- cost and time.\n\n**Excluded in advance:**\n- class A financial-measure theses (D382);\n- valuation conclusions (D385; the conflict with the frozen gold's conditional-valuation row is disclosed).\n\n**Limits** are set per run:\n- `max_calls` is 2.2 × the number of articles;\n- `max_usd` is $20;\n- `max_seconds` is 21,600. The c7 dev run of 115 articles took less than half of that.\n\n**Dev ×3** decides one thing, declared now: whether c8 clears the earlier line in every draw, rather than in one lucky draw. The line\nis helpful ≥ 190 of 234 on the frozen golden-v5 dev gold. The scorer is a lead, and I read the traces myself. Each draw is reported,\nnever only the best, together with:\n- the question measure against must_open, may_open and must_not;\n- Step 2 lines 1 and 2.\n\n## 6. Known items carried in, disclosed\n\n- **The lidar boundary.** In one c8 check send, the model opened a lidar phase-out policy question that the gold lists as must_not.\n  This is recorded and watched; no rule was added.\n- **The supplier statement.** It is now kept without links (D383). Alex's earlier ruling \"丢掉\" came before D383, so the overlap goes\n  to him as a report.\n- **Later linking (c6 Step 2 run).**\n  - 0 of 40 sampled rejected pairs should have been linked: both readers agreed.\n  - 2 of the 3 accepted pairs were judged wrong by both readers. Both link the company's overall revenue estimates to the thesis\n    \"will NVIDIA's $119 billion in supply commitments be matched by enough AI demand\"; the comparison scored one of them 0.55.\n  - Traced: in the same run, Step 1 filed the company's reported revenue and its next-quarter guidance under that same thesis at\n    extraction time (2 of its 7 direct links). Later linking therefore applies the boundary Step 1 already applies; the readers drew\n    it more strictly.\n  - The same class in earlier runs: the c4 run had 5 such links before the \"informs\" question gained its company-wide sentence, and\n    its two reruns had none. c5 and the test run had none of this class.\n  - The boundary (do company-wide results inform a thesis about demand against commitments?) is reported, and no rule is added. It is\n    measured again in acceptance.\n- **Quotes.** 2 of 288 kept items carry a passage that was not verified: one extra quotation mark, and one added word. This is the\n  known one-word rewrite class; the item is kept and the passage is marked unverified.\n- **The test set is exposed.** Golden-v5 test was used across configurations (D386). Its scores are not acceptance.\n","numLines":150,"startLine":1,"totalLines":150}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012B5MpxqYBVzqddDDEPW1wc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/independent-handoff.md:1:Independent cold read-only council investigation for Alex: is continuing with Jev currently the best-supported way to perform clean Step 2, and how confident should we be? You must investigate rather than merely follow a preferred conclusion. Scope: clean Step 2 retrieval/candidate comparison/grouping/revision as it affects stored and read evidence, plus direct downstream representation. Exclude unrelated steps, changing product behavior, model API requests, and live databases. Read AGENTS §§1,2,7.1,7.2,7.3, current docs/CURRENT_STATE.md and decisions D168/D202/D204/D381, clean-arch/PRD.md §§5–6, clean-arch/step2/compare.py and step3/library.py; historic step2-validation log §§9–13 and previous reports step2-score, step2-wrong-merge, step2-merge-fix. Most recent experiment lives in /Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/ including RESULTS-DRAFT.md, COUNCIL-LOOP.md, raw completed probe events and bodies, blind source packets and reader judgments, results-codex.md, results-claude.md. Verify exact runs/three draws, not only summaries; read complete original articles and question text for H01 acquisition, B05 70% versus customer potential, B10 deliveries, R05 discontinued concern, P01 denial and its second edge; preserve disputes. Compare Jev full (actual product form), Jev single, DeepSeek pair and any no-change/prior alternative including score/floor/materiality tests. Quantify only valid denominators; distinguish search, misses, wrong merges, revision links, storage vs usable product semantics; assess confidence vs randomness, candidate/format confounds, how repeated calls and scores relate to D381's ranking, validity of Jev as a replacement decision. What is evidence for keeping it, what would falsify, what is unresolved, and what single next no-cost research test should Alex do? Cite original evidence and exact files; do not elevate AI readers to frozen gold. No edits, no API sends, no paid experiments.
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-challenge.stderr:13:Challenge your own conclusion and my independent reflection (`codex-reflection.md`) before we tell Alex “Jev is the way.” Specifically: (1) fresh c8 1/57 is a wrong-merge line, not overall precision and ignores misses and false revision edges; previous 3/104 failed under other Step1 inputs, do not compare as controlled versions; (2) H01 is Jev-stable failure, DeepSeek 3/3 correct; B05 DeepSeek worse than Jev single, but full Jev also erred 2/3, so the same-pair experiment does not establish Jev beats DeepSeek on product or overall. Does “best supported” mean lowest integration burden rather than proven quality? (3) the historic 2026-10-27 D201 results show Jev 7/18 vs a generative analysis call handling 20/21 and 3/3 under a different old contract, inputs and lots of context; should we disclose it as not equivalent rather than ignore contrary evidence? (4) R05 is disputed and P01 full produces a second unadjudicated edge; do not report improvements or controls as unambiguous. (5) score-threshold 363 count is not independent; ± no numerical superiority claim. What single recommendation answers whether keep Jev, which would change your mind, and what is the cheapest source-backed next action? Cite exact evidence. Read-only no writes or paid calls, out of scope everything except Step2.
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-challenge.stderr:33:docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2349:- Decided: `poc-v23` (extraction v10, `matching.engine: "jev"`, model `jev-1.14.0`, with D168) is the default configuration of the replay (`scripts/replay.py`, which now also requires the ledger and archive it writes). The service's idea route keeps its adopted configuration `poc-v9` (D135/D145) on the sealed ledger and its search index; moving the service to the English library needs a new index and is a separate step. The generative matcher stays a configured alternative (`engine: "llm"`); old configurations and stored archives replay unchanged.
