reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:11:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/CURRENT_STATE.md; sed -n '129,190p' clean-arch/PRD.md; cat clean-arch/step2/compare.py clean-arch/step3/library.py\"","aggregated_output":"# Current state\n\n**Clean Steps 1–3, current (2026-11-09).** Record: [round log](../clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md) §§16–47.\n- **Step 1 configuration: c8, adopted by Alex (D387).** It runs DeepSeek Flash at high effort, with a 64,000-token output cap; a\n  format failure or a cut answer is resent once (D380). It carries Alex's decisions D382–D385:\n  - figures are conditions;\n  - material statements with no thesis are kept without links;\n  - only big matters open a thesis;\n  - no target prices.\n\n  [PRD §12](../clean-arch/PRD.md) holds its system prompt, closing check and schema; a run with no candidate files sends exactly the\n  accepted request.\n- **Acceptance (D386, [protocol](../clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/ACCEPTANCE-PROTOCOL.md)).** 50 fresh articles that no run,\n  old or new, had used: news from 2026-09-05 to 09-07, a holiday weekend with no NVIDIA posts. One draw; two readers and a third on\n  disputes.\n  - helpful 132/139 (core 83/83);\n  - wrongly opened theses 1 of 10;\n  - search 141/143 available repeat pairs, or 141/145 for the whole system (one 11-article story supplies 55 pairs, and revisions are\n    barely exercised);\n  - clear wrong merges 1/57, plus 2 overlaps;\n  - detail retention 22 = 22. This is a storage comparison: every record keeps its own text. It is not a usability test.\n\n  Dev ×3 on the frozen golden-v5 (a lead): helpful 214, 211 and 212 of 234; search 98.9–100%; clear wrong merges about 1%. Step 1\n  cost for the fresh set plus dev ×3: $4.20 at published rates.\n- **Steps 2 and 3** are built in isolation (`clean-arch/step2`, `clean-arch/step3`): search, comparison, grouping and later linking.\n  They are not integrated with production storage. **Step 4 (judging) is not built** on the clean path, so nothing yet shows the clean\n  system can judge an investment view.\n- **Open defects, by product impact** (reflection council, `council/av-codex.md`):\n  1. **The Ohio-guarantee yes/no question is never covered** in the three dev draws; a broader credit-support amount thesis opens\n     instead. Diagnosed (round log §48): the model folds the unsigned guarantee into the open aggregate thesis because the request\n     uses \"covers\" both for \"is evidence for\" and for \"already asks this question\". The same clause sits under link clutter.\n  2. **A revenue-share thesis was opened** (Vera Rubin's share of data-center revenue), the class-A boundary of D382.\n  3. **Link clutter.** A blind recheck of the 34 disputed fresh links found 19 weak and 2 wrong; 10 accepted links mixed in as controls were all useful; the other 49 links were not rechecked.\n     The model's own account names topic or theme overlap and inference chains, and c8's closing check never reads back a link. This is\n     a likely cause, with no fix proven. Alex approved designing a protected link check through the council; a first draft was stopped\n     because it would drop founding premises and contrary facts.\n  4. **Unverified passages:** 3 of 188 fresh records and 1–3 per dev draw. The mark now travels into Step 2's records, and a reader sees the found source text or an \"Unverified quote\" label. Jev's comparison input still carries the model's text without the mark; changing it changes measured Step 2 behaviour and needs its own measurement.\n  5. **The lidar policy thesis** recurs at the boundary (one dev draw in three).\n- **History.** These earlier states are superseded:\n  - the 2026-11-07 takeover (K5 → K6b; [takeover log](../clean-arch/takeover-2026-11-07/LOG.md));\n  - the 2026-11-08 close-out (K6b test 32/42; Step 2 first lines; [Step 2 log](../clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §12);\n  - the unfiled fix round and the c1–c7 arms (round log §§1–39).\n- **No run is live.** The branch is committed through the D388 line (round log §§48–59).\n\n**Clean Step 1 source investigation (2026-11-07):** A [source-loss census](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md) traces two separate losses in the historical cleaner and records reproducible corpus observations. Offline candidate comparisons expose both recovered content and source-identity or contamination risks. A subsequent [full-page diagnostic](../clean-arch/STEP1-FULL-PAGE-RESULTS.md) completed 102 calls on 17 selected articles and independent source-backed review. It exposes examples of both missing or misattributed source text and model errors that persist despite the right text being present. A Codex–Claude council is reviewing the results and earlier attempts; no source-cleaning candidate is adopted, and the source corpus and production defaults are unchanged.\n\nAlex has authorized correcting the test-source cleaner and test data when their defects are confirmed, while the frozen production configuration and ruler remain intact. The cleaner switched 615 originally fed articles off; a replay reproduces all 615, and direct inspection confirms an actual article excluded because its 9,874-character body was flattened to one line. Other examples include a wrong-site shell, paywall and long challenge code, so the false-exclusion count and a general article-identification repair remain to be established. The [council regroup](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-REGROUP-COUNCIL.md) and [source-preservation design](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PRESERVATION-DESIGN.md) describe an isolated candidate checked against all 34,084 records, with a fake-answer actual-runner handoff and provenance display in the replay renderer. The [five source-control calls](../clean-arch/STEP1-CAPTURE-CONTROLS-RESULTS.md) completed and were source-reviewed; two items contain summary details absent from their attached quotations, and one source-visible risk explanation was not returned. This is selected-case evidence, not a general quality result. Broader source-quality comparison, citation completeness and actual writer integration remain open.\n\n**Clean architecture Step 1 (2026-11-06, isolated work on `clean-arch`):** Alex reopened work with the clean architecture. An experimental prepared-source handoff now preserves the archived page, prior cleaned text, source-role decisions and selected-line mapping without changing the original database; quotations for prepared items must also occur contiguously in the original captured page. Two seven-item runs are available in the [clean architecture PRD](../clean-arch/PRD.md), one in the earlier selection order and one in recorded page-timestamp order. The source selector is not a production service and source ordering is not established event-arrival ordering. The runs expose unresolved semantic problems: citations can omit supporting facts despite an exact location match; model output can change a benchmark into a deployment, reverse a backup relation, omit a named customer or assign an unstated currency. A blinded 84-call check-placement/wording comparison and a 27-call same-article citation-completion diagnostic did not give a clean semantic fix and are not adopted. The latter missed an omitted connectivity condition and a reversed backup relationship, and left six expected evidence reviews unaddressed. A further five-round Codex–Claude council produced a narrowly scoped Step 1 link candidate, independently checked for prompt-contract drift. A six-article, 36-call blind comparison completed; the candidate still produced an explicitly self-contradictory link and both arms consistently omitted the same Kyber report and company response. It is not adopted and has not changed the active PRD prompt. A trace confirms that both the Kyber report and open Kyber thesis were supplied, and the raw model answers omitted the report and denial; the reason is not established. See ../poc/data/clean-step1-kyber-trace-2026-11-06/TRACE.md. An isolated six-call comparison showed that hiding the Kyber thesis did not recover the report or denial in any draw. A second six-call comparison returned them in 3/3 draws with the original Kyber paragraph alone and 0/3 with the full article; length, position and surrounding content changed together, so the reason is not isolated. Excerpt outputs have source-attribution limitations. Neither experiment changed state or established a production fix. (Superseded: the next task then was a whole-article coverage approach; the current next task is under \"One next task\".) See ../clean-arch/STEP1-IMPLEMENTATION-COUNCIL.md. The earlier four-round council agreed that no tested semantic fix is ready; its next investigation is a source-backed review of the 49-item historical run and its 160 evidence records, with overlapping issue flags and separate article-level omissions, then a separate comparison of the seven-item replays. See ../clean-arch/STEP1-COUNCIL-REVIEW.md. Preserve relevant evidence while identifying the next intervention; production/frozen history remains as described below. See [Step 1 run log](../clean-arch/STEP1-RUN-LOG.md) and [English reading page](../clean-arch/architecture.html).\n\n**Sealed (D379, 2026-11-06):** Alex asked to clean up and seal this version while he re-plans the system design. Work stops here and is sealed as it is: main's working tree is committed on branch `seal/2026-11-06` (tag `sealed-2026-11-06`) and merged into `main` on Alex's word (PR #10); the sealed split and two side experiments are pushed on their own branches (D379). No PRD 11 experiment is open, and the E1 next task below is withdrawn. The next task is Alex's re-planned system design.\n\n**E1 diagnosed (2026-11-06, Alex chose E1):** of 57 helpful matters frozen production wrote and then dropped, two readers agree on 17 link misses (4 core), 3 opening misses (1 core) and 24 small matters the standard itself drops; 10 are disputed on the fallback clause and 3 were quote refusals. Opening is not the experiment. The core of the link misses is the model reading a question's period, population or named configuration as a boundary; only 8 of the 13 window draws it dropped were lost to the product. No production change; $0.02 of probes. My recommendation was to fold that narrow change into the next extraction version; withdrawn by the seal (D379). Details: `docs/research/2026-11-06-e1-no-home-drops/README.md`, PRD 11 E1.\n\n**Split extraction sealed as a failed experiment (D378, 2026-11-06):** the split (a new-question call, an evidence call and record preparation) was benchmarked against the frozen poc-v108 and failed. Its stop line fired: helpful matters 156, 156, 159 against 166, 170, 162, with about 15% higher cost. One screened revision kept more noise than production. It is sealed in the worktree `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` (tag `split-extraction-failed-sealed-2026-11-06`), and nothing of it is in main. What it taught is written as proposed production experiments in `docs/prd/11-production-experiments-from-split.md`: E1 no-home helpful drops, E2 duplicate questions, E3 ladder checks with D186, E4 judge refusals, E5 D203's revision day, E6 the measurement kit. Each opens only on Alex's word.\n\n**Question-catalog limit removed (D377, 2026-11-05):** Alex also directed removal of the separate 150 KB catalog rejection. The research reader in this worktree now has neither the catalog nor evidence byte limit; it retains full scoped questions, historical boundaries, dependencies and evidence. A portable 326,996-byte catalog test returns all 400 new questions and expands evidence both after reading the catalog and directly; the old 150 KB guard fails the same test in a scratch copy. The saved 69-question pilot catalog and all 69 saved evidence expansions match their original content hashes. Details: `docs/research/2026-11-05-catalog-limit/`. The full suite remains pending until the paid seven-day run no longer competes for resources. The run remains in its isolated worktree and does not call this research reader.\n\n**Evidence reader limit removed (D376, 2026-11-05):** Alex directed removal of the reader's 1 MB evidence-expansion rejection. In this worktree, `research_context.Reader.expand` now returns the entire evidence payload without that cap, while the catalog's separate limit remained at that checkpoint; D377 subsequently removed it. On the saved pilot ledger, all 69 individual question expansions, the four-question 1,931,225-byte pilot request and the entire 3,454,076-byte catalog produce the exact same content as the pre-change high-limit baseline; the portable oversized-ledger test passes and fails when the old cap is restored in a scratch copy. Details and snapshot-specific measurements are in `docs/research/2026-11-05-reader-limit/`. The seven-day diagnostic runs in `/Users/lchingen/hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/poc/data/split-fresh-seven-day-2026-11-05-v2/` with its pinned code; it does not call this research reader. Read its status through its `snapshot.sh`; do not edit its package code while it is live. This change is not an acceptance of seven-day extraction or judgement quality.\n\n**Start-state correction (2026-11-05):** Alex requires a research library established from real material available before the test start, then extraction against that state. The prior empty-library diagnostic cannot represent this case. Our own mistakes and the current 68-question provenance audit are in `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/own-mistakes-2026-11-05.zh.md`. Two new A-only calls were run on 2026-08-29 articles using the 68-question prior-day snapshot; both proposed the same aggregate supply-commitments topic. A source/context review judged it distinct from the existing specific SK Hynix question. A third A-only call on an author-body excerpt about DLSS 5, already tracked by v22, passed; the prompt also restricts software updates, so the reason is not established. No writer or dedup ran; source-history completeness remains unverified, and this is not scored acceptance. Details: ../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/start-state-diagnostic-2026-11-05.zh.md.\n\n**Measurement correction (2026-11-05):** Alex clarified that a gold-worthy topic already in the supplied library should not open again; an absent worthy topic should open. The earlier simple-A claim of a \"missed\" new topic was not scored against that rule and is withdrawn as a correctness conclusion. In the actual experiment, a Kyber topic was shown and passing follows Alex's topic-level rule; whether its exact release question captures the shipping milestone remains unverified. The experimental pre-article snapshots differ from the replay's day-start snapshots; 9 of 10 inspected examples differ. The current uncommitted replay default does not build a cold-start library; committed main HEAD still uses the old seed and startup filing path. Historical lexical coverage has a confirmed unrelated-topic match. The frozen gold and past scores remain unchanged. Audit: `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/measurement-context-scrub-2026-11-05.zh.md`.\n\n**Simple new-question diagnostic (2026-11-05):** At Alex's direction, an isolated prompt returned new questions plus article evidence without asking for product database fields. Four previously seen real dev articles each ran once: three returned one candidate, one returned none, all four quotes matched their source, and the gold-listed GPU backstop amount question was not proposed. Whether that omission is a new-topic miss depends on whether the existing library covers the topic. Two other candidates require source and independent-significance review; the actual experimental snapshot already had a Kyber topic, so its pass follows Alex's topic-level rule; whether its exact release question captures the shipping milestone remains unverified. This is not a production or full-pipeline acceptance. Details: `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/simple-new-question-results.zh.md`.\n\n**Split prompt structure v3 read diagnostic (2026-11-05):** One real reading-stage call on a previously seen article found the revenue/margin guidance and Trainium scale but omitted the gold China revenue/market-loss matter. The prewritten stop condition was triggered and the linking call was not sent. This is one draw and not a production or end-to-end result. See `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/structure-v3-read-result.zh.md`. D374 removes the model sampling field from active settings and default configs; historical config files and archives retain their original bytes.\n\n**Split prompt structure v2 small test (2026-11-05):** Thirty calls on two previously seen golden-v5 dev articles completed in `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review`. The blind model reader found complete helpful matters in the fresh control versus new A+B of 6/19 vs 10/19, 11/19 vs 7/19, and 9/19 vs 12/19. The new arm also retained more gold not-kept matters in each draw; B missed useful margin guidance and one answer reused IDs, with a demonstrated matching collision. This is not a production acceptance. See `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/structure-v2-results.zh.md` and `reports/2026-11-05-parallel-structure-v2.html`.\n\n**Split extraction small test (2026-11-05):** At Alex's request, the isolated `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` worktree ran two real golden-v5 articles once through each split lane. An independent model reader found 7 of 19 helpful gold matters fully retained, two partial, two written then dropped, eight missed, and one retained gold not-kept matter. Three saved v108 draws on these articles retained 7, 9 and 12 of 19, but used 22 seeded questions while the new test used 8/10-question snapshots; these are not directly comparable. The experiment remains unaccepted; see `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/small-scored-test-20261105.md` and `reports/2026-11-05-split-small-test.html`.\n\nAs of November 5, 2026; decisions through D374. [Decisions](DECISIONS.md) holds what is decided now; the [design page](system-design.html) and the [module PRDs](prd/README.md) hold what the code must do; the [README](../README.md) holds startup, build and run instructions.\n\n## Future evidence scoring (D372)\n\nAlex decided future gold scoring should measure helpful versus not helpful evidence without requiring separate scores for core and supporting. Use frozen golden-v5 as-is: combine core and supporting as helpful, treat not-kept as unhelpful, and keep optional entries neutral. A counting report has not yet been implemented. Historical scores retain their original definitions. D373 removes the stop caused by a separate concern about three question-gold entries: keep the gold unchanged, continue scoring and disclose the concern; see `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/ruler-conflict-2026-11-05.zh.md`.\n\n## Latest decision (D369)\n\nAlex decided in D369 to capture core and supporting evidence without a strict boundary and in D370 to remove the 22 preset starting questions and separate earnings-report import. The replay entry now defaults to `poc-v118` (D374), refuses old seeded ledgers and baseline configurations, and continues to read existing non-seeded questions; earnings releases remain normal source items. This change has local contract tests but has not passed end-to-end product acceptance. The frozen historical rulers and results remain unchanged.\n\nThe small 2026-11-05 study used three archived NVDA articles and an 8/28 historical 68-question snapshot: without versus with an appended report, independent AI review found 14/27 versus 13/27 complete matters. This measured appended extraction context, not the distinct former coverage-start step; D370 removes both by Alex's decision. Full traces and limitations are in the separate `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` worktree under `docs/research/2026-11-05-real-input-evidence/existing-library-results-2026-11-05.md`.\n\n\n**One-day smoke completed (2026-11-05 UTC):** Alex requested a quick 1–2 day end-to-end test. One day, 2026-08-29, ran in `/Users/lchingen/hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/poc/data/v117-one-day-smoke-2026-11-05/`: config v117, an 8/28 historical library, 40 extraction calls, 93 evidence records written, four new questions, 54 judged and zero terminal failures. Two judgement attempts were refused and corrected. A Microsoft revenue forecast of $391.1bn was assigned to a rung whose upper bound excludes $391.1bn; the same article's actual quarterly revenue and next-quarter guidance were dropped with no home. Some source dates and bodies are not verified, and an independent 14-article sample review was incomplete due to truncated text. This is not semantic or production acceptance. Findings and scope: `docs/research/2026-11-05-real-input-evidence/one-day-v117-smoke-2026-11-05.zh.md` in that worktree. The clean source-text comparison and its condition-hidden review are complete; council discussion of next steps is ongoing.\n\n**D371, in progress:** Alex directed that present and forward-looking revenue and financial information should be tracked. Diagnose the same-day extraction and filing losses before designing a general change. No new financial tracking implementation has been accepted yet. The old frozen standard and gold are unchanged. The original prompt-only pilot failed design review. The user approved an exact prompt addition for an experiment; it is not a production implementation. Four paired real-article diagnostic calls per arm completed for the exact approved sentence: v74i versus experimental role-d371-pilot. Microsoft next-quarter guidance and NVIDIA FY2028 guidance still dropped in the treatment; a single draw is not acceptance or proof of causation. Details: docs/research/2026-11-05-real-input-evidence/d371-prompt-pilot-results.zh.md (isolated worktree). The requested full prompt audit and restructure were drafted experimentally; five real-article paired diagnostic requests per arm completed. Microsoft revenue guidance was missed in both arms, and the draft produced unsupported original dates, a quote refusal and suspect links. No production prompt change or acceptance; see restructure-pilot-results.zh.md in the isolated worktree.\n\n**Clean Step 1 coverage council (2026-11-06):** Codex and Claude Code independently reviewed the existing attempts and completed three discussion rounds. The proposed next research candidate moves the existing evidence-reading block ahead of the thesis-opening block while preserving the article, open-thesis state and paired output. It is a hypothesis, not an implemented or validated fix. A complete prompt/contract audit and independent review precede any controlled Luna comparison. Historical paragraph-based and source-first experiments, their noise and attribution problems, and the rejected overlap-based merge are recorded in [the council review](../clean-arch/STEP1-COVERAGE-COUNCIL.md). No new Luna experiment was run in the council.\n\n**Clean Step 1 coverage order result (2026-11-07):** The isolated 54-call clean comparison completed and all answers were reviewed blind. Moving the evidence-reading block recovered a known Kyber report and denial in one of three candidate answers against none in three controls, with attribution blur; it also produced unsupported gaming-to-AI links in two candidate answers and no controls. It is not adopted. A separate exact-quote example misattributes an executive's prediction in the standalone summary. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-COVERAGE-ORDER-RESULTS.md) record all selected cases, uncertainties and source-fidelity work; no production ledger was written.\n\n**Clean Step 1 fidelity council (2026-11-07):** Codex and Claude Code completed five rounds on source attribution, conditions, dates and meaning. No tested fix or root cause is established. The agreed source-to-record review of 49 articles / 160 evidence items and the subsequent isolated field-order comparison are complete; the later 54-answer / 217-record repeated sample remains separate. Prior source-first, quote-first and follow-up-review experiments and their limits are recorded in [the fidelity council](../clean-arch/STEP1-FIDELITY-COUNCIL.md). No Luna experiment or production change was made in this council.\n\n**Clean Step 1 fidelity review (2026-11-07):** All 49 saved source packets and 160 original evidence items have received a source-and-record review; focused independent cross-checks cover the findings and selected controls. The [review and article replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-FIDELITY-REVIEW.md) distinguish source contamination and missing source material from model meaning, citation, date and link issues. The [isolated schema property-order diagnostic](../clean-arch/STEP1-CITATION-ORDER-RESULTS.md) completed all 66 calls and condition-blinded article reviews. Several omissions and semantic errors persisted; it did not establish a fix and is not adopted. It used saved articles and before-states without changing production.\n\n**Clean Step 1 source handoff council (2026-11-07):** The ongoing [council and offline checks](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-HANDOFF-COUNCIL.md) reconstructed 16 matching article-34 controls, all omitting a Kyber report and denial; the cause remains unknown. The 20-article saved source-role overlap review distinguished previously cited AI-generated summary lines and an unrelated recommendation from authored body. The clean runner does not send the saved source-role labels; the council chose to first compare original and previously selected source text on ten known articles. The isolated 60-call source-text comparison and condition-hidden review are complete; [results](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-TEXT-RESULTS.md) show specific contamination reductions alongside persistent omissions and changed meaning. It did not advance state or write production. The isolated six-article low-versus-medium source-fidelity diagnostic completed 36/36 calls and condition-hidden review. [Results](../clean-arch/STEP1-REASONING-FIDELITY-RESULTS.md) show partial improvements alongside persistent source-qualification and attribution concerns; no production setting is changed. The [prompt audit](../clean-arch/STEP1-FIDELITY-PROMPT-AUDIT.md) maps the observed failures to the full request and signed standard. The six-article isolated schema-description comparison completed 36/36 calls and condition-hidden review; the candidate did not preserve the repeated example qualification and is not adopted. [Results](../clean-arch/STEP1-SUMMARY-FIDELITY-RESULTS.md). A further six-article 36-call low-versus-medium comparison used corpus-cleaned sources outside the original 49-item clean replay and a fixed exploratory context; results were mixed, including unsupported links and incorrect attribution/date fields. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-ADDITIONAL-SOURCES-RESULTS.md). The structured-claims candidate was not sent after review revealed scope and complexity concerns. Council review of the next action is ongoing. The date-text schema-description diagnostic completed 48/48 calls on eight known cases, with mixed results; the candidate is not adopted. A selected-run census found twelve literal \"null\" strings in date-text fields, but no production writer impact is established. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-DATE-DESCRIPTION-RESULTS.md). A further preregistered eight-source date comparison completed 48/48 calls; the candidate lost a supported company-article statement date in all three draws on one source and is not adopted. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-DATE-NEW-SOURCES-RESULTS.md). A bounded corpus census confirmed 163 source pages across three explicit generated-summary structures whose labels were removed while some summary text was retained; two NVIDIA cases were reproduced through the cleaner. [Source provenance investigation](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-CENSUS.md). A read-only source-lineage replay reproduced 363/363 cleaned pages with current inputs, preserved ambiguous original-line locations, and surfaced the generated-summary label loss for review. [Source investigation and lineage replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-CENSUS.md). An offline neighboring-context observation exposed only 144 or 149 of 163 known labels and introduced many other page lines; it is not adopted as a general source fix. [Observations and viewer](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-CONTEXT-OBSERVATIONS.md). A new [saved-quote integrity investigation](../clean-arch/STEP1-QUOTE-INTEGRITY-CENSUS.md) reconstructed 360 saved answers across eight overlapping cohorts, including 78 prepared-source calls. Five saved unverified passage instances were complete matches under the existing normalization; an in-memory candidate recovered their original slices, while ten others remained unverified. The normalized source-slice lookup is now installed only in the isolated clean Step 1 runner; saved-answer and entry-point checks are recorded there. Page provenance, Unicode completeness, production resource budgets and writer integration remain open. The existing writer stores only one quote, whereas 63 of 160 evidence items in the 49-article exploratory trace have multiple passages. A [source-first 49-article coverage review](../clean-arch/STEP1-COVERAGE-CENSUS.md) separates material missing from the model input from source-visible omissions in the saved answers, with uncertain matter boundaries labeled. (Superseded: the next investigation then was why supplied material is missed; the current next task is under \"One next task\".) The canonical prompt and production configuration remain unchanged. Adding fallible role annotations remains a separate untested candidate. Council streams and handoffs are in `poc/data/clean-step1-ongoing-council-2026-11-07/`.\n\n## What runs\n\n- **Pipeline (replay default `poc/config/poc-v118.json`, D370, D374).** Every item reads the relevant open questions from the library at the start of its day, then extraction v74i on `gpt-6-luna` proposes evidence and questions; Jev matching with the collision judge matches them; code writes the records; judging-v7 judges changes once a day; pull mode closes due questions. There is no separate seed or coverage-start report import, and reports as source items remain eligible. This default has not completed end-to-end acceptance. The [pipeline guide](poc/pipeline-plan.md) maps stages to code.\n- **Measured:** one company (NVDA) over one 14-day window (2026-07-01 to 07-14), the judgement scored at the window end on the frozen ruler (AGENTS §7.1). Three draws of `poc-v116` against three of `poc-v108` run in the same hours: 53 against 45 agreements of 57; operating margin and capex agree in 3 of 3 draws, never judged without the coverage-start reading ([acceptance](research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/acceptance-design.md)).\n- **Served data:** the service, the MCP server and the standalone page serve the sealed three-month ledger `served-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828` (68 theses, 34 groups; D358), built by the earlier `poc-v34` pipeline, not by the replay default. HTTP `/library`, MCP `thesis_library` and the standalone page share the dated bundle writer.\n- **Idea API:** `poc-v118-idea` (D290, D335, D374): two discovery calls and one adoption and weighting assessment; only adopted theses with positive weights appear, and nonempty plans total 100%. The [Idea contract](poc/idea-card.md) owns its fields and the limits of its scores.\n- **Interface:** the Thesis Library and Explore Ideas workspaces, with the confidence Trigger and an Action preview (no wallet connection or order execution). The [UI specification](poc/thesis-ui.md) and the [integration guide](../poc/ui/README.md) own its behaviour.\n- **Repository:** `main` holds the product, the frozen ruler and the research records; run databases, logs and model archives stay on disk (D353), located by the [data manifest](research/2026-10-25-english/data-manifest.md). Worktrees: `/Users/lchingen/hivemind` is the main checkout, where slice branches are cut from `main`; `/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28` (the extraction branch, merged on 2026-11-03) keeps in its ignored folders the stored end-to-end runs of `poc-v91` to `poc-v95` that later comparisons read (D359); `/Users/lchingen/hivemind-run-v109-2026-11-04` is the finished judging-v8 run's worktree. Older branches stay in Git without worktrees (D359); none is merged work in progress. The clean source-text comparison and its condition-hidden review are complete; council discussion of next steps is ongoing.\n\n## Open defects\n\n1. **Competitor and customer news is filed under the company's revenue questions** instead of the share, capital-spending or custom-chip question it informs (D355; filing is otherwise about 85% right). Share facts are often not written at all, an extraction recall miss. The total-revenue question holds about a quarter of all links (D354).\n2. **One bet opened twice** about one encounter in five: the collision judge splits a seed question from an identical candidate on a fine detail (D354).\n3. **A question the standard rules out** (the lidar legislation) was proposed by the extraction and opened in one draw of three (D354).\n4. **A thesis opened from an article gets no dependency of its own**: the default Jev matching asks for none (`views/jev_matching.py`), although D51 has the matching call choose them; themes still reach every company thesis through the theme block (D191, D195). Found in the 2026-11-05 consolidation; building it is decided (D368).\n5. **Historical coverage-start reading, retired by D370** (D364–D366): the capital-spending line is missed in about one read in six (the question is then left as it was); a labelled guidance figure is written with the fact tier; later reports reach the questions only through the daily stream; `scripts/preflight.py` does not replay the reading's check.\n6. **Measured on one company and one 14-day window**, the judgement scored at the window end only; no version of the current pipeline has completed a longer window (a 60-day run of `poc-v108` was stopped at Alex's word after 2026-07-15, [log row 8](research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md)). The China-share question (v17) swings between draws (0 of 3 in the acceptance, 2 of 3 in its control, 1 of 3 in the frozen draws): two readers found the starting figures did not cause the acceptance's drop, and the byte-identical extraction request kept a \"share fell to zero\" sentence in two candidate draws and dropped it in two control draws. One acceptance draw judged nothing on v16; its cause is not established.\n7. **What a judgement reads grows with the window** (D358). Argument records (D201) were measured as the remedy on 2026-11-04 and not built: judging-v8 agreed 12 of 17 against 15 of 17, judging-v9's card added little at about half again the judging cost; their code was removed at Alex's request ([log rows 4–11](research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md)).\n8. **Seen once, not traced.** A judgement named a shown theme as a dependency and the check refused it twice, stopping a draw for a day (2026-11-02, D266). The sealed run's review recorded that a succession's opening evidence is not linked to the new question (D358), not yet checked against today's code.\n9. **Earlier defects, unchanged.** Matching (build log row 49): another document's copy of a figure can be treated as new evidence; one matter can occupy several groups; same-answer candidate references caused seven chain or duplication decisions of the generative matcher; empty `original_who` values can count as the same party; different names for one population cause two of nine Jev merge misses. Nine 2026 aggregate-spending theses under `ai-capex` each have a different population; whether to combine them is a product choice. Extraction's instructions remain narrower than the PRD's important-question rule and omit the personal-memo exception, and extraction can change a quantity's meaning before matching (a guarantee amount against financing provided). Classification keeps its [recorded simulation defects](research/2026-10-25-english/classification-existing-defects.txt). The corpus audit recorded 97 official notices with 56 body and file-name collisions, to re-fetch before relying on a rebuilt corpus. An older intermittent search-test failure is untraced. Fresh Idea allocations vary while saved weights do not; the confidence chart applies the current plan to past states and is not a calibrated probability; broad analyst-prose quality and fresh Idea stability remain unaccepted (the [Idea contract](poc/idea-card.md) lists its limits); generative matching dependencies await `matching-v4`. The readers' doubts about the frozen standard, listed in the [extraction branch's state page](research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/current-state-extraction-branch-2026-11-03.md), remain for Alex.\n\nUpstream supply and the human review pool's output remain deferred.\n\n## One next task\n\n**Clean Step 4 (D396): design version 4 is written; it is not READY and waits for two choices of Alex's**\n([design](../clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/DESIGN.md); round log §69).\n\n- **Design status:** three review rounds (`gg`, `gl`, `gm`) each found serious points; version 4 answers the last and has not been\n  reviewed. Its core contract depends on C1 and C2 below.\n- **What the review of 2026-11-10 found** (council `gh`–`gm`, all on stored data, no paid send):\n  - Step 4 is the right next step, and inside it the first thing to build and test is the **ladder step alone**: the call that turns a\n    thesis Step 1 opened into one judged question with its answer ladder and due day. The frozen judge follows only when the ladder\n    step meets its lines.\n  - Production's own ladder sentences are not stable: one request, the same in three draws, gave a bullish end at the low amount\n    twice and at the high amount once, with three different middle bands; the code checks pass all three\n    (`clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/census/production_ladders.txt`).\n  - Of the 34 theses the unseeded draws opened, two blind readers find 12 to 16 that ask more than one thing, 7 to 9 of 18 number\n    questions with no reference figure in the opening article, and 4 that state a calendar due day.\n- **The next task:** put to Alex\n  1. **C1:** a topic that asks more than one thing gets one judged question with the rest shown as not judged (recommended as the\n     first version), or is split as D38 says, or is not judged;\n  2. **C2:** whether the ladder step carries D186 (ranges against guidance, the favourable end registered; recommended), its\n     direction half only, or neither;\n  then revise the design for his answers, review it with a fresh reviewer each round until one finds nothing serious, audit the\n  whole ladder request, and ask his word to build the ladder step and run Stage A (the ladder step against a control that quotes v74i, on the 34 stored theses and the 10 fresh ones).\n- **Open defects recorded by this review:** the frozen judge retried 10, 16 and 18 judgements in the seeded clean draws, 38 of 51\n  reasons a theme cited as a dependency (PRD 11, E4; frozen, a finding for Alex); the same matter takes different shapes between\n  draws (Kyber due end-2027 in one draw and end-2028 in another).\n- **The unseeded measurement (D395):** graded subset 4.0 against 6.33 seeded. Every miss is a question not opened in the ruler's shape.\n  Judged answers 16 of 16, on the ruler's wording and ladder; the system's own questions have never met a judge.\n- **Stage 2 of D392:** clean 18, 18, 17 against v108 17, 15, 16, inconclusive on q12.\n- **Known gap (D394):** capital spending (q12).\n\nNo long job is running.\n## 5. Step 2: find and compare related evidence\n\n### Why this is separate\n\nAn open thesis may accumulate many evidence records. Step 1 needs to understand the article and the open research; it does not need to read every prior statement to do that. Step 2 uses each proposed `{thesis, evidence}` association to find the history worth comparing.\n\n### Find candidate evidence\n\nThe proposed design places semantic search over the evidence library. It uses the thesis and incoming evidence to find top-N related evidence across the current entity, including evidence linked to other theses. The thesis is search context, not a restriction to evidence already linked to that thesis. This also applies when the proposed thesis is new. For example, “raised its revenue outlook” and “expects higher sales” can be related despite different wording. Alex chose this entity-wide scope on 2026-11-06.\n\nSearch returns **candidates**, not a final decision. Alex discussed a starting point of top 10; the implementation and quality requirements need to be measured.\n\nA match outside the returned set will not be considered by the comparison. Search quality therefore matters independently from the model's ability to compare the records it receives.\n\n### Compare with Jev\n\n**2026-11-08 (candidate, [round log](step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md) §§24, 31–35):**\n- Comparison reads the full passages; the 600-character cut is removed.\n- An unlinked record also searches by its own text.\n- `figures` never enter a search or comparison text.\n- Jev is asked the clean path's own piece question, aligned with line 2's test: a repeat reports the stored piece's main statement, and\n  an official's denial of a report is a revision.\n- When a thesis opens, every stored unlinked group from earlier articles is judged against it in batches of ten (a yes/no question; the\n  company's overall results inform only a revenue or earnings thesis). Each judged pair is recorded once.\n- On c6's records: search 97.9%, wrong merges 1.9% (2 of 104), both lines met; line 3 not re-measured.\n\nJev compares the new evidence with the returned evidence. The intended outcomes are:\n\n| Outcome | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Repeat | The new material presents the same statement again. | Another article reports the same company forecast. |\n| Revision | A later account changes the same statement. | The company revises its forecast from 12% to 25%. |\n| New evidence | It is not identified as a repeat or revision of the evidence compared. | A different analyst offers a separate forecast. |\n\nThe words, amount, speaker, source and time can matter to the comparison. A later statement about the same subject is not necessarily a revision. A matching amount alone does not prove that two statements are the same.\n\nThe result “new evidence” is the outcome of the comparison performed; it is not proof that no matching evidence exists elsewhere in the library. When the incoming evidence is judged a repeat of several candidates, it joins only the group Jev judges it most like: the highest same-statement probability, with ties going to the earliest group (Alex, 2026-11-08). A comparison that fails is not saved and is reported.\n\n**Repeat handoff:** when Jev identifies a repeat, the comparison must retain the identity of the existing evidence record it was compared against. Step 3 uses that identity to place the incoming record in the existing record's group, adding its source and any missing thesis link. The typed Jev response need not generate the record ID: the caller can associate the choice with the candidate it supplied. The rule for several matching candidates is stated above.\n\n**Step 2 acceptance (Alex, 2026-11-08).** Three lines apply:\n1. For incoming evidence whose earlier version is in the library, search returns it at least 90% of the time.\n2. At most 2% of merges are clearly wrong.\n3. Grouped dossiers keep no fewer details added by later reports than no merging does.\n\nThe first measurement against these lines is in the [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §12; the isolated modules are `step2/compare.py` and `step3/library.py`.\n\nThe diagram's intended sequence is: use `{thesis, incoming evidence}` to search the evidence library; pass the returned candidates and incoming evidence to Jev; pass the comparison result and the proposed association to Step 3. For a new thesis, search the same entity-wide evidence library. A concrete working draft of the Jev comparison request appears in [section 13](#13-step-2-prompt-jev-working-draft).\n\n## 6. Step 3: save evidence and links\n\nStep 3 takes the proposals from Step 1 and the comparison outcomes from Step 2 and writes the resulting research information.\n\n| Result | What is saved |\n|---|---|\n| New thesis with supporting evidence | The new thesis and its evidence link; save new evidence or reuse a matching record according to Step 2. |\n| New evidence for an existing thesis | The evidence and its link to the thesis. |\n| Repeat | Place the incoming record in the existing evidence record's group: add its source and any missing link to the relevant thesis, and keep the incoming record's own text and fields inside the group. Do not rewrite the existing record, and do not create a separate evidence record for the same statement. |\n| Revision | The new evidence and its source, linked to the earlier statement as a revision. |\n\nA revision does not erase the earlier statement. On 2026-11-06, Alex chose to keep one evidence record for a repeat and add the new source and any missing thesis link. On 2026-11-08 the Step 2 validation showed that keeping only the earlier text discards details a later report adds, such as a guidance condition; Alex then chose to keep each repeat's own text and source inside the stored record's group, without rewriting the original (\"A 继续；跑council loop 开始做\"; [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §7). How a judgement reads a group is being tested. Multiple articles reporting the same statement do not automatically establish independent original sources. The current production writer stores separate repeat records, so this is a change for the clean path and needs implementation and verification; the existing historical records remain available. Step 3 ends when the chosen thesis, evidence and relationships are saved. How they are physically stored and written is a separate implementation decision.\n\n\"\"\"Isolated Step 2 for the clean path (PRD §5): search the entity's stored evidence for each {thesis, incoming evidence} association,\nask Jev how the incoming evidence relates to each candidate, and turn the answers into the outcome Step 3 writes.\n\nSearch: one query per association (the thesis question, then the incoming summary and passages) against each group's original record\n(summary, passages, party, statement date, period), cosine similarity of embeddings, top 10; a record with no association, or one that\nlists the company's figures (a condition), also gets one entity-wide query of its own text (STEP1-CONDITIONS-DESIGN.md §10.1); the union\nof the results is compared. The figures never enter a search or comparison text, so a wrongly paired figure cannot change Step 2 (§14);\nthe comparison reads the full passages (§15). Comparison: Jev's piece question with the production criteria (views/jev_matching), the incoming piece as state, one question\nper candidate. Outcome: any same_statement makes the record a repeat of the candidate with the highest same_statement probability, ties\ngoing to the earliest group (Alex, 2026-11-08: only the group it is most like); a supersedes answer on another candidate becomes a\nrevision link; with no same_statement, supersedes answers make it a revision of those groups; otherwise it is new.\n\n`forward` runs articles in order against the library as it stood after the previous article: records of one article never see each\nother. A failed comparison is refused by the library, reported, and the run continues.\"\"\"\nimport concurrent.futures\nimport math\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]\nsys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT / 'src'))\nfrom hivemind.views import jev_matching  # noqa: E402\n\nTOP = 10\n\n\ndef stated(record):\n    return record['statement_date'] or record.get('statement_date_text') or 'unknown'\n\n\ndef doc_text(record):\n    return '\\n'.join([record['summary'], ' '.join(record['passages']), f\"party: {record['original_party'] or 'unknown'}\",\n                      f\"stated: {stated(record)}\", f\"period: {record['evidence_period'] or 'unknown'}\"])\n\n\ndef query_text(record, thesis_question):\n    return f\"thesis: {thesis_question}\\n{record['summary']}\\n{' '.join(record['passages'])}\"\n\n\ndef piece_text(record, label):\n    return (f\"{label} [{record['tier']}; {record['reporting_speaker']}; original source {record['original_party']}, stated \"\n            f\"{stated(record)}] {record['summary']} | quote: {' '.join(record['passages'])}\")\n\n\ndef query_vectors(vectors, record, thesis_questions):\n    \"\"\"The search queries of one record, in order: one per thesis association, then its own text when it has no association or lists\n    the company's figures.\"\"\"\n    queries = [vectors[query_text(record, thesis_questions[t])] for t in record['thesis_ids']]\n    if not record['thesis_ids'] or record.get('figures'):\n        queries.append(vectors[doc_text(record)])\n    return queries\n\n\n# Jev's piece criteria aligned with the Step 2 merge line's own test (PRD §5 line 2; AGENTS §7.1, one ruler): a repeat is a later report\n# of the stored piece's main statement. Production's wording (views/jev_matching.PIECES) judged \"the same evidence\", so a later report of\n# only a secondary statement inside a stored record (an official's denial carried in the report it denies) was merged as a repeat.\nPIECES = {'same_statement': \"the INCOMING PIECE reports the STORED PIECE's main statement: a retelling, another outlet carrying it, the \"\n                            \"same number in text or a table, or the same speaker repeating it; wording, precision and extra detail may \"\n                            \"differ. If the stored piece carries several statements and the incoming piece reports only a secondary one, \"\n                            \"this is not it\",\n          'supersedes': \"a later version of the stored piece's main statement, changed: the same speaker revises that guidance, \"\n                        \"forecast or event-progress statement, an official confirms or denies that reported claim, or the original \"\n                        \"source revises it; another statement on the same topic is not this\",\n          'different': \"a different fact, figure or matter, a figure for another period or quantity, or only a secondary statement of \"\n                       \"the stored piece, even if on the same topic\"}\n\n\ndef piece_question(text):\n    return {'type': 'choice', 'criteria': PIECES,\n            'instructions': 'How does the INCOMING PIECE relate to the STORED PIECE below? Use only the supplied text. STORED PIECE:\\n' + text}\n\n\nINFORMS = {'true': \"the STORED PIECE bears on the THESIS's own matter (the parties, products, policy or quantity the THESIS names) and \"\n                   \"makes its answer more or less likely, as evidence filed under it would\",\n           'false': \"the STORED PIECE concerns another matter, or is a company-wide result or event that does not speak to the THESIS's \"\n                    \"own matter, or would not change its answer\"}\n\n\ndef informs_question(text):\n    return {'type': 'noul', 'criteria': INFORMS,\n            'instructions': 'Does the STORED PIECE below inform the THESIS stated above? Use only the supplied text; a shared company, '\n                            'product family or word is not enough, and the company\\'s overall results, guidance and estimates inform a '\n                            'thesis about its revenue or earnings path, not one about a competitor, a supplier or a policy unless they '\n                            'speak to it. STORED PIECE:\\n' + text}\n\n\ndef later_link_judge(library, thesis_question, ask):\n    \"\"\"The judge `Library.link_later` calls: one Jev request per batch, the thesis as state, one yes/no question per stored group.\"\"\"\n    def judge(group_ids):\n        questions = {f'q{i}': informs_question(piece_text(library.groups[g]['original'], 'STORED PIECE')) for i, g in enumerate(group_ids)}\n        raw = ask(f'THESIS: {thesis_question}', questions)\n        return {g: raw[f'q{i}']['noul'] >= jev_matching.THRESHOLD for i, g in enumerate(group_ids)}\n    return judge\n\n\ndef cosine(a, b):\n    return sum(x * y for x, y in zip(a, b)) / (math.sqrt(sum(x * x for x in a)) * math.sqrt(sum(y * y for y in b)) or 1)\n\n\ndef search(library, vectors, record, thesis_questions):\n    \"\"\"Candidate group ids: each query's top 10 by cosine against the groups' original records, merged in rank order.\"\"\"\n    candidates = []\n    for query in query_vectors(vectors, record, thesis_questions):\n        ranked = sorted(library.order, key=lambda g: -cosine(query, vectors[doc_text(library.groups[g]['original'])]))[:TOP]\n        candidates += [g for g in ranked if g not in candidates]\n    return candidates\n\n\ndef outcome(answers, order):\n    \"\"\"answers: {group id: {'choice', 'probabilities'}}; order: group ids in creation order.\"\"\"\n    same = [g for g, a in answers.items() if a['choice'] == 'same_statement']\n    later = [g for g, a in answers.items() if a['choice'] == 'supersedes']\n    if same:\n        best = max(same, key=lambda g: (answers[g]['probabilities']['same_statement'], -order.index(g)))\n        return {'kind': 'repeat', 'repeat_of': [best], 'revises': later}\n    if later:\n        return {'kind': 'revision', 'revises': later}\n    return {'kind': 'new'}\n\n\ndef forward(articles, library, vectors, thesis_questions, ask, workers=8, opened=None):\n    \"\"\"articles: lists of Step 3 records in article order. ask(state, questions) returns Jev's answers by question id, or raises.\n    opened: per article, the thesis ids it opened; after the article's records are written, each is linked later to the stored groups\n    that arrived unlinked from earlier articles (`Library.link_later`). A thesis written to with pairs still pending is judged again,\n    against its opening boundary. Returns one decision per record.\"\"\"\n    decisions = []\n    for index, records in enumerate(articles):\n        def decide(record):\n            candidates = search(library, vectors, record, thesis_questions)\n            if not candidates:\n                return record, candidates, {}, {'kind': 'new'}\n            questions = {f'q{i}': piece_question(piece_text(library.groups[g]['original'], 'STORED PIECE'))\n                         for i, g in enumerate(candidates)}\n            incoming = piece_text(record, 'INCOMING PIECE')\n            try:\n                raw = ask(incoming, questions)\n            except Exception as first:   # retried once; a second failure is reported, never silently saved as new\n                try:\n                    raw = ask(incoming, questions)\n                except Exception as error:\n                    return record, candidates, {}, {'kind': 'failed', 'reason': f'{type(first).__name__}: {first}; retry: {type(error).__name__}: {error}'}\n            answers = {g: {'choice': raw[f'q{i}']['choice'], 'probabilities': raw[f'q{i}']['probabilities']} for i, g in enumerate(candidates)}\n            return record, candidates, answers, outcome(answers, library.order)\n        with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=workers) as pool:\n            results = list(pool.map(decide, records))\n        for record, candidates, answers, result in results:\n            entry = {'id': record['id'], 'candidates': candidates, 'answers': answers, 'outcome': result}\n            try:\n                entry['group'] = library.apply(record, result)\n            except Exception as error:\n                entry['refused'] = f'{type(error).__name__}: {error}'\n            decisions.append(entry)\n        article = records[0]['article'] if records else None\n        if article is None:\n            continue\n        written = {t for r in records for t in r['thesis_ids']}\n        retry = [t for t in written if t in library.pending and t not in (opened[index] if opened else [])]\n        for thesis_id in list(opened[index] if opened else []) + retry:   # opened here, or written to with pairs still pending\n            result = library.link_later(thesis_id, later_link_judge(library, thesis_questions[thesis_id], ask), article)\n            library.later_log.append({'thesis': thesis_id, 'article': article, **result})\n    return decisions\n\"\"\"Isolated Step 3 evidence library for the clean path (PRD §6): an in-memory logical store and its dossier text, not the live writer.\n\nA group is one stored statement. Its original record keeps its id and fields unchanged and gives the group its id. A later record that\nStep 2 judged a repeat joins the group as a member and keeps its own id, text, fields and source article. Thesis links belong to the\ngroup and remember the record that brought each one. A revision is its own group, linked to every group it revises; a repeat that also\nrevises another group adds that link to its own group, naming the member that established it.\n\nThe writer refuses rather than guesses: a failed comparison, several repeat targets, an unknown group, or a replayed record whose\nplacement differs from the first time raise `Refused` and write nothing. Physical storage and crash recovery are not provided here.\n\nLater linking (Alex, 2026-11-08: a material statement no thesis takes is kept and linked when a related thesis opens; design\nSTEP1-CONDITIONS-DESIGN.md §§11.1, 17): when a thesis opens, every group whose original arrived with no link, from an earlier article,\nis judged against it in batches. Each judged (thesis, group) pair is recorded once in `judged`; a batch that fails twice stays pending,\nis reported, and is judged again on the next write to that thesis, against the same opening boundary (`opened_at`).\"\"\"\nimport copy\n\nRECORD_FIELDS = ('id', 'article', 'publisher', 'publication_date', 'summary', 'passages', 'original_party', 'reporting_speaker', 'tier',\n                 'statement_date', 'evidence_period', 'thesis_ids')\n\n\nclass Refused(Exception):\n    \"\"\"A Step 2 outcome the library will not write; nothing was changed.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass Library:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.groups = {}       # group id → {'original': record, 'members': [record], 'links': {thesis id: record id}, 'revises': [{'group', 'by'}]}\n        self.placement = {}    # record id → group id\n        self.order = []        # group ids in the order they were created\n        self.judged = {}       # (thesis id, group id) → True | False, the later-linking ledger\n        self.later_log = []    # one entry per later-linking call: thesis, article, linked, pending, judged\n        self.opened_at = {}    # thesis id → the article that opened it: the boundary of its later linking\n        self.pending = {}      # thesis id → group ids whose judgement failed twice and waits for the next write to the thesis\n\n    def apply(self, record, outcome):\n        \"\"\"Write one record with its Step 2 outcome: {'kind': 'new' | 'repeat' | 'revision' | 'failed', 'repeat_of': [group ids],\n        'revises': [group ids], 'reason': text}. Returns the id of the group that holds the record.\"\"\"\n        missing = [f for f in RECORD_FIELDS if f not in record]\n        if missing:\n            raise Refused(f'record lacks {missing}')\n        kind, repeat_of, revises = outcome['kind'], list(outcome.get('repeat_of') or []), list(outcome.get('revises') or [])\n        if kind == 'failed':\n            raise Refused(f\"comparison failed for {record['id']}: {outcome.get('reason', 'no reason given')}\")\n        if kind not in ('new', 'repeat', 'revision'):\n            raise Refused(f'unknown outcome {kind!r}')\n        if (kind == 'repeat') != bool(repeat_of) or len(repeat_of) > 1:\n            raise Refused(f\"a repeat names exactly one group; got {repeat_of} for outcome {kind}\")\n        if kind == 'revision' and not revises:\n            raise Refused('a revision names the group it revises')\n        for group_id in repeat_of + revises:\n            if group_id not in self.groups:\n                raise Refused(f'unknown group {group_id}')\n        target = repeat_of[0] if repeat_of else record['id']\n        if record['id'] in self.placement:\n            if self.placement[record['id']] != target:\n                raise Refused(f\"{record['id']} was placed in {self.placement[record['id']]}, not {target}\")\n            group = self.groups[target]\n            stored = next(r for r in [group['original']] + group['members'] if r['id'] == record['id'])\n            if {k: v for k, v in stored.items() if k != 'thesis_ids'} != {k: v for k, v in record.items() if k != 'thesis_ids'}:\n                raise Refused(f\"{record['id']} names a stored record with different content; a changed record needs a new id\")\n            self._link(group, record)\n            return target\n        if kind == 'repeat':\n            group = self.groups[target]\n            group['members'].append(copy.deepcopy(record))\n        else:\n            group = {'original': copy.deepcopy(record), 'members': [], 'links': {}, 'revises': []}\n            self.groups[target] = group\n            self.order.append(target)\n        self.placement[record['id']] = target\n        self._link(group, record)\n        for revised in revises:\n            if revised != target and not any(r['group'] == revised for r in group['revises']):\n                group['revises'].append({'group': revised, 'by': record['id']})\n        return target\n\n    def link_later(self, thesis_id, judge, before_article, batch=10):\n        \"\"\"Judge every group whose original arrived unlinked from an article before `before_article` against a newly opened thesis.\n        judge(group ids) returns {group id: True | False} or raises. Returns {'linked': [...], 'pending': [...], 'judged': n}.\"\"\"\n        self.opened_at.setdefault(thesis_id, before_article)\n        before_article = self.opened_at[thesis_id]\n        todo = [g for g in self.order if not self.groups[g]['original']['thesis_ids']\n                and self.groups[g]['original']['article'] < before_article and (thesis_id, g) not in self.judged]\n        linked, pending = [], []\n        for start in range(0, len(todo), batch):\n            ids = todo[start:start + batch]\n            answers = None\n            for _attempt in range(2):\n                try:\n                    answers = judge(ids)\n                    break\n                except Exception:   # tried twice, then reported as pending, never saved as a judgement\n                    answers = None\n            if answers is None:\n                pending += ids\n                continue\n            for group_id in ids:\n                self.judged[(thesis_id, group_id)] = bool(answers[group_id])\n                if answers[group_id]:\n                    self.groups[group_id]['links'].setdefault(thesis_id, self.groups[group_id]['original']['id'])\n                    linked.append(group_id)\n        if pending:\n            self.pending[thesis_id] = pending\n        else:\n            self.pending.pop(thesis_id, None)\n        return {'linked': linked, 'pending': pending, 'judged': len(todo) - len(pending)}\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _link(group, record):\n        for thesis_id in record['thesis_ids']:\n            group['links'].setdefault(thesis_id, record['id'])\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def articles(group):\n        \"\"\"Distinct source articles of a group, oldest first; two records from one article count once.\"\"\"\n        seen = []\n        for record in [group['original']] + group['members']:\n            if record['article'] not in seen:\n                seen.append(record['article'])\n        return seen\n\n    def dossier(self, thesis_id):\n        \"\"\"Group ids linked to a thesis, in creation order.\"\"\"\n        return [g for g in self.order if thesis_id in self.groups[g]['links']]\n\n\ndef labelled_passages(record):\n    \"\"\"Each passage as a reader sees it: the source text Step 1 found, labelled 'Quote', or, when Step 1 did not find it (verified\n    value None), the model's text labelled 'Unverified quote'. The model's own text stays in the record's `passages`. A record stored\n    before verification was carried shows its passages as 'Quote'.\"\"\"\n    verified = record.get('verified_passages') or record['passages']\n    return [('Quote', found) if found is not None else ('Unverified quote', passage) for passage, found in zip(record['passages'], verified)]\n\n\ndef record_text(record, label):\n    attribution = record['original_party'] or 'unknown party'\n    if record['reporting_speaker'] and record['reporting_speaker'] != record['original_party']:\n        attribution += f\", reported by {record['reporting_speaker']}\"\n    head = (f\"[{label}] {record['publication_date']} · {record['publisher'] or 'unknown publisher'} · {attribution} · tier {record['tier']}\"\n            f\" · stated {record['statement_date'] or 'unknown'} · period {record['evidence_period'] or 'unknown'}\")\n    quotes = '\\n'.join(f'  {label_of}: \"{p}\"' for label_of, p in labelled_passages(record))\n    return f\"{head}\\n  {record['summary']}\\n{quotes}\"\n\n\ndef group_text(library, group_id, label, labels, member_text=True, notes=()):\n    \"\"\"A group as a reader sees it: the original record, then each later report of the same statement in arrival order, with its own\n    text when `member_text` is set and as a source line otherwise. Distinct articles are counted; repeats are not called confirmation.\"\"\"\n    group = library.groups[group_id]\n    lines = [record_text(group['original'], label)]\n    if group['members']:\n        articles = library.articles(group)\n        publishers = {r['publisher'] for r in [group['original']] + group['members']}\n        lines.append(f'  Later reports of the same statement: {len(group[\"members\"])} records from {len(articles) - 1} further articles '\n                     f'({len(publishers)} publishers in all). Articles repeating a statement do not by themselves confirm it independently.')\n        for member in group['members']:\n            if member_text:\n                party = '' if member['original_party'] == group['original']['original_party'] else f\" · {member['original_party']}\"\n                quotes = ' '.join(f'\"{p}\"' if kind == 'Quote' else f'\"{p}\" (unverified)' for kind, p in labelled_passages(member))\n                lines.append(f\"    - {member['publication_date']} · {member['publisher']}{party}: {member['summary']}\\n      Quote: {quotes}\")\n            else:\n                lines.append(f\"    - {member['publication_date']} · {member['publisher']}\")\n    for revised in group['revises']:\n        lines.append(f\"  Revises [{labels[revised['group']]}].\" if revised['group'] in labels else '  Revises an entry outside this dossier.')\n    lines += [f'  {note}' for note in notes]\n    return '\\n'.join(lines)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:13:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/CONCLUSIONS.md reports/step2-wrong-merge-council-2026-11-09/CONCLUSIONS.md reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/CONCLUSIONS.md'","aggregated_output":"# Step 2: should Jev see retrieval scores?\n\nResearch council, 2026-11-08. Scope: the isolated clean Step 2 evidence-comparison path. No production change, model test, or change to the concurrent Step 1 experiment.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nNeither a benefit nor harm has been established. Keep the present request until a controlled comparison shows an advantage. Similarity is calculated between a search query and a stored group's original text; it is not a probability of same statement. The query may contain a thesis and a candidate may have several scores from different queries. Jev does not receive these scores; it receives the incoming piece as state and one candidate per typed question. It returns a choice and probabilities. Jev's choice probability, not cosine, selects among multiple same-statement results.\n\n## Concrete observation\n\nIn saved c6 X4, incoming 12:e1 was grouped into 1:E1 although the two revenue-sharing arrangements were judged different. Recomputed from the cached exact-text vectors, cosine of the one thesis query against 1:E1's document is 0.8574. Its own document-vector cosine is 0.8654, but the record had one thesis query and that value was not its retrieval query. The saved Jev answer called same_statement at 0.89. This is one error; it neither establishes that score information will harm Jev nor measures a score distribution. Source: `poc/data/clean-step2-forward-c6-2026-11-08/{decisions.json,library.json,embeddings.json,merge-key.json,merge-third-answer.json}`. Query/doc text and cosine are reconstructed as `clean-arch/step2/compare.py` defines them.\n\n## Council\n\nClaude Code session 4c06c24f-8f77-4add-9443-7081a27b7903; original handoff, streams, stderr and challenge are saved alongside this record. Claude initially recommended an offline score-separation gate. Codex challenged it: overlapping marginal scores cannot rule out conditional help, and separation cannot establish Jev uses them. Claude agreed and withdrew that gate, its extrapolation of score ranges from another task, and claims about Jev's internal reasoning. Offline profiling can inform a test but cannot establish efficacy. Vendor describes Jev as a typed, non-text-generating decision model with independently evaluated questions (https://docs.typesafe.ai/introduction); evidence on LLM anchoring is only a hypothesis for Jev, not a finding (https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.01724).\n\n## Proposed discriminating test, not yet registered or run\n\nReconstruct identical saved candidate requests; interleave fresh no-score controls with true-score requests and a score-shuffled control, with fixed score representation and randomization, repeated draws, cache misses, and identical model, candidates and question content except score lines. Make each score's query origin explicit: multiple search queries may yield different scores. A shuffled control tests sensitivity to a number but needs care with singleton/equal-score cases and preserving query origin. A source-blind independent reader judges differing pairs and controls without seeing arm, score, or prior decision. Report wrong merges, missed repeats, incorrect revision classifications and group choices separately, all cases, with same-arm variability, cost and limitations; fixed-candidate comparisons do not prove forward-run performance. The fresh set has few or no revisions; use separately identified stored revision cases without presenting isolated pair results as product-path evidence. Freeze design, audit and preflight under repository rules before any paid send. A score can also encode context Jev did not directly receive (e.g. thesis wording or period), so improvement would not itself establish the mechanism.\n\nNo evidence supports a universal score threshold. This proposal does not alter the accepted configuration or authorize deployment.\n# Council: the fresh Step 2 wrong merge (X22)\n\nRead-only council, 2026-11-09. Codex and Claude Code independently traced the sources, the stored Jev request and answer, the Step 3 group and the reader's text; challenge round in `round2.jsonl`. Original user-requested scope: this one error, no product or ruler edits, no paid model calls.\n\n## What happened\n\nThe earlier 2026-09-05 Motley Fool source reported that Vera Rubin was in production and that management expected it to represent about 20% of Q3 data-centre revenue. The later 2026-09-06 Calcalist source reported that Nvidia said Vera Rubin systems were commercially delivered in August. The two source articles are preserved in `reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/blind-packets.json`, case 9 candidate C7 (key in blind-key.json); these are media reports, not the primary earnings-call transcript.\n\nArchived request for incoming 22:ev-11 against 13:e4 asked Jev to compare their source-backed text. It chose same_statement at 0.49, different 0.36, supersedes 0.15. `clean-arch/step2/compare.py:109` acts on the chosen label without a 0.5 floor, so the incoming record joined 13:e4. Retrieval found this candidate. The model provides no textual explanation; the reason for the choice is unproven.\n\nThe fresh merge review classified the two as different, and a third classified the merge wrong. Independent Codex review agrees; earlier Claude blind review called it overlap, and a separate Claude adjudicator called it different. Thus whether it is a clearly wrong merge rather than an overlap depends on reader judgment. We agree the delivery is not a straightforward repeat of the production statement and revenue forecast. The prior acceptance reference grouped underlying article matters as a later step and marked the delivery revision; that reference uses a broader revision concept than Step 2's comparison, and its unit is the article matter, not necessarily the system record. The most natural reading under the current comparison contract is a separate new statement; revision is a plausible interpretation of the milestone depending on the meaning of 'changed event-progress statement'. The primary statement of the original bundled production-and-forecast record is not formally identified. No change to the reference or contract is proposed.\n\n## Actual and potential impact\n\nThe clean prototype group retains the delivery record's own summary, quote and source as a member. Offline calling `group_text` on the saved group produces the label 'Later reports of the same statement' above that member: misleading grouping and label, not observed text deletion. The acceptance did not test whether this group changed a reader's investment judgment; Step 4 of this clean path was not built for this run. The production writer and live application were not changed. If a future reader treats group members as restatements, it could underweight a new milestone; that downstream consequence is a risk, not an observed result. The first article attributes the 20% forecast to management but does not explicitly attribute production status to Nvidia; its stored summary does, an attribution difference in this case without an established census.\n\n## A tempting threshold\n\nAn offline read of the 57 saved merges showed 2 selected repeats below 0.5: X22 at 0.49 (judged wrong by the original merge readers) and X47 at 0.47 (accepted by both original readers; Broadcom revenue forecasts). A 0.5 floor on this fixed draw would remove one wrong merge and one accepted merge. This is not a root cause proof or general performance measurement. No threshold change is recommended from this single comparison; old production code uses such a floor, while the clean prototype does not, and no decision about adding it to the clean path was established here.\n\nRelevant records: `poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/step2/decisions.json`, `library.json`, `jev.sqlite`, `merge-third-answer.json`; `poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/settled.json` and `prerun-prompt.md`; `clean-arch/step3/library.py`; `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §§43–44. The independent Codex review is `codex-review.md`. One full fresh test produced 1 clear wrong merge of 57 by its readers; this council did not reclassify its immutable result.\n# Step 2 wrong-merge council: candidate direction, not a proven fix\n\n2026-11-09. Alex requested a council to think through a general fix. Codex and Claude independently read the saved requests, source-backed records, decisions, current contracts and prior trials, challenged each other's proposals, and converged after further challenges. This is a read-only investigation; no model experiment, production/configuration change, new ruler, or change to the concurrent end-to-end run was made. Streams and handoffs are preserved in this directory.\n\n## What was found\n\nIn one fresh draw and three dev draws, 363 accepted-as-repeat outcomes received recorded reviews: 316 accepted, 43 overlap and 4 wrong. These are instance counts, not an independent population error rate: the three dev draws reuse the same articles and one source pair appears twice. The three different source-pair cases are: production/forecast versus commercial deliveries; revenue-level forecasts versus growth figures whose relation is not established by the records; and a cloud-provider revenue-share description versus an article describing a developer future-sales share. Same source or topic does not prove their specific statements identical. Source accuracy is separate from what a news report actually says. Reader/model disagreement remains and no historical label is changed. Raw source and count: `stored-census.md` and run-specific `merge-third-answer.json`, `library.json`, `decisions.json`.\n\nThe observed mechanism is that Jev selects repeat and the writer groups accordingly. The model does not produce a verbal account; the reason for these decisions is unproven. Some records bundle multiple related facts. The issue is not uniquely a low score: the cloud/developer case was selected at 0.84 in one draw. On the 363 fixed saved decisions a 0.5 floor would block one reader-marked wrong merge, four reader-accepted merges and one overlap; this is not a forward-run estimate. In an earlier 102-merge diagnostic an overly strict 'nothing new' rule reduced merges to 22, including valid retellings. Numbers are diagnostic, not an efficacy claim.\n\n## One candidate to investigate\n\nBefore grouping a pair that Jev says is a repeat, obtain an independent comparison, with the same source-backed input and without Jev's selection or probabilities: does the incoming statement actually report the old record's main statement? The checker must allow extra details and paraphrases and distinguish different facts, changed claims and cases whose relation cannot be established. This is a proposal for an experiment, not an established root fix, change to D381, a new automatic veto, or proof that another model's errors are independent. We do not choose a model or a disagreement rule in this council.\n\nUse original speaker, the subject, values, period, original date, hedges and actual source when relevant; do not infer the same original claim solely from similar topic, a matching number or common announcement. Different reporting outlets can repeat the same claim while retaining each source. Do not silently adopt D248 boundary 7's dormant exception or a new threshold. Current Jev comparison text excludes an explicit evidence-period field; adding it would be a separate changed input, not silently mixed into this comparison.\n\nThe reviewer must not treat a genuine extra detail as automatically disqualifying; X47 is an accepted example. Conversely a broad shared topic or a shared minor fact need not make a whole new record a repeat; X22 and AWS/three-cloud overlap are controls, with source-backed classifications and disagreement. The acceptance reference and Step 2 use different meanings/units for revision in X22; that concern stays separate and the frozen reference remains unchanged. The source itself does not show that the old production statement and the delivery claim were separate statements by the same original speaker, so the revision label is not settled by this case.\n\n## Evidence that would decide whether to proceed\n\n1. Start with a small, predeclared diagnostic of the three reader-marked wrong source pairs and counterexamples: accepted repeats with added details (including X44 and X47), true updates/denials, different speakers/periods, and known missed repeats. Use the identical Jev comparison payload for the independent comparator. It sees no prior result. A source-blind reader who did not write the proposal reviews the source and differences; same-day fresh Jev controls and request fidelity are required for model comparisons. A small pilot is a lead, not a performance claim.\n2. If the pilot provides an actual signal without obvious counterexample regressions, prospectively freeze an expanded evaluation of all 363 historical merged instances and known missed repeats and revisions, marking the model-written judgments and 43 contested overlaps; do not relabel frozen gold or infer that 363 are independent. Assess wrong groupings, correct repeats split, unmerged repeats, update errors and source fidelity; give every case and disagreement. Fixed-history comparison cannot establish new forward-history behavior, latency or downstream judgment impact.\n3. Only if those comparisons merit it, plan a separate isolated forward-path evaluation with a fresh same-day baseline, held-out articles and eventual reader/judgment effects, including cost and speed. Do not use the 50 fresh-test articles as previously unseen again. No new scorer substitutes for source-backed trace review.\n\nA useful test must show the independent comparison catches cases Jev misses without unnecessarily breaking true repeats or losing/relabeling updates. If errors are correlated, correct repeats are split, or the model's additional cost exceeds the observed value, do not adopt it. The pilot may be inconclusive, especially with few confirmed wrong pairs. It cannot prove absence of a problem.\n\nWhether a reviewer disagreement creates a new group, a pending case or some other outcome changes product behavior; it is not decided by the council and must be shown to Alex with evidence. Likewise source-matter revision vs statement revision and risk trade-offs need an explicit decision if the existing rules do not resolve them. The final delivered investment judgment has not been shown to suffer from X22; the currently running July end-to-end comparison does not contain this September case and cannot isolate this merge.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:15:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^## (9|10|11|12|13)[. :]|''^### (9|10|11|12|13)[. :]|D381|D168|D202|D204'\"' clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2338:## D168 · 2026-10-25 · Four corrections found by the D167 measurement: one repeat rule, one replace definition, one succession rule, one missing answer\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2348:- Asked \"要不要把 Jev 定为默认的匹配引擎\" after the D167/D168 measurement (log rows 44–49), Alex answered: \"要的。你的抽取，trace和当前抽好的db都很宝贵，这些都留下来然后开始做这个阶段的收尾\".\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2349:- Decided: `poc-v23` (extraction v10, `matching.engine: \"jev\"`, model `jev-1.14.0`, with D168) is the default configuration of the replay (`scripts/replay.py`, which now also requires the ledger and archive it writes). The service's idea route keeps its adopted configuration `poc-v9` (D135/D145) on the sealed ledger and its search index; moving the service to the English library needs a new index and is a separate step. The generative matcher stays a configured alternative (`engine: \"llm\"`); old configurations and stored archives replay unchanged.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2431:- Use all 53 July 27 items in the preserved v23 Jev library. First build a current-code canonical response fixture, reusing exact archived requests and completing only missing current requests. Older stored Jev calls predate D168 criterion wording and cannot be claimed as exact current requests. Keep the original files read-only. Fixture completion is not a fresh latency control.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2450:- The recorded August run predates D168 (its Jev requests lack one relation option and carry the older replace wording, and D168 changed matching and writing), so its answers cannot serve the current code for more than one item. [Study](../poc/reports/2026-10-26-multi-day-oracle/results.md) (`poc/concurrency_study/multi_day.py`, $0): the serial path and the parallel day (16 extraction, 8 Jev workers) each replay August 1–15 (221 items) from the July 31 ledger, every call answered by one deterministic oracle of the request bytes (the item's recorded extraction answer with each evidence line tagged by the request digest; Jev choices drawn from the digest; a valid judgement).\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2594:## D202 · 2026-10-27 · The writer retires nothing on a matching label: a replacement is recorded as a revision and both pieces stay in force\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2604:- Found while reading D202's regression week: the extraction answer's `original_day` is a free string (54 of the three-month run's 2,369 raw pieces carry a month, a range or text; 1,445 are empty) and the writer filled every empty day with the item's publication day, against D245's rule that an unknown stays unknown. A range read as \"older than\" the denial it answered dropped SemiAnalysis's Kyber delay report from the week ledger.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2606:- The judge's revision note is labelled as what it is: \"Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of eX; both are in force\" (D202's census: 19 of 37 recorded revisions carry a wrong label).\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2607:- Census of the other silent loss in the same path, read pair by pair ([labels](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/repeats-40-labels.json)): of 40 sampled `repeat` decisions (1,034 in the run), 32 are pickups, 8 merged a different or richer statement into an old piece and lost its content (an estimated 200 of 1,034). The mechanism is D202's: a one-sample label triggers an irreversible write (a repeat adds a source and discards the incoming text). The fix is the argument record's reader side (store every source record, relate `same_as`, collapse same-claim groups for the judge); not built here.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2608:- D201 measured further ([results](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/results.md), 2026-10-28 00:00–01:20, $0.85): a fresh 16-case gold written from the complete expansions before any send (action 11/12 and 4/4 held out; nothing wrongly retired 16/16; same-claim 8/12 with one class: a second outlet's own reporting treated as independent, which the contract could not express); arm 2 split claim identity from source independence (the class is gone; two new judgement weaknesses appeared); three draws per disagreeing case under both prompts show the arm-to-arm differences are within one draw's noise, while in 144 draws no must-not-touch piece was ever marked same or revised. Decided: the contract keeps arm 2's split; prompt arms stop here; the variable fields are annotations under D202 and the next build is the argument record (steps 5 and 6), where a wrong relation in one draw is corrected by the next instead of being frozen into the ledger.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2610:## D204 · 2026-10-28 · A repeat is stored as its own source record; the merge becomes a labelled relation\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2612:- Alex: \"补那个洞，跑一周验收，仔细思考很重要\". The hole (D203's census): on a `repeat` decision the writer adds the incoming item as an outlet of the old piece and discards the incoming text; 8 of 40 sampled repeats (20%, about 200 of 1,034 in the run) had merged a different or richer statement, and its content was gone. The mechanism is D202's: a one-draw label (same_statement ≥ 0.5) triggers an irreversible write.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2616:- Accepted ([results](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/results.md)): 40 of 40 sampled repeat decisions replayed through the new writer keep the piece, the relation and both pieces in force (the 8 lost contents are present); the week through the whole path ran with none failed at $0.23, wrote 45 same-claim relations and no merge, and the judge's reasons where a pair was in view treat the relays as one source (\"ultimately trace to SemiAnalysis\", \"not independent confirmation\"). The three-month run may resume on `poc-v34` with D202–D204 in the writer; that is Alex's call.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2781:Alex explicitly asked to deeply restructure the existing prompt after reading decisions, PRD and prior failures, then measure the effect. This authorizes a new isolated fourth-step prompt arm after D221 failed, not a production default, new storage layer, retrieval change, or full-pipeline run. The [registered design](research/2026-10-29-prompt-restructure/design.md) separates reading the whole current source, deciding the strength and limits of each claim, comparing history, and choosing a research action. D201's independent investment judgement remains the new-question threshold; D202–204's preservation and source-identity rules remain. D221's use-relative standard and D220's necessary-meaning standard are unchanged. Whole-prompt restructuring is one experimental configuration; sentence-level causal attribution is not claimed. Six known development sources plus two historical regression cases are sent three times per arm with the same schema constraints, payload, Haiku model and 6000-token ceiling; the treatment also orders evidence fields before action, within the existing $0.60 cap including two diagnostic continuations. All raw answers are read and recorded, including failures.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2966:  7. **The same statement carried by several outlets** (H7, \"A\"): code treats pieces with the same known original speaker, original day and figures as one (an unknown speaker or day never matches: 297 of the sealed run's 4,298 pieces have both unknown); the judge sees it once with the outlet count; every piece is still stored. Changes D204's \"both shown to the judge\" for this case, and is a chosen exception to AGENTS §3 \"equal values alone never establish identity\"; the risk put to him with the question (a qualification such as \"excluding China sales\" hidden; D204's 8 of 40 wrong merges) is measured on the G3 gold before it is switched on, as is the accuracy of the original-speaker and original-day fields it relies on.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:3172:`main` and `fix/extraction-quality-2026-10-28` both numbered from D181 after D170. At the merge the branch kept its numbers (D171–D274) and the 62 decisions `main` recorded were renumbered in order: D181→D275, D182→D276, D183→D277, D184→D278, D185→D279, D186→D280, D187→D281, D188→D282, D189→D283, D190→D284, D191→D285, D192→D286, D193→D287, D194→D288, D195→D289, D196→D290, D197→D291, D198→D292, D199→D293, D200→D294, D201→D295, D202→D296, D203→D297, D204→D298, D205→D299, D206→D300, D207→D301, D208→D302, D209→D303, D210→D304, D211→D305, D212→D306, D213→D307, D214→D308, D215→D309, D216→D310, D217→D311, D218→D312, D219→D313, D220→D314, D221→D315, D222→D316, D223→D317, D225→D318, D226→D319, D227→D320, D228→D321, D229→D322, D230→D323, D231→D324, D232→D325, D233→D326, D234→D327, D235→D328, D236→D329, D237→D330, D238→D331, D239→D332, D240→D333, D241→D334, D258→D335, D259→D336. References in `main`'s files were renumbered with them; commit messages keep the old numbers.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:3792:- The run (`english-v34-nvda-3m-2026-10-28`, poc-v34, the D202–D205 writer) completed 59 days, 2026-07-01 to 08-28: 1,232 items, 69 theses, 7,091 calls, $5.80. It is sealed read-only as `poc/data/sealed-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/` (SQLite online backups, SHA256SUMS, logs and scripts; the run folder is left resumable). The front end reads it from 16:35: the served defaults switched in the five places the README names (main checkout), `poc/mock/index.html` rebuilt, the API restarted in `service-3m-2026-10-28/` (`/days` 2026-07-01..08-28, 69 theses).\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:3796:- Amendment, 17:05. Alex, on seeing an empty thesis in the front end (\"why is this here? there is no evidence and why is this a thesis\"; then \"你直接把它移除，这是我们自己的失误\"): v68, \"What stand-alone CPU revenue will NVIDIA report for calendar 2027?\", opened on the run's last day by the succession rule (D168: same quantity, next period, after v24) from one statement (\"$20 billion in 2026 and more than $40 billion in 2027\"); the writer linked that statement to v7 and v24 (as a repeat of the $20 billion piece) and not to the successor it opened, so v68 stood with no evidence and, judging running only on arrivals, no judgement. Class: 1 of 69 theses with no evidence; 1 of 2 successions (v9 after v8 has 59 pieces). The sealed folder stays untouched; the front end, the API and the page build now read `poc/data/served-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/views.sqlite`, the same ledger with v68's rows removed (views, view_keys, rungs, successions, view_switches, views_fts; the append-only triggers dropped in that copy only; its README lists this). The writer gap (a succession's opening evidence is not linked to the successor) is an open defect in `writing.py` for the next slice.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:4101:## D381 · 2026-11-08 · Clean Step 2: a repeat joins only the group it is most like; three acceptance lines\nclean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md:52:    - and, for repeats, whether the later item is richer or qualified (D204's risk).\nclean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md:203:## 9. Alex's decision (2026-11-08)\nclean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md:209:## 10. Grouping round (2026-11-08, council loop)\nclean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md:279:## 11. Log rows\nclean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md:285:## 12. Close-out (2026-11-08)\nclean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md:338:## 13. Embedding score to Jev: interrupted exploratory probe (2026-11-09)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:17:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat RESULTS-DRAFT.md results-codex.md results-claude.md'","aggregated_output":"(eval):5: parse error near `end'\n(eval):5: parse error near `end'\n# Step 2 same-input paired diagnostic — completed, selected pairs\n\n2026-11-09. User authorized the six-case × three-arm × three-draw experiment. All 54 unique actual HTTP requests completed; no retry, no reported failures or missing usage. The completion marker, immutable request bodies, preflight and fsynced attempt/result events are in `probe-2026-11-09/`. Outputs below are the raw typed labels in draw order. Jev full sees the original whole candidate batch; Jev single and DeepSeek single receive the same pair text and definitions, though their providers and output formats differ. B01 and P01 are controls; P01 is a direct denial also quoted as background in the earlier source, not a clean later-forecast revision. The six source-backed opinions were frozen before sending, and R05's stored source-attribution field is known to be suspect. They are AI source opinions and not frozen gold.\n\n| Pair / source-backed lead | Jev full (3) | Jev single (3) | DeepSeek single (3) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| B01 same: valid Broadcom repeat | same, same, same | same, same, same | same, same, same |\n| B05 different: company 70% guidance vs customer implied doubling | supersedes, supersedes, different | different, different, different | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes |\n| B10 different: Vera Rubin deliveries vs expected revenue share | same, different, different | same, different, same | different, different, different |\n| H01 same: two reports of acquisition | different, different, different | different, different, different | same, same, same |\n| P01 supersedes: official denies reported Kyber delay | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes |\n| R05 different: company does not confirm/deny Shield discontinuation | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes | different, different, different |\n\nRegressions first: B05 DeepSeek selected the wrong relation in 3/3, while fresh Jev single got it right in 3/3. Jev full erred 2/3, showing request-shape sensitivity for this pair. This is a serious repeatable regression against the fresh single-question control, so the council recommends stopping the comparator line. The prewritten stop controls explicitly named B01 and P01, not B05; this is a new finding, not a claimed predeclared stop trigger. Do not add cases, amend prompts or move to product integration to chase an aggregate score. Selected improvement leads H01 and R05: DeepSeek 3/3 same/different against Jev full and single 0/3 correct, and B10 3/3 different against Jev single 1/3 different (Jev full 2/3 different). Both controls B01/P01 remain in 3/3 across arms. One same-day experiment on hand-selected pairs cannot estimate overall precision or recall, show ranking, compare full product behavior or measure source-level independence. DeepSeek model, provider and output format differ; pair comparison is not proof of the cause.\n\nRecorded usage: Jev full 78,795 input/7,112 output tokens; Jev single 15,867 input/773 output; DeepSeek 13,149 prompt (5,504 cached; 7,645 uncached), 8,582 completion including 8,450 reasoning tokens. All report usage. Reservation $0.182012616, not a bill. Using the repository's dated table (Jev input $0.042/M, DeepSeek input $0.30/M, cached input share .10, output $1.20/M), approximate arithmetic cost $0.0167; actual provider charges can differ.\n\nNo original text was dropped or rewritten by this diagnostic. No product code/config/gold changed. Independent blinded source/output rereads are in progress; final council must challenge interpretation and any disputed source and identify one next discriminating action. The competitor produces a 3-label answer, no group score/rank.\n\n## Deterministic existing product-outcome replay ($0)\n\nReplayed each fresh **full-request Jev answer** through unchanged `compare.outcome`, using the original candidate IDs and existing creation order. B05: 3/3 repeat of `14:e2`; the wrong `9:e5` revision appears 2/3. B10: 1/3 repeat of `13:e4`, 2/3 new. H01: 3/3 new, so all three repeat misses have actual Step 2 effect on this fixed history. R05: 3/3 revision of `119:e1`. P01: all three repeat `45:e2` and attach revision edges to **both** `45:e1` (the checked target) and `45:e3`; the additional edge was not adjudicated in this six-pair test. Thus P01's target answer staying correct is not evidence that every relation written on that record is correct. This is deterministic replay on the saved fixed candidate state, not a new forward run. The independent comparator has only pair labels, so no product group choice is claimed for it.\n\n## Independent blind reread (partial)\n\n`result-blind-codex.md` read all six complete source pairs and 54 anonymous labels without arm or draw names. It agreed with the pre-send readings for B01, B05, B10, H01 and P01. For R05 it chose different but considered `supersedes` unresolved, because the same publisher's subsequent coverage uses more cautious wording and reports a changed retailer listing; it does not explicitly retract the original inference and Nvidia does not confirm or deny discontinuation. Therefore describe R05 as a source-supported improvement lead with this qualification, not as an undisputed false edge or population gain. A second blinded source reader and independent council are still running. `results-codex.md` independently rechecked all 54 attempt IDs and usage and identified the correction to the mistakenly worded preregistered stop statement above.\n\nCouncil follow-up `results-codex-followup.md` inspected P01's additional `45:e3` edge against the full article. The denial article mentions cancellation of the backup design, then says Nvidia rejected “the claims,” but Nvidia's quoted response is broad and does not individually address the backup cancellation. The comparison input itself mentions only delay claims. The additional relation remains disputed; do not count it as either proved right or wrong, and do not treat P01's one-target success as acceptance of the full outcome.\n\nSecond source/output rereader `result-blind-claude.md` agrees on B01, B05, B10, H01 and P01, but for R05 reads the later article by the same publisher as potentially revising its earlier conclusion; it labels the opposite relation unresolved. The first reader chose different while leaving the supersedes readings unresolved. Earlier pre-send blind readers had both called it different. Thus R05 is genuinely disputed, and no case-level improvement may be counted there as established without a decision on the underlying boundary. Readers were blind to which model/arm produced an answer, but the shuffled answer choices appeared after the source in the same packet; the second reader explicitly says it could see them. A fresh third independent reader is examining only the R05 source pair without any outputs. This dispute does not affect the independent B05 regression, on which both source/output readers agree.\n\n`result-blind-claude.md` independently agreed on B01, B05, B10, H01 and P01 but read R05 as a possible later revision by the same original publisher; it explicitly noted that answer choices were visible in the same packet, although their producing arms were concealed. A fresh independent reader given R05 source only (`r05-adjudication.md`) favored different, while acknowledging the later-update interpretation. The disagreement remains open rather than being resolved by a vote. The independent output has only a label, no numeric confidence; three identical labels do not establish high confidence. `results-claude.md` agrees the historic stop rule did not specify B05 and that the result is insufficient for adoption; it favors a source-backed census of the 56 D392 Jev supersedes answers as a future diagnostic. `results-codex.md` favors a narrower saved-input comparison of candidate-context behavior first. Both preserve the experiment line as stopped; neither proposes a case-fitted prompt or immediate production change.\n**结论：独立比较器这条线应停止，不应围着 B05 调提示词。** 它在两个例子上答得更好，却在 B05 连续三次把两种不同的增长说法连成“修订”。不过，[RESULTS-DRAFT.md](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/RESULTS-DRAFT.md) 把它说成“触发预先写好的自动停止条件”，这句话需要改：最终规则只把 **B01 和 P01** 明确列为回退停止对照，B05 不在其中。B05 是严重的新回退，是停止这条方案的充分研究判断；不能事后改写成原先明确规定的触发条件。见 [最终结果规则](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/HARNESS-DESIGN.md:100)。\n\n**B05 的回退有原文依据。** 新记录是公司对 FY2028 收入增长 **70% 的正式预测**；旧记录是 Kress 说客户预测显示收入**可能翻倍**，并附 Huang 说不受供应限制时增长可能更高。旧文章同时写了 70% 和客户预测，支持它们是并列的不同说法，而不是一条修订另一条。\n\n比较器看到的文字也已经区分“公司预测”和“客户预测”，但有缺口：新记录的摘要和引文没有写 70% 受供应限制；旧记录的原始陈述日期未知。两篇报道对 70% 的个人说话人归属也不完全一致。**所以可以判断这条修订关系缺乏支持，但不能断言模型为什么犯错，或断言补上供应限制就一定能修好。** 原文见 [B05 来源](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/source-blind-packet.json)；实际两边文字见 [本次请求](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/probe-2026-11-09/bodies.json)。\n\n结果要分开看：\n\n| 例子 | Jev 完整候选 | Jev 单独比较 | 独立比较器 | 我能得出的结论 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| B05：两种增长说法应不同 | 1/3 判不同 | 3/3 判不同 | **0/3 判不同** | 相同对子文字下，新比较器比 Jev 单独比较退步；完整候选请求本身也有 2/3 错误。 |\n| B10：交付与收入占比应不同 | **2/3 判不同** | 1/3 判不同 | 3/3 判不同 | 新比较器对子判断更稳定；不能说它已证明分组优于完整产品路径。完整 Jev 有两次也没有把它并入错误组。 |\n| H01：同一收购公告 | 0/3 判重复 | 0/3 判重复 | 3/3 判重复 | 新比较器在这个漏合并例子上有改善线索。 |\n| R05：公司未确认停产 | 0/3 判不同 | 0/3 判不同 | 3/3 判不同 | 新比较器在这个错误修订例子上有改善线索；旧记录的原始说话人字段有已知归属问题。 |\n| B01、P01 | 两个对照均 3/3 符合预期 | 同左 | 同左 | B01 是重复报道对照。P01 只支持识别官方否认；不能代表后来修改预测等所有修订。 |\n\nB10 的完整请求中，第 1 次只有目标组被判重复；后两次没有任何候选被判重复，因此按现有代码分别是**错误并组一次、新建组两次**。独立比较器只对一个对子给标签，没有组排名或其他候选答案，不能直接写成“三次都把产品分到正确组”。见 [事件记录](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/probe-2026-11-09/events.jsonl) 和 [分组代码](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2/compare.py:109)。\n\n**完整请求和单独请求不能混为一个对照。** B05 两者结果不同，说明本次样本对请求形式敏感；它还不能证明是其他候选造成的，更不能把新比较器的差异全部归因于模型。提供商、请求格式和输出方式也不同。重复发送相同内容是三次请求，但不能保证提供商内部采样彼此独立。\n\n我核对了保存的计划、内容和事件：**54 个不同的案例／方式／次数组合，54 条发送记录、54 条完成记录**；事件中的请求哈希均匹配保存的内容，结果表与事件一致。每次都记录了用量。按仓库当时的费率计算约 **$0.01673**；**$0.18201 是预留估算，不是账单**。实际供应商收费未在这些文件中独立核实。检查文件见 [manifest](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/probe-2026-11-09/manifest.json)、[bodies](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/probe-2026-11-09/bodies.json) 和 [完成记录](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/probe-2026-11-09/complete)。\n\n这些案例是事先挑出的少量例子，来源判断有 AI 读者意见和个别归属、时间上的不确定性，**不是整体准确率或召回率**。文本在 clean 库中保留；错误分组或修订关系可能误导读者，但本次没有测到对最终判断的实际影响。\n\n**下一步建议只做一个零费用研究：**逐条检查这次完整请求里，哪些其他候选与目标说法相似、模型同时给了什么关系，以及这些关系写入后会怎样展示；用保存的实际输入和答案重放，不改提示词、不发送请求。决定性结果是：如果 B05 的错误关系在单独与完整请求之间变化，但其他类似案例没有一致可解释的候选干扰，就不能把“联合上下文”当作已找到的通用原因；反过来也只得到值得后续验证的机制线索，不能据此恢复已停止的比较器方案。\n\n本次只读；没有改文件、调用接口或打开运行中的数据库。\n# Step 2 同输入配对诊断：council 复核\n\n**结论：**\n- **结果本身属实：** 54 次调用全部完成，输入和历史请求一致，成本算得对。\n- **但结果稿里的停止理由写错了。** 按冻结规则（`HARNESS-DESIGN.md:100`），\"可重复退步\"只针对 B01 和 P01 两个对照。B05 不在这个定义里。照字面读，规则给出的是\"在 H01、R05、B10 上有选定案例改善，B05 是一个稳定的错答\"，而不是\"B05 触发停止\"。\n- **停下这条线我同意维持。** 一是你要求维持；二是对着产品真实走的完整批量对照，这个比较器只在 H01、R05 上明确更好。但要改的是记录下来的理由，不能拿\"看完结果再挑规则\"来重开这条线或采纳比较器。\n\n本轮没有改文件、没有调 API、没有打开数据库。\n\n## 1. 数量、输入一致性、花费\n- **数量：** `events.jsonl` 里 54 条 `may_have_been_sent`、54 条 `complete`，没有失败记录；`complete` 文件里写着 attempts 54、reserved $0.182。\n- **输入一致性：** Jev 完整批量请求的哈希和之前案例表里的历史请求完全一致。\n  - B05：8a47253e…（`case-key.json:56`）\n  - B10：70056b2c…（`:116`）\n  - B01：61792668…（`:8`）\n  - B05 三个臂里，新记录和已存记录的文字逐字相同（`bodies.json:20-22`）。\n- **花费：** 按用量和仓库里的单价算，Jev 输入约 $0.0040，DeepSeek 约 $0.0128，合计约 $0.0167，和结果稿一致。\n  - Jev 的输出 token 按仓库价目表计为 0，实际账单可能不同。\n  - Alex 对这次发送回复过\"开始\"（`HARNESS-DESIGN.md:35`）。\n\n## 2. B05：原文、两段原话，以及它算不算触发停止\n**两段原话（`bodies.json:22`）**\n- 新记录：Kress 于 08-26 说 FY2028 增长 \"will reach 70%\"，并且是 \"first-ever year-ahead revenue forecast\"。\n- 已存记录：Kress 说 \"customer forecasts currently indicate… may double next year\"，外加 Huang 的 \"a lot higher\" 那句。陈述日期是 unknown。\n\n**原文怎么说**\n- 第 9 篇同一篇文章里并列写了两句：70% 那句归给 Huang，\"客户预测显示翻倍\"那句归给 Kress（`blind-packets.json:1164`）。两句是同时并存的两个说法，不是前后两个版本。\n- 第 19 篇把 70% 归给 Kress，并且写了 \"a lot higher than 70%\"（`:1082`）。\n\n**Jev 看到的文字里缺了什么**\n- 第 9 篇\"两句并列\"这个事实，Jev 看不到。\n- 已存记录的陈述日期是空的。\n- 两边都没有发布日期。\n- 数字字段是按 PRD 有意排除的。\n- 说话人在两家媒体之间也对不上（Huang 对 Kress）。\n\n**为什么 B05 在记录层面是模糊的**\n- PIECES 里有一句 \"the same speaker revises that guidance, forecast\"。拿记录文字字面去套，这句话能支持 supersedes：同是 Kress，同一时期，数字不同，新记录有日期而旧记录没有。\n- Jev 自己在 6 次回答里都在 0.45–0.51 之间摇摆：\n  - 完整批量：0.51 / 0.48 / 0.46 判 supersedes 两次、different 一次（`events.jsonl:12, 44, 59`）\n  - 单问：0.48 对 0.46 / 0.47，三次都判 different（`:46, 57, 108`）\n- 所以，Jev 单问的\"3/3 判对\"，其实是三次都落在硬币边上。\n\n**放回冻结规则下的结论**\n- 按规则的字面，B05 不触发停止。\n- 但 B05 说明比较器并不是处处更好：在一个模糊的对子上，它 3 次都很确定地重犯了产品原来的错。\n- 对着产品走的完整批量对照：比较器 3 次全错，Jev 完整批量错 2 次。比较器略差。\n- 不应该围着 B05 去调提示词。\n\n## 3. B10：对完整批量产品对照，并没有明确改善\n- **Jev 完整批量：**\n  - 第 0 次判 same，0.45 对 0.41（`events.jsonl:42`）。\n  - 第 1、2 次目标对判 different，而且没有任何其他候选被判 same 或 supersedes（`:26, 73`）。照规则，这两次这条记录会成为新组。\n  - 所以 3 次里有 2 次，产品结果已经是对的。\n- **DeepSeek：** 3/3 判 different。比完整批量的 2/3 只好一点，达不到\"对照 3 次里至少错 2 次\"这个门槛。\n- **DeepSeek 只答了目标这一对。** 别的候选它没评，所以它对应的分组结果是测不出来的。\n- **结果稿里的\"B10 改善\"只是相对 Jev 单问**（单问 3 次里只对 1 次，而且都是 0.45 对 0.44 这种接近打平的回答）。\n\n## 4. 批量和单问的混杂\n- 完整批量请求里还包含两条 70% 的候选：q0（14:e2，Kress 说 70%）和 q2（9:e1，Huang 说 70% 并提到供给受限）（`bodies.json:20`）。这可能影响 Jev 对 q5 的回答。\n- 但实际差别只有 0.02–0.05。目前不能断定是批量造成的。\n- 规则要求用单问作为同输入比较的基准，这在方法上是公平的；代价是它不等于产品路径。\n- 两个基准下的结论：\n  - **对单问基准：** H01、R05、B10 有改善，B05 变差。\n  - **对完整批量（产品）基准：** 只有 H01、R05 明确改善（3/3 对 0/3），B10 只多对 1 次，B05 多错 1 次。\n\n## 5. 对用户的实际影响（只就这 6 个挑选出来的案例）\nclean 路径里，这几类错误都不会丢正文。\n- **H01：** Jev 稳定地判不同，0.59–0.60 对 0.37–0.38（`events.jsonl:25, 60, 80`），会多出一个重复组。DeepSeek 能避免。\n- **R05：** Jev 稳定地判 supersedes，约 0.86–0.88，会多一行误导读者的 \"Revises\"。DeepSeek 能避免。\n- **B05：** 如果用 DeepSeek，这行错误的 \"Revises\" 会 3 次都出现。\n- **B01、P01：** 三个臂都一致。\n\n## 6. 真正的分歧与语义参照的不确定处\n- **B05 算不算\"可重复退步\"：** 结果稿（`RESULTS-DRAFT.md:14`）和冻结规则（`HARNESS-DESIGN.md:100`）的说法冲突。更早的 council 证伪条件（`codex-challenge-v2.md:72`）会把 B05 列为决定性案例。三份依据并不一致。\n- **B05 的参照：** \"不同\"这个判断有原文支持（新旧两轮盲读者都这么判），但它部分依赖只有原文里才有的信息。只看记录文字，supersedes 也有一条字面上说得通的路径。说话人归属也不清楚。\n- **B10：** 两位读者都判 different。但 13:e4 本身有归属问题（`COUNCIL-LOOP.md:24`）。\n- **P01：** 只能作为\"官方否认已报道说法\"的有界对照。否认早已出现在旧文章里，时间先后不清楚，不能用来声称一般性的修订能力。\n- **R05：** 已存记录的 `original_party` 写成 Nvidia，但它的层级是 rumour。DeepSeek 没被这个字段误导。\n- **独立的盲读复核还在进行中**（`RESULTS-DRAFT.md:18`）。DeepSeek 的推理过程按设计全部丢弃了（`HARNESS-DESIGN.md:108`），所以无法从存档里拿到它对 B05 的自述。\n\n## 7. 一个通用的下一步研究方向（$0，不加提示词，不写实现，比较器这条线保持停止）\n**方向：** 对一次已完成运行里 Jev 发出的全部修订边，做一次完整清点。\n- 选 D392 d1：56 个 supersedes 回答，涉及 34 条记录。\n- 两位读者盲读原文。\n- 每一对同时记录：读者判断时用到的关键原文，是否就在比较文本里。\n- 这是这一轮的全体修订边，不是挑选出来的样本；但它只是一轮，用的文章和 dev 有重叠，回答之间也高度聚集。\n\n**它要回答的三个决定性问题：**\n1. **有没有至少一条\"后来改变\"的有效修订被两位读者认可？** 也就是同一说话人改了指引或预测，而且时间先后在原文里成立。\n   - 如果一条都没有：任何比较器都测不出\"有没有误伤有效修订\"。所有涉及修订的比较器测试，都应该等找到这样的对照之后再做。\n2. **不被支持的边是否集中在\"一条否认挂满多条边\"上？**\n   - 如果是：问题出在结构上（同一篇文章里的记录互相看不见，后来的重复再把否认扩散到每个子说法），属于 Step 2 的设计问题，不是换模型能解决的。\n3. **两位读者都不支持的边里，关键信息是否本来就在比较文本里？** 例如 R05 的 \"neither confirm nor deny\"。\n   - 是：这类错是判断错误。\n   - 否：这类错只能从输入或记录边界那一侧去研究。\n\n这只是对存档做阅读和统计，不产生全局的准确率或召回率。如果第 1 题答案是\"没有\"，那么修订边该怎么展示，就可能需要 Alex 做产品决定。\n\n**局限：** 这次只有 6 个挑选出来的案例，DeepSeek 没有分数、也不给排名（D381 的选组需要分数或排名），同一天单次实验，只能用来定位问题。这次会话没有写文件的工具，所以复核结论只在这条回复里。\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:20:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/CONCLUSIONS.md reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/RESULTS.md reports/step2-wrong-merge-council-2026-11-09/CONCLUSIONS.md reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/CONCLUSIONS.md'","aggregated_output":"# Step 2: should Jev see retrieval scores?\n\nResearch council, 2026-11-08. Scope: the isolated clean Step 2 evidence-comparison path. No production change, model test, or change to the concurrent Step 1 experiment.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nNeither a benefit nor harm has been established. Keep the present request until a controlled comparison shows an advantage. Similarity is calculated between a search query and a stored group's original text; it is not a probability of same statement. The query may contain a thesis and a candidate may have several scores from different queries. Jev does not receive these scores; it receives the incoming piece as state and one candidate per typed question. It returns a choice and probabilities. Jev's choice probability, not cosine, selects among multiple same-statement results.\n\n## Concrete observation\n\nIn saved c6 X4, incoming 12:e1 was grouped into 1:E1 although the two revenue-sharing arrangements were judged different. Recomputed from the cached exact-text vectors, cosine of the one thesis query against 1:E1's document is 0.8574. Its own document-vector cosine is 0.8654, but the record had one thesis query and that value was not its retrieval query. The saved Jev answer called same_statement at 0.89. This is one error; it neither establishes that score information will harm Jev nor measures a score distribution. Source: `poc/data/clean-step2-forward-c6-2026-11-08/{decisions.json,library.json,embeddings.json,merge-key.json,merge-third-answer.json}`. Query/doc text and cosine are reconstructed as `clean-arch/step2/compare.py` defines them.\n\n## Council\n\nClaude Code session 4c06c24f-8f77-4add-9443-7081a27b7903; original handoff, streams, stderr and challenge are saved alongside this record. Claude initially recommended an offline score-separation gate. Codex challenged it: overlapping marginal scores cannot rule out conditional help, and separation cannot establish Jev uses them. Claude agreed and withdrew that gate, its extrapolation of score ranges from another task, and claims about Jev's internal reasoning. Offline profiling can inform a test but cannot establish efficacy. Vendor describes Jev as a typed, non-text-generating decision model with independently evaluated questions (https://docs.typesafe.ai/introduction); evidence on LLM anchoring is only a hypothesis for Jev, not a finding (https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.01724).\n\n## Proposed discriminating test, not yet registered or run\n\nReconstruct identical saved candidate requests; interleave fresh no-score controls with true-score requests and a score-shuffled control, with fixed score representation and randomization, repeated draws, cache misses, and identical model, candidates and question content except score lines. Make each score's query origin explicit: multiple search queries may yield different scores. A shuffled control tests sensitivity to a number but needs care with singleton/equal-score cases and preserving query origin. A source-blind independent reader judges differing pairs and controls without seeing arm, score, or prior decision. Report wrong merges, missed repeats, incorrect revision classifications and group choices separately, all cases, with same-arm variability, cost and limitations; fixed-candidate comparisons do not prove forward-run performance. The fresh set has few or no revisions; use separately identified stored revision cases without presenting isolated pair results as product-path evidence. Freeze design, audit and preflight under repository rules before any paid send. A score can also encode context Jev did not directly receive (e.g. thesis wording or period), so improvement would not itself establish the mechanism.\n\nNo evidence supports a universal score threshold. This proposal does not alter the accepted configuration or authorize deployment.\n# Jev retrieval score probe: interrupted; partial result\n\nAlex requested a small council test of whether supplying embedding scores helps Jev. The isolated probe used fixed archived c8 inputs, 11 selected cases, two arms (original control, and real cosine metadata plus query type), 3 same-day draws per arm. It was not a new acceptance measurement, was not a forward run, and cannot measure a change in retrieval recall. The design, frozen manifest and hashes, preflight and source-blind review are in this folder. Frozen rulers, production and the concurrent Step 1 configuration did not change.\n\n## Status\n\nThe seventh case's first dispatch (`18:e2`, treatment draw 1) failed with `ConnectionResetError` after 36 successful answers. Whether that request reached the service is unknown. Per the predeclared single-attempt stop rule it was not retried; 29 further planned calls were not sent. Six cases have all six answers; five cases have no complete paired result. In particular the known wrong merge (`22:ev-11`) and revision-bearing example (`19:ev-guidance-70`) were not reached. Six completed cases carry 46 candidate comparisons; control and treatment each have three decisions per candidate. Logged successful input tokens 136,590, logged cost $0.005742; the ambiguous request's actual billing is unknown.\n\n## Observed outcome\n\nEvery one of the 46 candidate relation choices was identical in all six draws. The resulting groups and revision links were also identical in all six: 3:e1 repeat of 1:e3; 5:e1 new; 9:e1 new; 9:e6 repeat of 7:E1; 13:e11 repeat of 6:e1; 14:e3 repeat of 7:E4. No revision links occurred. For 5 of the 46 candidate comparisons, the mean reported same-statement probability differed across the arms; one example was 9:e6 against 7:E1, control 0.77/0.74/0.79, treatment 0.84/0.84/0.86. Probabilities do not establish accuracy or explain a cause.\n\nTwo independent blind readers each read source materials and all 46 candidates for these cases, and agreed: 3 same, 42 different, 1 overlap. In particular 13:e11 groups a three-cloud growth record into an AWS-only record; both blind readers call it an overlap. Do not count that as unambiguously right or wrong. The other three repeat targets were both called same, and both new cases had no candidates called same or revision. Later details remain preserved as member text; the score does not fix the overlap.\n\n## Interpretation and stop\n\nNo visible decision benefit or harm in the six completed selected cases. This is not evidence of equivalence or a measured precision/recall change. The wrong-merge case central to the prediction and the revision-bearing case were not run. The score input also adds query-type metadata and can encode thesis/period content missing from the comparator; an effect could not be attributed to numerical similarity alone. Complete result and two source-backed reviews do not exist for the intended 11 cases. Stop and report the interruption, no automatic resumption or replacement of the ambiguous call. The independent Codex partial review is `partial-review.out`; independent Claude follow-up is `partial-claude.jsonl`. No adoption or product change is justified by these results.\n\nCouncil post-read: Codex and Claude independently agreed on the six completed outcomes and their limits. Claude's partial review says 106 total candidates across eleven cases; the frozen packet and complete blind annotations contain 96, so 106 is a counting error and is not used. The three reader disagreements lie in unfinished cases. The connection reset's cause is not established; the logged exception is its type only.\n# Council: the fresh Step 2 wrong merge (X22)\n\nRead-only council, 2026-11-09. Codex and Claude Code independently traced the sources, the stored Jev request and answer, the Step 3 group and the reader's text; challenge round in `round2.jsonl`. Original user-requested scope: this one error, no product or ruler edits, no paid model calls.\n\n## What happened\n\nThe earlier 2026-09-05 Motley Fool source reported that Vera Rubin was in production and that management expected it to represent about 20% of Q3 data-centre revenue. The later 2026-09-06 Calcalist source reported that Nvidia said Vera Rubin systems were commercially delivered in August. The two source articles are preserved in `reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/blind-packets.json`, case 9 candidate C7 (key in blind-key.json); these are media reports, not the primary earnings-call transcript.\n\nArchived request for incoming 22:ev-11 against 13:e4 asked Jev to compare their source-backed text. It chose same_statement at 0.49, different 0.36, supersedes 0.15. `clean-arch/step2/compare.py:109` acts on the chosen label without a 0.5 floor, so the incoming record joined 13:e4. Retrieval found this candidate. The model provides no textual explanation; the reason for the choice is unproven.\n\nThe fresh merge review classified the two as different, and a third classified the merge wrong. Independent Codex review agrees; earlier Claude blind review called it overlap, and a separate Claude adjudicator called it different. Thus whether it is a clearly wrong merge rather than an overlap depends on reader judgment. We agree the delivery is not a straightforward repeat of the production statement and revenue forecast. The prior acceptance reference grouped underlying article matters as a later step and marked the delivery revision; that reference uses a broader revision concept than Step 2's comparison, and its unit is the article matter, not necessarily the system record. The most natural reading under the current comparison contract is a separate new statement; revision is a plausible interpretation of the milestone depending on the meaning of 'changed event-progress statement'. The primary statement of the original bundled production-and-forecast record is not formally identified. No change to the reference or contract is proposed.\n\n## Actual and potential impact\n\nThe clean prototype group retains the delivery record's own summary, quote and source as a member. Offline calling `group_text` on the saved group produces the label 'Later reports of the same statement' above that member: misleading grouping and label, not observed text deletion. The acceptance did not test whether this group changed a reader's investment judgment; Step 4 of this clean path was not built for this run. The production writer and live application were not changed. If a future reader treats group members as restatements, it could underweight a new milestone; that downstream consequence is a risk, not an observed result. The first article attributes the 20% forecast to management but does not explicitly attribute production status to Nvidia; its stored summary does, an attribution difference in this case without an established census.\n\n## A tempting threshold\n\nAn offline read of the 57 saved merges showed 2 selected repeats below 0.5: X22 at 0.49 (judged wrong by the original merge readers) and X47 at 0.47 (accepted by both original readers; Broadcom revenue forecasts). A 0.5 floor on this fixed draw would remove one wrong merge and one accepted merge. This is not a root cause proof or general performance measurement. No threshold change is recommended from this single comparison; old production code uses such a floor, while the clean prototype does not, and no decision about adding it to the clean path was established here.\n\nRelevant records: `poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/step2/decisions.json`, `library.json`, `jev.sqlite`, `merge-third-answer.json`; `poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/settled.json` and `prerun-prompt.md`; `clean-arch/step3/library.py`; `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §§43–44. The independent Codex review is `codex-review.md`. One full fresh test produced 1 clear wrong merge of 57 by its readers; this council did not reclassify its immutable result.\n# Step 2 wrong-merge council: candidate direction, not a proven fix\n\n2026-11-09. Alex requested a council to think through a general fix. Codex and Claude independently read the saved requests, source-backed records, decisions, current contracts and prior trials, challenged each other's proposals, and converged after further challenges. This is a read-only investigation; no model experiment, production/configuration change, new ruler, or change to the concurrent end-to-end run was made. Streams and handoffs are preserved in this directory.\n\n## What was found\n\nIn one fresh draw and three dev draws, 363 accepted-as-repeat outcomes received recorded reviews: 316 accepted, 43 overlap and 4 wrong. These are instance counts, not an independent population error rate: the three dev draws reuse the same articles and one source pair appears twice. The three different source-pair cases are: production/forecast versus commercial deliveries; revenue-level forecasts versus growth figures whose relation is not established by the records; and a cloud-provider revenue-share description versus an article describing a developer future-sales share. Same source or topic does not prove their specific statements identical. Source accuracy is separate from what a news report actually says. Reader/model disagreement remains and no historical label is changed. Raw source and count: `stored-census.md` and run-specific `merge-third-answer.json`, `library.json`, `decisions.json`.\n\nThe observed mechanism is that Jev selects repeat and the writer groups accordingly. The model does not produce a verbal account; the reason for these decisions is unproven. Some records bundle multiple related facts. The issue is not uniquely a low score: the cloud/developer case was selected at 0.84 in one draw. On the 363 fixed saved decisions a 0.5 floor would block one reader-marked wrong merge, four reader-accepted merges and one overlap; this is not a forward-run estimate. In an earlier 102-merge diagnostic an overly strict 'nothing new' rule reduced merges to 22, including valid retellings. Numbers are diagnostic, not an efficacy claim.\n\n## One candidate to investigate\n\nBefore grouping a pair that Jev says is a repeat, obtain an independent comparison, with the same source-backed input and without Jev's selection or probabilities: does the incoming statement actually report the old record's main statement? The checker must allow extra details and paraphrases and distinguish different facts, changed claims and cases whose relation cannot be established. This is a proposal for an experiment, not an established root fix, change to D381, a new automatic veto, or proof that another model's errors are independent. We do not choose a model or a disagreement rule in this council.\n\nUse original speaker, the subject, values, period, original date, hedges and actual source when relevant; do not infer the same original claim solely from similar topic, a matching number or common announcement. Different reporting outlets can repeat the same claim while retaining each source. Do not silently adopt D248 boundary 7's dormant exception or a new threshold. Current Jev comparison text excludes an explicit evidence-period field; adding it would be a separate changed input, not silently mixed into this comparison.\n\nThe reviewer must not treat a genuine extra detail as automatically disqualifying; X47 is an accepted example. Conversely a broad shared topic or a shared minor fact need not make a whole new record a repeat; X22 and AWS/three-cloud overlap are controls, with source-backed classifications and disagreement. The acceptance reference and Step 2 use different meanings/units for revision in X22; that concern stays separate and the frozen reference remains unchanged. The source itself does not show that the old production statement and the delivery claim were separate statements by the same original speaker, so the revision label is not settled by this case.\n\n## Evidence that would decide whether to proceed\n\n1. Start with a small, predeclared diagnostic of the three reader-marked wrong source pairs and counterexamples: accepted repeats with added details (including X44 and X47), true updates/denials, different speakers/periods, and known missed repeats. Use the identical Jev comparison payload for the independent comparator. It sees no prior result. A source-blind reader who did not write the proposal reviews the source and differences; same-day fresh Jev controls and request fidelity are required for model comparisons. A small pilot is a lead, not a performance claim.\n2. If the pilot provides an actual signal without obvious counterexample regressions, prospectively freeze an expanded evaluation of all 363 historical merged instances and known missed repeats and revisions, marking the model-written judgments and 43 contested overlaps; do not relabel frozen gold or infer that 363 are independent. Assess wrong groupings, correct repeats split, unmerged repeats, update errors and source fidelity; give every case and disagreement. Fixed-history comparison cannot establish new forward-history behavior, latency or downstream judgment impact.\n3. Only if those comparisons merit it, plan a separate isolated forward-path evaluation with a fresh same-day baseline, held-out articles and eventual reader/judgment effects, including cost and speed. Do not use the 50 fresh-test articles as previously unseen again. No new scorer substitutes for source-backed trace review.\n\nA useful test must show the independent comparison catches cases Jev misses without unnecessarily breaking true repeats or losing/relabeling updates. If errors are correlated, correct repeats are split, or the model's additional cost exceeds the observed value, do not adopt it. The pilot may be inconclusive, especially with few confirmed wrong pairs. It cannot prove absence of a problem.\n\nWhether a reviewer disagreement creates a new group, a pending case or some other outcome changes product behavior; it is not decided by the council and must be shown to Alex with evidence. Likewise source-matter revision vs statement revision and risk trade-offs need an explicit decision if the existing rules do not resolve them. The final delivered investment judgment has not been shown to suffer from X22; the currently running July end-to-end comparison does not contain this September case and cannot isolate this merge.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:22:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat clean-arch/step2/compare.py; sed -n '1,150p' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\"","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"Isolated Step 2 for the clean path (PRD §5): search the entity's stored evidence for each {thesis, incoming evidence} association,\nask Jev how the incoming evidence relates to each candidate, and turn the answers into the outcome Step 3 writes.\n\nSearch: one query per association (the thesis question, then the incoming summary and passages) against each group's original record\n(summary, passages, party, statement date, period), cosine similarity of embeddings, top 10; a record with no association, or one that\nlists the company's figures (a condition), also gets one entity-wide query of its own text (STEP1-CONDITIONS-DESIGN.md §10.1); the union\nof the results is compared. The figures never enter a search or comparison text, so a wrongly paired figure cannot change Step 2 (§14);\nthe comparison reads the full passages (§15). Comparison: Jev's piece question with the production criteria (views/jev_matching), the incoming piece as state, one question\nper candidate. Outcome: any same_statement makes the record a repeat of the candidate with the highest same_statement probability, ties\ngoing to the earliest group (Alex, 2026-11-08: only the group it is most like); a supersedes answer on another candidate becomes a\nrevision link; with no same_statement, supersedes answers make it a revision of those groups; otherwise it is new.\n\n`forward` runs articles in order against the library as it stood after the previous article: records of one article never see each\nother. A failed comparison is refused by the library, reported, and the run continues.\"\"\"\nimport concurrent.futures\nimport math\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]\nsys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT / 'src'))\nfrom hivemind.views import jev_matching  # noqa: E402\n\nTOP = 10\n\n\ndef stated(record):\n    return record['statement_date'] or record.get('statement_date_text') or 'unknown'\n\n\ndef doc_text(record):\n    return '\\n'.join([record['summary'], ' '.join(record['passages']), f\"party: {record['original_party'] or 'unknown'}\",\n                      f\"stated: {stated(record)}\", f\"period: {record['evidence_period'] or 'unknown'}\"])\n\n\ndef query_text(record, thesis_question):\n    return f\"thesis: {thesis_question}\\n{record['summary']}\\n{' '.join(record['passages'])}\"\n\n\ndef piece_text(record, label):\n    return (f\"{label} [{record['tier']}; {record['reporting_speaker']}; original source {record['original_party']}, stated \"\n            f\"{stated(record)}] {record['summary']} | quote: {' '.join(record['passages'])}\")\n\n\ndef query_vectors(vectors, record, thesis_questions):\n    \"\"\"The search queries of one record, in order: one per thesis association, then its own text when it has no association or lists\n    the company's figures.\"\"\"\n    queries = [vectors[query_text(record, thesis_questions[t])] for t in record['thesis_ids']]\n    if not record['thesis_ids'] or record.get('figures'):\n        queries.append(vectors[doc_text(record)])\n    return queries\n\n\n# Jev's piece criteria aligned with the Step 2 merge line's own test (PRD §5 line 2; AGENTS §7.1, one ruler): a repeat is a later report\n# of the stored piece's main statement. Production's wording (views/jev_matching.PIECES) judged \"the same evidence\", so a later report of\n# only a secondary statement inside a stored record (an official's denial carried in the report it denies) was merged as a repeat.\nPIECES = {'same_statement': \"the INCOMING PIECE reports the STORED PIECE's main statement: a retelling, another outlet carrying it, the \"\n                            \"same number in text or a table, or the same speaker repeating it; wording, precision and extra detail may \"\n                            \"differ. If the stored piece carries several statements and the incoming piece reports only a secondary one, \"\n                            \"this is not it\",\n          'supersedes': \"a later version of the stored piece's main statement, changed: the same speaker revises that guidance, \"\n                        \"forecast or event-progress statement, an official confirms or denies that reported claim, or the original \"\n                        \"source revises it; another statement on the same topic is not this\",\n          'different': \"a different fact, figure or matter, a figure for another period or quantity, or only a secondary statement of \"\n                       \"the stored piece, even if on the same topic\"}\n\n\ndef piece_question(text):\n    return {'type': 'choice', 'criteria': PIECES,\n            'instructions': 'How does the INCOMING PIECE relate to the STORED PIECE below? Use only the supplied text. STORED PIECE:\\n' + text}\n\n\nINFORMS = {'true': \"the STORED PIECE bears on the THESIS's own matter (the parties, products, policy or quantity the THESIS names) and \"\n                   \"makes its answer more or less likely, as evidence filed under it would\",\n           'false': \"the STORED PIECE concerns another matter, or is a company-wide result or event that does not speak to the THESIS's \"\n                    \"own matter, or would not change its answer\"}\n\n\ndef informs_question(text):\n    return {'type': 'noul', 'criteria': INFORMS,\n            'instructions': 'Does the STORED PIECE below inform the THESIS stated above? Use only the supplied text; a shared company, '\n                            'product family or word is not enough, and the company\\'s overall results, guidance and estimates inform a '\n                            'thesis about its revenue or earnings path, not one about a competitor, a supplier or a policy unless they '\n                            'speak to it. STORED PIECE:\\n' + text}\n\n\ndef later_link_judge(library, thesis_question, ask):\n    \"\"\"The judge `Library.link_later` calls: one Jev request per batch, the thesis as state, one yes/no question per stored group.\"\"\"\n    def judge(group_ids):\n        questions = {f'q{i}': informs_question(piece_text(library.groups[g]['original'], 'STORED PIECE')) for i, g in enumerate(group_ids)}\n        raw = ask(f'THESIS: {thesis_question}', questions)\n        return {g: raw[f'q{i}']['noul'] >= jev_matching.THRESHOLD for i, g in enumerate(group_ids)}\n    return judge\n\n\ndef cosine(a, b):\n    return sum(x * y for x, y in zip(a, b)) / (math.sqrt(sum(x * x for x in a)) * math.sqrt(sum(y * y for y in b)) or 1)\n\n\ndef search(library, vectors, record, thesis_questions):\n    \"\"\"Candidate group ids: each query's top 10 by cosine against the groups' original records, merged in rank order.\"\"\"\n    candidates = []\n    for query in query_vectors(vectors, record, thesis_questions):\n        ranked = sorted(library.order, key=lambda g: -cosine(query, vectors[doc_text(library.groups[g]['original'])]))[:TOP]\n        candidates += [g for g in ranked if g not in candidates]\n    return candidates\n\n\ndef outcome(answers, order):\n    \"\"\"answers: {group id: {'choice', 'probabilities'}}; order: group ids in creation order.\"\"\"\n    same = [g for g, a in answers.items() if a['choice'] == 'same_statement']\n    later = [g for g, a in answers.items() if a['choice'] == 'supersedes']\n    if same:\n        best = max(same, key=lambda g: (answers[g]['probabilities']['same_statement'], -order.index(g)))\n        return {'kind': 'repeat', 'repeat_of': [best], 'revises': later}\n    if later:\n        return {'kind': 'revision', 'revises': later}\n    return {'kind': 'new'}\n\n\ndef forward(articles, library, vectors, thesis_questions, ask, workers=8, opened=None):\n    \"\"\"articles: lists of Step 3 records in article order. ask(state, questions) returns Jev's answers by question id, or raises.\n    opened: per article, the thesis ids it opened; after the article's records are written, each is linked later to the stored groups\n    that arrived unlinked from earlier articles (`Library.link_later`). A thesis written to with pairs still pending is judged again,\n    against its opening boundary. Returns one decision per record.\"\"\"\n    decisions = []\n    for index, records in enumerate(articles):\n        def decide(record):\n            candidates = search(library, vectors, record, thesis_questions)\n            if not candidates:\n                return record, candidates, {}, {'kind': 'new'}\n            questions = {f'q{i}': piece_question(piece_text(library.groups[g]['original'], 'STORED PIECE'))\n                         for i, g in enumerate(candidates)}\n            incoming = piece_text(record, 'INCOMING PIECE')\n            try:\n                raw = ask(incoming, questions)\n            except Exception as first:   # retried once; a second failure is reported, never silently saved as new\n                try:\n                    raw = ask(incoming, questions)\n                except Exception as error:\n                    return record, candidates, {}, {'kind': 'failed', 'reason': f'{type(first).__name__}: {first}; retry: {type(error).__name__}: {error}'}\n            answers = {g: {'choice': raw[f'q{i}']['choice'], 'probabilities': raw[f'q{i}']['probabilities']} for i, g in enumerate(candidates)}\n            return record, candidates, answers, outcome(answers, library.order)\n        with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=workers) as pool:\n            results = list(pool.map(decide, records))\n        for record, candidates, answers, result in results:\n            entry = {'id': record['id'], 'candidates': candidates, 'answers': answers, 'outcome': result}\n            try:\n                entry['group'] = library.apply(record, result)\n            except Exception as error:\n                entry['refused'] = f'{type(error).__name__}: {error}'\n            decisions.append(entry)\n        article = records[0]['article'] if records else None\n        if article is None:\n            continue\n        written = {t for r in records for t in r['thesis_ids']}\n        retry = [t for t in written if t in library.pending and t not in (opened[index] if opened else [])]\n        for thesis_id in list(opened[index] if opened else []) + retry:   # opened here, or written to with pairs still pending\n            result = library.link_later(thesis_id, later_link_judge(library, thesis_questions[thesis_id], ask), article)\n            library.later_log.append({'thesis': thesis_id, 'article': article, **result})\n    return decisions\n# Current state\n\n**Clean Steps 1–3, current (2026-11-09).** Record: [round log](../clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md) §§16–47.\n- **Step 1 configuration: c8, adopted by Alex (D387).** It runs DeepSeek Flash at high effort, with a 64,000-token output cap; a\n  format failure or a cut answer is resent once (D380). It carries Alex's decisions D382–D385:\n  - figures are conditions;\n  - material statements with no thesis are kept without links;\n  - only big matters open a thesis;\n  - no target prices.\n\n  [PRD §12](../clean-arch/PRD.md) holds its system prompt, closing check and schema; a run with no candidate files sends exactly the\n  accepted request.\n- **Acceptance (D386, [protocol](../clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/ACCEPTANCE-PROTOCOL.md)).** 50 fresh articles that no run,\n  old or new, had used: news from 2026-09-05 to 09-07, a holiday weekend with no NVIDIA posts. One draw; two readers and a third on\n  disputes.\n  - helpful 132/139 (core 83/83);\n  - wrongly opened theses 1 of 10;\n  - search 141/143 available repeat pairs, or 141/145 for the whole system (one 11-article story supplies 55 pairs, and revisions are\n    barely exercised);\n  - clear wrong merges 1/57, plus 2 overlaps;\n  - detail retention 22 = 22. This is a storage comparison: every record keeps its own text. It is not a usability test.\n\n  Dev ×3 on the frozen golden-v5 (a lead): helpful 214, 211 and 212 of 234; search 98.9–100%; clear wrong merges about 1%. Step 1\n  cost for the fresh set plus dev ×3: $4.20 at published rates.\n- **Steps 2 and 3** are built in isolation (`clean-arch/step2`, `clean-arch/step3`): search, comparison, grouping and later linking.\n  They are not integrated with production storage. **Step 4 (judging) is not built** on the clean path, so nothing yet shows the clean\n  system can judge an investment view.\n- **Open defects, by product impact** (reflection council, `council/av-codex.md`):\n  1. **The Ohio-guarantee yes/no question is never covered** in the three dev draws; a broader credit-support amount thesis opens\n     instead. Diagnosed (round log §48): the model folds the unsigned guarantee into the open aggregate thesis because the request\n     uses \"covers\" both for \"is evidence for\" and for \"already asks this question\". The same clause sits under link clutter.\n  2. **A revenue-share thesis was opened** (Vera Rubin's share of data-center revenue), the class-A boundary of D382.\n  3. **Link clutter.** A blind recheck of the 34 disputed fresh links found 19 weak and 2 wrong; 10 accepted links mixed in as controls were all useful; the other 49 links were not rechecked.\n     The model's own account names topic or theme overlap and inference chains, and c8's closing check never reads back a link. This is\n     a likely cause, with no fix proven. Alex approved designing a protected link check through the council; a first draft was stopped\n     because it would drop founding premises and contrary facts.\n  4. **Unverified passages:** 3 of 188 fresh records and 1–3 per dev draw. The mark now travels into Step 2's records, and a reader sees the found source text or an \"Unverified quote\" label. Jev's comparison input still carries the model's text without the mark; changing it changes measured Step 2 behaviour and needs its own measurement.\n  5. **The lidar policy thesis** recurs at the boundary (one dev draw in three).\n- **History.** These earlier states are superseded:\n  - the 2026-11-07 takeover (K5 → K6b; [takeover log](../clean-arch/takeover-2026-11-07/LOG.md));\n  - the 2026-11-08 close-out (K6b test 32/42; Step 2 first lines; [Step 2 log](../clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §12);\n  - the unfiled fix round and the c1–c7 arms (round log §§1–39).\n- **No run is live.** The branch is committed through the D388 line (round log §§48–59).\n\n**Clean Step 1 source investigation (2026-11-07):** A [source-loss census](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md) traces two separate losses in the historical cleaner and records reproducible corpus observations. Offline candidate comparisons expose both recovered content and source-identity or contamination risks. A subsequent [full-page diagnostic](../clean-arch/STEP1-FULL-PAGE-RESULTS.md) completed 102 calls on 17 selected articles and independent source-backed review. It exposes examples of both missing or misattributed source text and model errors that persist despite the right text being present. A Codex–Claude council is reviewing the results and earlier attempts; no source-cleaning candidate is adopted, and the source corpus and production defaults are unchanged.\n\nAlex has authorized correcting the test-source cleaner and test data when their defects are confirmed, while the frozen production configuration and ruler remain intact. The cleaner switched 615 originally fed articles off; a replay reproduces all 615, and direct inspection confirms an actual article excluded because its 9,874-character body was flattened to one line. Other examples include a wrong-site shell, paywall and long challenge code, so the false-exclusion count and a general article-identification repair remain to be established. The [council regroup](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-REGROUP-COUNCIL.md) and [source-preservation design](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PRESERVATION-DESIGN.md) describe an isolated candidate checked against all 34,084 records, with a fake-answer actual-runner handoff and provenance display in the replay renderer. The [five source-control calls](../clean-arch/STEP1-CAPTURE-CONTROLS-RESULTS.md) completed and were source-reviewed; two items contain summary details absent from their attached quotations, and one source-visible risk explanation was not returned. This is selected-case evidence, not a general quality result. Broader source-quality comparison, citation completeness and actual writer integration remain open.\n\n**Clean architecture Step 1 (2026-11-06, isolated work on `clean-arch`):** Alex reopened work with the clean architecture. An experimental prepared-source handoff now preserves the archived page, prior cleaned text, source-role decisions and selected-line mapping without changing the original database; quotations for prepared items must also occur contiguously in the original captured page. Two seven-item runs are available in the [clean architecture PRD](../clean-arch/PRD.md), one in the earlier selection order and one in recorded page-timestamp order. The source selector is not a production service and source ordering is not established event-arrival ordering. The runs expose unresolved semantic problems: citations can omit supporting facts despite an exact location match; model output can change a benchmark into a deployment, reverse a backup relation, omit a named customer or assign an unstated currency. A blinded 84-call check-placement/wording comparison and a 27-call same-article citation-completion diagnostic did not give a clean semantic fix and are not adopted. The latter missed an omitted connectivity condition and a reversed backup relationship, and left six expected evidence reviews unaddressed. A further five-round Codex–Claude council produced a narrowly scoped Step 1 link candidate, independently checked for prompt-contract drift. A six-article, 36-call blind comparison completed; the candidate still produced an explicitly self-contradictory link and both arms consistently omitted the same Kyber report and company response. It is not adopted and has not changed the active PRD prompt. A trace confirms that both the Kyber report and open Kyber thesis were supplied, and the raw model answers omitted the report and denial; the reason is not established. See ../poc/data/clean-step1-kyber-trace-2026-11-06/TRACE.md. An isolated six-call comparison showed that hiding the Kyber thesis did not recover the report or denial in any draw. A second six-call comparison returned them in 3/3 draws with the original Kyber paragraph alone and 0/3 with the full article; length, position and surrounding content changed together, so the reason is not isolated. Excerpt outputs have source-attribution limitations. Neither experiment changed state or established a production fix. (Superseded: the next task then was a whole-article coverage approach; the current next task is under \"One next task\".) See ../clean-arch/STEP1-IMPLEMENTATION-COUNCIL.md. The earlier four-round council agreed that no tested semantic fix is ready; its next investigation is a source-backed review of the 49-item historical run and its 160 evidence records, with overlapping issue flags and separate article-level omissions, then a separate comparison of the seven-item replays. See ../clean-arch/STEP1-COUNCIL-REVIEW.md. Preserve relevant evidence while identifying the next intervention; production/frozen history remains as described below. See [Step 1 run log](../clean-arch/STEP1-RUN-LOG.md) and [English reading page](../clean-arch/architecture.html).\n\n**Sealed (D379, 2026-11-06):** Alex asked to clean up and seal this version while he re-plans the system design. Work stops here and is sealed as it is: main's working tree is committed on branch `seal/2026-11-06` (tag `sealed-2026-11-06`) and merged into `main` on Alex's word (PR #10); the sealed split and two side experiments are pushed on their own branches (D379). No PRD 11 experiment is open, and the E1 next task below is withdrawn. The next task is Alex's re-planned system design.\n\n**E1 diagnosed (2026-11-06, Alex chose E1):** of 57 helpful matters frozen production wrote and then dropped, two readers agree on 17 link misses (4 core), 3 opening misses (1 core) and 24 small matters the standard itself drops; 10 are disputed on the fallback clause and 3 were quote refusals. Opening is not the experiment. The core of the link misses is the model reading a question's period, population or named configuration as a boundary; only 8 of the 13 window draws it dropped were lost to the product. No production change; $0.02 of probes. My recommendation was to fold that narrow change into the next extraction version; withdrawn by the seal (D379). Details: `docs/research/2026-11-06-e1-no-home-drops/README.md`, PRD 11 E1.\n\n**Split extraction sealed as a failed experiment (D378, 2026-11-06):** the split (a new-question call, an evidence call and record preparation) was benchmarked against the frozen poc-v108 and failed. Its stop line fired: helpful matters 156, 156, 159 against 166, 170, 162, with about 15% higher cost. One screened revision kept more noise than production. It is sealed in the worktree `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` (tag `split-extraction-failed-sealed-2026-11-06`), and nothing of it is in main. What it taught is written as proposed production experiments in `docs/prd/11-production-experiments-from-split.md`: E1 no-home helpful drops, E2 duplicate questions, E3 ladder checks with D186, E4 judge refusals, E5 D203's revision day, E6 the measurement kit. Each opens only on Alex's word.\n\n**Question-catalog limit removed (D377, 2026-11-05):** Alex also directed removal of the separate 150 KB catalog rejection. The research reader in this worktree now has neither the catalog nor evidence byte limit; it retains full scoped questions, historical boundaries, dependencies and evidence. A portable 326,996-byte catalog test returns all 400 new questions and expands evidence both after reading the catalog and directly; the old 150 KB guard fails the same test in a scratch copy. The saved 69-question pilot catalog and all 69 saved evidence expansions match their original content hashes. Details: `docs/research/2026-11-05-catalog-limit/`. The full suite remains pending until the paid seven-day run no longer competes for resources. The run remains in its isolated worktree and does not call this research reader.\n\n**Evidence reader limit removed (D376, 2026-11-05):** Alex directed removal of the reader's 1 MB evidence-expansion rejection. In this worktree, `research_context.Reader.expand` now returns the entire evidence payload without that cap, while the catalog's separate limit remained at that checkpoint; D377 subsequently removed it. On the saved pilot ledger, all 69 individual question expansions, the four-question 1,931,225-byte pilot request and the entire 3,454,076-byte catalog produce the exact same content as the pre-change high-limit baseline; the portable oversized-ledger test passes and fails when the old cap is restored in a scratch copy. Details and snapshot-specific measurements are in `docs/research/2026-11-05-reader-limit/`. The seven-day diagnostic runs in `/Users/lchingen/hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/poc/data/split-fresh-seven-day-2026-11-05-v2/` with its pinned code; it does not call this research reader. Read its status through its `snapshot.sh`; do not edit its package code while it is live. This change is not an acceptance of seven-day extraction or judgement quality.\n\n**Start-state correction (2026-11-05):** Alex requires a research library established from real material available before the test start, then extraction against that state. The prior empty-library diagnostic cannot represent this case. Our own mistakes and the current 68-question provenance audit are in `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/own-mistakes-2026-11-05.zh.md`. Two new A-only calls were run on 2026-08-29 articles using the 68-question prior-day snapshot; both proposed the same aggregate supply-commitments topic. A source/context review judged it distinct from the existing specific SK Hynix question. A third A-only call on an author-body excerpt about DLSS 5, already tracked by v22, passed; the prompt also restricts software updates, so the reason is not established. No writer or dedup ran; source-history completeness remains unverified, and this is not scored acceptance. Details: ../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/start-state-diagnostic-2026-11-05.zh.md.\n\n**Measurement correction (2026-11-05):** Alex clarified that a gold-worthy topic already in the supplied library should not open again; an absent worthy topic should open. The earlier simple-A claim of a \"missed\" new topic was not scored against that rule and is withdrawn as a correctness conclusion. In the actual experiment, a Kyber topic was shown and passing follows Alex's topic-level rule; whether its exact release question captures the shipping milestone remains unverified. The experimental pre-article snapshots differ from the replay's day-start snapshots; 9 of 10 inspected examples differ. The current uncommitted replay default does not build a cold-start library; committed main HEAD still uses the old seed and startup filing path. Historical lexical coverage has a confirmed unrelated-topic match. The frozen gold and past scores remain unchanged. Audit: `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/measurement-context-scrub-2026-11-05.zh.md`.\n\n**Simple new-question diagnostic (2026-11-05):** At Alex's direction, an isolated prompt returned new questions plus article evidence without asking for product database fields. Four previously seen real dev articles each ran once: three returned one candidate, one returned none, all four quotes matched their source, and the gold-listed GPU backstop amount question was not proposed. Whether that omission is a new-topic miss depends on whether the existing library covers the topic. Two other candidates require source and independent-significance review; the actual experimental snapshot already had a Kyber topic, so its pass follows Alex's topic-level rule; whether its exact release question captures the shipping milestone remains unverified. This is not a production or full-pipeline acceptance. Details: `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/simple-new-question-results.zh.md`.\n\n**Split prompt structure v3 read diagnostic (2026-11-05):** One real reading-stage call on a previously seen article found the revenue/margin guidance and Trainium scale but omitted the gold China revenue/market-loss matter. The prewritten stop condition was triggered and the linking call was not sent. This is one draw and not a production or end-to-end result. See `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/structure-v3-read-result.zh.md`. D374 removes the model sampling field from active settings and default configs; historical config files and archives retain their original bytes.\n\n**Split prompt structure v2 small test (2026-11-05):** Thirty calls on two previously seen golden-v5 dev articles completed in `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review`. The blind model reader found complete helpful matters in the fresh control versus new A+B of 6/19 vs 10/19, 11/19 vs 7/19, and 9/19 vs 12/19. The new arm also retained more gold not-kept matters in each draw; B missed useful margin guidance and one answer reused IDs, with a demonstrated matching collision. This is not a production acceptance. See `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/structure-v2-results.zh.md` and `reports/2026-11-05-parallel-structure-v2.html`.\n\n**Split extraction small test (2026-11-05):** At Alex's request, the isolated `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` worktree ran two real golden-v5 articles once through each split lane. An independent model reader found 7 of 19 helpful gold matters fully retained, two partial, two written then dropped, eight missed, and one retained gold not-kept matter. Three saved v108 draws on these articles retained 7, 9 and 12 of 19, but used 22 seeded questions while the new test used 8/10-question snapshots; these are not directly comparable. The experiment remains unaccepted; see `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/small-scored-test-20261105.md` and `reports/2026-11-05-split-small-test.html`.\n\nAs of November 5, 2026; decisions through D374. [Decisions](DECISIONS.md) holds what is decided now; the [design page](system-design.html) and the [module PRDs](prd/README.md) hold what the code must do; the [README](../README.md) holds startup, build and run instructions.\n\n## Future evidence scoring (D372)\n\nAlex decided future gold scoring should measure helpful versus not helpful evidence without requiring separate scores for core and supporting. Use frozen golden-v5 as-is: combine core and supporting as helpful, treat not-kept as unhelpful, and keep optional entries neutral. A counting report has not yet been implemented. Historical scores retain their original definitions. D373 removes the stop caused by a separate concern about three question-gold entries: keep the gold unchanged, continue scoring and disclose the concern; see `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/ruler-conflict-2026-11-05.zh.md`.\n\n## Latest decision (D369)\n\nAlex decided in D369 to capture core and supporting evidence without a strict boundary and in D370 to remove the 22 preset starting questions and separate earnings-report import. The replay entry now defaults to `poc-v118` (D374), refuses old seeded ledgers and baseline configurations, and continues to read existing non-seeded questions; earnings releases remain normal source items. This change has local contract tests but has not passed end-to-end product acceptance. The frozen historical rulers and results remain unchanged.\n\nThe small 2026-11-05 study used three archived NVDA articles and an 8/28 historical 68-question snapshot: without versus with an appended report, independent AI review found 14/27 versus 13/27 complete matters. This measured appended extraction context, not the distinct former coverage-start step; D370 removes both by Alex's decision. Full traces and limitations are in the separate `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` worktree under `docs/research/2026-11-05-real-input-evidence/existing-library-results-2026-11-05.md`.\n\n\n**One-day smoke completed (2026-11-05 UTC):** Alex requested a quick 1–2 day end-to-end test. One day, 2026-08-29, ran in `/Users/lchingen/hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/poc/data/v117-one-day-smoke-2026-11-05/`: config v117, an 8/28 historical library, 40 extraction calls, 93 evidence records written, four new questions, 54 judged and zero terminal failures. Two judgement attempts were refused and corrected. A Microsoft revenue forecast of $391.1bn was assigned to a rung whose upper bound excludes $391.1bn; the same article's actual quarterly revenue and next-quarter guidance were dropped with no home. Some source dates and bodies are not verified, and an independent 14-article sample review was incomplete due to truncated text. This is not semantic or production acceptance. Findings and scope: `docs/research/2026-11-05-real-input-evidence/one-day-v117-smoke-2026-11-05.zh.md` in that worktree. The clean source-text comparison and its condition-hidden review are complete; council discussion of next steps is ongoing.\n\n**D371, in progress:** Alex directed that present and forward-looking revenue and financial information should be tracked. Diagnose the same-day extraction and filing losses before designing a general change. No new financial tracking implementation has been accepted yet. The old frozen standard and gold are unchanged. The original prompt-only pilot failed design review. The user approved an exact prompt addition for an experiment; it is not a production implementation. Four paired real-article diagnostic calls per arm completed for the exact approved sentence: v74i versus experimental role-d371-pilot. Microsoft next-quarter guidance and NVIDIA FY2028 guidance still dropped in the treatment; a single draw is not acceptance or proof of causation. Details: docs/research/2026-11-05-real-input-evidence/d371-prompt-pilot-results.zh.md (isolated worktree). The requested full prompt audit and restructure were drafted experimentally; five real-article paired diagnostic requests per arm completed. Microsoft revenue guidance was missed in both arms, and the draft produced unsupported original dates, a quote refusal and suspect links. No production prompt change or acceptance; see restructure-pilot-results.zh.md in the isolated worktree.\n\n**Clean Step 1 coverage council (2026-11-06):** Codex and Claude Code independently reviewed the existing attempts and completed three discussion rounds. The proposed next research candidate moves the existing evidence-reading block ahead of the thesis-opening block while preserving the article, open-thesis state and paired output. It is a hypothesis, not an implemented or validated fix. A complete prompt/contract audit and independent review precede any controlled Luna comparison. Historical paragraph-based and source-first experiments, their noise and attribution problems, and the rejected overlap-based merge are recorded in [the council review](../clean-arch/STEP1-COVERAGE-COUNCIL.md). No new Luna experiment was run in the council.\n\n**Clean Step 1 coverage order result (2026-11-07):** The isolated 54-call clean comparison completed and all answers were reviewed blind. Moving the evidence-reading block recovered a known Kyber report and denial in one of three candidate answers against none in three controls, with attribution blur; it also produced unsupported gaming-to-AI links in two candidate answers and no controls. It is not adopted. A separate exact-quote example misattributes an executive's prediction in the standalone summary. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-COVERAGE-ORDER-RESULTS.md) record all selected cases, uncertainties and source-fidelity work; no production ledger was written.\n\n**Clean Step 1 fidelity council (2026-11-07):** Codex and Claude Code completed five rounds on source attribution, conditions, dates and meaning. No tested fix or root cause is established. The agreed source-to-record review of 49 articles / 160 evidence items and the subsequent isolated field-order comparison are complete; the later 54-answer / 217-record repeated sample remains separate. Prior source-first, quote-first and follow-up-review experiments and their limits are recorded in [the fidelity council](../clean-arch/STEP1-FIDELITY-COUNCIL.md). No Luna experiment or production change was made in this council.\n\n**Clean Step 1 fidelity review (2026-11-07):** All 49 saved source packets and 160 original evidence items have received a source-and-record review; focused independent cross-checks cover the findings and selected controls. The [review and article replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-FIDELITY-REVIEW.md) distinguish source contamination and missing source material from model meaning, citation, date and link issues. The [isolated schema property-order diagnostic](../clean-arch/STEP1-CITATION-ORDER-RESULTS.md) completed all 66 calls and condition-blinded article reviews. Several omissions and semantic errors persisted; it did not establish a fix and is not adopted. It used saved articles and before-states without changing production.\n\n**Clean Step 1 source handoff council (2026-11-07):** The ongoing [council and offline checks](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-HANDOFF-COUNCIL.md) reconstructed 16 matching article-34 controls, all omitting a Kyber report and denial; the cause remains unknown. The 20-article saved source-role overlap review distinguished previously cited AI-generated summary lines and an unrelated recommendation from authored body. The clean runner does not send the saved source-role labels; the council chose to first compare original and previously selected source text on ten known articles. The isolated 60-call source-text comparison and condition-hidden review are complete; [results](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-TEXT-RESULTS.md) show specific contamination reductions alongside persistent omissions and changed meaning. It did not advance state or write production. The isolated six-article low-versus-medium source-fidelity diagnostic completed 36/36 calls and condition-hidden review. [Results](../clean-arch/STEP1-REASONING-FIDELITY-RESULTS.md) show partial improvements alongside persistent source-qualification and attribution concerns; no production setting is changed. The [prompt audit](../clean-arch/STEP1-FIDELITY-PROMPT-AUDIT.md) maps the observed failures to the full request and signed standard. The six-article isolated schema-description comparison completed 36/36 calls and condition-hidden review; the candidate did not preserve the repeated example qualification and is not adopted. [Results](../clean-arch/STEP1-SUMMARY-FIDELITY-RESULTS.md). A further six-article 36-call low-versus-medium comparison used corpus-cleaned sources outside the original 49-item clean replay and a fixed exploratory context; results were mixed, including unsupported links and incorrect attribution/date fields. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-ADDITIONAL-SOURCES-RESULTS.md). The structured-claims candidate was not sent after review revealed scope and complexity concerns. Council review of the next action is ongoing. The date-text schema-description diagnostic completed 48/48 calls on eight known cases, with mixed results; the candidate is not adopted. A selected-run census found twelve literal \"null\" strings in date-text fields, but no production writer impact is established. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-DATE-DESCRIPTION-RESULTS.md). A further preregistered eight-source date comparison completed 48/48 calls; the candidate lost a supported company-article statement date in all three draws on one source and is not adopted. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-DATE-NEW-SOURCES-RESULTS.md). A bounded corpus census confirmed 163 source pages across three explicit generated-summary structures whose labels were removed while some summary text was retained; two NVIDIA cases were reproduced through the cleaner. [Source provenance investigation](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-CENSUS.md). A read-only source-lineage replay reproduced 363/363 cleaned pages with current inputs, preserved ambiguous original-line locations, and surfaced the generated-summary label loss for review. [Source investigation and lineage replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-CENSUS.md). An offline neighboring-context observation exposed only 144 or 149 of 163 known labels and introduced many other page lines; it is not adopted as a general source fix. [Observations and viewer](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-CONTEXT-OBSERVATIONS.md). A new [saved-quote integrity investigation](../clean-arch/STEP1-QUOTE-INTEGRITY-CENSUS.md) reconstructed 360 saved answers across eight overlapping cohorts, including 78 prepared-source calls. Five saved unverified passage instances were complete matches under the existing normalization; an in-memory candidate recovered their original slices, while ten others remained unverified. The normalized source-slice lookup is now installed only in the isolated clean Step 1 runner; saved-answer and entry-point checks are recorded there. Page provenance, Unicode completeness, production resource budgets and writer integration remain open. The existing writer stores only one quote, whereas 63 of 160 evidence items in the 49-article exploratory trace have multiple passages. A [source-first 49-article coverage review](../clean-arch/STEP1-COVERAGE-CENSUS.md) separates material missing from the model input from source-visible omissions in the saved answers, with uncertain matter boundaries labeled. (Superseded: the next investigation then was why supplied material is missed; the current next task is under \"One next task\".) The canonical prompt and production configuration remain unchanged. Adding fallible role annotations remains a separate untested candidate. Council streams and handoffs are in `poc/data/clean-step1-ongoing-council-2026-11-07/`.\n\n## What runs\n\n- **Pipeline (replay default `poc/config/poc-v118.json`, D370, D374).** Every item reads the relevant open questions from the library at the start of its day, then extraction v74i on `gpt-6-luna` proposes evidence and questions; Jev matching with the collision judge matches them; code writes the records; judging-v7 judges changes once a day; pull mode closes due questions. There is no separate seed or coverage-start report import, and reports as source items remain eligible. This default has not completed end-to-end acceptance. The [pipeline guide](poc/pipeline-plan.md) maps stages to code.\n- **Measured:** one company (NVDA) over one 14-day window (2026-07-01 to 07-14), the judgement scored at the window end on the frozen ruler (AGENTS §7.1). Three draws of `poc-v116` against three of `poc-v108` run in the same hours: 53 against 45 agreements of 57; operating margin and capex agree in 3 of 3 draws, never judged without the coverage-start reading ([acceptance](research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/acceptance-design.md)).\n- **Served data:** the service, the MCP server and the standalone page serve the sealed three-month ledger `served-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828` (68 theses, 34 groups; D358), built by the earlier `poc-v34` pipeline, not by the replay default. HTTP `/library`, MCP `thesis_library` and the standalone page share the dated bundle writer.\n- **Idea API:** `poc-v118-idea` (D290, D335, D374): two discovery calls and one adoption and weighting assessment; only adopted theses with positive weights appear, and nonempty plans total 100%. The [Idea contract](poc/idea-card.md) owns its fields and the limits of its scores.\n- **Interface:** the Thesis Library and Explore Ideas workspaces, with the confidence Trigger and an Action preview (no wallet connection or order execution). The [UI specification](poc/thesis-ui.md) and the [integration guide](../poc/ui/README.md) own its behaviour.\n- **Repository:** `main` holds the product, the frozen ruler and the research records; run databases, logs and model archives stay on disk (D353), located by the [data manifest](research/2026-10-25-english/data-manifest.md). Worktrees: `/Users/lchingen/hivemind` is the main checkout, where slice branches are cut from `main`; `/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28` (the extraction branch, merged on 2026-11-03) keeps in its ignored folders the stored end-to-end runs of `poc-v91` to `poc-v95` that later comparisons read (D359); `/Users/lchingen/hivemind-run-v109-2026-11-04` is the finished judging-v8 run's worktree. Older branches stay in Git without worktrees (D359); none is merged work in progress. The clean source-text comparison and its condition-hidden review are complete; council discussion of next steps is ongoing.\n\n## Open defects\n\n1. **Competitor and customer news is filed under the company's revenue questions** instead of the share, capital-spending or custom-chip question it informs (D355; filing is otherwise about 85% right). Share facts are often not written at all, an extraction recall miss. The total-revenue question holds about a quarter of all links (D354).\n2. **One bet opened twice** about one encounter in five: the collision judge splits a seed question from an identical candidate on a fine detail (D354).\n3. **A question the standard rules out** (the lidar legislation) was proposed by the extraction and opened in one draw of three (D354).\n4. **A thesis opened from an article gets no dependency of its own**: the default Jev matching asks for none (`views/jev_matching.py`), although D51 has the matching call choose them; themes still reach every company thesis through the theme block (D191, D195). Found in the 2026-11-05 consolidation; building it is decided (D368).\n5. **Historical coverage-start reading, retired by D370** (D364–D366): the capital-spending line is missed in about one read in six (the question is then left as it was); a labelled guidance figure is written with the fact tier; later reports reach the questions only through the daily stream; `scripts/preflight.py` does not replay the reading's check.\n6. **Measured on one company and one 14-day window**, the judgement scored at the window end only; no version of the current pipeline has completed a longer window (a 60-day run of `poc-v108` was stopped at Alex's word after 2026-07-15, [log row 8](research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md)). The China-share question (v17) swings between draws (0 of 3 in the acceptance, 2 of 3 in its control, 1 of 3 in the frozen draws): two readers found the starting figures did not cause the acceptance's drop, and the byte-identical extraction request kept a \"share fell to zero\" sentence in two candidate draws and dropped it in two control draws. One acceptance draw judged nothing on v16; its cause is not established.\n7. **What a judgement reads grows with the window** (D358). Argument records (D201) were measured as the remedy on 2026-11-04 and not built: judging-v8 agreed 12 of 17 against 15 of 17, judging-v9's card added little at about half again the judging cost; their code was removed at Alex's request ([log rows 4–11](research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md)).\n8. **Seen once, not traced.** A judgement named a shown theme as a dependency and the check refused it twice, stopping a draw for a day (2026-11-02, D266). The sealed run's review recorded that a succession's opening evidence is not linked to the new question (D358), not yet checked against today's code.\n9. **Earlier defects, unchanged.** Matching (build log row 49): another document's copy of a figure can be treated as new evidence; one matter can occupy several groups; same-answer candidate references caused seven chain or duplication decisions of the generative matcher; empty `original_who` values can count as the same party; different names for one population cause two of nine Jev merge misses. Nine 2026 aggregate-spending theses under `ai-capex` each have a different population; whether to combine them is a product choice. Extraction's instructions remain narrower than the PRD's important-question rule and omit the personal-memo exception, and extraction can change a quantity's meaning before matching (a guarantee amount against financing provided). Classification keeps its [recorded simulation defects](research/2026-10-25-english/classification-existing-defects.txt). The corpus audit recorded 97 official notices with 56 body and file-name collisions, to re-fetch before relying on a rebuilt corpus. An older intermittent search-test failure is untraced. Fresh Idea allocations vary while saved weights do not; the confidence chart applies the current plan to past states and is not a calibrated probability; broad analyst-prose quality and fresh Idea stability remain unaccepted (the [Idea contract](poc/idea-card.md) lists its limits); generative matching dependencies await `matching-v4`. The readers' doubts about the frozen standard, listed in the [extraction branch's state page](research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/current-state-extraction-branch-2026-11-03.md), remain for Alex.\n\nUpstream supply and the human review pool's output remain deferred.\n\n## One next task\n\n**Clean Step 4 (D396): design version 4 is written; it is not READY and waits for two choices of Alex's**\n([design](../clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/DESIGN.md); round log §69).\n\n- **Design status:** three review rounds (`gg`, `gl`, `gm`) each found serious points; version 4 answers the last and has not been\n  reviewed. Its core contract depends on C1 and C2 below.\n- **What the review of 2026-11-10 found** (council `gh`–`gm`, all on stored data, no paid send):\n  - Step 4 is the right next step, and inside it the first thing to build and test is the **ladder step alone**: the call that turns a\n    thesis Step 1 opened into one judged question with its answer ladder and due day. The frozen judge follows only when the ladder\n    step meets its lines.\n  - Production's own ladder sentences are not stable: one request, the same in three draws, gave a bullish end at the low amount\n    twice and at the high amount once, with three different middle bands; the code checks pass all three\n    (`clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/census/production_ladders.txt`).\n  - Of the 34 theses the unseeded draws opened, two blind readers find 12 to 16 that ask more than one thing, 7 to 9 of 18 number\n    questions with no reference figure in the opening article, and 4 that state a calendar due day.\n- **The next task:** put to Alex\n  1. **C1:** a topic that asks more than one thing gets one judged question with the rest shown as not judged (recommended as the\n     first version), or is split as D38 says, or is not judged;\n  2. **C2:** whether the ladder step carries D186 (ranges against guidance, the favourable end registered; recommended), its\n     direction half only, or neither;\n  then revise the design for his answers, review it with a fresh reviewer each round until one finds nothing serious, audit the\n  whole ladder request, and ask his word to build the ladder step and run Stage A (the ladder step against a control that quotes v74i, on the 34 stored theses and the 10 fresh ones).\n- **Open defects recorded by this review:** the frozen judge retried 10, 16 and 18 judgements in the seeded clean draws, 38 of 51\n  reasons a theme cited as a dependency (PRD 11, E4; frozen, a finding for Alex); the same matter takes different shapes between\n  draws (Kyber due end-2027 in one draw and end-2028 in another).\n- **The unseeded measurement (D395):** graded subset 4.0 against 6.33 seeded. Every miss is a question not opened in the ruler's shape.\n  Judged answers 16 of 16, on the ruler's wording and ladder; the system's own questions have never met a judge.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:24:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '203,390p' clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md; sed -n '4101,4125p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; sed -n '129,190p' clean-arch/PRD.md\"","aggregated_output":"## 9. Alex's decision (2026-11-08)\n\nOn the question in §7, Alex replied: \"A 继续；跑council loop 开始做\". **Decided:** each repeat's own text and source are kept inside the\nstored record's group, and the original is not rewritten. Recorded in the clean PRD, §5 repeat handoff and §6. Still not confirmed:\nthe Step 2 pass lines, and the rule for several matches.\n\n## 10. Grouping round (2026-11-08, council loop)\n\n**Frame.** Alex chose A (§9).\n- Goal: build grouping and test whether readers notice what later reports add, without double counting or being misled, compared with\n  source-only merging and with no merging (the full-information control).\n- No model judge exists (PRD §7), so blind readers act as the dossier's readers.\n\n**Council A** (`council/group/a-*`, `b-*`).\n- Agreed:\n  - the group contract: the original is never rewritten; members keep their own id, text, fields and source; links carry their\n    provenance; revisions are links; a replay is idempotent; one unit per item; distinct articles are counted;\n  - the dossier view;\n  - three views built from one stored history;\n  - a key written by independent readers before any view is read;\n  - one view per reader.\n- Settled in the challenge:\n  - several matches (15 items): kept as their own entry with `also_matches` references in both merged views. This is a **test\n    convention only**; the product rule stays Alex's.\n  - What was built: the isolated library with only the decided rows, refusing a failed comparison and several targets.\n\n**Built.**\n- `clean-arch/step3/library.py`: groups, members, links, revisions, refusals and the dossier text.\n- `test_library.py`: 12 tests, one per table row. All 5 mutants were caught (the original rewritten, a replay appended, articles not\n  distinct, a failed comparison saved, several targets joined).\n- `build_views.py` replays the forward simulation's 282 stored decisions through the library: 195 groups, 0 refused. This is a\n  fixed-decision reconstruction, not a new forward run.\n\n**Reader test** (`reader-test/`).\n- Questions and scoring rules were frozen first (`FROZEN.sha256`, checked by the scorer): 39 questions over three dossiers.\n- Key: two readers; a third settled the 16 questions where the drafts differed.\n- 18 masked view readers; 3 blind graders. Tally: `score_reader_test.py`, 234 grades.\n\n| Dossier | Detail: none / source / group (out of 12) | Unsupported claims: none / source / group | Characters: none / source / group |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| financing | 7.0 / 1.0 / 6.5 | 0 / 4 / 0 | 43.7k / 23.0k / 42.5k |\n| competition | 7.5 / 2.0 / 8.0 | 2 / 2 / 2 | 18.1k / 15.2k / 22.5k |\n| roadmap | 10.5 / 0.0 / 10.5 | 2 / 0 / 0 | 62.9k / 40.2k / 65.9k |\n\n- Detail in total: none 25, source 3, group 25, out of 36.\n- Double counting: 0 in every view, on 4 independence questions.\n- Controls, comparison and independence scores are about equal; group is slightly ahead on comparison (9 against 8 against 6).\n- No frozen falsifier fired. Detail: group trails none in financing by 0.25 question per reader, under the 1-question threshold. The\n  wrong-group questions scored the same in every view.\n\n**Traces read.**\n- C3: group readers saw both Lisa Su accounts, but read the ambiguous sentence as covering MI450 only. That is a judgement point, not a\n  missed detail.\n- R5: group readers put \"orders\" and \"sales\" side by side, while none readers added an inferred year.\n- F1 is partial everywhere because grading was strict.\n\n**Council B** (`council/group/c-*`).\n- The test supports preserving later text. It does not prove equivalence with no merging, overall dossier quality (the questions were\n  chosen for added details), or wrong-group safety (the questions pointed readers at the facts).\n- Size is about no merging, up to 25% more in competition: groups bring members whose own links point elsewhere. Grouping gives\n  structure, not compression.\n- Next class: wrong groups.\n\n**Wrong-group census ($0; the 9 no-home merges, read against exactly what Jev saw):**\n- M2, Sharon AI into Firmus: the incoming record's quote is the whole paragraph, Firmus sentence included, so Jev matched on the quote.\n  Step 1 bundle on the incoming side.\n- M57, NVL576 into NVL144: the stored record's own summary also states the NVL576 claim, so the merge is defensible. Step 1 bundle on\n  the stored side.\n- M30, analysts' 82% and 41% into the analysts' revenue path: different figures. A Jev error.\n- 6 arguable (the same announcement, the same figure, or a relayed report): under grouping both texts sit side by side.\n- So 2 real wrong groups out of 102, and their texts survive.\n\n**Open for Alex:**\n- the several-match rule (pending, best match, or own entry with references);\n- the Step 2 pass lines.\n\n## 11. Log rows\n\n| Arm | Hypothesis | Change | Result | Kept |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Grouping (A) | Keeping each repeat's text keeps added details noticeable without double counting | Isolated library and group view | Detail 25/36, the same as no merging, against 3/36 source-only; 0 double counting; size about the same as no merging | yes (Alex's decision; tested) |\n\n## 12. Close-out (2026-11-08)\n\n**Alex's decisions (2026-11-08):**\n- a record that is a repeat of several groups joins only the one Jev judges it most like (the highest same_statement probability; ties\n  go to the earliest group);\n- the Step 2 lines: (1) search at least 90%; (2) clear wrong merges at most 2%; (3) grouped dossiers keep no fewer later details than\n  no merging.\n\nBoth are recorded in PRD §5.\n\n**Built.**\n- `clean-arch/step2/compare.py`: search, Jev comparison, the outcome with best match, forward order, and a failed comparison refused and\n  reported. `test_compare.py`: 6 tests; all 4 mutants caught.\n- `run_forward.py` reads a stored Step 1 trace directly.\n- **Run** (`poc/data/clean-step2-forward-2026-11-08/`, a fresh Jev draw): 282 records made 178 groups.\n  - 104 repeats, 3 revisions, 0 refused; 16 records had several same_statement answers.\n  - The cached embedding texts were verified identical: 282 of 282 documents, 313 of 313 queries.\n\n**Council A** (`council/group/d-*`):\n- report search as recall over known labelled counterparts;\n- blind-review every merge, previously labelled positives included;\n- verify the cache texts;\n- re-run the reader test on the new views.\n\n| Line | Result | Met |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Search | 112/113 known labelled counterparts had a holding group among the candidates (99.1%). For 107 of them the group's original, which is what Jev saw, is itself a counterpart. This is not recall over all real repeats. | yes |\n| 2. Clear wrong merges | 104 merges read by two blind readers; 85 accepted by both; the 19 rejected by either went to a third reader: acceptable 1, overlap 15, **wrong 3** = 2.9% | **no** (missed by one merge) |\n| 3. Later details | Reader test re-run on the rebuilt views with the frozen questions and key: detail none 27.5, source 3, group **28** of 36; unsupported claims 3 / 2 / 2; double counting 0 | yes |\n\n**Line 2 detail.**\n- The 3 wrong merges:\n  - NVL576 into the NVL144 group, twice. The group's original Step 1 record states both claims.\n  - Analysts' 82% and 41% growth into the analysts' revenue-path record: different figures, a Jev error.\n- 15 overlaps, which the third reader called imperfect but not misleading to a reader of the record's own text. Among them:\n  - orders against sales ($1tn);\n  - expected revenue against committed off-take (Firmus);\n  - bundled records: the business model into the Sharon group, the Kyber delay report into the backup-rack group, the Taiwan raid\n    alongside the Singapore mansion.\n- My own reading had 5 wrong (Sharon AI into Firmus, and the business-model bundle into the Sharon group, both overlap to the third\n  reader). Both readings miss the line.\n\n**Council B** (`council/group/e-*`):\n- do not offer the result as a pass;\n- report 3 wrong, 15 overlap and 1 acceptable after dispute;\n- a later look at bundled records would explain only part of it;\n- stop and report.\n\n**Verdict.** Step 2 meets lines 1 and 3 and misses line 2 by one merge, in one Jev draw. Per Alex's instruction, the work stops here,\nand the census goes to him.\n\n**Not done:** the unprompted wrong-group reading test, and a fix for line 2.\n\n## 13. Embedding score to Jev: interrupted exploratory probe (2026-11-09)\n\nAlex asked whether giving Jev the retrieval similarity score helps and authorized a small council test. The frozen candidate lists were\nreplayed in an isolated probe, comparing the original Jev request with one adding the real cosine scores and query-type labels. This\nis not a production change or a new acceptance measurement. The [design, source-backed blind reviews and full result](../../reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/RESULTS.md)\nremain separate from the Step 1 work.\n\n- The planned sample was 11 selected cases, 3 same-day sends per arm. **36 of 66 calls completed:** 6 cases; the next request ended\n  in a connection reset after dispatch and its outcome is unknown. The remaining 29 calls were not sent. The predeclared stop held;\n  no automatic resend or resume occurred.\n- On the six completed cases, all 46 candidate choices, the selected groups and revision links were the same in both arms, in all\n  draws. Two blind readers agreed on all 46: 3 same, 42 different and 1 overlap. The overlap was grouped as a repeat in both arms;\n  it is not treated as an unambiguously correct or incorrect merge. The score changed some returned probabilities, not decisions.\n- The known wrong merge and the selected revision-bearing case were not reached. **No decision improvement was observed, but the\n  interrupted, selected sample cannot establish equivalence, precision, recall or revision quality.** The effect of scores remains\n  unresolved; neither the prompt nor the configuration was changed. Recorded successful call cost was $0.005742; billing for the\n  interrupted request is unknown.\n## D381 · 2026-11-08 · Clean Step 2: a repeat joins only the group it is most like; three acceptance lines\n\n- Alex, on a record that repeats several groups: \"只放进最像的那组\". On the lines he chose \"三条线\": search at least 90%; clear wrong merges\n  at most 2%; grouped dossiers keep no fewer later details than no merging. Grouping option A (\"A 继续\"): each repeat keeps its own text\n  and source in the group. Record: `clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md` §§9–12; `clean-arch/PRD.md` §§5–6.\n\n## D382 · 2026-11-08 · Financial figures are conditions, not theses; a thesis is a view with a reason\n\n- Alex: \"我觉得财务类型不应该是thesis，而更多的是条件。观点应该是独特的view，而不是喊出来的数字，除非有理由 - 这个理由恰恰才是观点\". On the\n  recommendation to keep a useful company figure no view takes as a condition of the company, he replied \"好\". Record:\n  `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §§16–17.\n\n## D383 · 2026-11-08 · D209 amended again: a material statement no thesis takes is kept unlinked and linked later\n\n- Alex chose \"先存着，有观点再挂\": a statement the model judges useful but no thesis takes yet is kept in the company's record, linked when\n  a related thesis opens; what the closing check rejects is still dropped, including the cash flow he ruled out. Amends D209 beyond D382.\n  Record: `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §27.\n\n## D384 · 2026-11-08 · Clean Step 1 opens only the big matters, by the signed standard as the frozen gold applies it\n\n- Alex chose \"按金标：只开大事\": a thesis opens only for the standard's reasons (an obligation the company takes on, its main product's\n  next node, a policy step, a checkable shared quantity); another company's single project, partnership or product line is evidence.\n  The reader-based small/fragment line gives way to must_open covered and must_not not opened. Class A (main-driver quantity) stays\n  out under D382. Record: `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §36.\n\n## 5. Step 2: find and compare related evidence\n\n### Why this is separate\n\nAn open thesis may accumulate many evidence records. Step 1 needs to understand the article and the open research; it does not need to read every prior statement to do that. Step 2 uses each proposed `{thesis, evidence}` association to find the history worth comparing.\n\n### Find candidate evidence\n\nThe proposed design places semantic search over the evidence library. It uses the thesis and incoming evidence to find top-N related evidence across the current entity, including evidence linked to other theses. The thesis is search context, not a restriction to evidence already linked to that thesis. This also applies when the proposed thesis is new. For example, “raised its revenue outlook” and “expects higher sales” can be related despite different wording. Alex chose this entity-wide scope on 2026-11-06.\n\nSearch returns **candidates**, not a final decision. Alex discussed a starting point of top 10; the implementation and quality requirements need to be measured.\n\nA match outside the returned set will not be considered by the comparison. Search quality therefore matters independently from the model's ability to compare the records it receives.\n\n### Compare with Jev\n\n**2026-11-08 (candidate, [round log](step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md) §§24, 31–35):**\n- Comparison reads the full passages; the 600-character cut is removed.\n- An unlinked record also searches by its own text.\n- `figures` never enter a search or comparison text.\n- Jev is asked the clean path's own piece question, aligned with line 2's test: a repeat reports the stored piece's main statement, and\n  an official's denial of a report is a revision.\n- When a thesis opens, every stored unlinked group from earlier articles is judged against it in batches of ten (a yes/no question; the\n  company's overall results inform only a revenue or earnings thesis). Each judged pair is recorded once.\n- On c6's records: search 97.9%, wrong merges 1.9% (2 of 104), both lines met; line 3 not re-measured.\n\nJev compares the new evidence with the returned evidence. The intended outcomes are:\n\n| Outcome | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Repeat | The new material presents the same statement again. | Another article reports the same company forecast. |\n| Revision | A later account changes the same statement. | The company revises its forecast from 12% to 25%. |\n| New evidence | It is not identified as a repeat or revision of the evidence compared. | A different analyst offers a separate forecast. |\n\nThe words, amount, speaker, source and time can matter to the comparison. A later statement about the same subject is not necessarily a revision. A matching amount alone does not prove that two statements are the same.\n\nThe result “new evidence” is the outcome of the comparison performed; it is not proof that no matching evidence exists elsewhere in the library. When the incoming evidence is judged a repeat of several candidates, it joins only the group Jev judges it most like: the highest same-statement probability, with ties going to the earliest group (Alex, 2026-11-08). A comparison that fails is not saved and is reported.\n\n**Repeat handoff:** when Jev identifies a repeat, the comparison must retain the identity of the existing evidence record it was compared against. Step 3 uses that identity to place the incoming record in the existing record's group, adding its source and any missing thesis link. The typed Jev response need not generate the record ID: the caller can associate the choice with the candidate it supplied. The rule for several matching candidates is stated above.\n\n**Step 2 acceptance (Alex, 2026-11-08).** Three lines apply:\n1. For incoming evidence whose earlier version is in the library, search returns it at least 90% of the time.\n2. At most 2% of merges are clearly wrong.\n3. Grouped dossiers keep no fewer details added by later reports than no merging does.\n\nThe first measurement against these lines is in the [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §12; the isolated modules are `step2/compare.py` and `step3/library.py`.\n\nThe diagram's intended sequence is: use `{thesis, incoming evidence}` to search the evidence library; pass the returned candidates and incoming evidence to Jev; pass the comparison result and the proposed association to Step 3. For a new thesis, search the same entity-wide evidence library. A concrete working draft of the Jev comparison request appears in [section 13](#13-step-2-prompt-jev-working-draft).\n\n## 6. Step 3: save evidence and links\n\nStep 3 takes the proposals from Step 1 and the comparison outcomes from Step 2 and writes the resulting research information.\n\n| Result | What is saved |\n|---|---|\n| New thesis with supporting evidence | The new thesis and its evidence link; save new evidence or reuse a matching record according to Step 2. |\n| New evidence for an existing thesis | The evidence and its link to the thesis. |\n| Repeat | Place the incoming record in the existing evidence record's group: add its source and any missing link to the relevant thesis, and keep the incoming record's own text and fields inside the group. Do not rewrite the existing record, and do not create a separate evidence record for the same statement. |\n| Revision | The new evidence and its source, linked to the earlier statement as a revision. |\n\nA revision does not erase the earlier statement. On 2026-11-06, Alex chose to keep one evidence record for a repeat and add the new source and any missing thesis link. On 2026-11-08 the Step 2 validation showed that keeping only the earlier text discards details a later report adds, such as a guidance condition; Alex then chose to keep each repeat's own text and source inside the stored record's group, without rewriting the original (\"A 继续；跑council loop 开始做\"; [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §7). How a judgement reads a group is being tested. Multiple articles reporting the same statement do not automatically establish independent original sources. The current production writer stores separate repeat records, so this is a change for the clean path and needs implementation and verification; the existing historical records remain available. Step 3 ends when the chosen thesis, evidence and relationships are saved. How they are physically stored and written is a separate implementation decision.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:26:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat COUNCIL-LOOP.md result-blind-codex.md result-blind-claude.md r05-adjudication.md results-codex-followup.md'","aggregated_output":"(eval):5: parse error near `end'\n(eval):5: parse error near `end'\n# Step 2 identity: council loop (2026-11-09)\n\n## Frame\n\n- Objective: diagnose and find a general, measurable improvement for wrong grouping, missed grouping and incorrect update relations on the clean Step 2 path, without changing frozen ruler files or the adopted configuration.\n- Reference: c8 Step 1 plus current clean Step 2 Jev 1.14.0, the original saved requests and forward decisions. D381's chosen acceptance lines: at least 90% search, at most 2% clear wrong merges, no loss of later details in grouped dossiers. These do not by themselves cover missed grouping, incorrect revision edges, downstream utility, or an independent comparator's quality; no new pass line has been approved.\n- The ruler remains AGENTS §7.1 golden-v5: dev SHA 6f9476bb… and test af2145cd… (`golden-v5.sha256`); for the historical clean Step 2 run its frozen source-backed reference and D381 line are retained with their stated scope. New AI source readers are diagnostic opinions, not replacements for the frozen ruler. Pairwise selected examples are not a benchmark.\n- Scope: Step 2 comparison, candidate context and its actual grouping/revision consequences; unrelated Step 1 changes, downstream redesign, production migration, broader acceptance and frozen-file edits are outside scope. Users' eventual decision is required for a changed product behavior. A paid run beyond a few-cent probe or a full forward acceptance requires Alex's explicit word (AGENTS §9); budget for any proposed probe must be fixed from actual bytes before sending. No new paid diagnostic has been sent.\n\n## Census and trace\n\nSee `INVESTIGATION.md`, `stored-census.md` in the prior report and `claude-r1.md`. Stored outcomes: fresh 1 clear wrong/57 merge decisions plus two overlaps; three dev draws 1 wrong instance each, including a repeated pair, so not independent samples. 84 revision edges over the four stored runs, not a measured error rate. A false revision on 19→9:e5 remains independent of earlier separate groups for the 70% guidance; 30→20 is a wrong revision on an already duplicated announcement. Earlier 20→8 was a missed repeat according to direct source reading. Deliberately chosen blind source cases have disagreement; no performance inference.\n\n## Council A and challenges\n\nPrior independent Claude: `claude-r1.md`; independent new Codex: `codex-independent-v2.md`; recorded own reflection before reading: `ROUND-REFLECTION.md`. Codex first proposed a joint context approach; after the challenge `codex-challenge-v2.txt`, it withdrew that as first test (`codex-challenge-v2.md`) because it mixed changing model, context, output and selection and overlooked the explicit exclusion of mispaired figure fields. Recommends same-input independent comparator alongside fresh Jev full-batch and single-question controls; independently audit typed data before considering extra context. 19's basis text could help but it is not established necessary or sufficient. B08 and B11 remain contested; never use consensus as a replacement ruler.\n\nThe 12-pair selected packet, archive reconstruction, provider requests and correction of request category order and original question IDs are recorded in `INVESTIGATION.md`, `PILOT-DESIGN.md` and request-audit rounds 1–2. They do not constitute a send-ready sender, response validator, acceptance plan or cost authorization. 108-call draft is a discussion artifact, not the authorized first probe. All 188 fresh records were counted: 74 have figures, 72 have at least one basis, with 158 figure objects; this establishes scope, not semantic reliability. Clean PRD §5 and conditions design §14 exclude fields from search and comparison because figures can be attached to the wrong measures.\n\nD392 is complete (main commit 9552c8d); do not claim it is live. Recheck current process state before any send. Further Claude challenge is in progress, and the frozen control/test design and paid-call safety gates remain open.\n\nCurrent process check during council: D392 Stage 2 ended, but a separate q12 c13 paid probe started in the main checkout (`q12_probe.py ... --send`, PID 50314 at observation). Do not send Step 2 calls or run heavy tasks beside it; recheck status later. Do not inspect its live database or alter its work.\n\nDirect check: completed `reader-packet.json` source `gnews:26fd862b0d58999b7451b493` (2026-09-05) describes Nvidia's Thursday $12.9bn announcement; source `gnews:243636e860a787a59c4d0c7a` (2026-09-06) describes its Thursday $13bn announcement, same company and target. This supports the 20→8 missed repeat as a source-backed lead. Neither article independently proves the truth of the acquisition. Fresh record `13:e4` attributes the production statement to Nvidia while the stored quotation states it in the reporter's voice; this limits source-attribution confidence, separately from the clear distinction between the revenue-share forecast and August deliveries. No Step 1 change follows in this scope.\n\nThe previously frozen 12-pair pilot covers the later 30→20 false revision but not the antecedent 20→8 false split. It must not be sent or reported as testing missed repeats; fixing the candidate list changes the frozen diagnostic manifest and requires a new hash and independent source-backed controls before any send. Earlier planned same-input comparator also does not fix retrieval misses or define disagreement handling; its source observations cannot estimate full-forward performance.\nThe direct 20→8 trace was inspected in awareness of the saved Jev outcome; a new source-blind reader must inspect that pair before it is used as an independent positive control. A reader who saw the model decision is not blind.\n\n## New Claude challenge and cross-review\n\n`claude-challenge-v2.md` independently agrees that the prior 12-pair packet omits 20→8 and 14→9, that comparison input excludes figures intentionally, and that revision links need their own review. It retracts its earlier confident B08 revision label after seeing the record's main statement and reader disagreement. Two independent source readers consider B05 different and B06 the same statement. Its suggested 20-pair, five-arm, three-draw plan is a 300-call, roughly $0.65 estimated probe; this exceeds the project's few-cent probe allowance and is not authorized by a request for a council review. Its claims that a second model failing proves an input limitation, that a missed merge alone causes a later false revision, or that an added article publication date establishes statement chronology are not established. Its source-backed useful detail is that the 70% basis is an unverified model-written paraphrase outside the attached passages; the actual comparison already distinguishes management guidance and customer forecasts. Its B08 conclusion remains a source interpretation, not a gold correction. Both reviewers are being challenged on these points, scope and a smaller test (`claude-challenge-v3.txt`, `codex-challenge-v3.txt`).\n\n## Additional blind checks, frozen before any new comparator answer\n\nTwo new source-blind readers (`blind-missed-codex.md`, `blind-missed-claude.md`) independently judge both H01 (20→8, announcement $12.9bn vs $13bn) and H02 (14→9, 70% outlook) to be the same main statement, while documenting uncertainty in reported individual speaker attribution and rounding. These are independent AI opinions, not frozen gold or evidence that the underlying announcement happened.\n\nFor potential revision controls, a fixed seed 2401 selected six from the 56 Step 2 `supersedes` answers in the completed D392 clean d1 draw; the list is frozen in `blind-revisions-key.json`. Both source articles were taken from that draw's saved Step 1 requests, with identity checks. `blind-revisions.json` sha256 bed863cc577bc7b4a80a98d25ec45d7fb57b593f9eec850001158b26486bbdd4. Reviewers see no Jev outputs or selection reason. The selection is conditional on Jev's supersedes answers and is not an estimate of overall Step 2 accuracy; this tiny sample is to find source-backed controls and preserve disputes.\n\nAfter direct challenge, Claude withdrew the claim that two models failing proves the source text is insufficient, withdrew the date-only and unreviewed-figures arms from the first probe, and acknowledged the spending authorization limit. It agreed that preventing an earlier false split does not by itself establish why the later model chose an incorrect revision. It now recommends a same-input Jev full-batch, Jev single-question, independent-model single-question diagnostic, with proper controls and separate interpretation of disputed evidence (`claude-challenge-v3.md`). Both Claude and Codex agree B09 alone is an imperfect positive revision control because the original report already contains the denial; do not present it as satisfying the regression-control gap. The new H01/H02 blind reviews completed after Claude's reply and both independently judged same; the source-backed six-revision audit is still underway. No sender is implemented, no probe is authorized merely by council agreement, and no new model probe has been sent.\n\n## Source-backed revision sample: council B input\n\nTwo independent blind readers received all six fixed-sample pairs. They agree R05 is different (Nvidia explained Shield TV stock shortages without confirming or denying a discontinuation report), R06 is the same statement (two reports of the LangChain release), and R02 is uncertain (a broad platform statement does not clearly address a specific rack delay). They disagreed on R01 (same vs supersedes), R03 (different vs uncertain) and R04 (different vs supersedes). An independent third blinded reader read only the three disputes (`blind-revisions-third.md`): R01 uncertain, R03 different, R04 different. These are AI source opinions, not frozen gold; majority does not remove the recorded disputes. The two unanimous non-revision opinions are leads that the Jev-emitted revision edges were incorrect, subject to source/contract review. The six were randomly drawn conditional on a Jev supersedes choice; several concern the same Kyber story, and a sample of six cannot estimate error rate. The old and new full articles and records are in `blind-revisions.json`; its fixed mapping is in `blind-revisions-key.json`. No clear two-reader valid revision control has been established by this sample; B09 retains the caveat that the denial was already included in the earlier article. No experiment can claim to protect valid revisions until a source-backed control is available.\n\nCodex's source-and-contract challenge (`codex-results-challenge.md`) found R05 a strong wrong-link example, but argues R06 is not equally decisive: an announcement by a related company might also be read as official confirmation, so preserve the ambiguity despite two blind same-statement opinions. It recommends locating a valid revision control before any repair claim and warns that the observed 1/57 wrong-merge rate and targeted missed-repeat cases cannot be compared as population rates. Additional selected possible controls P01/P02 were source-blinded in `blind-positive-revisions.json` (sha256 4fa4fd14003978eea185466ce20b22a78435cc1ed70d2a2640b02945d665f616); these were manually selected as possible controls, not random. One reader finds P01 an official denial of the stored main claim and P02 uncertain; a second blinded reader is in progress. The 20→8 and 14→9 original Jev requests and original answers were independently reconstructed against the completed archive, `missed-pair-requests.json` sha256 09ce47d0220ee29931e053dde957814243c166b231317ca96cf1903f98d20a50. The two candidate control Jev requests also matched the completed D392 archive, `positive-pair-requests.json` sha256 44b0a154fe8ac9e1aa37872fbf9fec5a301189250c577f9ec6b8f95c7ea79f77. This reconstruction does not constitute a source adjudication or send readiness.\n\nP01 (54:e3→45:e1) is now source-blinded by two independent readers: both judge official denial of the Kyber NVL144 delay as `supersedes` (`blind-positive-revisions-codex.md`, `blind-positive-revisions-claude.md`). Both note the original source article already includes the denial, the original response time is unknown, and the old record bundles a separate NVL576 claim not explicitly addressed by the denial. This is a usable but bounded stress control under the current definitions, not an uncontested example of a chronologically later revision or proof that all accompanying statements were denied. P02 remains uncertain/different and is not a positive control. Neither model opinion edits the frozen reference.\n\nIndependent follow-up `codex-positive-review.md` accepts P01 as a bounded control for the current report–official-denial relationship. Its caveats do not prohibit a selected-case diagnostic; they do prohibit claiming protection of later forecast revisions without a separate source-backed chronological control. It does not settle NVL576. No reviewer adjudication is treated as frozen gold.\n\nA provisional six-pair shortlist is generated offline in `shortlist-requests-DRAFT.json` (sha256 6b04ccf0919bef6b5aeac6a480dfdcfa7d51b3dc05c1375a0ac2a2965a934654) by `prepare-shortlist.py`. It comprises B01 (valid repeat), B05 (wrong revision), B10 (wrong merge), H01/H02 (missed repeats, H02 has speaker attribution caveat), and P01 (bounded official-denial control). It is not frozen or ready to send: an independent request review is running, a sender and failure controls do not exist, P01 cannot alone establish later-forecast revision protection, and historical Jev answers are not same-day controls. At the repository's dated rates and conservative input/output reservation formula, one draw's 18 calls reserve about $0.0594, and three draws about $0.1782; these estimates are not guaranteed bills. AGENTS §9 requires Alex's explicit word before paid sends beyond a few cents, and a full forward acceptance also has its own authorization boundary. No paid Step 2 call has been sent in this round.\n\n`shortlist-audit.md` independently checked all six historical full bodies against their archives, candidate IDs and original question IDs, and regenerated all DeepSeek bodies in memory: they match. It confirmed reservations of $0.059389 per draw and $0.178167 for three, at dated rates, not enforced limits. It identified a duplicate-ID precondition gap in the offline preparation script, which was corrected with an exact count check; existing draft bytes were not overwritten. It also confirmed the sender, per-attempt accounting, failure/finish-reason validation, cache separation, frozen source/reader references and authorization boundary are still unbuilt. The shortlist remains a draft and no send is cleared.\n\nR05's Jev answer is `supersedes` 0.90, even though the incoming summary says Nvidia would neither confirm nor deny discontinuation; this is not a low-score-only failure. The historical Jev requests and answers for R05 and disputed R06 were reconstructed byte for byte from the completed D392 archive in `revision-extra-requests.json` (sha256 e0e75db060dafaede976330311f4fe05937c50d3186b0a9254bb35b2ddbfe2a8). The six-pair shortlist predates R05 and should not be frozen as complete; any new shortlist including it must be regenerated, costed and independently audited. Original-party attribution in the stored R05 record is also suspect; the comparator sees that field, so avoid claiming to know which signal caused Jev's answer.\n\nCouncil B discussion retains a real sequencing disagreement: Claude proposes first characterizing Jev full-batch vs single-pair repeatability, whereas Codex proposes including the independent comparator in the first paired diagnostic. Either approach needs fresh controls and a bounded source-backed revision control. A passing rerun does not erase the previously observed defect, and a non-reproduced selected case does not establish that a whole class is random noise. Both reviewers were sent a targeted final challenge; no verdict is inferred before they answer.\n\n## Final challenge: convergence and next experiment\n\n`codex-final-challenge.md` and `claude-final-challenge.md` converge on a paired diagnostic: fresh Jev full batch, fresh Jev single pair, and an independent same-input comparison in the same round. Claude withdrew its proposed Jev-only stop gate: a later passing draw does not undo an already observed bad write, and a few changing outcomes cannot establish class-wide noise. Both retain R06 as disputed because `same_statement` and official confirmation can overlap; it is excluded from a decisive tally. Both accept P01 only as a bounded direct-denial control, not a later-forecast-change control. Group ranking, disagreement handling and actual forward effects remain untested. Repeated sends are needed to claim repeatability; a single draw can find an obvious failure but cannot establish superiority.\n\nAfter R05 was reviewed, the offline shortlist was rebuilt to include it alongside B01, B05, B10, H01 and P01. Current `shortlist-requests-DRAFT.json` SHA-256 cb142acfe3491dcf6ccfaa70545ffe3c011e3646a2348b36954b04ec2f3321ce. Dated-price reservations are $0.060671 for one draw of 18 calls and $0.182013 for three draws of 54 calls, excluding uncertain billing, retries and differing provider rates. This supersedes the earlier six-case draft, which contained H02 instead of R05. The updated shortlist is undergoing independent request audit; it is not a frozen manifest, complete sender, approval or a result. Historical source and response files remain immutable. The q12 c13 process previously observed no longer appeared in a later process check, but run status must be rechecked before any new sends. The next actual work is finish no-cost input/control/sender and independent reviews; preserve user authority for sends beyond the few-cent boundary and for any change to production behavior.\n\n`shortlist-audit-r2.md` independently verified the revised six historical archive bodies and original answers, all 18 prepared provider bodies and hashes, unique-case precondition, choice order and reservation calculation. It confirms source packets and blind-reader judgments are not pinned, the response validator and sender are unbuilt, and the set cannot measure overall error rates, retrieval or group ranking. Review of offline request construction does not clear a paid send or constitute an experimental result.\n\n## Paired diagnostic sent and stopped on a regression\n\nAlex said “开始” after the six-case, three-arm, three-draw scope and estimated spend were explained. The isolated sender passed design review r4, 18 fake tests, ten targeted mutation checks, a final implementation review r5 and the clean Jev archived-response/identity checks. Preflight for frozen v108 reproduced 243 extraction answers, 726 judged/12 dropped, 170 matching judgments with two skipped, and two earlier schema-refused rows; it does not itself exercise clean Step 2. The 54 actual requests completed without retry or reported failure, with the full request bodies, per-attempt events and completion marker in `probe-2026-11-09/`. The result table and limitations are `RESULTS-DRAFT.md`.\n\nThe council recommends rejecting this candidate because of a serious new regression: on B05 the independent model called `supersedes` 3/3 while Jev single called `different` 3/3 (fresh Jev full called `supersedes` 2/3). Both prior blind source readers judged B05 different. Controls B01 and P01 were preserved in all 3 draws of each arm. H01 and R05 changed in a source-supported direction 3/3 for independent versus 0/3 for both Jev arms; B10 differs by Jev request shape and draw. These are selected-case observations, not system precision or recall. Product change is not authorized or justified; stop this line, do not add cases or fit a follow-up prompt. Each result is being re-read independently and source-blind, with all 54 answers shuffled in `result-blind-packet.json`; council B reviews independently without sending extra experimental requests.\n\nCorrection after Council B: the preregistered explicit stop controls named B01 and P01, not B05. The B05 3/3 vs 0/3 regression is a new serious finding and reason to stop, not an automatic predeclared stop trigger. Full raw evidence and the disagreement between fresh full versus single Jev remain disclosed in RESULTS-DRAFT.md.\n\nFinal council B complete: `results-codex.md`, `results-claude.md` and `results-codex-followup.md`. Both agree 54 calls completed, the comparator cannot be adopted, B05's source-backed regression is important, and it did **not** trip an explicitly predeclared B01/P01 stop condition. In 3/3 H01 the independent pair judgment matches the original announcement where full/single Jev miss it; in B05 the independent comparator answers supersedes 3/3 while Jev-single answers different 3/3 and Jev-full answers different once. R05's outcome is disputed: both initial source-only readers said different, but an arm-blind reader chose supersedes because the original publisher's later story is more cautious; a fresh output-blind source adjudicator chose different while acknowledging the other interpretation. This remains a dispute, not a majority-vote gold. P01's additional full-Jev revision edge is also disputed. The judges of the 54 shuffled answers were blind to arms but the choice list appeared in the same packet as the source; one explicitly acknowledged seeing it, so do not describe the reading as entirely output-blind. Under the stopped-candidate rule no follow-up tuning or paid send is initiated. A complete source-backed census of the 56 D392 d1 supersedes answers is a possible next no-cost research angle, not a measured conclusion or a requested new benchmark. Per the user-authorized test, no production changes and no new reference are adopted.\nI judged all **six pairs and 54 anonymous answers**: 34 consistent with my reading, 14 inconsistent, and 6 unresolved. These are diagnostic opinions based only on the supplied packet, not frozen gold.\n\nFor example, in T02, Nvidia’s reported **70% revenue forecast** and its customers’ reported **potential for revenue to double** concern the same period, but describe different things. One does not automatically replace the other.\n\n## Independent readings\n\n**T01 — `same_statement`.** Both articles attribute the same fiscal 2027 and 2028 AI chip revenue outlook to Broadcom. The incoming source says “about $115 billion” for fiscal 2027 and “roughly $230 billion” for fiscal 2028; the stored source says “$115 billion” and “$230 billion” for those years. Both articles are dated September 6. The incoming record adds customers and says the 2027 forecast was raised from over $100 billion; those details do not establish a different main forecast. Neither article supplies the exact day of the underlying statement.\n\n**T02 — `different`.** The stored record’s main statement is that Kress said **customer forecasts** indicated revenue “may double next year.” The incoming record’s main statement is the company’s **70% fiscal 2028 revenue growth forecast**. The stored article itself separately reports both: the 70% guidance and the customer-demand indication. The incoming article also says unconstrained growth could be “a lot higher” than 70%, but that overlap with the stored record’s secondary Huang statement does not make the main statements identical. The incoming $673 billion–$700 billion figure is the article’s calculation from consensus, not a separately attributed Nvidia forecast. The articles were published September 5 and 6; that order does not establish a revision of customer demand.\n\n**T03 — `supersedes`.** The stored main claim is SemiAnalysis’s report that “Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028.” The incoming article reports that Nvidia “rejected the claims” and said its roadmap remains intact. That is a direct official denial of the main delay claim under the requested definition. It does not prove which party is right or specifically settle the stored record’s secondary NVL576 claim. The stored article already contains Nvidia’s denial, so this is not necessarily a newly issued response. Its publication metadata says July 5 while its text and URL refer to Monday, July 6; the precise sequence is therefore uncertain.\n\n**T04 — `same_statement`.** Both articles report Nvidia’s Thursday announcement of a Hugging Face acquisition: the stored article calls it a “$12.9 billion deal to purchase,” while the incoming article says “$13 billion.” The amounts are compatible with rounding, and the parties and announced transaction match. The incoming September 6 article refers to reporting by EU Tech Loop and Euronews; the stored article is dated September 5. “Acquisition” in the incoming wording does not establish that the deal closed, and neither record establishes regulatory approval.\n\n**T05 — `different`.** The stored main statement says Vera Rubin is “already in production” and forecasts about **20% of data-center revenue** in fiscal Q3. The incoming statement says systems based on Vera Rubin “had already been delivered commercially to customers during August 2026.” Production and commercial delivery are related stages, but they are distinct claims; delivery does not confirm or replace the 20% forecast. Both reports attribute their information to Nvidia around the earnings call, but the article dates alone do not prove that the underlying statements were made on different days.\n\n**T06 — `different`, with a boundary ambiguity.** The stored article’s main conclusion is that the base Shield TV **appears** discontinued, based on retail availability; it explicitly says it could “simply be an out-of-stock situation.” The later article quotes Nvidia: the base model is “largely out of stock due to demand” and it has “no updates” on future availability. Nvidia neither confirms nor denies discontinuation. The July 13 article also reports that B&H changed its status from “discontinued” to “No Longer Available.” It describes the future as “in limbo,” but does not explicitly retract the July 8 article’s qualified conclusion. I therefore read the official response as a distinct statement. There is a reasonable unresolved boundary over whether the same publisher’s later, more cautious framing should count as changing its earlier main statement.\n\n## Anonymous answer assessments\n\nEach answer below is judged against the reading above. The answer’s choice is shown, followed by my assessment.\n\n**T01**\n\n| Answer | Choice | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A02 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A03 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A04 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A05 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A06 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A07 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A08 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A09 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n\nAll nine match the same attributed 2027 and 2028 forecast; the added prior forecast and customer context do not change that main statement.\n\n**T02**\n\n| Answer | Choice | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A02 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A03 | `supersedes` | Inconsistent |\n| A04 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A05 | `supersedes` | Inconsistent |\n| A06 | `supersedes` | Inconsistent |\n| A07 | `supersedes` | Inconsistent |\n| A08 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A09 | `supersedes` | Inconsistent |\n\nThe four `different` answers preserve the distinction between customer-indicated demand and supply-constrained company guidance. The five `supersedes` answers treat the 70% guidance as replacing or officially confirming or denying the customer-demand statement; the sources instead present both together.\n\n**T03**\n\n| Answer | Choice | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A02 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A03 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A04 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A05 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A06 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A07 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A08 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A09 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n\nAll nine identify Nvidia’s reported direct rejection of the main Kyber delay claim. The label does not decide the truth of the dispute.\n\n**T04**\n\n| Answer | Choice | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | `different` | Inconsistent |\n| A02 | `different` | Inconsistent |\n| A03 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A04 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A05 | `different` | Inconsistent |\n| A06 | `different` | Inconsistent |\n| A07 | `different` | Inconsistent |\n| A08 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A09 | `different` | Inconsistent |\n\nThe three `same_statement` answers fit the same announced deal. The six `different` answers are not supported by a distinct transaction or established closing event; $13 billion is compatible with a rounded $12.9 billion.\n\n**T05**\n\n| Answer | Choice | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A02 | `same_statement` | Inconsistent |\n| A03 | `same_statement` | Inconsistent |\n| A04 | `same_statement` | Inconsistent |\n| A05 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A06 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A07 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A08 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A09 | `different` | Consistent |\n\nThe six `different` answers distinguish actual commercial deliveries from production status and a revenue-share forecast. The three `same_statement` answers conflate those related but distinct claims.\n\n**T06**\n\n| Answer | Choice | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | `supersedes` | Unresolved |\n| A02 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A03 | `supersedes` | Unresolved |\n| A04 | `supersedes` | Unresolved |\n| A05 | `supersedes` | Unresolved |\n| A06 | `supersedes` | Unresolved |\n| A07 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A08 | `supersedes` | Unresolved |\n| A09 | `different` | Consistent |\n\nThe three `different` answers match my reading: Nvidia directly addresses stock but leaves discontinuation unanswered. I cannot conclusively reject the six `supersedes` answers because the same publisher’s later framing is more cautious and reports a changed retailer listing. The later article does not explicitly retract its earlier inference, however, and Nvidia does not deny it.**结论：6 组、54 个答案全部判完，37 个与我的读法一致，14 个不一致，3 个未决。**\n\n我的六个独立读法是：T01 `same_statement`，T02 `different`，T03 `supersedes`，T04 `same_statement`，T05 `different`，T06 `supersedes`（六组里最没把握的一组）。\n\n先说三点：\n\n- 我只读了这一个文件，没有写入或修改任何东西。\n- 文件里每组的九个选择紧跟在原文后面，我读的时候不可能看不到。下面每个判断只用原文和记录来论证，没有用票数；T02 和 T04 里我的读法与九个里的多数相反。\n- 这是一个 AI 读者的诊断意见，不是金标。\n\n## T01：`same_statement`（把握高）\n\n两条记录说的是同一个预测：Broadcom 预计 AI 芯片收入 2027 财年约 1150 亿美元，2028 财年约 2300 亿美元。\n\n- **存量原文：** \"Broadcom told investors that it expects its AI chip revenue to double to $115 billion in its fiscal 2027, and then to double yet again to $230 billion in its fiscal 2028.\"\n- **新进原文：** \"The company recently announced raising the forecast for its AI chip revenue to about $115 billion in the fiscal year ending October 2027, up from a prior forecast of over $100 billion. It also expects this to double to roughly $230 billion in fiscal 2028.\"\n\n限定：\n\n- **说话人：** 两边都是 Broadcom 自己。\n- **时间：** 存量说是财报电话会（\"in the chipmaker's earnings call\"），新进只说 \"recently\"。两篇原文都没给电话会的日期，所以\"同一场合\"是从数字和财年完全相同推出来的，不是原文明说的。\n- **主次：** 新进第一句 \"custom chips are increasingly being used by companies such as Meta, Google, and OpenAI\" 是文章作者的描述，不是 Broadcom 的预测。\"up from a prior forecast of over $100 billion\" 说的是更早的旧预测；存量里已经是 1150 亿这个新数，所以新进没有改动存量。\n- **歧义：** 没有实质歧义。\n\n| 答案 | 选择 | 判定 | 原因 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | same_statement | 一致 | 同一方、同一组数字和财年 |\n| A02 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A03 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A04 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A05 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A06 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A07 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A08 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A09 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n\n## T02：`different`（把握较高）\n\n同一个场合说了两件事：公司给的指引是增长 70%（受供给限制），客户预测显示的需求是可能翻倍。存量记的是后者，新进记的是前者。\n\n- **存量原文：** \"CFO Colette Kress highlighted that customer forecasts currently indicate Nvidia's revenue may double next year. Huang also said Nvidia's revenue growth could be \"a lot higher\" if the company were not supply-constrained.\"\n- **新进原文：** \"CFO Colette Kress told investors on August 26 that fiscal 2028 revenue growth will reach 70% … marking the company's first-ever year-ahead revenue forecast.\"\n- **两者并存的证据（存量原文）：** \"He said Nvidia expects revenue to grow about 70% in its fiscal 2028 … But management says even that forecast doesn't reflect the full scope of demand\"。\n- **两者并存的证据（新进原文）：** \"Huang told analysts the unconstrained growth rate would be \"a lot higher\" than 70%\"，以及 \"Management said the 70% figure shows supply constraints … rather than a ceiling on actual demand\"。\n\n不是 `supersedes` 的原因：70% 不是后来才说、用来改掉\"翻倍\"的。两句话互不否定，官方也没有确认或否认\"客户预测显示翻倍\"。\n\n限定：\n\n- **说话人：** 新进把 70% 归给 Kress，而且是转述（\"CNBC reported that\"）。存量原文把 70% 归给黄仁勋（\"CEO Jensen Huang … said Nvidia expects revenue to grow about 70%\"）。两篇文章对\"谁说的 70%\"说法不一。\n- **时间：** 新进给了 8 月 26 日；存量原文没有日期。\"同一场合\"是我从两篇都引了 \"a lot higher\" 这句话推出来的。存量的 \"next year\" 按所在小节标题（\"Supply constraints could limit Nvidia's fiscal 2028 growth\"）读作 2028 财年，字面上有歧义。\n- **主次：** 新进第二段 \"$673 billion to $700 billion\" 是文章自己的算术，不是 Nvidia 的话。存量第二句（\"a lot higher\"）在新进原文里有，但新进记录没有收。\n- **歧义：** 两条说的是同一件大事（2028 财年收入增长），但命题不同。如果规则把\"同一件事\"算作同一条，结论会变。\n\n| 答案 | 选择 | 判定 | 原因 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | different | 一致 | 指引 70% 与\"客户预测显示翻倍\"是两个命题 |\n| A02 | different | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A03 | supersedes | 不一致 | 两句同场并存，70% 没有改掉或否定\"翻倍\" |\n| A04 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A05 | supersedes | 不一致 | 同 A03 |\n| A06 | supersedes | 不一致 | 同 A03 |\n| A07 | supersedes | 不一致 | 同 A03 |\n| A08 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A09 | supersedes | 不一致 | 同 A03 |\n\n## T03：`supersedes`（把握较高，靠的是\"官方直接否认\"这一条）\n\n- **存量原文（SemiAnalysis 的说法）：** \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\"。\n- **新进原文：** \"Nvidia rejected the claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\"\n\n限定：\n\n- **说话人：** 存量的原始说话方是 SemiAnalysis，新进是 Nvidia。SemiAnalysis 没有改口；这是当事公司否认第三方的说法，是争议，不是撤回。\n- **时间：** 否认并不比存量文章新。存量的 CNBC 原文里就有 \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\"，只是存量记录没收这一句。\n- **日期字段：** 存量来源的 `publication_date` 写 2026-07-05，但网址是 `/2026/07/06/`，正文说 \"in a post on Monday\"，而 2026-07-06 才是星期一。字段和原文不一致。\n- **具体度：** 新进是转述（\"telling media outlets\"），没有直接引语。否认是笼统的\"路线图不变\"，没有逐条回应 Kyber、2028、PCB 中板或 NVL576。\n- **歧义：** \"官方否认\"不等于\"说法不成立\"。标签成立，但存量那条仍是有效的一方观点。\n\n| 答案 | 选择 | 判定 | 原因 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | supersedes | 一致 | 当事公司直接否认该报告 |\n| A02 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A03 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A04 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A05 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A06 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A07 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A08 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A09 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n\n## T04：`same_statement`（把握高）\n\n同一方、同一个星期四、同一笔交易；129 亿和 130 亿只是四舍五入的差别。\n\n- **存量原文：** \"On Thursday, NVIDIA – market cap: $5.55 trillion – announced a $12.9 billion deal to purchase Hugging Face\"。\n- **新进原文：** \"American chipmaker NVIDIA announced on Thursday its acquisition of the artificial intelligence library Hugging Face for $13 billion (EUR11.2bn).\"\n\n限定：\n\n- **时间：** 存量文章（9 月 5 日，周六）说 \"announcement this week … On Thursday\"，新进文章（9 月 6 日，周日）说 \"on Thursday\"。两者都指 2026-09-03。\n- **转述链条：** 新进是三手转述（\"reported by EU Tech Loop and shared on Euronews\"），数字更粗。\n- **主次：** 存量第二段（竞购激烈、1800 万开发者、20 万企业客户）是文章的描述，不是 Nvidia 公告的内容。新进没有这些，不影响主陈述相同。\n- **歧义：** 新进标题 \"NVIDIA Acquires\" 像是已完成，但正文是 \"announced\"，并提到可能有 \"a regulatory review of the deal's terms\"。存量也是\"已宣布、未完成\"，状态一致。\n\n| 答案 | 选择 | 判定 | 原因 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | different | 不一致 | 同一方、同一天、同一笔交易，金额只差四舍五入 |\n| A02 | different | 不一致 | 同上 |\n| A03 | same_statement | 一致 | 同一公告 |\n| A04 | same_statement | 一致 | 同一公告 |\n| A05 | different | 不一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A06 | different | 不一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A07 | different | 不一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A08 | same_statement | 一致 | 同一公告 |\n| A09 | different | 不一致 | 同 A01 |\n\n## T05：`different`（把握中到较高）\n\n存量说的是\"已在生产，第三财季约占数据中心收入 20%\"；新进说的是\"8 月已向客户商业交付\"。投产和交付是两个节点，20% 这个数新进没有，8 月交付存量没有。\n\n- **存量原文：** \"Nvidia's big upcoming growth driver is the Vera Rubin system, which is already in production. Management expects the new platform to account for about 20% of data center segment revenue in the third quarter.\"\n- **新进原文：** \"During the analyst call following the earnings release, Nvidia said that systems based on Vera Rubin had already been delivered commercially to customers during August 2026.\"\n\n不是 `supersedes` 的原因：交付没有改掉或否定\"在生产\"和\"20%\"，三者同时成立。\n\n限定：\n\n- **说话人：** 存量原文里 \"already in production\" 是作者的叙述，没有写是 Nvidia 说的；只有 \"Management expects … 20%\" 明确归给管理层。存量摘要写成 \"Nvidia said … already in production\"，归属比原文强。\n- **时间：** 新进记录的 `statement_date` \"August 2026\" 是交付发生的月份，不是说话的日期。说话是在财报后的分析师电话会，原文说财报是 \"A week and a half ago\"（文章日期 9 月 6 日）。存量没有日期。\n- **关联：** 第二财季截至 7 月 26 日，所以 8 月落在第三财季内。8 月交付是\"第三财季占 20%\"的佐证，属于同一件事的不同陈述。\n- **歧义：** 如果规则把\"同一产品的爬坡\"算同一条，会读成 `same_statement`；按命题是否相同来读，是 `different`。\n\n| 答案 | 选择 | 判定 | 原因 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | different | 一致 | 投产加 20% 预期，与 8 月交付是不同命题 |\n| A02 | same_statement | 不一致 | 两条记录没有一个共同的事实点（数字、时间、节点都不同） |\n| A03 | same_statement | 不一致 | 同 A02 |\n| A04 | same_statement | 不一致 | 同 A02 |\n| A05 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A06 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A07 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A08 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A09 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n\n## T06：`supersedes`（把握中等，六组里最不干净）\n\n新进就是存量里那封\"已去信询问\"的回信：同一家媒体、同一位作者（Craig Donaldson），五天后。它把存量的主陈述从\"看来已悄悄停产\"改成了\"Nvidia 说是因需求缺货，未来不明\"。\n\n- **存量原文：** \"it now appears that the cheaper Shield TV's time on the market has come to an end, as Nvidia seems to have quietly discontinued it (via AFTV News)\"，以及 \"Pocket-lint has reached out to Nvidia for official confirmation … we'll update this story if we hear back.\"\n- **新进原文：** \"Pocket-lint reached out to Nvidia for clarification on the Shield TV's future and received an email response\"，以及 \"\"The SHIELD TV base model is largely out of stock due to demand. We don't have any updates to share regarding future availability at this time.\"\"\n- **存量的关键证据也变了：** \"B&H Photo Video even labeled the Shield TV as \"discontinued\" before recently changing the product status to \"No Longer Available\"\"。\n\n限定：\n\n- **官方没有确认或否认停产。** 新进原文明说 \"the company sidestepped whether the device has been discontinued\"，小标题是 \"Nvidia won't say the Shield TV is discontinued / But it's not confirming that it's coming back either\"。所以\"官方直接确认或否认\"这一条字面上不满足；我的 `supersedes` 靠的是\"原来的出处改了主陈述\"这一条。\n- **说话人：** 存量记录的 `original_party` 写的是 Nvidia，但原文里\"停产\"是 Pocket-lint（引 AFTV News）根据零售商页面的推断。原文还说 \"Nvidia doesn't explicitly say the Shield TV has been discontinued on its website\"。这个字段和原文不符。\n- **主次：** 新进里 \"SHIELD TV Pro remains available\" 和持续更新是次要内容，存量里也有对应。\n- **歧义：** 按字面严格读，官方既没确认也没否认，新进就是另一个说话人的另一条陈述，`different` 说得通。所以选 `different` 的答案我记为未决，不记为不一致。\n\n| 答案 | 选择 | 判定 | 原因 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | supersedes | 一致 | 同一出处的后续，改了主陈述并带来官方回应 |\n| A02 | different | 未决 | 原文明说官方回避了停产问题，按字面严格读法站得住 |\n| A03 | supersedes | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A04 | supersedes | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A05 | supersedes | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A06 | supersedes | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A07 | different | 未决 | 同 A02 |\n| A08 | supersedes | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A09 | different | 未决 | 同 A02 |\n\n## 计数\n\n按组：\n\n| 组 | 我的读法 | 一致 | 不一致 | 未决 | 合计 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| T01 | same_statement | 9 | 0 | 0 | 9 |\n| T02 | different | 4 | 5 | 0 | 9 |\n| T03 | supersedes | 9 | 0 | 0 | 9 |\n| T04 | same_statement | 3 | 6 | 0 | 9 |\n| T05 | different | 6 | 3 | 0 | 9 |\n| T06 | supersedes | 6 | 0 | 3 | 9 |\n| 合计 | | 37 | 14 | 3 | 54 |\n\n按答案编号（文件没有说明同一编号在六组里是不是同一个作答者，这张表只在是的前提下才有意义）：\n\n| 答案 | 一致 | 不一致 | 未决 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | 5 | 1 | 0 |\n| A02 | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n| A03 | 4 | 2 | 0 |\n| A04 | 5 | 1 | 0 |\n| A05 | 4 | 2 | 0 |\n| A06 | 4 | 2 | 0 |\n| A07 | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n| A08 | 6 | 0 | 0 |\n| A09 | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n**判断：不同。** 新记录是 Nvidia 对缺货情况的新回应，涉及同一台设备，但没有重复、正式确认或否认旧记录的主要判断：标准版 Shield TV 似乎已经停产。这是基于所给材料的独立意见，不是金标。\n\n旧文章于 **2026 年 7 月 8 日**由 Pocket-lint 的 Craig Donaldson 发表。它的主要判断是：标准版 Shield TV 的销售寿命似乎已经结束，Nvidia “**seems to have quietly discontinued it**”，并注明这一线索来自 **AFTV News**。但文章同时明确留有余地：“**it’s possible this is simply an out-of-stock situation**”。当时 Pocket-lint 尚未得到 Nvidia 的正式确认，并表示已向公司询问。\n\n新文章于 **2026 年 7 月 13 日**由同一出版方、同一作者发表，报道了 Nvidia 的邮件回复。Nvidia 说：\n\n> “The SHIELD TV base model is largely out of stock due to demand. We don't have any updates to share regarding future availability at this time.”\n\nNvidia 还说 Pro 版仍有售，Shield 平台继续获得更新。**这些是 Nvidia 的正式回应，但不是对停产问题的正式回答**：缺货原因被归为需求旺盛，未来是否补货以及设备是否停产，都没有得到明确答复。文章也写道，公司 “**sidestepped whether the device has been discontinued**”。\n\n这两篇文章之间确实有变化：7 月 8 日，作者认为停产迹象很强；7 月 13 日，作者说设备的未来 “**remains in limbo**”。但后文没有说 Pocket-lint 或 AFTV News 撤回或更正了早先的判断。后文提到的是零售商 **B&H** 把商品状态从 “**discontinued**” 改为 “**No Longer Available**”；这是零售商标签的变化，不能当作出版方撤稿，也不能当作 Nvidia 否认停产。\n\n另一种合理解释是把新文章看成对旧报道的**后续更新**：同一作者拿到了先前等待的公司回应，也用了更谨慎的措辞。但它没有明确修正旧判断，因此如果要判断两条记录所表达的主要声明，较合适的是“不同”；真正的停产状态仍然**不确定**。\n\n两条记录都把 `original_party` 写成 Nvidia，但旧文章的停产判断是 **Pocket-lint 引述 AFTV News 并根据零售商情况作出的推断**，不是 Nvidia 的声明。新记录才有直接归属于 Nvidia 的邮件内容。两条记录都没有给出 Nvidia 具体发言日期；**7 月 8 日和 7 月 13 日是文章发表日期**，不能据此断定邮件是在 7 月 13 日发出的。**P01 对 `45:e3` 的修订链接应标为“有争议”，不能算已验证正确。** 因此，P01 的目标关系答对了，不能说明这条记录写出的所有关系都正确。\n\n`45:e3` 的主陈述是：SemiAnalysis 报道，两机柜拼接的备用方案被取消，并认为 Nvidia 缺少经过验证的扩展方案。新文章先报道 Kyber 延期，**也提到了同一备用方案被取消**，然后说 Nvidia 拒绝了“这些说法”。这使较宽的否认解读有一定依据；但 Nvidia 的实际表述只是“路线图完好”，没有具体说明是否否认备用方案取消，也没有逐项回应扩展方案问题。两篇都属转述，原始回应时间不清楚。\n\n实际 Jev 请求中的新记录只概括为 Nvidia 拒绝“延期说法”，没有带上新文章提到备用方案的上下文。按现行规则，只有回应了旧记录的**主陈述**才算修订；是否可以把这句宽泛回应扩展到 `45:e3`，现有材料不足以确定。见 [完整来源 P01](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/blind-positive-revisions.json)、[完整 Jev 请求，P01 q2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/probe-2026-11-09/bodies.json) 和 [比较规则](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2/compare.py:57)。\n\n三次完整 Jev 回答都把新记录放进 `45:e2`，同时连向 `45:e1` 和 `45:e3`。盲读对照只审了 `45:e1`，所以它支持的是 **`45:e1` 这条关系的判断**，不是整个写入结果的正确性。独立比较器也只回答了 `45:e1` 这一对。见 [结果重放](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/RESULTS-DRAFT.md)。\n\n**R05 我也要收窄之前的结论。** 我仍倾向于“不属于修订”：Nvidia 解释了缺货，但没有确认或否认停产。新的盲读指出，同一家媒体后来采用了更谨慎的表述，且零售商更改了商品状态；这使“后续报道是否改变了原推测”存在合理边界争议。因此，独立比较器的 `different` 是**有来源支持的改善线索**，不应再称为无争议地纠正了错误链接。见 [结果盲读 T06](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/result-blind-codex.md)。\n\n没有修改文件、调用模型或打开运行中的数据库。\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:30:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat reports/step2-wrong-merge-council-2026-11-09/CONCLUSIONS.md reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/CONCLUSIONS.md'","aggregated_output":"# Council: the fresh Step 2 wrong merge (X22)\n\nRead-only council, 2026-11-09. Codex and Claude Code independently traced the sources, the stored Jev request and answer, the Step 3 group and the reader's text; challenge round in `round2.jsonl`. Original user-requested scope: this one error, no product or ruler edits, no paid model calls.\n\n## What happened\n\nThe earlier 2026-09-05 Motley Fool source reported that Vera Rubin was in production and that management expected it to represent about 20% of Q3 data-centre revenue. The later 2026-09-06 Calcalist source reported that Nvidia said Vera Rubin systems were commercially delivered in August. The two source articles are preserved in `reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/blind-packets.json`, case 9 candidate C7 (key in blind-key.json); these are media reports, not the primary earnings-call transcript.\n\nArchived request for incoming 22:ev-11 against 13:e4 asked Jev to compare their source-backed text. It chose same_statement at 0.49, different 0.36, supersedes 0.15. `clean-arch/step2/compare.py:109` acts on the chosen label without a 0.5 floor, so the incoming record joined 13:e4. Retrieval found this candidate. The model provides no textual explanation; the reason for the choice is unproven.\n\nThe fresh merge review classified the two as different, and a third classified the merge wrong. Independent Codex review agrees; earlier Claude blind review called it overlap, and a separate Claude adjudicator called it different. Thus whether it is a clearly wrong merge rather than an overlap depends on reader judgment. We agree the delivery is not a straightforward repeat of the production statement and revenue forecast. The prior acceptance reference grouped underlying article matters as a later step and marked the delivery revision; that reference uses a broader revision concept than Step 2's comparison, and its unit is the article matter, not necessarily the system record. The most natural reading under the current comparison contract is a separate new statement; revision is a plausible interpretation of the milestone depending on the meaning of 'changed event-progress statement'. The primary statement of the original bundled production-and-forecast record is not formally identified. No change to the reference or contract is proposed.\n\n## Actual and potential impact\n\nThe clean prototype group retains the delivery record's own summary, quote and source as a member. Offline calling `group_text` on the saved group produces the label 'Later reports of the same statement' above that member: misleading grouping and label, not observed text deletion. The acceptance did not test whether this group changed a reader's investment judgment; Step 4 of this clean path was not built for this run. The production writer and live application were not changed. If a future reader treats group members as restatements, it could underweight a new milestone; that downstream consequence is a risk, not an observed result. The first article attributes the 20% forecast to management but does not explicitly attribute production status to Nvidia; its stored summary does, an attribution difference in this case without an established census.\n\n## A tempting threshold\n\nAn offline read of the 57 saved merges showed 2 selected repeats below 0.5: X22 at 0.49 (judged wrong by the original merge readers) and X47 at 0.47 (accepted by both original readers; Broadcom revenue forecasts). A 0.5 floor on this fixed draw would remove one wrong merge and one accepted merge. This is not a root cause proof or general performance measurement. No threshold change is recommended from this single comparison; old production code uses such a floor, while the clean prototype does not, and no decision about adding it to the clean path was established here.\n\nRelevant records: `poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/step2/decisions.json`, `library.json`, `jev.sqlite`, `merge-third-answer.json`; `poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/settled.json` and `prerun-prompt.md`; `clean-arch/step3/library.py`; `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §§43–44. The independent Codex review is `codex-review.md`. One full fresh test produced 1 clear wrong merge of 57 by its readers; this council did not reclassify its immutable result.\n# Step 2 wrong-merge council: candidate direction, not a proven fix\n\n2026-11-09. Alex requested a council to think through a general fix. Codex and Claude independently read the saved requests, source-backed records, decisions, current contracts and prior trials, challenged each other's proposals, and converged after further challenges. This is a read-only investigation; no model experiment, production/configuration change, new ruler, or change to the concurrent end-to-end run was made. Streams and handoffs are preserved in this directory.\n\n## What was found\n\nIn one fresh draw and three dev draws, 363 accepted-as-repeat outcomes received recorded reviews: 316 accepted, 43 overlap and 4 wrong. These are instance counts, not an independent population error rate: the three dev draws reuse the same articles and one source pair appears twice. The three different source-pair cases are: production/forecast versus commercial deliveries; revenue-level forecasts versus growth figures whose relation is not established by the records; and a cloud-provider revenue-share description versus an article describing a developer future-sales share. Same source or topic does not prove their specific statements identical. Source accuracy is separate from what a news report actually says. Reader/model disagreement remains and no historical label is changed. Raw source and count: `stored-census.md` and run-specific `merge-third-answer.json`, `library.json`, `decisions.json`.\n\nThe observed mechanism is that Jev selects repeat and the writer groups accordingly. The model does not produce a verbal account; the reason for these decisions is unproven. Some records bundle multiple related facts. The issue is not uniquely a low score: the cloud/developer case was selected at 0.84 in one draw. On the 363 fixed saved decisions a 0.5 floor would block one reader-marked wrong merge, four reader-accepted merges and one overlap; this is not a forward-run estimate. In an earlier 102-merge diagnostic an overly strict 'nothing new' rule reduced merges to 22, including valid retellings. Numbers are diagnostic, not an efficacy claim.\n\n## One candidate to investigate\n\nBefore grouping a pair that Jev says is a repeat, obtain an independent comparison, with the same source-backed input and without Jev's selection or probabilities: does the incoming statement actually report the old record's main statement? The checker must allow extra details and paraphrases and distinguish different facts, changed claims and cases whose relation cannot be established. This is a proposal for an experiment, not an established root fix, change to D381, a new automatic veto, or proof that another model's errors are independent. We do not choose a model or a disagreement rule in this council.\n\nUse original speaker, the subject, values, period, original date, hedges and actual source when relevant; do not infer the same original claim solely from similar topic, a matching number or common announcement. Different reporting outlets can repeat the same claim while retaining each source. Do not silently adopt D248 boundary 7's dormant exception or a new threshold. Current Jev comparison text excludes an explicit evidence-period field; adding it would be a separate changed input, not silently mixed into this comparison.\n\nThe reviewer must not treat a genuine extra detail as automatically disqualifying; X47 is an accepted example. Conversely a broad shared topic or a shared minor fact need not make a whole new record a repeat; X22 and AWS/three-cloud overlap are controls, with source-backed classifications and disagreement. The acceptance reference and Step 2 use different meanings/units for revision in X22; that concern stays separate and the frozen reference remains unchanged. The source itself does not show that the old production statement and the delivery claim were separate statements by the same original speaker, so the revision label is not settled by this case.\n\n## Evidence that would decide whether to proceed\n\n1. Start with a small, predeclared diagnostic of the three reader-marked wrong source pairs and counterexamples: accepted repeats with added details (including X44 and X47), true updates/denials, different speakers/periods, and known missed repeats. Use the identical Jev comparison payload for the independent comparator. It sees no prior result. A source-blind reader who did not write the proposal reviews the source and differences; same-day fresh Jev controls and request fidelity are required for model comparisons. A small pilot is a lead, not a performance claim.\n2. If the pilot provides an actual signal without obvious counterexample regressions, prospectively freeze an expanded evaluation of all 363 historical merged instances and known missed repeats and revisions, marking the model-written judgments and 43 contested overlaps; do not relabel frozen gold or infer that 363 are independent. Assess wrong groupings, correct repeats split, unmerged repeats, update errors and source fidelity; give every case and disagreement. Fixed-history comparison cannot establish new forward-history behavior, latency or downstream judgment impact.\n3. Only if those comparisons merit it, plan a separate isolated forward-path evaluation with a fresh same-day baseline, held-out articles and eventual reader/judgment effects, including cost and speed. Do not use the 50 fresh-test articles as previously unseen again. No new scorer substitutes for source-backed trace review.\n\nA useful test must show the independent comparison catches cases Jev misses without unnecessarily breaking true repeats or losing/relabeling updates. If errors are correlated, correct repeats are split, or the model's additional cost exceeds the observed value, do not adopt it. The pilot may be inconclusive, especially with few confirmed wrong pairs. It cannot prove absence of a problem.\n\nWhether a reviewer disagreement creates a new group, a pending case or some other outcome changes product behavior; it is not decided by the council and must be shown to Alex with evidence. Likewise source-matter revision vs statement revision and risk trade-offs need an explicit decision if the existing rules do not resolve them. The final delivered investment judgment has not been shown to suffer from X22; the currently running July end-to-end comparison does not contain this September case and cannot isolate this merge.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:32:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' clean-arch/step2/compare.py; sed -n '2338,2347p;2594,2619p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"Isolated Step 2 for the clean path (PRD §5): search the entity's stored evidence for each {thesis, incoming evidence} association,\nask Jev how the incoming evidence relates to each candidate, and turn the answers into the outcome Step 3 writes.\n\nSearch: one query per association (the thesis question, then the incoming summary and passages) against each group's original record\n(summary, passages, party, statement date, period), cosine similarity of embeddings, top 10; a record with no association, or one that\nlists the company's figures (a condition), also gets one entity-wide query of its own text (STEP1-CONDITIONS-DESIGN.md §10.1); the union\nof the results is compared. The figures never enter a search or comparison text, so a wrongly paired figure cannot change Step 2 (§14);\nthe comparison reads the full passages (§15). Comparison: Jev's piece question with the production criteria (views/jev_matching), the incoming piece as state, one question\nper candidate. Outcome: any same_statement makes the record a repeat of the candidate with the highest same_statement probability, ties\ngoing to the earliest group (Alex, 2026-11-08: only the group it is most like); a supersedes answer on another candidate becomes a\nrevision link; with no same_statement, supersedes answers make it a revision of those groups; otherwise it is new.\n\n`forward` runs articles in order against the library as it stood after the previous article: records of one article never see each\nother. A failed comparison is refused by the library, reported, and the run continues.\"\"\"\nimport concurrent.futures\nimport math\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]\nsys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT / 'src'))\nfrom hivemind.views import jev_matching  # noqa: E402\n\nTOP = 10\n\n\ndef stated(record):\n    return record['statement_date'] or record.get('statement_date_text') or 'unknown'\n\n\ndef doc_text(record):\n    return '\\n'.join([record['summary'], ' '.join(record['passages']), f\"party: {record['original_party'] or 'unknown'}\",\n                      f\"stated: {stated(record)}\", f\"period: {record['evidence_period'] or 'unknown'}\"])\n\n\ndef query_text(record, thesis_question):\n    return f\"thesis: {thesis_question}\\n{record['summary']}\\n{' '.join(record['passages'])}\"\n\n\ndef piece_text(record, label):\n    return (f\"{label} [{record['tier']}; {record['reporting_speaker']}; original source {record['original_party']}, stated \"\n            f\"{stated(record)}] {record['summary']} | quote: {' '.join(record['passages'])}\")\n\n\ndef query_vectors(vectors, record, thesis_questions):\n    \"\"\"The search queries of one record, in order: one per thesis association, then its own text when it has no association or lists\n    the company's figures.\"\"\"\n    queries = [vectors[query_text(record, thesis_questions[t])] for t in record['thesis_ids']]\n    if not record['thesis_ids'] or record.get('figures'):\n        queries.append(vectors[doc_text(record)])\n    return queries\n\n\n# Jev's piece criteria aligned with the Step 2 merge line's own test (PRD §5 line 2; AGENTS §7.1, one ruler): a repeat is a later report\n# of the stored piece's main statement. Production's wording (views/jev_matching.PIECES) judged \"the same evidence\", so a later report of\n# only a secondary statement inside a stored record (an official's denial carried in the report it denies) was merged as a repeat.\nPIECES = {'same_statement': \"the INCOMING PIECE reports the STORED PIECE's main statement: a retelling, another outlet carrying it, the \"\n                            \"same number in text or a table, or the same speaker repeating it; wording, precision and extra detail may \"\n                            \"differ. If the stored piece carries several statements and the incoming piece reports only a secondary one, \"\n                            \"this is not it\",\n          'supersedes': \"a later version of the stored piece's main statement, changed: the same speaker revises that guidance, \"\n                        \"forecast or event-progress statement, an official confirms or denies that reported claim, or the original \"\n                        \"source revises it; another statement on the same topic is not this\",\n          'different': \"a different fact, figure or matter, a figure for another period or quantity, or only a secondary statement of \"\n                       \"the stored piece, even if on the same topic\"}\n\n\ndef piece_question(text):\n    return {'type': 'choice', 'criteria': PIECES,\n            'instructions': 'How does the INCOMING PIECE relate to the STORED PIECE below? Use only the supplied text. STORED PIECE:\\n' + text}\n\n\nINFORMS = {'true': \"the STORED PIECE bears on the THESIS's own matter (the parties, products, policy or quantity the THESIS names) and \"\n                   \"makes its answer more or less likely, as evidence filed under it would\",\n           'false': \"the STORED PIECE concerns another matter, or is a company-wide result or event that does not speak to the THESIS's \"\n                    \"own matter, or would not change its answer\"}\n\n\ndef informs_question(text):\n    return {'type': 'noul', 'criteria': INFORMS,\n            'instructions': 'Does the STORED PIECE below inform the THESIS stated above? Use only the supplied text; a shared company, '\n                            'product family or word is not enough, and the company\\'s overall results, guidance and estimates inform a '\n                            'thesis about its revenue or earnings path, not one about a competitor, a supplier or a policy unless they '\n                            'speak to it. STORED PIECE:\\n' + text}\n\n\ndef later_link_judge(library, thesis_question, ask):\n    \"\"\"The judge `Library.link_later` calls: one Jev request per batch, the thesis as state, one yes/no question per stored group.\"\"\"\n    def judge(group_ids):\n        questions = {f'q{i}': informs_question(piece_text(library.groups[g]['original'], 'STORED PIECE')) for i, g in enumerate(group_ids)}\n        raw = ask(f'THESIS: {thesis_question}', questions)\n        return {g: raw[f'q{i}']['noul'] >= jev_matching.THRESHOLD for i, g in enumerate(group_ids)}\n    return judge\n\n\ndef cosine(a, b):\n    return sum(x * y for x, y in zip(a, b)) / (math.sqrt(sum(x * x for x in a)) * math.sqrt(sum(y * y for y in b)) or 1)\n\n\ndef search(library, vectors, record, thesis_questions):\n    \"\"\"Candidate group ids: each query's top 10 by cosine against the groups' original records, merged in rank order.\"\"\"\n    candidates = []\n    for query in query_vectors(vectors, record, thesis_questions):\n        ranked = sorted(library.order, key=lambda g: -cosine(query, vectors[doc_text(library.groups[g]['original'])]))[:TOP]\n        candidates += [g for g in ranked if g not in candidates]\n    return candidates\n\n\ndef outcome(answers, order):\n    \"\"\"answers: {group id: {'choice', 'probabilities'}}; order: group ids in creation order.\"\"\"\n    same = [g for g, a in answers.items() if a['choice'] == 'same_statement']\n    later = [g for g, a in answers.items() if a['choice'] == 'supersedes']\n    if same:\n        best = max(same, key=lambda g: (answers[g]['probabilities']['same_statement'], -order.index(g)))\n        return {'kind': 'repeat', 'repeat_of': [best], 'revises': later}\n    if later:\n        return {'kind': 'revision', 'revises': later}\n    return {'kind': 'new'}\n\n\ndef forward(articles, library, vectors, thesis_questions, ask, workers=8, opened=None):\n    \"\"\"articles: lists of Step 3 records in article order. ask(state, questions) returns Jev's answers by question id, or raises.\n    opened: per article, the thesis ids it opened; after the article's records are written, each is linked later to the stored groups\n    that arrived unlinked from earlier articles (`Library.link_later`). A thesis written to with pairs still pending is judged again,\n    against its opening boundary. Returns one decision per record.\"\"\"\n    decisions = []\n    for index, records in enumerate(articles):\n        def decide(record):\n            candidates = search(library, vectors, record, thesis_questions)\n            if not candidates:\n                return record, candidates, {}, {'kind': 'new'}\n            questions = {f'q{i}': piece_question(piece_text(library.groups[g]['original'], 'STORED PIECE'))\n                         for i, g in enumerate(candidates)}\n            incoming = piece_text(record, 'INCOMING PIECE')\n            try:\n                raw = ask(incoming, questions)\n            except Exception as first:   # retried once; a second failure is reported, never silently saved as new\n                try:\n                    raw = ask(incoming, questions)\n                except Exception as error:\n                    return record, candidates, {}, {'kind': 'failed', 'reason': f'{type(first).__name__}: {first}; retry: {type(error).__name__}: {error}'}\n            answers = {g: {'choice': raw[f'q{i}']['choice'], 'probabilities': raw[f'q{i}']['probabilities']} for i, g in enumerate(candidates)}\n            return record, candidates, answers, outcome(answers, library.order)\n        with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=workers) as pool:\n            results = list(pool.map(decide, records))\n        for record, candidates, answers, result in results:\n            entry = {'id': record['id'], 'candidates': candidates, 'answers': answers, 'outcome': result}\n            try:\n                entry['group'] = library.apply(record, result)\n            except Exception as error:\n                entry['refused'] = f'{type(error).__name__}: {error}'\n            decisions.append(entry)\n        article = records[0]['article'] if records else None\n        if article is None:\n            continue\n        written = {t for r in records for t in r['thesis_ids']}\n        retry = [t for t in written if t in library.pending and t not in (opened[index] if opened else [])]\n        for thesis_id in list(opened[index] if opened else []) + retry:   # opened here, or written to with pairs still pending\n            result = library.link_later(thesis_id, later_link_judge(library, thesis_questions[thesis_id], ask), article)\n            library.later_log.append({'thesis': thesis_id, 'article': article, **result})\n    return decisions\n## D168 · 2026-10-25 · Four corrections found by the D167 measurement: one repeat rule, one replace definition, one succession rule, one missing answer\n\nFound while measuring the two D167 arms (log rows 44–48); each is general, follows the PRD, and was measured on stored data or by re-sending stored requests before it entered the code.\n- **A repeat may name the piece shown for the same evidence under another of its theses** (`matching.check`). 06-views step 6: a repeat adds a source and any new thesis link; the writer already resolves a piece to one stored identity for all its theses. The check required the target under the destination thesis and failed an item twice (9 of 17 such refusals over four ledgers were this case). A replacement still stays within its thesis.\n- **The Jev replace criterion carries the PRD's full definition**: a later version of the same statement or number, changed (the same speaker revises it, an official confirms or denies that claim, the original source revises it); another statement on the same topic is not this (06-views lines 191, 197, 306). Probed against a fresh control, three samples: wrong replacements 10 of 10 removed on the seed and week, 6 of 7 out of sample on August; the right ones that stay are the guidance revisions and a source revising its own figure. A second wording ('the same party') was rejected by its pre-registered rule.\n- **The writer's replacement rule, generalised from the PRD**: an incoming third-party view or rumour replaces only the same party's earlier piece (subsumes the view-over-view rule of D167). On every stored ledger it refuses 6 written replacements, all a commentator's view over another party's rumour, none right.\n- **Succession**: the code's rule uses the shared typed-field rule (`identity.conflicts`: 'n/a' matches any basis or unit), so an unstated basis no longer blocks a succession (adds exactly 2 over all ledgers, both right); and the Jev relation question gains the answer 'the same defined quantity or event series for another period', with the direction, the typed fields and the successor check in code, so a succession between differently worded model scopes can be judged (probe: recovers the one such case, changes nothing gold-labelled for the worse).\n\n## D169 · 2026-10-25 · Jev is the default matching engine\n\n## D202 · 2026-10-27 · The writer retires nothing on a matching label: a replacement is recorded as a revision and both pieces stay in force\n\n- Alex, on the arm1 result and the proposal: \"你接着做，需要非常严谨的看结果和测量\". Census first ([labels](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/replacements-47-labels.json), every pair read): of the run's 47 retirements, 22 are the owner's later value or status of the same claim (right under rule E), 20 retired a statement that should have stayed (10 a contradiction or qualification, among them a denial erasing the delay report, \"subject to final agreements\" erasing the $500bn announcement; 10 a different matter), 5 were restatements mislabelled. Jev's `supersedes` label does not separate them: 17 of 22 right and 12 of 20 wrong carry it; with \"confirms or denies\" split out (`jev_contradicts.py`) 4 wrong ones flip to `contradicts` but so do 3 right ones. No label the matcher gives is safe to retire on.\n- Decided: `writing` no longer calls `store.replace`. A `replace` decision stores the incoming piece, links it wherever the target is linked, and records the pair in a new `revisions` table (old, new, said day, seq); both pieces stay in force. `replacement_refused` becomes `revision_refused` with the same conditions (a stated day, a tier at least the old piece's, the same party for views and rumours): refused means stored as new without the relation, as before. The late-older-statement rule is unchanged. `replacements` is history: read by every present reader as before, never written again. `store.evidence_in_force` carries `revises` (the predecessor by revision) and judging prints \"It revises eX (both in force)\" so the judge sees the path; the library UI and service do not show revisions yet (open). The Jev criteria are unchanged: the split was measured and not adopted (3 false `contradicts` on right revisions against 4 catches).\n- The decision word stays `replace` in matching records, schemas and the generative matcher's prompt; its meaning in the writer is \"revises\". Renaming it across prompts, records and scripts is a separate wording slice (D36).\n- Not decided here: the step 4 analysis call in production (D201's reader of both texts), which would decide `same_as` / `revises` / `opposes` itself; until it exists the matcher's label is recorded as an opinion and nothing is lost to it.\n- Regression week ([results](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/results.md), `english-v34b-nvda-week-2026-10-27`, $0.23, 98 items, none failed): the whole path runs with the revision rule; `replacements` gains no row. Counts differ from the v18 week (129 against 176 evidence rows, 12 against 9 theses) because extraction is a sampler, not because of the writer; the writer's effect is proved on the decisions themselves. Reading the week's one `replace` decision found a second silent loss in the same code path: an incoming statement said before its target was logged in `late_statements` and linked nowhere (both weeks dropped SemiAnalysis's Kyber delay report this way; the three-month run dropped the SharonAI backstop figure; 1 of 3,216 decisions, each a material statement). Fixed in the same slice: an older statement is stored and linked as one more piece, no revision recorded, the log row kept. Test red on the old writer, green after; suite 209. Observed, not fixed here: 34 of 1,530 stored pieces carry a non-ISO `original_day` (\"2026-07-05 to 2026-07-06\", \"2026-03\"), which is what made the Kyber report \"older\"; an extraction-contract item for the next pass.\n\n## D203 · 2026-10-28 · A statement day is an ISO day or unknown; the writer never fills it with the publication day\n\n- Found while reading D202's regression week: the extraction answer's `original_day` is a free string (54 of the three-month run's 2,369 raw pieces carry a month, a range or text; 1,445 are empty) and the writer filled every empty day with the item's publication day, against D245's rule that an unknown stays unknown. A range read as \"older than\" the denial it answered dropped SemiAnalysis's Kyber delay report from the week ledger.\n- Decided, at the owners: `extraction.statement_day` keeps an ISO day and blanks anything else when the answer is sifted (the quote and `event_time` keep the period as written); `writing._store_piece` stores the stated day or '' and the revision path stores the stated day too (the ordering day `new_day` orders the revision only). Readers print the blank; matching's `said_day` still falls back to the publication day for ordering, never for storage. Tests: `test_a_statement_day_is_an_iso_day_or_unknown…` (red on the old sift), `test_an_unknown_statement_day_is_stored_unknown…` (red on the old writer); suite 211; preflight B/C/D unchanged.\n- The judge's revision note is labelled as what it is: \"Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of eX; both are in force\" (D202's census: 19 of 37 recorded revisions carry a wrong label).\n- Census of the other silent loss in the same path, read pair by pair ([labels](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/repeats-40-labels.json)): of 40 sampled `repeat` decisions (1,034 in the run), 32 are pickups, 8 merged a different or richer statement into an old piece and lost its content (an estimated 200 of 1,034). The mechanism is D202's: a one-sample label triggers an irreversible write (a repeat adds a source and discards the incoming text). The fix is the argument record's reader side (store every source record, relate `same_as`, collapse same-claim groups for the judge); not built here.\n- D201 measured further ([results](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/results.md), 2026-10-28 00:00–01:20, $0.85): a fresh 16-case gold written from the complete expansions before any send (action 11/12 and 4/4 held out; nothing wrongly retired 16/16; same-claim 8/12 with one class: a second outlet's own reporting treated as independent, which the contract could not express); arm 2 split claim identity from source independence (the class is gone; two new judgement weaknesses appeared); three draws per disagreeing case under both prompts show the arm-to-arm differences are within one draw's noise, while in 144 draws no must-not-touch piece was ever marked same or revised. Decided: the contract keeps arm 2's split; prompt arms stop here; the variable fields are annotations under D202 and the next build is the argument record (steps 5 and 6), where a wrong relation in one draw is corrected by the next instead of being frozen into the ledger.\n\n## D204 · 2026-10-28 · A repeat is stored as its own source record; the merge becomes a labelled relation\n\n- Alex: \"补那个洞，跑一周验收，仔细思考很重要\". The hole (D203's census): on a `repeat` decision the writer adds the incoming item as an outlet of the old piece and discards the incoming text; 8 of 40 sampled repeats (20%, about 200 of 1,034 in the run) had merged a different or richer statement, and its content was gone. The mechanism is D202's: a one-draw label (same_statement ≥ 0.5) triggers an irreversible write.\n- Choice weighed. (A) Store the incoming piece, link it to its own destinations, record the pair in `same_claims`, show both to the judge with the relation labelled as the matcher's opinion. (B) Keep the merge and store the text as a variant of the old piece, hidden from the judge. Under (A) true pickups (about 80%) appear twice in view for their first readings, labelled; under (B) the 20% wrong merges stay hidden from the judge. The standard (rule A: an outlet, not a fact; rule C: support counts sources) is served by the label; losing content is the failure that is not recoverable at read time. Decided: (A).\n- Decided: `writing` stores every incoming piece once with all its destination links; a `repeat` decision records (target, new, seq) in `same_claims`; the old piece gains no source row and no new link (the new piece carries the outlet and the links; a link written on a one-draw label is a write like any other). `store.evidence_in_force` carries `same_as` (the in-force pieces of its group); the judge's request prints \"Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as eX; the argument counts one claim\". Readers of `evidence`, `evidence_links` and `evidence_sources` keep their shapes; the library UI's per-row outlet count now counts one row's sources (the claim's outlets are its `same_claims` group), an open UI item.\n- Acceptance: tests red on the old writer (`test_a_repeated_statement_is_stored_as_its_own_record…`, the two D167 tests whose assertions pinned the merge, changed with this decision), a mutant that merges again fails them; the 40 sampled repeat decisions replayed through the new writer at $0 (40/40 stored, relation recorded, the 8 lost contents present); then one week through the whole path (`english-v34c-nvda-week`), counting `same_claims` rows, pairs both in the judge's view, and judgements citing both members of a pair (the double-count exposure the label must hold).\n- Accepted ([results](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/results.md)): 40 of 40 sampled repeat decisions replayed through the new writer keep the piece, the relation and both pieces in force (the 8 lost contents are present); the week through the whole path ran with none failed at $0.23, wrote 45 same-claim relations and no merge, and the judge's reasons where a pair was in view treat the relays as one source (\"ultimately trace to SemiAnalysis\", \"not independent confirmation\"). The three-month run may resume on `poc-v34` with D202–D204 in the writer; that is Alex's call.\n\n## D205 · 2026-10-28 · The judging call's output budget is 16,000 tokens\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:34:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat result-blind-codex.md result-blind-claude.md r05-adjudication.md results-codex-followup.md'","aggregated_output":"(eval):5: parse error near `end'\n(eval):5: parse error near `end'\nI judged all **six pairs and 54 anonymous answers**: 34 consistent with my reading, 14 inconsistent, and 6 unresolved. These are diagnostic opinions based only on the supplied packet, not frozen gold.\n\nFor example, in T02, Nvidia’s reported **70% revenue forecast** and its customers’ reported **potential for revenue to double** concern the same period, but describe different things. One does not automatically replace the other.\n\n## Independent readings\n\n**T01 — `same_statement`.** Both articles attribute the same fiscal 2027 and 2028 AI chip revenue outlook to Broadcom. The incoming source says “about $115 billion” for fiscal 2027 and “roughly $230 billion” for fiscal 2028; the stored source says “$115 billion” and “$230 billion” for those years. Both articles are dated September 6. The incoming record adds customers and says the 2027 forecast was raised from over $100 billion; those details do not establish a different main forecast. Neither article supplies the exact day of the underlying statement.\n\n**T02 — `different`.** The stored record’s main statement is that Kress said **customer forecasts** indicated revenue “may double next year.” The incoming record’s main statement is the company’s **70% fiscal 2028 revenue growth forecast**. The stored article itself separately reports both: the 70% guidance and the customer-demand indication. The incoming article also says unconstrained growth could be “a lot higher” than 70%, but that overlap with the stored record’s secondary Huang statement does not make the main statements identical. The incoming $673 billion–$700 billion figure is the article’s calculation from consensus, not a separately attributed Nvidia forecast. The articles were published September 5 and 6; that order does not establish a revision of customer demand.\n\n**T03 — `supersedes`.** The stored main claim is SemiAnalysis’s report that “Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028.” The incoming article reports that Nvidia “rejected the claims” and said its roadmap remains intact. That is a direct official denial of the main delay claim under the requested definition. It does not prove which party is right or specifically settle the stored record’s secondary NVL576 claim. The stored article already contains Nvidia’s denial, so this is not necessarily a newly issued response. Its publication metadata says July 5 while its text and URL refer to Monday, July 6; the precise sequence is therefore uncertain.\n\n**T04 — `same_statement`.** Both articles report Nvidia’s Thursday announcement of a Hugging Face acquisition: the stored article calls it a “$12.9 billion deal to purchase,” while the incoming article says “$13 billion.” The amounts are compatible with rounding, and the parties and announced transaction match. The incoming September 6 article refers to reporting by EU Tech Loop and Euronews; the stored article is dated September 5. “Acquisition” in the incoming wording does not establish that the deal closed, and neither record establishes regulatory approval.\n\n**T05 — `different`.** The stored main statement says Vera Rubin is “already in production” and forecasts about **20% of data-center revenue** in fiscal Q3. The incoming statement says systems based on Vera Rubin “had already been delivered commercially to customers during August 2026.” Production and commercial delivery are related stages, but they are distinct claims; delivery does not confirm or replace the 20% forecast. Both reports attribute their information to Nvidia around the earnings call, but the article dates alone do not prove that the underlying statements were made on different days.\n\n**T06 — `different`, with a boundary ambiguity.** The stored article’s main conclusion is that the base Shield TV **appears** discontinued, based on retail availability; it explicitly says it could “simply be an out-of-stock situation.” The later article quotes Nvidia: the base model is “largely out of stock due to demand” and it has “no updates” on future availability. Nvidia neither confirms nor denies discontinuation. The July 13 article also reports that B&H changed its status from “discontinued” to “No Longer Available.” It describes the future as “in limbo,” but does not explicitly retract the July 8 article’s qualified conclusion. I therefore read the official response as a distinct statement. There is a reasonable unresolved boundary over whether the same publisher’s later, more cautious framing should count as changing its earlier main statement.\n\n## Anonymous answer assessments\n\nEach answer below is judged against the reading above. The answer’s choice is shown, followed by my assessment.\n\n**T01**\n\n| Answer | Choice | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A02 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A03 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A04 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A05 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A06 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A07 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A08 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A09 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n\nAll nine match the same attributed 2027 and 2028 forecast; the added prior forecast and customer context do not change that main statement.\n\n**T02**\n\n| Answer | Choice | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A02 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A03 | `supersedes` | Inconsistent |\n| A04 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A05 | `supersedes` | Inconsistent |\n| A06 | `supersedes` | Inconsistent |\n| A07 | `supersedes` | Inconsistent |\n| A08 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A09 | `supersedes` | Inconsistent |\n\nThe four `different` answers preserve the distinction between customer-indicated demand and supply-constrained company guidance. The five `supersedes` answers treat the 70% guidance as replacing or officially confirming or denying the customer-demand statement; the sources instead present both together.\n\n**T03**\n\n| Answer | Choice | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A02 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A03 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A04 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A05 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A06 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A07 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A08 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n| A09 | `supersedes` | Consistent |\n\nAll nine identify Nvidia’s reported direct rejection of the main Kyber delay claim. The label does not decide the truth of the dispute.\n\n**T04**\n\n| Answer | Choice | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | `different` | Inconsistent |\n| A02 | `different` | Inconsistent |\n| A03 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A04 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A05 | `different` | Inconsistent |\n| A06 | `different` | Inconsistent |\n| A07 | `different` | Inconsistent |\n| A08 | `same_statement` | Consistent |\n| A09 | `different` | Inconsistent |\n\nThe three `same_statement` answers fit the same announced deal. The six `different` answers are not supported by a distinct transaction or established closing event; $13 billion is compatible with a rounded $12.9 billion.\n\n**T05**\n\n| Answer | Choice | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A02 | `same_statement` | Inconsistent |\n| A03 | `same_statement` | Inconsistent |\n| A04 | `same_statement` | Inconsistent |\n| A05 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A06 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A07 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A08 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A09 | `different` | Consistent |\n\nThe six `different` answers distinguish actual commercial deliveries from production status and a revenue-share forecast. The three `same_statement` answers conflate those related but distinct claims.\n\n**T06**\n\n| Answer | Choice | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| A01 | `supersedes` | Unresolved |\n| A02 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A03 | `supersedes` | Unresolved |\n| A04 | `supersedes` | Unresolved |\n| A05 | `supersedes` | Unresolved |\n| A06 | `supersedes` | Unresolved |\n| A07 | `different` | Consistent |\n| A08 | `supersedes` | Unresolved |\n| A09 | `different` | Consistent |\n\nThe three `different` answers match my reading: Nvidia directly addresses stock but leaves discontinuation unanswered. I cannot conclusively reject the six `supersedes` answers because the same publisher’s later framing is more cautious and reports a changed retailer listing. The later article does not explicitly retract its earlier inference, however, and Nvidia does not deny it.**结论：6 组、54 个答案全部判完，37 个与我的读法一致，14 个不一致，3 个未决。**\n\n我的六个独立读法是：T01 `same_statement`，T02 `different`，T03 `supersedes`，T04 `same_statement`，T05 `different`，T06 `supersedes`（六组里最没把握的一组）。\n\n先说三点：\n\n- 我只读了这一个文件，没有写入或修改任何东西。\n- 文件里每组的九个选择紧跟在原文后面，我读的时候不可能看不到。下面每个判断只用原文和记录来论证，没有用票数；T02 和 T04 里我的读法与九个里的多数相反。\n- 这是一个 AI 读者的诊断意见，不是金标。\n\n## T01：`same_statement`（把握高）\n\n两条记录说的是同一个预测：Broadcom 预计 AI 芯片收入 2027 财年约 1150 亿美元，2028 财年约 2300 亿美元。\n\n- **存量原文：** \"Broadcom told investors that it expects its AI chip revenue to double to $115 billion in its fiscal 2027, and then to double yet again to $230 billion in its fiscal 2028.\"\n- **新进原文：** \"The company recently announced raising the forecast for its AI chip revenue to about $115 billion in the fiscal year ending October 2027, up from a prior forecast of over $100 billion. It also expects this to double to roughly $230 billion in fiscal 2028.\"\n\n限定：\n\n- **说话人：** 两边都是 Broadcom 自己。\n- **时间：** 存量说是财报电话会（\"in the chipmaker's earnings call\"），新进只说 \"recently\"。两篇原文都没给电话会的日期，所以\"同一场合\"是从数字和财年完全相同推出来的，不是原文明说的。\n- **主次：** 新进第一句 \"custom chips are increasingly being used by companies such as Meta, Google, and OpenAI\" 是文章作者的描述，不是 Broadcom 的预测。\"up from a prior forecast of over $100 billion\" 说的是更早的旧预测；存量里已经是 1150 亿这个新数，所以新进没有改动存量。\n- **歧义：** 没有实质歧义。\n\n| 答案 | 选择 | 判定 | 原因 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | same_statement | 一致 | 同一方、同一组数字和财年 |\n| A02 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A03 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A04 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A05 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A06 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A07 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A08 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A09 | same_statement | 一致 | 同上 |\n\n## T02：`different`（把握较高）\n\n同一个场合说了两件事：公司给的指引是增长 70%（受供给限制），客户预测显示的需求是可能翻倍。存量记的是后者，新进记的是前者。\n\n- **存量原文：** \"CFO Colette Kress highlighted that customer forecasts currently indicate Nvidia's revenue may double next year. Huang also said Nvidia's revenue growth could be \"a lot higher\" if the company were not supply-constrained.\"\n- **新进原文：** \"CFO Colette Kress told investors on August 26 that fiscal 2028 revenue growth will reach 70% … marking the company's first-ever year-ahead revenue forecast.\"\n- **两者并存的证据（存量原文）：** \"He said Nvidia expects revenue to grow about 70% in its fiscal 2028 … But management says even that forecast doesn't reflect the full scope of demand\"。\n- **两者并存的证据（新进原文）：** \"Huang told analysts the unconstrained growth rate would be \"a lot higher\" than 70%\"，以及 \"Management said the 70% figure shows supply constraints … rather than a ceiling on actual demand\"。\n\n不是 `supersedes` 的原因：70% 不是后来才说、用来改掉\"翻倍\"的。两句话互不否定，官方也没有确认或否认\"客户预测显示翻倍\"。\n\n限定：\n\n- **说话人：** 新进把 70% 归给 Kress，而且是转述（\"CNBC reported that\"）。存量原文把 70% 归给黄仁勋（\"CEO Jensen Huang … said Nvidia expects revenue to grow about 70%\"）。两篇文章对\"谁说的 70%\"说法不一。\n- **时间：** 新进给了 8 月 26 日；存量原文没有日期。\"同一场合\"是我从两篇都引了 \"a lot higher\" 这句话推出来的。存量的 \"next year\" 按所在小节标题（\"Supply constraints could limit Nvidia's fiscal 2028 growth\"）读作 2028 财年，字面上有歧义。\n- **主次：** 新进第二段 \"$673 billion to $700 billion\" 是文章自己的算术，不是 Nvidia 的话。存量第二句（\"a lot higher\"）在新进原文里有，但新进记录没有收。\n- **歧义：** 两条说的是同一件大事（2028 财年收入增长），但命题不同。如果规则把\"同一件事\"算作同一条，结论会变。\n\n| 答案 | 选择 | 判定 | 原因 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | different | 一致 | 指引 70% 与\"客户预测显示翻倍\"是两个命题 |\n| A02 | different | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A03 | supersedes | 不一致 | 两句同场并存，70% 没有改掉或否定\"翻倍\" |\n| A04 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A05 | supersedes | 不一致 | 同 A03 |\n| A06 | supersedes | 不一致 | 同 A03 |\n| A07 | supersedes | 不一致 | 同 A03 |\n| A08 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A09 | supersedes | 不一致 | 同 A03 |\n\n## T03：`supersedes`（把握较高，靠的是\"官方直接否认\"这一条）\n\n- **存量原文（SemiAnalysis 的说法）：** \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\"。\n- **新进原文：** \"Nvidia rejected the claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\"\n\n限定：\n\n- **说话人：** 存量的原始说话方是 SemiAnalysis，新进是 Nvidia。SemiAnalysis 没有改口；这是当事公司否认第三方的说法，是争议，不是撤回。\n- **时间：** 否认并不比存量文章新。存量的 CNBC 原文里就有 \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\"，只是存量记录没收这一句。\n- **日期字段：** 存量来源的 `publication_date` 写 2026-07-05，但网址是 `/2026/07/06/`，正文说 \"in a post on Monday\"，而 2026-07-06 才是星期一。字段和原文不一致。\n- **具体度：** 新进是转述（\"telling media outlets\"），没有直接引语。否认是笼统的\"路线图不变\"，没有逐条回应 Kyber、2028、PCB 中板或 NVL576。\n- **歧义：** \"官方否认\"不等于\"说法不成立\"。标签成立，但存量那条仍是有效的一方观点。\n\n| 答案 | 选择 | 判定 | 原因 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | supersedes | 一致 | 当事公司直接否认该报告 |\n| A02 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A03 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A04 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A05 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A06 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A07 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A08 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n| A09 | supersedes | 一致 | 同上 |\n\n## T04：`same_statement`（把握高）\n\n同一方、同一个星期四、同一笔交易；129 亿和 130 亿只是四舍五入的差别。\n\n- **存量原文：** \"On Thursday, NVIDIA – market cap: $5.55 trillion – announced a $12.9 billion deal to purchase Hugging Face\"。\n- **新进原文：** \"American chipmaker NVIDIA announced on Thursday its acquisition of the artificial intelligence library Hugging Face for $13 billion (EUR11.2bn).\"\n\n限定：\n\n- **时间：** 存量文章（9 月 5 日，周六）说 \"announcement this week … On Thursday\"，新进文章（9 月 6 日，周日）说 \"on Thursday\"。两者都指 2026-09-03。\n- **转述链条：** 新进是三手转述（\"reported by EU Tech Loop and shared on Euronews\"），数字更粗。\n- **主次：** 存量第二段（竞购激烈、1800 万开发者、20 万企业客户）是文章的描述，不是 Nvidia 公告的内容。新进没有这些，不影响主陈述相同。\n- **歧义：** 新进标题 \"NVIDIA Acquires\" 像是已完成，但正文是 \"announced\"，并提到可能有 \"a regulatory review of the deal's terms\"。存量也是\"已宣布、未完成\"，状态一致。\n\n| 答案 | 选择 | 判定 | 原因 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | different | 不一致 | 同一方、同一天、同一笔交易，金额只差四舍五入 |\n| A02 | different | 不一致 | 同上 |\n| A03 | same_statement | 一致 | 同一公告 |\n| A04 | same_statement | 一致 | 同一公告 |\n| A05 | different | 不一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A06 | different | 不一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A07 | different | 不一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A08 | same_statement | 一致 | 同一公告 |\n| A09 | different | 不一致 | 同 A01 |\n\n## T05：`different`（把握中到较高）\n\n存量说的是\"已在生产，第三财季约占数据中心收入 20%\"；新进说的是\"8 月已向客户商业交付\"。投产和交付是两个节点，20% 这个数新进没有，8 月交付存量没有。\n\n- **存量原文：** \"Nvidia's big upcoming growth driver is the Vera Rubin system, which is already in production. Management expects the new platform to account for about 20% of data center segment revenue in the third quarter.\"\n- **新进原文：** \"During the analyst call following the earnings release, Nvidia said that systems based on Vera Rubin had already been delivered commercially to customers during August 2026.\"\n\n不是 `supersedes` 的原因：交付没有改掉或否定\"在生产\"和\"20%\"，三者同时成立。\n\n限定：\n\n- **说话人：** 存量原文里 \"already in production\" 是作者的叙述，没有写是 Nvidia 说的；只有 \"Management expects … 20%\" 明确归给管理层。存量摘要写成 \"Nvidia said … already in production\"，归属比原文强。\n- **时间：** 新进记录的 `statement_date` \"August 2026\" 是交付发生的月份，不是说话的日期。说话是在财报后的分析师电话会，原文说财报是 \"A week and a half ago\"（文章日期 9 月 6 日）。存量没有日期。\n- **关联：** 第二财季截至 7 月 26 日，所以 8 月落在第三财季内。8 月交付是\"第三财季占 20%\"的佐证，属于同一件事的不同陈述。\n- **歧义：** 如果规则把\"同一产品的爬坡\"算同一条，会读成 `same_statement`；按命题是否相同来读，是 `different`。\n\n| 答案 | 选择 | 判定 | 原因 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | different | 一致 | 投产加 20% 预期，与 8 月交付是不同命题 |\n| A02 | same_statement | 不一致 | 两条记录没有一个共同的事实点（数字、时间、节点都不同） |\n| A03 | same_statement | 不一致 | 同 A02 |\n| A04 | same_statement | 不一致 | 同 A02 |\n| A05 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A06 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A07 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A08 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A09 | different | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n\n## T06：`supersedes`（把握中等，六组里最不干净）\n\n新进就是存量里那封\"已去信询问\"的回信：同一家媒体、同一位作者（Craig Donaldson），五天后。它把存量的主陈述从\"看来已悄悄停产\"改成了\"Nvidia 说是因需求缺货，未来不明\"。\n\n- **存量原文：** \"it now appears that the cheaper Shield TV's time on the market has come to an end, as Nvidia seems to have quietly discontinued it (via AFTV News)\"，以及 \"Pocket-lint has reached out to Nvidia for official confirmation … we'll update this story if we hear back.\"\n- **新进原文：** \"Pocket-lint reached out to Nvidia for clarification on the Shield TV's future and received an email response\"，以及 \"\"The SHIELD TV base model is largely out of stock due to demand. We don't have any updates to share regarding future availability at this time.\"\"\n- **存量的关键证据也变了：** \"B&H Photo Video even labeled the Shield TV as \"discontinued\" before recently changing the product status to \"No Longer Available\"\"。\n\n限定：\n\n- **官方没有确认或否认停产。** 新进原文明说 \"the company sidestepped whether the device has been discontinued\"，小标题是 \"Nvidia won't say the Shield TV is discontinued / But it's not confirming that it's coming back either\"。所以\"官方直接确认或否认\"这一条字面上不满足；我的 `supersedes` 靠的是\"原来的出处改了主陈述\"这一条。\n- **说话人：** 存量记录的 `original_party` 写的是 Nvidia，但原文里\"停产\"是 Pocket-lint（引 AFTV News）根据零售商页面的推断。原文还说 \"Nvidia doesn't explicitly say the Shield TV has been discontinued on its website\"。这个字段和原文不符。\n- **主次：** 新进里 \"SHIELD TV Pro remains available\" 和持续更新是次要内容，存量里也有对应。\n- **歧义：** 按字面严格读，官方既没确认也没否认，新进就是另一个说话人的另一条陈述，`different` 说得通。所以选 `different` 的答案我记为未决，不记为不一致。\n\n| 答案 | 选择 | 判定 | 原因 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | supersedes | 一致 | 同一出处的后续，改了主陈述并带来官方回应 |\n| A02 | different | 未决 | 原文明说官方回避了停产问题，按字面严格读法站得住 |\n| A03 | supersedes | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A04 | supersedes | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A05 | supersedes | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A06 | supersedes | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A07 | different | 未决 | 同 A02 |\n| A08 | supersedes | 一致 | 同 A01 |\n| A09 | different | 未决 | 同 A02 |\n\n## 计数\n\n按组：\n\n| 组 | 我的读法 | 一致 | 不一致 | 未决 | 合计 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| T01 | same_statement | 9 | 0 | 0 | 9 |\n| T02 | different | 4 | 5 | 0 | 9 |\n| T03 | supersedes | 9 | 0 | 0 | 9 |\n| T04 | same_statement | 3 | 6 | 0 | 9 |\n| T05 | different | 6 | 3 | 0 | 9 |\n| T06 | supersedes | 6 | 0 | 3 | 9 |\n| 合计 | | 37 | 14 | 3 | 54 |\n\n按答案编号（文件没有说明同一编号在六组里是不是同一个作答者，这张表只在是的前提下才有意义）：\n\n| 答案 | 一致 | 不一致 | 未决 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A01 | 5 | 1 | 0 |\n| A02 | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n| A03 | 4 | 2 | 0 |\n| A04 | 5 | 1 | 0 |\n| A05 | 4 | 2 | 0 |\n| A06 | 4 | 2 | 0 |\n| A07 | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n| A08 | 6 | 0 | 0 |\n| A09 | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n**判断：不同。** 新记录是 Nvidia 对缺货情况的新回应，涉及同一台设备，但没有重复、正式确认或否认旧记录的主要判断：标准版 Shield TV 似乎已经停产。这是基于所给材料的独立意见，不是金标。\n\n旧文章于 **2026 年 7 月 8 日**由 Pocket-lint 的 Craig Donaldson 发表。它的主要判断是：标准版 Shield TV 的销售寿命似乎已经结束，Nvidia “**seems to have quietly discontinued it**”，并注明这一线索来自 **AFTV News**。但文章同时明确留有余地：“**it’s possible this is simply an out-of-stock situation**”。当时 Pocket-lint 尚未得到 Nvidia 的正式确认，并表示已向公司询问。\n\n新文章于 **2026 年 7 月 13 日**由同一出版方、同一作者发表，报道了 Nvidia 的邮件回复。Nvidia 说：\n\n> “The SHIELD TV base model is largely out of stock due to demand. We don't have any updates to share regarding future availability at this time.”\n\nNvidia 还说 Pro 版仍有售，Shield 平台继续获得更新。**这些是 Nvidia 的正式回应，但不是对停产问题的正式回答**：缺货原因被归为需求旺盛，未来是否补货以及设备是否停产，都没有得到明确答复。文章也写道，公司 “**sidestepped whether the device has been discontinued**”。\n\n这两篇文章之间确实有变化：7 月 8 日，作者认为停产迹象很强；7 月 13 日，作者说设备的未来 “**remains in limbo**”。但后文没有说 Pocket-lint 或 AFTV News 撤回或更正了早先的判断。后文提到的是零售商 **B&H** 把商品状态从 “**discontinued**” 改为 “**No Longer Available**”；这是零售商标签的变化，不能当作出版方撤稿，也不能当作 Nvidia 否认停产。\n\n另一种合理解释是把新文章看成对旧报道的**后续更新**：同一作者拿到了先前等待的公司回应，也用了更谨慎的措辞。但它没有明确修正旧判断，因此如果要判断两条记录所表达的主要声明，较合适的是“不同”；真正的停产状态仍然**不确定**。\n\n两条记录都把 `original_party` 写成 Nvidia，但旧文章的停产判断是 **Pocket-lint 引述 AFTV News 并根据零售商情况作出的推断**，不是 Nvidia 的声明。新记录才有直接归属于 Nvidia 的邮件内容。两条记录都没有给出 Nvidia 具体发言日期；**7 月 8 日和 7 月 13 日是文章发表日期**，不能据此断定邮件是在 7 月 13 日发出的。**P01 对 `45:e3` 的修订链接应标为“有争议”，不能算已验证正确。** 因此，P01 的目标关系答对了，不能说明这条记录写出的所有关系都正确。\n\n`45:e3` 的主陈述是：SemiAnalysis 报道，两机柜拼接的备用方案被取消，并认为 Nvidia 缺少经过验证的扩展方案。新文章先报道 Kyber 延期，**也提到了同一备用方案被取消**，然后说 Nvidia 拒绝了“这些说法”。这使较宽的否认解读有一定依据；但 Nvidia 的实际表述只是“路线图完好”，没有具体说明是否否认备用方案取消，也没有逐项回应扩展方案问题。两篇都属转述，原始回应时间不清楚。\n\n实际 Jev 请求中的新记录只概括为 Nvidia 拒绝“延期说法”，没有带上新文章提到备用方案的上下文。按现行规则，只有回应了旧记录的**主陈述**才算修订；是否可以把这句宽泛回应扩展到 `45:e3`，现有材料不足以确定。见 [完整来源 P01](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/blind-positive-revisions.json)、[完整 Jev 请求，P01 q2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/probe-2026-11-09/bodies.json) 和 [比较规则](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2/compare.py:57)。\n\n三次完整 Jev 回答都把新记录放进 `45:e2`，同时连向 `45:e1` 和 `45:e3`。盲读对照只审了 `45:e1`，所以它支持的是 **`45:e1` 这条关系的判断**，不是整个写入结果的正确性。独立比较器也只回答了 `45:e1` 这一对。见 [结果重放](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/RESULTS-DRAFT.md)。\n\n**R05 我也要收窄之前的结论。** 我仍倾向于“不属于修订”：Nvidia 解释了缺货，但没有确认或否认停产。新的盲读指出，同一家媒体后来采用了更谨慎的表述，且零售商更改了商品状态；这使“后续报道是否改变了原推测”存在合理边界争议。因此，独立比较器的 `different` 是**有来源支持的改善线索**，不应再称为无争议地纠正了错误链接。见 [结果盲读 T06](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/result-blind-codex.md)。\n\n没有修改文件、调用模型或打开运行中的数据库。\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
