"remains available,\\\" and if it still has ample inventory, it would make sense for Nvidia to shift its focus to that device instead. After all, the Shield TV Pro is the better option and more popular anyway, thanks to its extra RAM and storage, USB ports, Gigabit Ethernet, and support for 64-bit apps.\\nNvidia hasn't ruled out a new Shield TV either\\nThis whole situation surrounding the base Shield TV's availability, and whether it'll ever come back, has also gotten me thinking about whether Nvidia will ever release new Shield TV models. Both the Shield TV and Shield TV Pro are now more than six years old, and they're long overdue for a refresh.\\nNvidia hasn't officially confirmed any plans for new Shield TV hardware, but it also hasn't ruled out the possibility.\\nIn an interview with Ars Technica back in January, Nvidia's Senior VP of Hardware Engineering, Andrew Bell, was asked about the possibility of future Shield TV models. His response was encouraging:\\n\\\"We talk about it all the time -- I'd love to.\\\"\\nBell went on to say that if Nvidia comes up with a new Shield TV concept the company is passionate about, it could happen.\\nOverall, the future of the base Shield TV remains uncertain, as does whether Nvidia will ever release new Shield TV models. What is clear, though, is that if you're looking to buy a Shield TV today, the Shield TV Pro is your best bet. Nvidia also has no plans to stop supporting its existing Shield TV lineup, with software updates continuing to roll out occasionally.\\nAndroid 11.1 powered by Android TV\\n4K\\nGigabit ethernet, HDMI 2.0b, two USB 3.0 Type A\\n4K streaming media player with Dolby Vision, Dolby Atmos, GeForce NOW cloud gaming, Google Assistant built-in, and more.\\nThe conversation buzzed with nostalgia and frustration, as @Scott praised the durability but criticized stagnant innovation, while @Samantha spotlighted Nvidia's unclear plans for the Shield's future. Voices like @Brer showed eagerness for a fresh model to replace aging units, highlighting the market demand. Some chimed in with hardware gripes, citing outdated tech like no HDMI 2.1 and overheating issues. As @Scott summed it up, the platform should be able to do far more after all these years, capturing the thread’s hunger for a true upgrade.\\ndetailed a long-term positive experience with Nvidia Shield Pro devices but lamented the frustrating remote design, especially the misplaced Netflix button. They expressed impatience for a major platform update, pointing out that six years without substantial changes feels like an eternity in tech.\\nI’ve been using the Nvidia Shield for more than ten years. I have two systems, both being the Pro versions. I’ve had extremely good luck with these systems. They’ve been operating flawlessly for years. However, the remote control could have been better designed. I find myself accidentally hitting the large Netflix button that’s towards the bottom of the remote, which can be highly annoying at times. That button is located at a very poor position on the remote. I’ve been waiting for Nvidia to do a major update to this platform, as six years is an extremely LONG period of time for any platform, even though it’s being routinely updated. When it comes to electronic platforms, that’s the equivalent of a lifetime. While the platform still operates fairly well, there’s far more that it should be able to do.\\nNvidia's stance on Shield TV's future feels evasive. Is it retired, or just rebooting in silence? What would you want from a successor?\\nNo giant Netflix button, an ergonomic remote that isn't shaped like an Toblerone, higher audio output level. That’s about it.\\nNo hdmi 2.1 or 2.2 . Overheating old junk , degrades picture due to lack of HDMI 2.1. Software updates cannot make up for old hardware.\\nI am so in for a new Shield. I want to get my Dad off of Amazon Fire with their OS changes, but I don't really want to buy a Shield Pro knowing its so old. (even though it is still the best)\\nThe 2019 Shield Models had the exact same hardware as the 2017 models. The only difference besides the rebranded name was that they shipped with a newer build of ATV--which the 2017 models had already received via OTS anyway. So those 2019 models have even older hardware than a lot of folks (including this writer, apparently) realize.\"\n  },\n  \"stored_record\": {\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia appears to have quietly discontinued its standard (tube-shaped) $150 Shield TV streaming device, according to Pocket-lint (citing AFTV News): the unit is no longer purchasable directly from Nvidia or at most major retailers, with authorized dealer B&H Photo Video listing it as 'Discontinued', Best Buy saying it is 'no longer available in new condition' and Newegg saying it is out of stock and may or may not be restocked. Pocket-lint had no official confirmation from Nvidia, said it had asked the company, and noted the $200 Shield TV Pro remains on sale. The item leaves open that this could simply be an out-of-stock situation.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n      \"Unfortunately, it now appears that the cheaper Shield TV's time on the market has come to an end, as Nvidia seems to have quietly discontinued it (via AFTV News ). The Shield TV is no longer available to purchase directly from Nvidia, and the same is true across most major retailers.\",\n      \"In particular, B&H Photo Video , an Nvidia-authorized dealer, lists the Shield TV as \\\"Discontinued\\\" on its product page (as you can see in the image above). Best Buy doesn't go quite that far, but if you try to buy the streamer new, it says, \\\"This item is no longer available in new condition.\\\" Newegg has a similarly discouraging message, stating, \\\"This item is currently out of stock and it may or may not be restocked.\\\"\",\n      \"Pocket-lint has reached out to Nvidia for official confirmation on whether the Shield TV has been discontinued, and we'll update this story if we hear back.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"statement_date_text\": null\n  },\n  \"stored_source\": {\n    \"id\": \"gnews:abe8c5a94dc8d1530e9cd9a7\",\n    \"publisher\": \"pocket-lint.com\",\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-08\",\n    \"published_at\": \"Wed, 08 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT\",\n    \"publisher_type\": \"media\",\n    \"authors\": null,\n    \"category\": \"T7\",\n    \"source_uri\": \"https://www.pocket-lint.com/nvidia-might-have-quietly-discontinued-the-shield-tv/\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia might have quietly discontinued the Shield TV - Pocket-lint\",\n    \"text\": \"By Craig Donaldson\\nAlthough it's been more than six years since they launched in 2019, Nvidia's Shield TV and Shield TV Pro have remained two of the most popular streaming devices on the market, especially among power users, and for good reason.\\nFor starters, despite their age, they still pack impressive hardware, largely thanks to Nvidia's Tegra X1+ processor. They also run Android TV, giving users the freedom to customize their device and sideload apps. That's increasingly valuable, especially now that Amazon has started shifting its Fire TV Sticks to Vega OS, which doesn't support app sideloading at all.\\nSince launch, Nvidia has offered two versions of the Shield TV: the standard tube-shaped Shield TV and the Shield TV Pro . The Pro costs $200 and comes with 3GB of RAM and 16GB of storage, compared to the standard Shield TV's 2GB of RAM and 8GB of storage.\\nThat said, the regular Shield TV was still a compelling option. At $150, it costs $50 less than the Shield TV Pro while still delivering the same speedy Nvidia Tegra X1+ processor. If you didn't need the extra RAM or storage, it was an easy way to get most of the Shield experience for less.\\nUnfortunately, it now appears that the cheaper Shield TV's time on the market has come to an end, as Nvidia seems to have quietly discontinued it (via AFTV News ). The Shield TV is no longer available to purchase directly from Nvidia, and the same is true across most major retailers.\\nWhile it's possible this is simply an out-of-stock situation, there are several signs suggesting this is the end of the road for Nvidia's tube-shaped Shield TV.\\nIt doesn't look like the Shield TV is coming back\\nDon't worry, the Shield TV Pro is still available\\nNvidia doesn't explicitly say the Shield TV has been discontinued on its website in the US, but several of its major retail partners strongly suggest that's the case.\\nIn particular, B&H Photo Video , an Nvidia-authorized dealer, lists the Shield TV as \\\"Discontinued\\\" on its product page (as you can see in the image above).\\nBest Buy doesn't go quite that far, but if you try to buy the streamer new, it says, \\\"This item is no longer available in new condition.\\\" Newegg has a similarly discouraging message, stating, \\\"This item is currently out of stock and it may or may not be restocked.\\\"\\nConsidering B&H Photo Video explicitly labels the Shield TV as discontinued, and it's sold out virtually everywhere else, it's not looking good for Nvidia's tube-shaped streamer's future.\\n...I want to be clear that this only appears to affect the regular $150 Shield TV, not the $200 Shield TV Pro, which is still available on Nvidia's website and at various retailers.\\nPocket-lint has reached out to Nvidia for official confirmation on whether the Shield TV has been discontinued, and we'll update this story if we hear back.\\nGiven the ongoing global memory shortage, I wouldn't be surprised if Nvidia decided to discontinue its cheaper Shield TV to keep the more popular Shield TV Pro in stock. I want to be clear that this only appears to affect the regular $150 Shield TV, not the $200 Shield TV Pro, which is still available on Nvidia's website and at various retailers, such as B&H, Amazon, Best Buy, and more. But there is always a possibility that once the Shield TV Pro's stock dries up, it could meet the same fate as the Shield TV.\\nNvidia said it plans to continue updating Shield TV\\nOne big question that many Shield TV fans have had for years now is whether Nvidia will ever release new Shield TV models . 2019 is getting increasingly far in the rear-view mirror, and while the Shield TV and Shield TV Pro still hold up decently in 2026, they aren't exactly perfect, especially since they lack support for modern AV1 video codecs, with a major limitation being the absence of HDR10+ support.\\nIn an interview earlier this year with Ars Technica , when asked about future Shield TV models coming, Nvidia's senior VP of hardware engineering, Andrew Bell, said, \\\"We talk about it all the time -- I'd love to,\\\" adding that if Nvidia lands on a new Shield TV concept it's passionate about, it could happen.\\nSo while Bell didn't specifically state that a new Shield TV is on the way, it doesn't seem like the idea is entirely off the table either. And Bell also said the company has no plans to stop updating its Shield TVs, with its last update arriving back in February.\\n​​​​​​​Hopefully, Nvidia surprises all of us and announces a new Shield TV in the not-so-distant future.\\nI, for one, would love to see Nvidia release a brand-new Shield TV with upgraded hardware and support for more modern AV1 video codecs. But I also acknowledge that, given the ongoing memory shortage and Nvidia's massive focus on AI right now, a new Shield TV might not be at the top of Nvidia's priority list, and this may be wishful thinking, unfortunately.\\nAll in all, while it sucks that the regular Shield TV might be discontinued now, the Shield TV Pro is still available, so if you've always wanted to try out the Shield TV experience, that's still an option at least. Hopefully, Nvidia surprises all of us and announces a new Shield TV in the not-so-distant future.\\n4K HDR, Dolby Vision, HDR10\\n16GB of storage, 3GB of RAM\\nThe Shield TV Pro is Nvidia's higher-end Android TV-based streaming player that features a set-top box design, the Tegra X1+ processor, advanced Dolby audio-visual technologies, and more.\\nI have a Shield TV Pro in my bedroom, and a Roku Ultra in my living room. I don't care if it is from 2019, when my Ultra goes, it will be replaced by a Shield TV Pro. Best streaming device I have ever owned.\\nThey have to focus on making things nobody wants, no naturally that requires them to stop making things people actually want.\\nYou completely dismissed the original 2017 variants. The tube shaped device was the second generation. The first generation devices both looked like the most recent pro but the non-pro did not include a hard drive. Still using a first generation non-pro in my home theater setup and it works like a charm for everything from network cast HDHomerun streams to Plex (although I dont recommend running Plex Server on theses devices).\\nNvidia quietly shelving Shield TV feels like a cliffhanger with no sequel. If you're done, say so - don't leave fans in limbo.\"\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"P01\",\n  \"incoming_record\": {\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's delay claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia rejected the claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"statement_date_text\": null\n  },\n  \"incoming_source\": {\n    \"id\": \"gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e\",\n    \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"published_at\": \"Mon, 06 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT\",\n    \"publisher_type\": \"media\",\n    \"authors\": null,\n    \"category\": \"T7\",\n    \"source_uri\": \"https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/jim-cramer-says-buy-nvidia-223000100.html\",\n    \"title\": \"Jim Cramer Says Buy Nvidia as Chipmaker Rejects 2028 AI Delay Claims\",\n    \"text\": \"Jim Cramer Says Buy Nvidia as Chipmaker Rejects 2028 AI Delay Claims\\nJim Cramer doubled down on Nvidia on Monday, urging investors to buy the stock as the chipmaker rejected claims that its next-generation AI rack systems face delays until 2028.\\nThe clash pits Nvidia against research firm SemiAnalysis, which alleges manufacturing setbacks have hit the Kyber NVL144 architecture showcased at GTC earlier this year.\\nSemiAnalysis Claims Put Nvidia's Kyber Timeline in Doubt\\nSemiAnalysis claims the high-density rack design built for Rubin Ultra GPUs has slipped by more than 12 months. The firm blamed persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\\nThe firm also claimed Nvidia scrapped its NVL72x2 back-to-back rack after pushback from hyperscaler customers.\\nNvidia's supply chain felt the report within hours. Japan's Ibiden, which counts Nvidia as its largest client, fell as much as 10% on Monday, Bloomberg reported.\\nKingboard Laminates tumbled 18% in Hong Kong, while Samsung Electro-Mechanics slid 11% in Seoul.\\nNvidia rejected the claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact. The chipmaker, fresh off launching a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups, has faced this script before.\\nWhen Blackwell delay reports surfaced in August 2024, Nvidia insisted production would ramp on schedule. It then fixed a design flaw and shipped several billion dollars of Blackwell hardware within months.\\nJim Cramer Backs Nvidia Despite the Noise\\nCramer reaffirmed his bullish stance and urged investors to buy Nvidia. He told CNBC that chip stocks are staging a \\\"revenge trade\\\" after last week's \\\"misguided selling.\\\"\\nThe numbers frame his conviction. The Philadelphia Semiconductor Index gained 87.8% in the second quarter, its best quarter since records began in 1994, Axios reported.\\nNvidia missed most of that rally. The stock traded near $196.58 at this writing, up almost 2% over the last 24 hours.\\nLast week tested the sector's nerve. AI chip stocks cracked after Michael Burry's bubble warning, while memory stocks plunged sharply on supply glut fears.\\nCramer, however, sees the pullback as an opportunity. He named his five AI stock picks earlier this month, favoring chip suppliers over Big Tech giants.\\nNvidia's next earnings report will show whether rack-level friction reaches data center revenue. Until then, investors must weigh Cramer's conviction against a laggard chart and SemiAnalysis' supply chain warnings.\\nRead the Original story Jim Cramer Says Buy Nvidia as Chipmaker Rejects 2028 AI Delay Claims by Lockridge Okoth at beincrypto.com\"\n  },\n  \"stored_record\": {\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture — the rack-scale system slated to debut with the Vera Rubin Ultra generation in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture. SemiAnalysis added that NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n      \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n      \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"statement_date_text\": \"Monday\"\n  },\n  \"stored_source\": {\n    \"id\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587\",\n    \"publisher\": \"cnbc.com\",\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"published_at\": \"Sun, 05 Jul 2026 07:00:00 GMT\",\n    \"publisher_type\": \"media\",\n    \"authors\": null,\n    \"category\": \"T7\",\n    \"source_uri\": \"https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/06/nvidia-kyber-rack-system-delays-manufacturing-taiwan-rubin-chips-.html\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"text\": \"Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026.\\nNvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\\nNvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\nShares of the artificial intelligence chip giant climbed about 1% on Monday.\\nKyber is a server cabinet that packs 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips into a single unit so they can work together as one giant computer, providing the horsepower AI companies need to train and run their most advanced models.\\nThe design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\\nThe setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\\n\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\\nNVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\\nThe reported delay adds to mounting strains across Nvidia's product lines, underscoring concerns that Nvidia's breakneck annual release cadence is colliding with manufacturing limits.\\nA backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\\nThat leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\\nThe delays \\\"should not be over analyzed as affecting the long-term criticality of Nvidia to AI data infrastructure buildouts,\\\" said Paul Triolo, a partner at consultancy DGA-Albright Stonebridge Group, noting the company \\\"has faced these kinds of challenges before, and has worked with vendors to overcome technical issues.\\\"\\nWith power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \\\"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\\\"\\nNvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud. SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\\nWhile Huawei and other domestic manufacturers \\\"will potentially gain some time,\\\" China's hardware ecosystem is increasingly diverging from — rather than chasing — the Nvidia-led model, Triolo said. \\\"The issue is no longer really catching up, but how good will China's alternative AI stack be by 2030,\\\" he said.\"\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:52:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'material|floor|threshold|0.5|search|1.9%|57|miss|recall' reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/CONCLUSIONS.md reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/stored-census.md clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md | tail -70\"","aggregated_output":"clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1427:    not checked for missed links, and line 3 was not repeated.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1436:  4. **Fixes judged on their motivating cases.** Rule: include untouched cases, and read both false merges and missed links.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1466:**Kept, conditionally:** search and later-link judgement on group originals, provided the group is a true repeat. Stored groups where a\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1467:member adds a material detail are to be checked.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1471:- on stored data: unresolved links, quote and figure issues, mapping collisions, later-link misses;\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1482:- **Readers:** two independent readers list the matters from the source first, then judge kept items, misses, theses against the\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1486:  - search at least 90% of reader-identified repeats **and revisions**;\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1522:Prompt tokens on d1-57 went from 9,398 to 10,552 (+12%). Each addition carries D382–D385 or an audit amendment.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1546:  - 40 random rejected pairs: both readers say do not file. That is 0 misses in 40.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1550:    links), and the comparison scored one pair 0.55. Later linking applies Step 1's own boundary.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1570:- Manifest sha256 `81f3f579…`.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1609:- Step 2 placed the 188 records in 131 groups (57 repeats, 0 refused). Later linking judged 182 pairs and linked 1.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1616:| Helpful matters kept | 132 / 139 = **95.0%** (core 83 / 83). All 7 misses are supporting: two author estimates, Huang's agent-compute claim (kept in another article), the capital-return policy, a driver exploit report, handheld openness, a Kioxia SSD | ≥ 81.2%: **met** |\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1619:| Wrong merges | **1 / 57** = 1.8%, none unjudged. 52 were accepted by both readers; of the 5 disputed, the third reader found 2 acceptable, 2 overlap and 1 wrong (Vera Rubin deliveries merged into production status and revenue share) | ≤ 2%: **met** |\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1620:| Detail retention | grouped **22 / 26** = ungrouped 22 / 26. This is structural: all 188 records are stored with their own text. The 4 details missing are Step 1 omissions | no fewer: **met** |\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1627:Both readers judged them shared quantities that the standard allows. By my reading the count is 3, which misses the line. This is\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1668:**Reflection council** (a fresh thread, `council/ap-codex.md`, then `aq-codex.md`). It confirmed helpful, search and theses. It\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1669:retracted its first claim of 3/57 wrong merges after reading the third reader's prompt: `merge_acceptable: false` is forced on\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1671:- report search both ways: 141/143 for search alone, 141/145 (97.2%) for the whole system. One 11-member group supplies 55 pairs, and\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1760:- dev search was 98.9–100%;\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1771:- a passing total can hide a missing question or an unusable grouping;\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1780:It would not rank clutter above a repeatedly missing material question. KEEP: read the source and answers directly; challenge before\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1782:of the Ohio miss.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1806:- no key material found by pattern;\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1822:## 48. Commit; the Ohio-guarantee miss diagnosed; one mechanism under two classes (2026-11-09)\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1830:- It is missed in 3 of 3 c8 dev draws. The test gold has none. It is q23 of the frozen judgement gold.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1831:- The other dev misses are different: 2026 capex opened as 2027 in two draws, and DRAM in one draw.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1939:- The question to Alex is about a **material** unsigned obligation that merits a separate investment judgment, not every unsigned\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1945:**Alex's answer (D388).** To the exact question, \"单独开 (Recommended)\": a material unsigned obligation keeps its own signing\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1988:**The model's own account** (`thesis_account.py`, `council/bs-asks.json`, `probe-r14-c10/thesis-account.json`; six questions, $0.025):\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2031:**Results** (`probe-r15-c11`, `probe-r15-c11-ohio`; one draw per case, three for Ohio; $0.57). Blind reading of c11's theses alone\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2049:  standard, and four review rounds missed it. The field did its job: the comparison is now with the company.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2118:  material unsigned obligation of NVIDIA beside an open total; they are completed or unsized investments, third-party matters and\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2139:- The code contract enforces opens exactly when every condition holds, and refuses missing, repeated or empty items.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2169:- fresh-47: \"$2.5–$3 billion — material and unsigned\", with no comparison.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2170:- fresh-22: \"the $500 billion-plus platform scale means any Nvidia guarantee would be material\". It infers a guarantee the article does\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2172:- Control fresh-50: \"$2 billion is immaterial to Nvidia\".\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2176:- fresh-47's cause is not shown to be a missing anchor. The request asks for company_impact but does not require large_enough to\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2187:**Alex's answer.** He chose \"再修一轮小步骤 (Recommended)\" over setting a scale threshold or stopping. Only the two causes the council\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2192:If the $2.5–3bn stake still opens, the remaining question is the scale threshold itself, which goes to him.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2219:| Nscale stake | about $2bn | small (blind readers) | about 0.9% | about 0.5% |\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2268:## 57. The programme-versus-deal fix: three drafts blocked; the boundary goes to Alex (2026-11-09)\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2275:   Blocked: D248 compares questions, not what a programme's deals feed. An unsigned material plan can merit its own signing question.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2299:- **dev-34 opens once in one draw:** \"Will NVIDIA sign a binding AI Compute Partnership backstop/floor-utilization agreement with Firmus\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2394:  blind adjudication; per-question gold items; refusals counted as misses; a stated worst-case cost; a condition 4 that a failed control\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2424:  - i006 (the $3.5bn May-filing guarantees), written by the control (1 of 1) and seeded d3, but missing in seeded d1 and d2. One control\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2428:- With the questions present, c8 delivers what production missed: the 66% margin for q10, which v108 never wrote, and Broadcom's\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2466:- **`calibrate.py`.** The six stored v108 draws give the frozen thresholds (exploratory): +3 with all questions, +4 without q09, q12 and\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2534:- The difference is +5 with all questions and +5 without the noise questions, so both thresholds (+3 and +4) are met, and there is no\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2559:- clean judging: $0.48, $0.52 and $0.51, plus $0.09 for the stopped attempt;\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2569:    split, broader and missing questions.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2574:threshold is exploratory.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2616:- It must be tested on missed and irrelevant connections, Alex's cash example included.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2621:## 65. q12, arm c13 (a closing-check derivation step): also fails line 1; the model does not recall the identity unprompted (2026-11-09)\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2628:- The probe (`poc/data/clean-q12-2026-11-09/probe-c13/`) is 42 calls, $0.57, 0 errors. The control is today's c8 arm from the c12\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2642:**The mechanism:** the model holds the identity \"free cash flow = operating cash flow − capital expenditures\" but does not recall it from\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2643:a generic instruction or a generic example. Placement (c13) and wording (c12) both leave this one recall step undone.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2686:## 67. The unseeded measurement's result (D395): graded subset 4.0 against 6.33; every miss is a question not opened in the ruler's shape (2026-11-10)\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2710:**The nine subset misses, each a question not opened as SAME:**\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2711:- **q04 and q13, 3 misses.** Each draw opens one hyperscaler-capex question, for one year (d1 and d3: 2027; d2: 2026). Alex's D38 (\"one\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2712:  view per year (this year and next)\") makes them two questions, so the second year is a genuine opening miss.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2713:- **q05, 3 misses.** Memory is opened each time, but as a broader price question (d1) or as HBM capacity (d2 and d3), not the 2027\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2715:- **q09, 1 miss.** d2 opened credit support at a 2027-12-31 check date, not SAME by the frozen limit.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2716:- **q23, 2 misses.** The Ohio signing question was opened in 1 of 3.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2721:- The miss counts and matchings hold.\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2759:  wording, ladder and deadline. It is also true by construction that every miss is \"a question not opened\": an unmatched question\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2762:**What version 1 of the design missed** (each checked in the files):\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2808:4. the reader table missed readers outside this path, and two old checks were not met (the quarterly rule, a blank `settled_by`);\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2810:   thresholds with no basis; a hold-out that is not blind);\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:2830:4. Readers and old checks still missing (`views_open_at_day_start`, `views_for_prompt`; the quarterly rule over `looks_at`; the typed\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
