,\"probabilities\":{\"different\":0.1,\"same_statement\":0.02,\"supersedes\":0.88}}\nR05 jev_single 1 {\"choice\":\"supersedes\",\"confidence\":0.82,\"probabilities\":{\"different\":0.1,\"same_statement\":0.02,\"supersedes\":0.88}}\nR05 jev_single 2 {\"choice\":\"supersedes\",\"confidence\":0.79,\"probabilities\":{\"different\":0.12,\"same_statement\":0.02,\"supersedes\":0.86}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:56:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1590,1624p;1650,1682p;1448,1470p' clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md; rg -n 'material|floor|0\\\\.5|ranking|probabilit' reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/CONCLUSIONS.md\"","aggregated_output":"ended in agreement with changes.\n\n**Agreed corrections (c8 = c7 plus these):**\n1. Remove the K6b example \"an analyst valued the shares at $80, 15 times next year's earnings of $5.30\" (c7-system line 36), which\n   contradicts D385.\n2. Closing line 12 against the standard's \"不拆成两条，除非是两件事\" (line 436): \"A report and another party's denial or confirmation of it\n   are two items, as are an estimate and a valuation someone draws from it; one party's statement keeps its own planned or rumoured\n   details.\"\n3. Line 55: \"… inform an open thesis about its revenue or earnings path when they bear on that path; a different period is not on its\n   own a reason to leave them unlinked.\" This keeps Alex's margin ruling and D371, with relevance as the test.\n\n**Code corrections:**\n- `score_final.py` refuses a line-2 verdict while any pair is unjudged.\n- `tally_questions.py` refuses mismatched counts.\n- Later linking retries pending pairs on the next write to the thesis and at a run's start (design §11.1), keeping the original\n  opening boundary, and reports what stays pending.\n- `map_reference` collisions are counted and inspected against the source.\n\n**Kept, conditionally:** search and later-link judgement on group originals, provided the group is a true repeat. Stored groups where a\nmember adds a material detail are to be checked.\n\n**Before freezing:**\n- the whole-request two-way audit (prompt-audit skill, blind second reader);\n- **A single reader session over all 50 articles stopped after 5.** It returned `status: incomplete`. The third reader was re-run in\n  batches of ten articles, plus one call for the groups. Post-run judging is batched the same way.\n- **Four settled matters sit in two groups.** A reader's matter bundled two points, such as \"70% growth guidance, while customer\n  forecasts indicate a doubling\". Search is therefore counted over pairs (a later member and an earlier member of the same group),\n  so a matter can belong to two pairs. This mirrors the Step 1 bundling mechanism of the Step 2 log.\n\n**Post-run judge prompt.** It gains a neutral `optional` verdict, so that D372's optional entries are not called noise. Noise is\nreported without a line. I settled this wording myself.\n\n**Launched** (c8 pins identical in every run folder):\n- dev ×3 at 08:43, in `poc/data/clean-step1-c8-dev-d{1,2,3}-2026-11-09/`;\n- the fresh Step 1 run at 08:51, after the lists were frozen, in `poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/step1/`.\n\n## 43. Acceptance on the fresh set: every line met; links are the weak point (2026-11-09)\n\nThe fresh Step 1 run finished: 50 of 50 articles complete in 52 calls. Two format failures were resent once and passed; in one of\nthem the model had added a field the schema does not have.\n- Cost: $0.66 at published rates (input $0.3/M, output $1.2/M, no cache share applied).\n- 188 kept items and 10 opened theses.\n- Step 2 placed the 188 records in 131 groups (57 repeats, 0 refused). Later linking judged 182 pairs and linked 1.\n\n**Readers.** Two readers per batch of ten articles, and a third reader on the 23 disputes (8 matters, 15 links). On the two articles\nI had traced myself (9 and 42) the readers agree with me.\n\n| Line | Result | Pass |\n|---|---|---|\n| Helpful matters kept | 132 / 139 = **95.0%** (core 83 / 83). All 7 misses are supporting: two author estimates, Huang's agent-compute claim (kept in another article), the capital-return policy, a driver exploit report, handheld openness, a Kioxia SSD | ≥ 81.2%: **met** |\n| Wrongly opened theses | **1** of 10, by both readers: clean-5, \"will Vera Rubin account for about 20% of data-center revenue in fiscal Q3\", a revenue share and so a financial measure (D382) | ≤ 2: **met** |\n| Search | **141 / 143** settled repeat pairs = 98.6%; 2 pairs not exercised because a member was not kept | ≥ 90%: **met** |\n| Wrong merges | **1 / 57** = 1.8%, none unjudged. 52 were accepted by both readers; of the 5 disputed, the third reader found 2 acceptable, 2 overlap and 1 wrong (Vera Rubin deliveries merged into production status and revenue share) | ≤ 2%: **met** |\n| Detail retention | grouped **22 / 26** = ungrouped 22 / 26. This is structural: all 188 records are stored with their own text. The 4 details missing are Step 1 omissions | no fewer: **met** |\n\n**My disagreement with the readers on theses.** I would also call two theses wrong:\n- clean-9, global semiconductor revenue in 2026 (Deloitte's estimate): an industry aggregate that adds little beyond NVIDIA's own\n  guidance;\n| Measure | Draw 1 | Draw 2 | Draw 3 | c7 (earlier) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Helpful, of 234 (line 190) | 214 | 211 | 212 | 212 |\n| Core, of 86 | 85 | 86 | 86 | 86 |\n| Not-kept spans kept | 27 | 27 | 30 | 30 |\n| Theses opened | 8 | 10 | 10 | 10 |\n| must_open / may_open / none / must_not (one reader) | 4/3/1/0 | 3/3/3/1 (lidar) | 3/2/5/0 | 4/2/3/1 |\n| must_open covered, of 28 | 25 | 24 | 27 | 27 |\n| Search (reference mapped onto the draw) | 98/99 = 99.0% | 91/92 = 98.9% | 97/97 = 100% | |\n| Clear wrong merges (third reader) | 1/100 | 1/100 | 1/106 | |\n| Overlaps | 14 | 12 | 15 | |\n\n- **Stable across the three draws:** the six core theses (credit support, Vera Rubin, China H200, hyperscaler capex, Kyber, supply\n  commitments).\n- **Varies:** the edges (memory prices, Rubin Ultra, China budget shift, lidar, DeepSeek inquiry, custom ASIC pool, inference-chip\n  startups).\n- **Not covered in any draw:** the Ohio-guarantee yes/no question. The broader credit-support amount thesis is opened instead.\n\n**Reflection council** (a fresh thread, `council/ap-codex.md`, then `aq-codex.md`). It confirmed helpful, search and theses. It\nretracted its first claim of 3/57 wrong merges after reading the third reader's prompt: `merge_acceptable: false` is forced on\noverlaps, which the prompt defines as \"not misleading\", and the agreed line is clear wrong merges (D381). Agreed caveats:\n- report search both ways: 141/143 for search alone, 141/145 (97.2%) for the whole system. One 11-member group supplies 55 pairs, and\n  revisions are barely exercised;\n- line 3 is a storage comparison, not a usability test;\n- my thesis dissent is recorded as a breadth concern, not a count: the council reads clean-9 and clean-10 as supported shared\n  quantities;\n- c8 did not raise links per item, but that says nothing about whether the links are right.\n\nVerdict: REPORT AS STATED.\n\n**Link recheck (the council's cheapest next step).** A fresh senior-analyst reader saw the 34 links not judged right, mixed blind\nwith 10 links judged right, and asked whether the item helps assess the question:\n\n7:Neither a benefit nor harm has been established. Keep the present request until a controlled comparison shows an advantage. Similarity is calculated between a search query and a stored group's original text; it is not a probability of same statement. The query may contain a thesis and a candidate may have several scores from different queries. Jev does not receive these scores; it receives the incoming piece as state and one candidate per typed question. It returns a choice and probabilities. Jev's choice probability, not cosine, selects among multiple same-statement results.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:57:{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"node -e 'let p=require(\\\"./blind-missed.json\\\").find(x=>x.id==\\\"H01\\\"); console.log(JSON.stringify(p,null,2));' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:60:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"node -e 'let p=require(\\\"./blind-missed.json\\\").find(x=>x.id==\\\"H01\\\"); console.log(JSON.stringify(p,null,2));' \"","aggregated_output":"(eval):5: parse error near `end'\n(eval):5: parse error near `end'\n{\n  \"id\": \"H01\",\n  \"incoming_record\": {\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia announced on Thursday that it is acquiring the open-source AI model repository Hugging Face for $13 billion (EUR 11.2 billion). The item gives no closing date and attributes the report to EU Tech Loop and Euronews; it does not say whether the deal has regulatory approval.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n      \"American chipmaker NVIDIA announced on Thursday its acquisition of the artificial intelligence library Hugging Face for $13 billion (EUR11.2bn).\",\n      \"The deal, reported by EU Tech Loop and shared on Euronews, marks a significant investment in a platform that began as a chat app for teenagers and evolved into a major repository for open-source and open-weight AI models.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"statement_date_text\": \"Thursday\"\n  },\n  \"incoming_source\": {\n    \"id\": \"gnews:243636e860a787a59c4d0c7a\",\n    \"title\": \"NVIDIA Acquires Hugging Face for $13 Billion: A Boost for Europe's Open-Source AI\",\n    \"publisher\": \"indexbox.io\",\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-06\",\n    \"text\": \"NVIDIA Acquires Hugging Face for $13 Billion: A Boost for Europe's Open-Source AI\\nAmerican chipmaker NVIDIA announced on Thursday its acquisition of the artificial intelligence library Hugging Face for $13 billion (EUR11.2bn). The deal, reported by EU Tech Loop and shared on Euronews, marks a significant investment in a platform that began as a chat app for teenagers and evolved into a major repository for open-source and open-weight AI models.\\nHugging Face, founded by French entrepreneurs with half of its employees based in Paris, is expected to play a key role in Europe's open-source AI ecosystem. The acquisition is likely to draw attention from competition regulators and AI industry observers in Paris and Brussels, who may seek enforcement of the EU AI Act and a regulatory review of the deal's terms.\\nNVIDIA, now the world's largest publicly traded company by market capitalization, has recently expanded its support for open AI models, offering dozens for free on its platforms and advocating for competition among both closed-source and open-source LLM providers.\\nFor EU innovators lacking the resources to train large models like those from Anthropic or OpenAI, Hugging Face's platforms could become a valuable tool. Hugging Face co-founder Clement Delangue commented in a 2024 interview with Le Monde that open-source can address many AI problems, as proprietary models are opaque and difficult to analyze for bias or sources of truth.\\nThe European approach to AI emphasizes regulatory frameworks, but innovation extends beyond LLM makers like Mistral or DeepL. Dutch company ASML supplies semiconductor manufacturing equipment globally, while Polish-founded ElevenLabs has developed speech and voice processing models integrated into thousands of AI products.\\nOpen models can also be customized for government use, hosted locally on sensitive data, potentially reducing vendor lock-in and reliance on non-EU infrastructure. This would require greater expertise in public agencies but could help de-risk European systems from dependence on Chinese or American technology.\\nBuilding EU data centers is a regulatory priority, but the software within them is equally important. For everyday users and small companies, open models offer extensive access at little or no cost, benefiting researchers and practitioners in arts and sciences with limited budgets.\\nDelangue testified before a U.S. House Committee in 2023 that open science and open-source AI distribute economic gains by enabling many small companies and startups to build with AI, fostering innovation and fair competition. Regardless of the model used, consumers and small firms can fork, tune, customize, and upgrade it.\"\n  },\n  \"stored_record\": {\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia announced on Thursday (the week of the 2026-09-05 article) a $12.9 billion deal to purchase Hugging Face, a New York-based startup that hosts a repository for open-source AI models and, per the article, has more than 18 million developers and researchers using more than 3 million models and more than 200,000 enterprise customers; the article says Hugging Face was the focus of an intense bidding war. The deal is reported as announced, not completed.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n      \"On Thursday, NVIDIA – market cap: $5.55 trillion – announced a $12.9 billion deal to purchase Hugging Face, a New York-based startup that has built a repository for open-source AI models.\",\n      \"Hugging Face was the focus of an intense bidding war given its place in the AI sphere – it has more than 18 million developers and researchers using more than 3 million models, and more than 200,000 enterprise customers.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"statement_date_text\": \"Thursday, in the week of the 2026-09-05 article\"\n  },\n  \"stored_source\": {\n    \"id\": \"gnews:26fd862b0d58999b7451b493\",\n    \"title\": \"John McGuire bought NVIDIA stock before NVIDIA made a $12.9 billion AI deal\",\n    \"publisher\": \"augustafreepress.com\",\n    \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-05\",\n    \"text\": \"MAGA Congressman John McGuire , Mr. “I Don’t Even Trades,” bought between $1,001 and $15,000 of NVIDIA stock ahead of NVIDIA’s announcement this week that it is buying AI startup Hugging Face .\\nThis is a matter of public record; McGuire filed a disclosure form on Wednesday noting the purchase with the Office of the Clerk of the House of Representatives .\\nThe purchase was made on Aug. 18 , per the disclosure.\\nOn Thursday, NVIDIA – market cap: $5.55 trillion – announced a $12.9 billion deal to purchase Hugging Face, a New York-based startup that has built a repository for open-source AI models.\\nHugging Face was the focus of an intense bidding war given its place in the AI sphere – it has more than 18 million developers and researchers using more than 3 million models, and more than 200,000 enterprise customers.\\nMcGuire would know better than the rest of us just how valuable Hugging Face was on the open market because he serves on House Oversight subcommittees focused on cybersecurity, information technology and military affairs .\\nAssuming McGuire pays attention to what goes on in the subcommittee meetings, he’s getting access to classified and other sensitive information that the rest of us aren’t privy to, including the latest developments in who wants to buy who – valuable information, if you’re in a position to trade on it, like a congressman would be.\\nIt’s one thing to have access to classified and sensitive information; entirely another thing to take that information straight to the bank.\\nOf note here: McGuire, oddly, doesn’t seem to want to take credit for using the inside info to better his own personal bottom line.\\nAsked by a Lynchburg TV station last month about his reluctance to schedule a debate with his Democratic opponent, Tom Perriello , McGuire made the reluctance about Perriello having accused him of “insider trading,” claiming: “I don’t even do trades.”\\nInstead of patting himself on the back after getting what you could look at as praise from your opponent for having the sense to make money off what you know, McGuire signaled that he expected an apology from Perriello for pointing out the publicly available information about his stock trades.\\nWeird, right?\\nMaybe he’s telling us there that he gets it, that it doesn’t look good, after all.\\nCredit here on the stock trades becoming public knowledge in the first place goes to Cville Right Now , which broke the story on McGuire actually doing trades , reporting back on July 13 that McGuire, after voting with the House majority to pass the 21st Century Road to Housing Act “sold off over $1,000 of BlackRock Inc. stock , partially divesting himself from one of the companies the legislation targets three days before the bill became law,” and “also divulged purchasing between $1,001 and $15,000 in Space Explorations Technologies Corp ., the company founded by Elon Musk in 2002.”\\nThat information comes from a public disclosure form on file with the Office of the Clerk of the U.S. House of Representatives – filed by McGuire, with his signature.\\nThe BlackRock Inc. stock dump was dated July 7 ; the SpaceX dump was dated June 15 .\\nHere’s where I added to the reporting done last month by CRN : Mr. “I Don’t Even Do Trades” dumped more BlackRock stock – in separate trades valued between $1,001 and $15,000 – on July 10 and July 14 , per a filing made with the clerk’s office on Aug. 7 .\\nThat Aug. 7 filing also includes a notation of a sale of between $1,001 and $15,000 in stock of Applied Materials Inc ., a Silicon Valley company – market cap: $360.9 billion – that supplies equipment, services and software for the manufacture of semiconductor chips for electronics, flat panel displays for computers, smartphones, televisions, and solar products.\\nOne other from the Aug. 7 filing: the sale of between $1,001 and $15,000 of stock in Palo Alto Networks Inc. , another Silicon Valley company – market cap: $271.6 billion – that focuses on cybersecurity, with its core product being a platform that includes advanced firewalls and cloud-based offerings that extend those firewalls to cover other aspects of security.\\nYou’d think he’d be proud of himself for all of this.\\nThe guy is $390,000 better off because he “does trades.”\\nMe, personally, I’d be bragging about that.\\nChris Graham is the founder and editor of Augusta Free Press . A 1994 alum of the University of Virginia , Chris is the author and co-author of seven books, including Poverty of Imagination , a memoir published in 2019. For his commentaries on news, sports and politics, go to his YouTube page , TikTok , BlueSky , or subscribe to his Street Knowledge podcast . Email Chris at [email protected] . Support AFP by signing up for our newsletter and/or powering our work with a one-time or monthly contribution .\\nJMU rallied from 10 down in the final 3:56 to force OT, but VCU’s Blake Harper poured in 11 of his game-high 30 points in the extra period to help the Rams to a 92-87 win over the Dukes on Sunday in a preseason contest at the Siegel Center. VCU led by 10 with 3:56...\\nIndiana native Kyle Guy helped the UVA Basketball program win the NCAA title in 2019, and he was voted the Most Outstanding Player in the NCAA Tournament that year.\\nWe have reached 57 percent of what we need to fund critical operations for 2026. If you have been waiting for the right time to support AFP , this is it.\"\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:65:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '101,160p' clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md; sed -n '47,65p' /Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/INVESTIGATION.md\"","aggregated_output":"## 3. Search: recall at 10 (`score_retrieval_v2.py`, forward simulation)\n\n| Query | Per association, raw history (391) | Deduplicated history | Forward simulation, real Jev decisions |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R0, production, within thesis | 82.1% | | |\n| R0e, word overlap, entity-wide | 78.5% | | |\n| R1, thesis + evidence (agreed) | 90.0% | 125/125 | 112/113 |\n| R2, evidence only | 95.9% | 113/113 | |\n\n- Revisions: R1 found 22 of 25.\n- Cross-thesis positives: only 3, so no conclusion can be drawn on cross-thesis search.\n- The raw-history misses come from many copies of the same story crowding the top 10; merging removes that crowding.\n- 112/113 counts only known matches, from one Jev draw.\n\n## 4. Comparison (Jev)\n\n**Pair level on the labelled pairs** (`score_jev.py`):\n\n| Arm | Agreement | Wrong merges |\n|---|---|---|\n| J0, production question | 82.4% | 30 (9.4%; 14 from one bundle record) |\n| J1, cross-thesis wording | 80.8% | 22 (7.5%) |\n\nPair level is not the verdict. A record is saved or discarded whole, and one true match is enough.\n\n**Product path, per incoming record** (`score_product_path.py`; J0 over the R1 top 10):\n\n| Arm | Merged into a true same statement | No true match | Duplicates kept |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| J0 | 106 | 6 | 4 |\n| J1 | 97 | 2 | 13 |\n\n**Forward simulation** (`forward_sim.py`, J0, article order, the library each decision would see): 282 items become 180 records.\n- 102 merged.\n- Both loss readers say 93 of those have a true same-statement home. 9 do not: by my read, 3 clear (Sharon AI into Firmus; analysts' 82%\n  and 41% into the analysts' revenue-doubling record; NVL576 into NVL144) and 6 arguable.\n- 10 cite the incoming source on an extra record that the incoming record does not state.\n- 14 true repeats in the results were left unmerged.\n\n## 5. What merging loses (`loss-answer-G/H-*`, all 102 merged items, two readers)\n\n- Both readers say a material fact is lost that no other record holds in 66 of 102 (agreement 93/102). The readers over-flag.\n- My hand-read of a random 12 of the 66:\n  - 3 material: Q1 net income, EPS and margin; Q2 guidance \"±2%, assumes no China data-centre revenue\"; the Taiwan raid on Supermicro\n    bundled into a Singapore record;\n  - 3 moderate;\n  - 6 trivial.\n- A random 4 of the no-loss items: 4 had no loss.\n- This is a small diagnostic sample, not a population estimate.\n- **Mechanism:**\n  - Step 1 records carry one matter, often several facts.\n  - Later articles retell a statement and add figures or qualifications.\n  - The current rule keeps only the earlier text and adds the source, so those additions are discarded.\n\n**Probe J2** (`jev-J2-merges.json`, $0.004): same_statement only when the incoming record adds nothing an analyst would keep.\n- Merges fell from 102 to 22 on the same 102 merges. This is not a new forward run.\n- All 3 material and all 3 moderate losses were kept, but so were 5 of 6 trivial and 3 of 4 no-loss items.\n- Jev cannot separate trivial from material additions any better than the readers can, so a materiality gate either loses facts or\n  undoes most of the merging.\n\nDo not confuse a record whose top-level outcome is `revision` with a revision relationship: `compare.outcome` attaches each `supersedes` candidate even if the incoming record joins a repeat group. Counted by exact `outcome.revises` path over the four completed saved runs:\n\n| Saved run | incoming records with ≥1 revision link | links emitted | of these records: repeat / standalone revision |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Fresh | 3 | 3 | 3 / 0 |\n| Dev draw 1 | 12 | 24 | 9 / 3 |\n| Dev draw 2 | 12 | 31 | 10 / 2 |\n| Dev draw 3 | 12 | 26 | 10 / 2 |\n\nTotal 84 emitted revision links over 39 records; these are four recorded runs with repeated inputs, not independent samples. No semantic verdict has yet been obtained for all 84. Many dev links concern Kyber reporting and denial; this is not a statement that they are wrong. These counts revise the informal characterization that only 2–3 revisions per draw existed: that described top-level outcomes, not the full relation path. The fresh references and original stored data remain unchanged.\n\nAll three fresh revision links read against saved evidence texts:\n\n1. `19:ev-guidance-70` → `9:e5`: 70% supply-constrained guidance versus customer-implied potential for roughly 100% growth with supply limitations. Jev chose supersedes 0.48 versus different 0.46. Two earlier source-blind readers independently chose different. This is a sourced lead for an incorrect link, not a new frozen ruler.\n2. `30:e1` → `20:ev-1`: both describe the same announced Hugging Face acquisition, one approximately $12.9bn with a retention detail and the other a rounded $13bn. Jev chose supersedes 0.67. Whether added deal details are a changed claim or a richer report of the same announcement needs independent source-backed judgment; the quote alone does not establish a correction. Do not pre-label it.\n3. `40:e1` → `31:e1`: the earlier record reports an unofficial leaked DLSS 5 runtime and explicitly notes no official game confirmation; the later record reports an official launch in that game. Jev chose supersedes 0.98. An update/confirmation is plausible, but it is not automatically a valid link without a source-backed reader determining whether the new report changes the old main statement.\n\nAll three incoming records were also grouped as repeats elsewhere. Any candidate that only screens repeat targets but leaves all `supersedes` edges untouched cannot address the first finding. Conversely a blanket revision veto would also erase plausible updates such as case 3. This is a contract and design requirement for a general comparison test, not evidence that a new gate will work.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/codex-independent.jsonl:70:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"agent_message","text":"**结论：目前继续用 Jev 做 clean Step 2，是现有证据支持得最充分的选择；但我对“它已经足够可靠”的信心较低，对“现在没有证据支持替换它”的信心较高。**\n\n一个具体例子：两篇文章报道同一笔 Nvidia 收购，Jev 三次都把它们当作不同消息；DeepSeek 三次都认出是同一公告。但在另一组文章里，DeepSeek 三次都把“公司预测增长 70%”错误连成对“客户预测可能翻倍”的修订。它目前也没有提供产品需要的多个候选之间的排序。所以，**保留 Jev 是暂时的研究判断，不是 Jev 已经被证明最好或可以放心交付。**\n\n## 实际比较结果\n\n我核对了保存的逐次回答：六个选定案例、三种方式、各三次，共 **54 条完成记录**。下表中的“相同、不同、修订”是对指定候选的判断；三次是三次请求，不代表三个独立案例，也不能保证服务内部采样彼此独立。[原始事件](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/probe-2026-11-09/events.jsonl)、[请求内容](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/probe-2026-11-09/bodies.json)。\n\n| 案例 | Jev 完整候选 | Jev 单对 | DeepSeek 单对 | 原文支持的判断 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| B01：Broadcom 同一预测 | 相同 3/3 | 相同 3/3 | 相同 3/3 | 同一预测；新增细节不改变主陈述。 |\n| B05：70% 与客户可能翻倍 | 修订 2/3，不同 1/3 | 不同 3/3 | 修订 3/3 | 原文支持不同；DeepSeek 有稳定回退，完整 Jev 也有错误。 |\n| B10：Vera Rubin 投产、收入占比与交付 | 相同 1/3，不同 2/3 | 相同 2/3，不同 1/3 | 不同 3/3 | 交付和已有的投产、收入预测是不同陈述；其是否属于“明确错误”还是有重叠，历史读者有分歧。 |\n| H01：同一收购公告 | 不同 3/3 | 不同 3/3 | 相同 3/3 | 原文支持同一公告；这是 Jev 稳定漏合并的例子。 |\n| P01：官方否认延期 | 修订 3/3 | 修订 3/3 | 修订 3/3 | 目标关系有依据，但只覆盖这一个否认案例。 |\n| R05：Shield TV 是否停产 | 修订 3/3 | 修订 3/3 | 不同 3/3 | **有争议**：我倾向不同，但不能把修订判定为无争议的错误。 |\n\n这不是总体排名。Jev 完整候选是当前产品形式；另外两列只比较一个对子。B05 中，完整 Jev 和单对 Jev 给出不同结果，说明**这次结果对请求形式敏感**，但不能证明原因就是其他候选干扰。DeepSeek 同时换了提供商、模型和输出形式，因此也不能把差异单独归因于模型。\n\n完整 Jev 的保存回答经现有代码重放后，B10 一次并入旧组、两次新建；H01 三次都新建。DeepSeek 没有判断其他候选，不能由它的单对回答推断整条记录最终会进入哪个组。[重放结果](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/RESULTS-DRAFT.md)、[分组代码](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step2/compare.py:109)。\n\n## 原文里最影响判断的地方\n\n- **H01：**两篇保存的文章都说 Nvidia 周四宣布收购 Hugging Face；129 亿和约 130 亿美元可由四舍五入解释。文章支持“报道了同一公告”，但不证明交易已完成，也不证明公告本身真实。Jev 三次没认出重复，DeepSeek 三次认出。[两篇原文和记录](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/blind-missed.json)。\n\n- **B05：**旧文章同时写了 70% 公司指引和客户预测可能翻倍，新文章写 70% 指引。它们可以同时成立，不支持把前者说成修改了后者。限制是：送给比较器的新记录没有包含原文中的供给约束，旧陈述日期未知，两篇报道对 70% 的个人说话人归属也不一致。**这些限制削弱对犯错原因的判断，不改变原文对“两个说法不同”的支持。**[原文](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/source-blind-packet.json)、[输入细节](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/INVESTIGATION.md:87)。\n\n- **B10：**一篇说产品已投产、管理层预计其占第三财季数据中心收入约 20%；另一篇说八月已有商业交付。交付是新节点，不是那个收入预测的复述。旧记录把“已投产”归给 Nvidia，比文章本身的归属更强；历史审阅对“明确错误”与“有重叠”也有分歧。[原文](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/source-blind-packet.json)、[历史分歧](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/reports/step2-wrong-merge-council-2026-11-09/CONCLUSIONS.md)。\n\n- **R05：**旧文章推测标准版 Shield TV 似乎停产，同时承认可能只是缺货。后文拿到 Nvidia 回信：缺货由需求造成，未来供应没有更新；公司没有确认或否认停产。另一种合理读法是，同一作者的后续报道改变了先前判断。旧记录还把媒体推断的原始说话方写成 Nvidia。我倾向将两条看作不同陈述，但保留另一读法，不用多数票把它变成标准答案。[两篇原文](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/blind-revisions.json)、[分歧](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/result-blind-claude.md)、[只看原文的复核](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/r05-adjudication.md)。\n\n- **P01：**官方否认延期的目标关系有依据，但否认已经出现在旧文章里，日期也有冲突，所以它不能证明识别“后来修改预测”的一般能力。更关键的是，完整 Jev 三次都除了连接延期报告，还连接了“备用机柜方案被取消”这一条。文章先提到该方案，再笼统说 Nvidia 拒绝这些说法；实际比较输入只说否认延期。**第二条链接是否成立仍有争议**，不能用目标关系答对来证明整个写入结果正确。[原文](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/blind-positive-revisions.json)、[第二条链接](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/results-codex-followup.md)。\n\n这些阅读是对保存的媒体文本的判断；盲读者也是 AI，**不是 Alex 签过的金标**。部分结果盲读虽然隐藏了哪个模型产生答案，但答案选项和原文放在同一包里，不能称为完全看不到输出的盲读。\n\n## 为什么暂时保留 Jev，为什么还不能放心\n\n**支持保留的证据：**\n\n1. Jev 已经完成当前路径：搜索候选后逐一比较；有多个重复时，按其给出的同一陈述概率选择一组。这是 Alex 的 D381 决定。DeepSeek 的实验只返回单对标签，没有提供这一排序，也没有测完整历史向前运行的结果。[PRD](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/PRD.md:129)、[D381](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4101)。\n2. 一次新样本中，搜索找到 **141/143** 个已保存、已标注的重复对；另外两对因一条记录未保存而无法搜索，放到整个路径是 **141/145**。但一个大组贡献 55 对，修订几乎没有覆盖，所以不能把 98.6% 当作所有真实重复和修订的召回率。[记录](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1605)。\n3. 同一次中，第三读者判定明确错误合并 **1/57**，另有两个有重叠的案例；此前一次是 **3/104**，没有达到 Alex 的 2% 线。三个开发运行分别是 **1/100、1/100、1/106**，但重复使用文章，不能当成三个独立数据集。结果随样本和读者判断变化，不能从一次通过推断稳定通过。[新样本和三次开发运行](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1619)、[此前未通过](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md:285)。\n\n**限制同样重要：**\n\n- 搜到了不等于认对了。H01 的候选已找到，Jev 仍三次漏合并；搜索成绩不覆盖这类比较错误。\n- “正文保留下来”不等于读者一定正确理解。当前代码保留每条成员自己的文本，却把它展示在“同一陈述的后续报道”之下；B10 因此会有误导性标题。此前针对后来新增细节的阅读测试里，分组与不合并分别得到 **25/36 与 25/36**；这支持保留文本，但测试没有证明错误分组安全。新样本的 **22/26 对 22/26** 更主要是结构性保存检查，不是读者理解的证明。[阅读测试](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md:237)、[展示代码](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step3/library.py)。\n- 修订链接没有被 D381 的三条验收线充分测到。四个保存运行共写出 **84 条修订链接，来自 39 条新记录**；其中有的记录同时并入重复组。它们不是 84 个独立案例，也没有全部经过原文判断。B05 证明，即使记录并入了正确的 70% 组，仍可能额外写出错误修订链接。[逐运行计数](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/INVESTIGATION.md:47)。\n- D202 和 D204 的教训是：不能因一次模型判断就丢掉旧证据或新文本。clean 路径保留文本是重要进步，但错误关系仍会影响读者如何理解两条记录；目前没有证明 B10 改变了最终投资判断。[D202 和 D204](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2594)。\n\n## 其他办法的证据\n\n- **给 Jev 看搜索相似度：**原计划 11 个案例，完成了 6 个；这 6 个的 46 个候选关系在三次对照与三次处理里没有变化。关键错误合并和修订案例没跑到。因此没有看到收益，也不能说无效或等价。[中断实验](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/RESULTS.md)。\n- **设置 0.5 门槛：**对保存的 363 次合并决定离线回放，会挡住 1 次读者认为错误的合并，也挡住 4 次读者接受的合并和 1 次有重叠的合并。高分错误也存在；这不足以支持门槛。[复核](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/reports/step2-merge-fix-council-2026-11-09/CONCLUSIONS.md)。\n- **只在没有新增重要内容时合并：**旧试验在同一批 102 次合并中只保留 22 次；虽然留下了抽查中的重要新增内容，也把不少无关紧要的变化拆开。这个办法没有证明能可靠分辨重要与不重要。[历史测试](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md:135)。\n- **DeepSeek 替换或加一道复核：**H01 值得重视，B05 同样值得重视。它尚未回答多候选排序、不同意见如何处理、搜索漏掉候选怎么办，以及完整写入后的效果。B05 是结果出现后的严重回退，**不是预先明确写下的 B01/P01 自动停止条件**。[实验规则与纠正](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/COUNCIL-LOOP.md:71)。\n\n要推翻“继续用 Jev”的建议，需要一个完整候选方案在相同的保存输入及之后独立的新文章上，既能按 D381 选择正确组，又能减少错误合并、漏合并和错误修订，同时保住真实重复与真实更新；还要看读者实际看到的内容。反过来，如果系统性检查发现 Jev 的错误链接或漏合并经常改变读者对证据的理解，即使替代方案还没准备好，也会推翻“当前可靠”的判断。\n\n**我建议的唯一下一步免费研究：**逐条检查已完成的 D392 第一次开发运行中 **56 个 Jev 判为修订的候选回答**，把每个回答放回原始文章、当时的完整候选、实际写入的所有链接和读者看到的文字中。分别列出有原文支持、无原文支持、争议未决，以及输入缺失的信息；同时保留真实否认和真实更新作为反例。56 是候选回答数，不能直接称为 56 个独立错误或 56 条最终链接。这个检查能先回答：B05、P01 是否只是孤例，以及问题在比较、写入关系还是展示。它不需要改代码、发请求或改变任何标准。[待查范围](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/results-claude.md:84)。\n\n本次只读；没有编辑文件、发送模型请求或打开运行中的数据库。"}}
