reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/claude-independent.jsonl:32:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014YL8REse9cUB7wSesbi1FQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Step 2 same-input paired diagnostic — completed, selected pairs\n2\t\n3\t2026-11-09. User authorized the six-case × three-arm × three-draw experiment. All 54 unique actual HTTP requests completed; no retry, no reported failures or missing usage. The completion marker, immutable request bodies, preflight and fsynced attempt/result events are in `probe-2026-11-09/`. Outputs below are the raw typed labels in draw order. Jev full sees the original whole candidate batch; Jev single and DeepSeek single receive the same pair text and definitions, though their providers and output formats differ. B01 and P01 are controls; P01 is a direct denial also quoted as background in the earlier source, not a clean later-forecast revision. The six source-backed opinions were frozen before sending, and R05's stored source-attribution field is known to be suspect. They are AI source opinions and not frozen gold.\n4\t\n5\t| Pair / source-backed lead | Jev full (3) | Jev single (3) | DeepSeek single (3) |\n6\t|---|---|---|---|\n7\t| B01 same: valid Broadcom repeat | same, same, same | same, same, same | same, same, same |\n8\t| B05 different: company 70% guidance vs customer implied doubling | supersedes, supersedes, different | different, different, different | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes |\n9\t| B10 different: Vera Rubin deliveries vs expected revenue share | same, different, different | same, different, same | different, different, different |\n10\t| H01 same: two reports of acquisition | different, different, different | different, different, different | same, same, same |\n11\t| P01 supersedes: official denies reported Kyber delay | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes |\n12\t| R05 different: company does not confirm/deny Shield discontinuation | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes | different, different, different |\n13\t\n14\tRegressions first: B05 DeepSeek selected the wrong relation in 3/3, while fresh Jev single got it right in 3/3. Jev full erred 2/3, showing request-shape sensitivity for this pair. This is a serious repeatable regression against the fresh single-question control, so the council recommends stopping the comparator line. The prewritten stop controls explicitly named B01 and P01, not B05; this is a new finding, not a claimed predeclared stop trigger. Do not add cases, amend prompts or move to product integration to chase an aggregate score. Selected improvement leads H01 and R05: DeepSeek 3/3 same/different against Jev full and single 0/3 correct, and B10 3/3 different against Jev single 1/3 different (Jev full 2/3 different). Both controls B01/P01 remain in 3/3 across arms. One same-day experiment on hand-selected pairs cannot estimate overall precision or recall, show ranking, compare full product behavior or measure source-level independence. DeepSeek model, provider and output format differ; pair comparison is not proof of the cause.\n15\t\n16\tRecorded usage: Jev full 78,795 input/7,112 output tokens; Jev single 15,867 input/773 output; DeepSeek 13,149 prompt (5,504 cached; 7,645 uncached), 8,582 completion including 8,450 reasoning tokens. All report usage. Reservation $0.182012616, not a bill. Using the repository's dated table (Jev input $0.042/M, DeepSeek input $0.30/M, cached input share .10, output $1.20/M), approximate arithmetic cost $0.0167; actual provider charges can differ.\n17\t\n18\tNo original text was dropped or rewritten by this diagnostic. No product code/config/gold changed. Independent blinded source/output rereads are in progress; final council must challenge interpretation and any disputed source and identify one next discriminating action. The competitor produces a 3-label answer, no group score/rank.\n19\t\n20\t## Deterministic existing product-outcome replay ($0)\n21\t\n22\tReplayed each fresh **full-request Jev answer** through unchanged `compare.outcome`, using the original candidate IDs and existing creation order. B05: 3/3 repeat of `14:e2`; the wrong `9:e5` revision appears 2/3. B10: 1/3 repeat of `13:e4`, 2/3 new. H01: 3/3 new, so all three repeat misses have actual Step 2 effect on this fixed history. R05: 3/3 revision of `119:e1`. P01: all three repeat `45:e2` and attach revision edges to **both** `45:e1` (the checked target) and `45:e3`; the additional edge was not adjudicated in this six-pair test. Thus P01's target answer staying correct is not evidence that every relation written on that record is correct. This is deterministic replay on the saved fixed candidate state, not a new forward run. The independent comparator has only pair labels, so no product group choice is claimed for it.\n23\t\n24\t## Independent blind reread (partial)\n25\t\n26\t`result-blind-codex.md` read all six complete source pairs and 54 anonymous labels without arm or draw names. It agreed with the pre-send readings for B01, B05, B10, H01 and P01. For R05 it chose different but considered `supersedes` unresolved, because the same publisher's subsequent coverage uses more cautious wording and reports a changed retailer listing; it does not explicitly retract the original inference and Nvidia does not confirm or deny discontinuation. Therefore describe R05 as a source-supported improvement lead with this qualification, not as an undisputed false edge or population gain. A second blinded source reader and independent council are still running. `results-codex.md` independently rechecked all 54 attempt IDs and usage and identified the correction to the mistakenly worded preregistered stop statement above.\n27\t\n28\tCouncil follow-up `results-codex-followup.md` inspected P01's additional `45:e3` edge against the full article. The denial article mentions cancellation of the backup design, then says Nvidia rejected “the claims,” but Nvidia's quoted response is broad and does not individually address the backup cancellation. The comparison input itself mentions only delay claims. The additional relation remains disputed; do not count it as either proved right or wrong, and do not treat P01's one-target success as acceptance of the full outcome.\n29\t\n30\tSecond source/output rereader `result-blind-claude.md` agrees on B01, B05, B10, H01 and P01, but for R05 reads the later article by the same publisher as potentially revising its earlier conclusion; it labels the opposite relation unresolved. The first reader chose different while leaving the supersedes readings unresolved. Earlier pre-send blind readers had both called it different. Thus R05 is genuinely disputed, and no case-level improvement may be counted there as established without a decision on the underlying boundary. Readers were blind to which model/arm produced an answer, but the shuffled answer choices appeared after the source in the same packet; the second reader explicitly says it could see them. A fresh third independent reader is examining only the R05 source pair without any outputs. This dispute does not affect the independent B05 regression, on which both source/output readers agree.\n31\t\n32\t`result-blind-claude.md` independently agreed on B01, B05, B10, H01 and P01 but read R05 as a possible later revision by the same original publisher; it explicitly noted that answer choices were visible in the same packet, although their producing arms were concealed. A fresh independent reader given R05 source only (`r05-adjudication.md`) favored different, while acknowledging the later-update interpretation. The disagreement remains open rather than being resolved by a vote. The independent output has only a label, no numeric confidence; three identical labels do not establish high confidence. `results-claude.md` agrees the historic stop rule did not specify B05 and that the result is insufficient for adoption; it favors a source-backed census of the 56 D392 Jev supersedes answers as a future diagnostic. `results-codex.md` favors a narrower saved-input comparison of candidate-context behavior first. Both preserve the experiment line as stopped; neither proposes a case-fitted prompt or immediate production change.\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78ccad56-ed8d-4e29-91b7-f576c6d2916d","uuid":"08cc3616-80d3-4559-adcf-aa97b6ed1640","timestamp":"2026-11-09T21:06:35.618Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/RESULTS-DRAFT.md","content":"# Step 2 same-input paired diagnostic — completed, selected pairs\n\n2026-11-09. User authorized the six-case × three-arm × three-draw experiment. All 54 unique actual HTTP requests completed; no retry, no reported failures or missing usage. The completion marker, immutable request bodies, preflight and fsynced attempt/result events are in `probe-2026-11-09/`. Outputs below are the raw typed labels in draw order. Jev full sees the original whole candidate batch; Jev single and DeepSeek single receive the same pair text and definitions, though their providers and output formats differ. B01 and P01 are controls; P01 is a direct denial also quoted as background in the earlier source, not a clean later-forecast revision. The six source-backed opinions were frozen before sending, and R05's stored source-attribution field is known to be suspect. They are AI source opinions and not frozen gold.\n\n| Pair / source-backed lead | Jev full (3) | Jev single (3) | DeepSeek single (3) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| B01 same: valid Broadcom repeat | same, same, same | same, same, same | same, same, same |\n| B05 different: company 70% guidance vs customer implied doubling | supersedes, supersedes, different | different, different, different | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes |\n| B10 different: Vera Rubin deliveries vs expected revenue share | same, different, different | same, different, same | different, different, different |\n| H01 same: two reports of acquisition | different, different, different | different, different, different | same, same, same |\n| P01 supersedes: official denies reported Kyber delay | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes |\n| R05 different: company does not confirm/deny Shield discontinuation | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes | supersedes, supersedes, supersedes | different, different, different |\n\nRegressions first: B05 DeepSeek selected the wrong relation in 3/3, while fresh Jev single got it right in 3/3. Jev full erred 2/3, showing request-shape sensitivity for this pair. This is a serious repeatable regression against the fresh single-question control, so the council recommends stopping the comparator line. The prewritten stop controls explicitly named B01 and P01, not B05; this is a new finding, not a claimed predeclared stop trigger. Do not add cases, amend prompts or move to product integration to chase an aggregate score. Selected improvement leads H01 and R05: DeepSeek 3/3 same/different against Jev full and single 0/3 correct, and B10 3/3 different against Jev single 1/3 different (Jev full 2/3 different). Both controls B01/P01 remain in 3/3 across arms. One same-day experiment on hand-selected pairs cannot estimate overall precision or recall, show ranking, compare full product behavior or measure source-level independence. DeepSeek model, provider and output format differ; pair comparison is not proof of the cause.\n\nRecorded usage: Jev full 78,795 input/7,112 output tokens; Jev single 15,867 input/773 output; DeepSeek 13,149 prompt (5,504 cached; 7,645 uncached), 8,582 completion including 8,450 reasoning tokens. All report usage. Reservation $0.182012616, not a bill. Using the repository's dated table (Jev input $0.042/M, DeepSeek input $0.30/M, cached input share .10, output $1.20/M), approximate arithmetic cost $0.0167; actual provider charges can differ.\n\nNo original text was dropped or rewritten by this diagnostic. No product code/config/gold changed. Independent blinded source/output rereads are in progress; final council must challenge interpretation and any disputed source and identify one next discriminating action. The competitor produces a 3-label answer, no group score/rank.\n\n## Deterministic existing product-outcome replay ($0)\n\nReplayed each fresh **full-request Jev answer** through unchanged `compare.outcome`, using the original candidate IDs and existing creation order. B05: 3/3 repeat of `14:e2`; the wrong `9:e5` revision appears 2/3. B10: 1/3 repeat of `13:e4`, 2/3 new. H01: 3/3 new, so all three repeat misses have actual Step 2 effect on this fixed history. R05: 3/3 revision of `119:e1`. P01: all three repeat `45:e2` and attach revision edges to **both** `45:e1` (the checked target) and `45:e3`; the additional edge was not adjudicated in this six-pair test. Thus P01's target answer staying correct is not evidence that every relation written on that record is correct. This is deterministic replay on the saved fixed candidate state, not a new forward run. The independent comparator has only pair labels, so no product group choice is claimed for it.\n\n## Independent blind reread (partial)\n\n`result-blind-codex.md` read all six complete source pairs and 54 anonymous labels without arm or draw names. It agreed with the pre-send readings for B01, B05, B10, H01 and P01. For R05 it chose different but considered `supersedes` unresolved, because the same publisher's subsequent coverage uses more cautious wording and reports a changed retailer listing; it does not explicitly retract the original inference and Nvidia does not confirm or deny discontinuation. Therefore describe R05 as a source-supported improvement lead with this qualification, not as an undisputed false edge or population gain. A second blinded source reader and independent council are still running. `results-codex.md` independently rechecked all 54 attempt IDs and usage and identified the correction to the mistakenly worded preregistered stop statement above.\n\nCouncil follow-up `results-codex-followup.md` inspected P01's additional `45:e3` edge against the full article. The denial article mentions cancellation of the backup design, then says Nvidia rejected “the claims,” but Nvidia's quoted response is broad and does not individually address the backup cancellation. The comparison input itself mentions only delay claims. The additional relation remains disputed; do not count it as either proved right or wrong, and do not treat P01's one-target success as acceptance of the full outcome.\n\nSecond source/output rereader `result-blind-claude.md` agrees on B01, B05, B10, H01 and P01, but for R05 reads the later article by the same publisher as potentially revising its earlier conclusion; it labels the opposite relation unresolved. The first reader chose different while leaving the supersedes readings unresolved. Earlier pre-send blind readers had both called it different. Thus R05 is genuinely disputed, and no case-level improvement may be counted there as established without a decision on the underlying boundary. Readers were blind to which model/arm produced an answer, but the shuffled answer choices appeared after the source in the same packet; the second reader explicitly says it could see them. A fresh third independent reader is examining only the R05 source pair without any outputs. This dispute does not affect the independent B05 regression, on which both source/output readers agree.\n\n`result-blind-claude.md` independently agreed on B01, B05, B10, H01 and P01 but read R05 as a possible later revision by the same original publisher; it explicitly noted that answer choices were visible in the same packet, although their producing arms were concealed. A fresh independent reader given R05 source only (`r05-adjudication.md`) favored different, while acknowledging the later-update interpretation. The disagreement remains open rather than being resolved by a vote. The independent output has only a label, no numeric confidence; three identical labels do not establish high confidence. `results-claude.md` agrees the historic stop rule did not specify B05 and that the result is insufficient for adoption; it favors a source-backed census of the 56 D392 Jev supersedes answers as a future diagnostic. `results-codex.md` favors a narrower saved-input comparison of candidate-context behavior first. Both preserve the experiment line as stopped; neither proposes a case-fitted prompt or immediate production change.\n","numLines":33,"startLine":1,"totalLines":33}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014YL8REse9cUB7wSesbi1FQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/claude-independent.jsonl:34:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WQR5rgKnwHPAXfDdSgiNx2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Step 2 identity: council loop (2026-11-09)\n2\t\n3\t## Frame\n4\t\n5\t- Objective: diagnose and find a general, measurable improvement for wrong grouping, missed grouping and incorrect update relations on the clean Step 2 path, without changing frozen ruler files or the adopted configuration.\n6\t- Reference: c8 Step 1 plus current clean Step 2 Jev 1.14.0, the original saved requests and forward decisions. D381's chosen acceptance lines: at least 90% search, at most 2% clear wrong merges, no loss of later details in grouped dossiers. These do not by themselves cover missed grouping, incorrect revision edges, downstream utility, or an independent comparator's quality; no new pass line has been approved.\n7\t- The ruler remains AGENTS §7.1 golden-v5: dev SHA 6f9476bb… and test af2145cd… (`golden-v5.sha256`); for the historical clean Step 2 run its frozen source-backed reference and D381 line are retained with their stated scope. New AI source readers are diagnostic opinions, not replacements for the frozen ruler. Pairwise selected examples are not a benchmark.\n8\t- Scope: Step 2 comparison, candidate context and its actual grouping/revision consequences; unrelated Step 1 changes, downstream redesign, production migration, broader acceptance and frozen-file edits are outside scope. Users' eventual decision is required for a changed product behavior. A paid run beyond a few-cent probe or a full forward acceptance requires Alex's explicit word (AGENTS §9); budget for any proposed probe must be fixed from actual bytes before sending. No new paid diagnostic has been sent.\n9\t\n10\t## Census and trace\n11\t\n12\tSee `INVESTIGATION.md`, `stored-census.md` in the prior report and `claude-r1.md`. Stored outcomes: fresh 1 clear wrong/57 merge decisions plus two overlaps; three dev draws 1 wrong instance each, including a repeated pair, so not independent samples. 84 revision edges over the four stored runs, not a measured error rate. A false revision on 19→9:e5 remains independent of earlier separate groups for the 70% guidance; 30→20 is a wrong revision on an already duplicated announcement. Earlier 20→8 was a missed repeat according to direct source reading. Deliberately chosen blind source cases have disagreement; no performance inference.\n13\t\n14\t## Council A and challenges\n15\t\n16\tPrior independent Claude: `claude-r1.md`; independent new Codex: `codex-independent-v2.md`; recorded own reflection before reading: `ROUND-REFLECTION.md`. Codex first proposed a joint context approach; after the challenge `codex-challenge-v2.txt`, it withdrew that as first test (`codex-challenge-v2.md`) because it mixed changing model, context, output and selection and overlooked the explicit exclusion of mispaired figure fields. Recommends same-input independent comparator alongside fresh Jev full-batch and single-question controls; independently audit typed data before considering extra context. 19's basis text could help but it is not established necessary or sufficient. B08 and B11 remain contested; never use consensus as a replacement ruler.\n17\t\n18\tThe 12-pair selected packet, archive reconstruction, provider requests and correction of request category order and original question IDs are recorded in `INVESTIGATION.md`, `PILOT-DESIGN.md` and request-audit rounds 1–2. They do not constitute a send-ready sender, response validator, acceptance plan or cost authorization. 108-call draft is a discussion artifact, not the authorized first probe. All 188 fresh records were counted: 74 have figures, 72 have at least one basis, with 158 figure objects; this establishes scope, not semantic reliability. Clean PRD §5 and conditions design §14 exclude fields from search and comparison because figures can be attached to the wrong measures.\n19\t\n20\tD392 is complete (main commit 9552c8d); do not claim it is live. Recheck current process state before any send. Further Claude challenge is in progress, and the frozen control/test design and paid-call safety gates remain open.\n21\t\n22\tCurrent process check during council: D392 Stage 2 ended, but a separate q12 c13 paid probe started in the main checkout (`q12_probe.py ... --send`, PID 50314 at observation). Do not send Step 2 calls or run heavy tasks beside it; recheck status later. Do not inspect its live database or alter its work.\n23\t\n24\tDirect check: completed `reader-packet.json` source `gnews:26fd862b0d58999b7451b493` (2026-09-05) describes Nvidia's Thursday $12.9bn announcement; source `gnews:243636e860a787a59c4d0c7a` (2026-09-06) describes its Thursday $13bn announcement, same company and target. This supports the 20→8 missed repeat as a source-backed lead. Neither article independently proves the truth of the acquisition. Fresh record `13:e4` attributes the production statement to Nvidia while the stored quotation states it in the reporter's voice; this limits source-attribution confidence, separately from the clear distinction between the revenue-share forecast and August deliveries. No Step 1 change follows in this scope.\n25\t\n26\tThe previously frozen 12-pair pilot covers the later 30→20 false revision but not the antecedent 20→8 false split. It must not be sent or reported as testing missed repeats; fixing the candidate list changes the frozen diagnostic manifest and requires a new hash and independent source-backed controls before any send. Earlier planned same-input comparator also does not fix retrieval misses or define disagreement handling; its source observations cannot estimate full-forward performance.\n27\tThe direct 20→8 trace was inspected in awareness of the saved Jev outcome; a new source-blind reader must inspect that pair before it is used as an independent positive control. A reader who saw the model decision is not blind.\n28\t\n29\t## New Claude challenge and cross-review\n30\t\n31\t`claude-challenge-v2.md` independently agrees that the prior 12-pair packet omits 20→8 and 14→9, that comparison input excludes figures intentionally, and that revision links need their own review. It retracts its earlier confident B08 revision label after seeing the record's main statement and reader disagreement. Two independent source readers consider B05 different and B06 the same statement. Its suggested 20-pair, five-arm, three-draw plan is a 300-call, roughly $0.65 estimated probe; this exceeds the project's few-cent probe allowance and is not authorized by a request for a council review. Its claims that a second model failing proves an input limitation, that a missed merge alone causes a later false revision, or that an added article publication date establishes statement chronology are not established. Its source-backed useful detail is that the 70% basis is an unverified model-written paraphrase outside the attached passages; the actual comparison already distinguishes management guidance and customer forecasts. Its B08 conclusion remains a source interpretation, not a gold correction. Both reviewers are being challenged on these points, scope and a smaller test (`claude-challenge-v3.txt`, `codex-challenge-v3.txt`).\n32\t\n33\t## Additional blind checks, frozen before any new comparator answer\n34\t\n35\tTwo new source-blind readers (`blind-missed-codex.md`, `blind-missed-claude.md`) independently judge both H01 (20→8, announcement $12.9bn vs $13bn) and H02 (14→9, 70% outlook) to be the same main statement, while documenting uncertainty in reported individual speaker attribution and rounding. These are independent AI opinions, not frozen gold or evidence that the underlying announcement happened.\n36\t\n37\tFor potential revision controls, a fixed seed 2401 selected six from the 56 Step 2 `supersedes` answers in the completed D392 clean d1 draw; the list is frozen in `blind-revisions-key.json`. Both source articles were taken from that draw's saved Step 1 requests, with identity checks. `blind-revisions.json` sha256 bed863cc577bc7b4a80a98d25ec45d7fb57b593f9eec850001158b26486bbdd4. Reviewers see no Jev outputs or selection reason. The selection is conditional on Jev's supersedes answers and is not an estimate of overall Step 2 accuracy; this tiny sample is to find source-backed controls and preserve disputes.\n38\t\n39\tAfter direct challenge, Claude withdrew the claim that two models failing proves the source text is insufficient, withdrew the date-only and unreviewed-figures arms from the first probe, and acknowledged the spending authorization limit. It agreed that preventing an earlier false split does not by itself establish why the later model chose an incorrect revision. It now recommends a same-input Jev full-batch, Jev single-question, independent-model single-question diagnostic, with proper controls and separate interpretation of disputed evidence (`claude-challenge-v3.md`). Both Claude and Codex agree B09 alone is an imperfect positive revision control because the original report already contains the denial; do not present it as satisfying the regression-control gap. The new H01/H02 blind reviews completed after Claude's reply and both independently judged same; the source-backed six-revision audit is still underway. No sender is implemented, no probe is authorized merely by council agreement, and no new model probe has been sent.\n40\t\n41\t## Source-backed revision sample: council B input\n42\t\n43\tTwo independent blind readers received all six fixed-sample pairs. They agree R05 is different (Nvidia explained Shield TV stock shortages without confirming or denying a discontinuation report), R06 is the same statement (two reports of the LangChain release), and R02 is uncertain (a broad platform statement does not clearly address a specific rack delay). They disagreed on R01 (same vs supersedes), R03 (different vs uncertain) and R04 (different vs supersedes). An independent third blinded reader read only the three disputes (`blind-revisions-third.md`): R01 uncertain, R03 different, R04 different. These are AI source opinions, not frozen gold; majority does not remove the recorded disputes. The two unanimous non-revision opinions are leads that the Jev-emitted revision edges were incorrect, subject to source/contract review. The six were randomly drawn conditional on a Jev supersedes choice; several concern the same Kyber story, and a sample of six cannot estimate error rate. The old and new full articles and records are in `blind-revisions.json`; its fixed mapping is in `blind-revisions-key.json`. No clear two-reader valid revision control has been established by this sample; B09 retains the caveat that the denial was already included in the earlier article. No experiment can claim to protect valid revisions until a source-backed control is available.\n44\t\n45\tCodex's source-and-contract challenge (`codex-results-challenge.md`) found R05 a strong wrong-link example, but argues R06 is not equally decisive: an announcement by a related company might also be read as official confirmation, so preserve the ambiguity despite two blind same-statement opinions. It recommends locating a valid revision control before any repair claim and warns that the observed 1/57 wrong-merge rate and targeted missed-repeat cases cannot be compared as population rates. Additional selected possible controls P01/P02 were source-blinded in `blind-positive-revisions.json` (sha256 4fa4fd14003978eea185466ce20b22a78435cc1ed70d2a2640b02945d665f616); these were manually selected as possible controls, not random. One reader finds P01 an official denial of the stored main claim and P02 uncertain; a second blinded reader is in progress. The 20→8 and 14→9 original Jev requests and original answers were independently reconstructed against the completed archive, `missed-pair-requests.json` sha256 09ce47d0220ee29931e053dde957814243c166b231317ca96cf1903f98d20a50. The two candidate control Jev requests also matched the completed D392 archive, `positive-pair-requests.json` sha256 44b0a154fe8ac9e1aa37872fbf9fec5a301189250c577f9ec6b8f95c7ea79f77. This reconstruction does not constitute a source adjudication or send readiness.\n46\t\n47\tP01 (54:e3→45:e1) is now source-blinded by two independent readers: both judge official denial of the Kyber NVL144 delay as `supersedes` (`blind-positive-revisions-codex.md`, `blind-positive-revisions-claude.md`). Both note the original source article already includes the denial, the original response time is unknown, and the old record bundles a separate NVL576 claim not explicitly addressed by the denial. This is a usable but bounded stress control under the current definitions, not an uncontested example of a chronologically later revision or proof that all accompanying statements were denied. P02 remains uncertain/different and is not a positive control. Neither model opinion edits the frozen reference.\n48\t\n49\tIndependent follow-up `codex-positive-review.md` accepts P01 as a bounded control for the current report–official-denial relationship. Its caveats do not prohibit a selected-case diagnostic; they do prohibit claiming protection of later forecast revisions without a separate source-backed chronological control. It does not settle NVL576. No reviewer adjudication is treated as frozen gold.\n50\t\n51\tA provisional six-pair shortlist is generated offline in `shortlist-requests-DRAFT.json` (sha256 6b04ccf0919bef6b5aeac6a480dfdcfa7d51b3dc05c1375a0ac2a2965a934654) by `prepare-shortlist.py`. It comprises B01 (valid repeat), B05 (wrong revision), B10 (wrong merge), H01/H02 (missed repeats, H02 has speaker attribution caveat), and P01 (bounded official-denial control). It is not frozen or ready to send: an independent request review is running, a sender and failure controls do not exist, P01 cannot alone establish later-forecast revision protection, and historical Jev answers are not same-day controls. At the repository's dated rates and conservative input/output reservation formula, one draw's 18 calls reserve about $0.0594, and three draws about $0.1782; these estimates are not guaranteed bills. AGENTS §9 requires Alex's explicit word before paid sends beyond a few cents, and a full forward acceptance also has its own authorization boundary. No paid Step 2 call has been sent in this round.\n52\t\n53\t`shortlist-audit.md` independently checked all six historical full bodies against their archives, candidate IDs and original question IDs, and regenerated all DeepSeek bodies in memory: they match. It confirmed reservations of $0.059389 per draw and $0.178167 for three, at dated rates, not enforced limits. It identified a duplicate-ID precondition gap in the offline preparation script, which was corrected with an exact count check; existing draft bytes were not overwritten. It also confirmed the sender, per-attempt accounting, failure/finish-reason validation, cache separation, frozen source/reader references and authorization boundary are still unbuilt. The shortlist remains a draft and no send is cleared.\n54\t\n55\tR05's Jev answer is `supersedes` 0.90, even though the incoming summary says Nvidia would neither confirm nor deny discontinuation; this is not a low-score-only failure. The historical Jev requests and answers for R05 and disputed R06 were reconstructed byte for byte from the completed D392 archive in `revision-extra-requests.json` (sha256 e0e75db060dafaede976330311f4fe05937c50d3186b0a9254bb35b2ddbfe2a8). The six-pair shortlist predates R05 and should not be frozen as complete; any new shortlist including it must be regenerated, costed and independently audited. Original-party attribution in the stored R05 record is also suspect; the comparator sees that field, so avoid claiming to know which signal caused Jev's answer.\n56\t\n57\tCouncil B discussion retains a real sequencing disagreement: Claude proposes first characterizing Jev full-batch vs single-pair repeatability, whereas Codex proposes including the independent comparator in the first paired diagnostic. Either approach needs fresh controls and a bounded source-backed revision control. A passing rerun does not erase the previously observed defect, and a non-reproduced selected case does not establish that a whole class is random noise. Both reviewers were sent a targeted final challenge; no verdict is inferred before they answer.\n58\t\n59\t## Final challenge: convergence and next experiment\n60\t\n61\t`codex-final-challenge.md` and `claude-final-challenge.md` converge on a paired diagnostic: fresh Jev full batch, fresh Jev single pair, and an independent same-input comparison in the same round. Claude withdrew its proposed Jev-only stop gate: a later passing draw does not undo an already observed bad write, and a few changing outcomes cannot establish class-wide noise. Both retain R06 as disputed because `same_statement` and official confirmation can overlap; it is excluded from a decisive tally. Both accept P01 only as a bounded direct-denial control, not a later-forecast-change control. Group ranking, disagreement handling and actual forward effects remain untested. Repeated sends are needed to claim repeatability; a single draw can find an obvious failure but cannot establish superiority.\n62\t\n63\tAfter R05 was reviewed, the offline shortlist was rebuilt to include it alongside B01, B05, B10, H01 and P01. Current `shortlist-requests-DRAFT.json` SHA-256 cb142acfe3491dcf6ccfaa70545ffe3c011e3646a2348b36954b04ec2f3321ce. Dated-price reservations are $0.060671 for one draw of 18 calls and $0.182013 for three draws of 54 calls, excluding uncertain billing, retries and differing provider rates. This supersedes the earlier six-case draft, which contained H02 instead of R05. The updated shortlist is undergoing independent request audit; it is not a frozen manifest, complete sender, approval or a result. Historical source and response files remain immutable. The q12 c13 process previously observed no longer appeared in a later process check, but run status must be rechecked before any new sends. The next actual work is finish no-cost input/control/sender and independent reviews; preserve user authority for sends beyond the few-cent boundary and for any change to production behavior.\n64\t\n65\t`shortlist-audit-r2.md` independently verified the revised six historical archive bodies and original answers, all 18 prepared provider bodies and hashes, unique-case precondition, choice order and reservation calculation. It confirms source packets and blind-reader judgments are not pinned, the response validator and sender are unbuilt, and the set cannot measure overall error rates, retrieval or group ranking. Review of offline request construction does not clear a paid send or constitute an experimental result.\n66\t\n67\t## Paired diagnostic sent and stopped on a regression\n68\t\n69\tAlex said “开始” after the six-case, three-arm, three-draw scope and estimated spend were explained. The isolated sender passed design review r4, 18 fake tests, ten targeted mutation checks, a final implementation review r5 and the clean Jev archived-response/identity checks. Preflight for frozen v108 reproduced 243 extraction answers, 726 judged/12 dropped, 170 matching judgments with two skipped, and two earlier schema-refused rows; it does not itself exercise clean Step 2. The 54 actual requests completed without retry or reported failure, with the full request bodies, per-attempt events and completion marker in `probe-2026-11-09/`. The result table and limitations are `RESULTS-DRAFT.md`.\n70\t\n71\tThe council recommends rejecting this candidate because of a serious new regression: on B05 the independent model called `supersedes` 3/3 while Jev single called `different` 3/3 (fresh Jev full called `supersedes` 2/3). Both prior blind source readers judged B05 different. Controls B01 and P01 were preserved in all 3 draws of each arm. H01 and R05 changed in a source-supported direction 3/3 for independent versus 0/3 for both Jev arms; B10 differs by Jev request shape and draw. These are selected-case observations, not system precision or recall. Product change is not authorized or justified; stop this line, do not add cases or fit a follow-up prompt. Each result is being re-read independently and source-blind, with all 54 answers shuffled in `result-blind-packet.json`; council B reviews independently without sending extra experimental requests.\n72\t\n73\tCorrection after Council B: the preregistered explicit stop controls named B01 and P01, not B05. The B05 3/3 vs 0/3 regression is a new serious finding and reason to stop, not an automatic predeclared stop trigger. Full raw evidence and the disagreement between fresh full versus single Jev remain disclosed in RESULTS-DRAFT.md.\n74\t\n75\tFinal council B complete: `results-codex.md`, `results-claude.md` and `results-codex-followup.md`. Both agree 54 calls completed, the comparator cannot be adopted, B05's source-backed regression is important, and it did **not** trip an explicitly predeclared B01/P01 stop condition. In 3/3 H01 the independent pair judgment matches the original announcement where full/single Jev miss it; in B05 the independent comparator answers supersedes 3/3 while Jev-single answers different 3/3 and Jev-full answers different once. R05's outcome is disputed: both initial source-only readers said different, but an arm-blind reader chose supersedes because the original publisher's later story is more cautious; a fresh output-blind source adjudicator chose different while acknowledging the other interpretation. This remains a dispute, not a majority-vote gold. P01's additional full-Jev revision edge is also disputed. The judges of the 54 shuffled answers were blind to arms but the choice list appeared in the same packet as the source; one explicitly acknowledged seeing it, so do not describe the reading as entirely output-blind. Under the stopped-candidate rule no follow-up tuning or paid send is initiated. A complete source-backed census of the 56 D392 d1 supersedes answers is a possible next no-cost research angle, not a measured conclusion or a requested new benchmark. Per the user-authorized test, no production changes and no new reference are adopted.\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78ccad56-ed8d-4e29-91b7-f576c6d2916d","uuid":"095f23b8-a0a7-48c7-9e1b-3c7eb0aaeefc","timestamp":"2026-11-09T21:06:36.361Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind-step2-identity-2026-11-09/clean-arch/step2-identity-2026-11-09/COUNCIL-LOOP.md","content":"# Step 2 identity: council loop (2026-11-09)\n\n## Frame\n\n- Objective: diagnose and find a general, measurable improvement for wrong grouping, missed grouping and incorrect update relations on the clean Step 2 path, without changing frozen ruler files or the adopted configuration.\n- Reference: c8 Step 1 plus current clean Step 2 Jev 1.14.0, the original saved requests and forward decisions. D381's chosen acceptance lines: at least 90% search, at most 2% clear wrong merges, no loss of later details in grouped dossiers. These do not by themselves cover missed grouping, incorrect revision edges, downstream utility, or an independent comparator's quality; no new pass line has been approved.\n- The ruler remains AGENTS §7.1 golden-v5: dev SHA 6f9476bb… and test af2145cd… (`golden-v5.sha256`); for the historical clean Step 2 run its frozen source-backed reference and D381 line are retained with their stated scope. New AI source readers are diagnostic opinions, not replacements for the frozen ruler. Pairwise selected examples are not a benchmark.\n- Scope: Step 2 comparison, candidate context and its actual grouping/revision consequences; unrelated Step 1 changes, downstream redesign, production migration, broader acceptance and frozen-file edits are outside scope. Users' eventual decision is required for a changed product behavior. A paid run beyond a few-cent probe or a full forward acceptance requires Alex's explicit word (AGENTS §9); budget for any proposed probe must be fixed from actual bytes before sending. No new paid diagnostic has been sent.\n\n## Census and trace\n\nSee `INVESTIGATION.md`, `stored-census.md` in the prior report and `claude-r1.md`. Stored outcomes: fresh 1 clear wrong/57 merge decisions plus two overlaps; three dev draws 1 wrong instance each, including a repeated pair, so not independent samples. 84 revision edges over the four stored runs, not a measured error rate. A false revision on 19→9:e5 remains independent of earlier separate groups for the 70% guidance; 30→20 is a wrong revision on an already duplicated announcement. Earlier 20→8 was a missed repeat according to direct source reading. Deliberately chosen blind source cases have disagreement; no performance inference.\n\n## Council A and challenges\n\nPrior independent Claude: `claude-r1.md`; independent new Codex: `codex-independent-v2.md`; recorded own reflection before reading: `ROUND-REFLECTION.md`. Codex first proposed a joint context approach; after the challenge `codex-challenge-v2.txt`, it withdrew that as first test (`codex-challenge-v2.md`) because it mixed changing model, context, output and selection and overlooked the explicit exclusion of mispaired figure fields. Recommends same-input independent comparator alongside fresh Jev full-batch and single-question controls; independently audit typed data before considering extra context. 19's basis text could help but it is not established necessary or sufficient. B08 and B11 remain contested; never use consensus as a replacement ruler.\n\nThe 12-pair selected packet, archive reconstruction, provider requests and correction of request category order and original question IDs are recorded in `INVESTIGATION.md`, `PILOT-DESIGN.md` and request-audit rounds 1–2. They do not constitute a send-ready sender, response validator, acceptance plan or cost authorization. 108-call draft is a discussion artifact, not the authorized first probe. All 188 fresh records were counted: 74 have figures, 72 have at least one basis, with 158 figure objects; this establishes scope, not semantic reliability. Clean PRD §5 and conditions design §14 exclude fields from search and comparison because figures can be attached to the wrong measures.\n\nD392 is complete (main commit 9552c8d); do not claim it is live. Recheck current process state before any send. Further Claude challenge is in progress, and the frozen control/test design and paid-call safety gates remain open.\n\nCurrent process check during council: D392 Stage 2 ended, but a separate q12 c13 paid probe started in the main checkout (`q12_probe.py ... --send`, PID 50314 at observation). Do not send Step 2 calls or run heavy tasks beside it; recheck status later. Do not inspect its live database or alter its work.\n\nDirect check: completed `reader-packet.json` source `gnews:26fd862b0d58999b7451b493` (2026-09-05) describes Nvidia's Thursday $12.9bn announcement; source `gnews:243636e860a787a59c4d0c7a` (2026-09-06) describes its Thursday $13bn announcement, same company and target. This supports the 20→8 missed repeat as a source-backed lead. Neither article independently proves the truth of the acquisition. Fresh record `13:e4` attributes the production statement to Nvidia while the stored quotation states it in the reporter's voice; this limits source-attribution confidence, separately from the clear distinction between the revenue-share forecast and August deliveries. No Step 1 change follows in this scope.\n\nThe previously frozen 12-pair pilot covers the later 30→20 false revision but not the antecedent 20→8 false split. It must not be sent or reported as testing missed repeats; fixing the candidate list changes the frozen diagnostic manifest and requires a new hash and independent source-backed controls before any send. Earlier planned same-input comparator also does not fix retrieval misses or define disagreement handling; its source observations cannot estimate full-forward performance.\nThe direct 20→8 trace was inspected in awareness of the saved Jev outcome; a new source-blind reader must inspect that pair before it is used as an independent positive control. A reader who saw the model decision is not blind.\n\n## New Claude challenge and cross-review\n\n`claude-challenge-v2.md` independently agrees that the prior 12-pair packet omits 20→8 and 14→9, that comparison input excludes figures intentionally, and that revision links need their own review. It retracts its earlier confident B08 revision label after seeing the record's main statement and reader disagreement. Two independent source readers consider B05 different and B06 the same statement. Its suggested 20-pair, five-arm, three-draw plan is a 300-call, roughly $0.65 estimated probe; this exceeds the project's few-cent probe allowance and is not authorized by a request for a council review. Its claims that a second model failing proves an input limitation, that a missed merge alone causes a later false revision, or that an added article publication date establishes statement chronology are not established. Its source-backed useful detail is that the 70% basis is an unverified model-written paraphrase outside the attached passages; the actual comparison already distinguishes management guidance and customer forecasts. Its B08 conclusion remains a source interpretation, not a gold correction. Both reviewers are being challenged on these points, scope and a smaller test (`claude-challenge-v3.txt`, `codex-challenge-v3.txt`).\n\n## Additional blind checks, frozen before any new comparator answer\n\nTwo new source-blind readers (`blind-missed-codex.md`, `blind-missed-claude.md`) independently judge both H01 (20→8, announcement $12.9bn vs $13bn) and H02 (14→9, 70% outlook) to be the same main statement, while documenting uncertainty in reported individual speaker attribution and rounding. These are independent AI opinions, not frozen gold or evidence that the underlying announcement happened.\n\nFor potential revision controls, a fixed seed 2401 selected six from the 56 Step 2 `supersedes` answers in the completed D392 clean d1 draw; the list is frozen in `blind-revisions-key.json`. Both source articles were taken from that draw's saved Step 1 requests, with identity checks. `blind-revisions.json` sha256 bed863cc577bc7b4a80a98d25ec45d7fb57b593f9eec850001158b26486bbdd4. Reviewers see no Jev outputs or selection reason. The selection is conditional on Jev's supersedes answers and is not an estimate of overall Step 2 accuracy; this tiny sample is to find source-backed controls and preserve disputes.\n\nAfter direct challenge, Claude withdrew the claim that two models failing proves the source text is insufficient, withdrew the date-only and unreviewed-figures arms from the first probe, and acknowledged the spending authorization limit. It agreed that preventing an earlier false split does not by itself establish why the later model chose an incorrect revision. It now recommends a same-input Jev full-batch, Jev single-question, independent-model single-question diagnostic, with proper controls and separate interpretation of disputed evidence (`claude-challenge-v3.md`). Both Claude and Codex agree B09 alone is an imperfect positive revision control because the original report already contains the denial; do not present it as satisfying the regression-control gap. The new H01/H02 blind reviews completed after Claude's reply and both independently judged same; the source-backed six-revision audit is still underway. No sender is implemented, no probe is authorized merely by council agreement, and no new model probe has been sent.\n\n## Source-backed revision sample: council B input\n\nTwo independent blind readers received all six fixed-sample pairs. They agree R05 is different (Nvidia explained Shield TV stock shortages without confirming or denying a discontinuation report), R06 is the same statement (two reports of the LangChain release), and R02 is uncertain (a broad platform statement does not clearly address a specific rack delay). They disagreed on R01 (same vs supersedes), R03 (different vs uncertain) and R04 (different vs supersedes). An independent third blinded reader read only the three disputes (`blind-revisions-third.md`): R01 uncertain, R03 different, R04 different. These are AI source opinions, not frozen gold; majority does not remove the recorded disputes. The two unanimous non-revision opinions are leads that the Jev-emitted revision edges were incorrect, subject to source/contract review. The six were randomly drawn conditional on a Jev supersedes choice; several concern the same Kyber story, and a sample of six cannot estimate error rate. The old and new full articles and records are in `blind-revisions.json`; its fixed mapping is in `blind-revisions-key.json`. No clear two-reader valid revision control has been established by this sample; B09 retains the caveat that the denial was already included in the earlier article. No experiment can claim to protect valid revisions until a source-backed control is available.\n\nCodex's source-and-contract challenge (`codex-results-challenge.md`) found R05 a strong wrong-link example, but argues R06 is not equally decisive: an announcement by a related company might also be read as official confirmation, so preserve the ambiguity despite two blind same-statement opinions. It recommends locating a valid revision control before any repair claim and warns that the observed 1/57 wrong-merge rate and targeted missed-repeat cases cannot be compared as population rates. Additional selected possible controls P01/P02 were source-blinded in `blind-positive-revisions.json` (sha256 4fa4fd14003978eea185466ce20b22a78435cc1ed70d2a2640b02945d665f616); these were manually selected as possible controls, not random. One reader finds P01 an official denial of the stored main claim and P02 uncertain; a second blinded reader is in progress. The 20→8 and 14→9 original Jev requests and original answers were independently reconstructed against the completed archive, `missed-pair-requests.json` sha256 09ce47d0220ee29931e053dde957814243c166b231317ca96cf1903f98d20a50. The two candidate control Jev requests also matched the completed D392 archive, `positive-pair-requests.json` sha256 44b0a154fe8ac9e1aa37872fbf9fec5a301189250c577f9ec6b8f95c7ea79f77. This reconstruction does not constitute a source adjudication or send readiness.\n\nP01 (54:e3→45:e1) is now source-blinded by two independent readers: both judge official denial of the Kyber NVL144 delay as `supersedes` (`blind-positive-revisions-codex.md`, `blind-positive-revisions-claude.md`). Both note the original source article already includes the denial, the original response time is unknown, and the old record bundles a separate NVL576 claim not explicitly addressed by the denial. This is a usable but bounded stress control under the current definitions, not an uncontested example of a chronologically later revision or proof that all accompanying statements were denied. P02 remains uncertain/different and is not a positive control. Neither model opinion edits the frozen reference.\n\nIndependent follow-up `codex-positive-review.md` accepts P01 as a bounded control for the current report–official-denial relationship. Its caveats do not prohibit a selected-case diagnostic; they do prohibit claiming protection of later forecast revisions without a separate source-backed chronological control. It does not settle NVL576. No reviewer adjudication is treated as frozen gold.\n\nA provisional six-pair shortlist is generated offline in `shortlist-requests-DRAFT.json` (sha256 6b04ccf0919bef6b5aeac6a480dfdcfa7d51b3dc05c1375a0ac2a2965a934654) by `prepare-shortlist.py`. It comprises B01 (valid repeat), B05 (wrong revision), B10 (wrong merge), H01/H02 (missed repeats, H02 has speaker attribution caveat), and P01 (bounded official-denial control). It is not frozen or ready to send: an independent request review is running, a sender and failure controls do not exist, P01 cannot alone establish later-forecast revision protection, and historical Jev answers are not same-day controls. At the repository's dated rates and conservative input/output reservation formula, one draw's 18 calls reserve about $0.0594, and three draws about $0.1782; these estimates are not guaranteed bills. AGENTS §9 requires Alex's explicit word before paid sends beyond a few cents, and a full forward acceptance also has its own authorization boundary. No paid Step 2 call has been sent in this round.\n\n`shortlist-audit.md` independently checked all six historical full bodies against their archives, candidate IDs and original question IDs, and regenerated all DeepSeek bodies in memory: they match. It confirmed reservations of $0.059389 per draw and $0.178167 for three, at dated rates, not enforced limits. It identified a duplicate-ID precondition gap in the offline preparation script, which was corrected with an exact count check; existing draft bytes were not overwritten. It also confirmed the sender, per-attempt accounting, failure/finish-reason validation, cache separation, frozen source/reader references and authorization boundary are still unbuilt. The shortlist remains a draft and no send is cleared.\n\nR05's Jev answer is `supersedes` 0.90, even though the incoming summary says Nvidia would neither confirm nor deny discontinuation; this is not a low-score-only failure. The historical Jev requests and answers for R05 and disputed R06 were reconstructed byte for byte from the completed D392 archive in `revision-extra-requests.json` (sha256 e0e75db060dafaede976330311f4fe05937c50d3186b0a9254bb35b2ddbfe2a8). The six-pair shortlist predates R05 and should not be frozen as complete; any new shortlist including it must be regenerated, costed and independently audited. Original-party attribution in the stored R05 record is also suspect; the comparator sees that field, so avoid claiming to know which signal caused Jev's answer.\n\nCouncil B discussion retains a real sequencing disagreement: Claude proposes first characterizing Jev full-batch vs single-pair repeatability, whereas Codex proposes including the independent comparator in the first paired diagnostic. Either approach needs fresh controls and a bounded source-backed revision control. A passing rerun does not erase the previously observed defect, and a non-reproduced selected case does not establish that a whole class is random noise. Both reviewers were sent a targeted final challenge; no verdict is inferred before they answer.\n\n## Final challenge: convergence and next experiment\n\n`codex-final-challenge.md` and `claude-final-challenge.md` converge on a paired diagnostic: fresh Jev full batch, fresh Jev single pair, and an independent same-input comparison in the same round. Claude withdrew its proposed Jev-only stop gate: a later passing draw does not undo an already observed bad write, and a few changing outcomes cannot establish class-wide noise. Both retain R06 as disputed because `same_statement` and official confirmation can overlap; it is excluded from a decisive tally. Both accept P01 only as a bounded direct-denial control, not a later-forecast-change control. Group ranking, disagreement handling and actual forward effects remain untested. Repeated sends are needed to claim repeatability; a single draw can find an obvious failure but cannot establish superiority.\n\nAfter R05 was reviewed, the offline shortlist was rebuilt to include it alongside B01, B05, B10, H01 and P01. Current `shortlist-requests-DRAFT.json` SHA-256 cb142acfe3491dcf6ccfaa70545ffe3c011e3646a2348b36954b04ec2f3321ce. Dated-price reservations are $0.060671 for one draw of 18 calls and $0.182013 for three draws of 54 calls, excluding uncertain billing, retries and differing provider rates. This supersedes the earlier six-case draft, which contained H02 instead of R05. The updated shortlist is undergoing independent request audit; it is not a frozen manifest, complete sender, approval or a result. Historical source and response files remain immutable. The q12 c13 process previously observed no longer appeared in a later process check, but run status must be rechecked before any new sends. The next actual work is finish no-cost input/control/sender and independent reviews; preserve user authority for sends beyond the few-cent boundary and for any change to production behavior.\n\n`shortlist-audit-r2.md` independently verified the revised six historical archive bodies and original answers, all 18 prepared provider bodies and hashes, unique-case precondition, choice order and reservation calculation. It confirms source packets and blind-reader judgments are not pinned, the response validator and sender are unbuilt, and the set cannot measure overall error rates, retrieval or group ranking. Review of offline request construction does not clear a paid send or constitute an experimental result.\n\n## Paired diagnostic sent and stopped on a regression\n\nAlex said “开始” after the six-case, three-arm, three-draw scope and estimated spend were explained. The isolated sender passed design review r4, 18 fake tests, ten targeted mutation checks, a final implementation review r5 and the clean Jev archived-response/identity checks. Preflight for frozen v108 reproduced 243 extraction answers, 726 judged/12 dropped, 170 matching judgments with two skipped, and two earlier schema-refused rows; it does not itself exercise clean Step 2. The 54 actual requests completed without retry or reported failure, with the full request bodies, per-attempt events and completion marker in `probe-2026-11-09/`. The result table and limitations are `RESULTS-DRAFT.md`.\n\nThe council recommends rejecting this candidate because of a serious new regression: on B05 the independent model called `supersedes` 3/3 while Jev single called `different` 3/3 (fresh Jev full called `supersedes` 2/3). Both prior blind source readers judged B05 different. Controls B01 and P01 were preserved in all 3 draws of each arm. H01 and R05 changed in a source-supported direction 3/3 for independent versus 0/3 for both Jev arms; B10 differs by Jev request shape and draw. These are selected-case observations, not system precision or recall. Product change is not authorized or justified; stop this line, do not add cases or fit a follow-up prompt. Each result is being re-read independently and source-blind, with all 54 answers shuffled in `result-blind-packet.json`; council B reviews independently without sending extra experimental requests.\n\nCorrection after Council B: the preregistered explicit stop controls named B01 and P01, not B05. The B05 3/3 vs 0/3 regression is a new serious finding and reason to stop, not an automatic predeclared stop trigger. Full raw evidence and the disagreement between fresh full versus single Jev remain disclosed in RESULTS-DRAFT.md.\n\nFinal council B complete: `results-codex.md`, `results-claude.md` and `results-codex-followup.md`. Both agree 54 calls completed, the comparator cannot be adopted, B05's source-backed regression is important, and it did **not** trip an explicitly predeclared B01/P01 stop condition. In 3/3 H01 the independent pair judgment matches the original announcement where full/single Jev miss it; in B05 the independent comparator answers supersedes 3/3 while Jev-single answers different 3/3 and Jev-full answers different once. R05's outcome is disputed: both initial source-only readers said different, but an arm-blind reader chose supersedes because the original publisher's later story is more cautious; a fresh output-blind source adjudicator chose different while acknowledging the other interpretation. This remains a dispute, not a majority-vote gold. P01's additional full-Jev revision edge is also disputed. The judges of the 54 shuffled answers were blind to arms but the choice list appeared in the same packet as the source; one explicitly acknowledged seeing it, so do not describe the reading as entirely output-blind. Under the stopped-candidate rule no follow-up tuning or paid send is initiated. A complete source-backed census of the 56 D392 d1 supersedes answers is a possible next no-cost research angle, not a measured conclusion or a requested new benchmark. Per the user-authorized test, no production changes and no new reference are adopted.\n","numLines":76,"startLine":1,"totalLines":76}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WQR5rgKnwHPAXfDdSgiNx2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/claude-independent.jsonl:36:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015p8yZJYHsEGwfUCZv8qk5B","type":"tool_result","content":"4101\t## D381 · 2026-11-08 · Clean Step 2: a repeat joins only the group it is most like; three acceptance lines\n4102\t\n4103\t- Alex, on a record that repeats several groups: \"只放进最像的那组\". On the lines he chose \"三条线\": search at least 90%; clear wrong merges\n4104\t  at most 2%; grouped dossiers keep no fewer later details than no merging. Grouping option A (\"A 继续\"): each repeat keeps its own text\n4105\t  and source in the group. Record: `clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md` §§9–12; `clean-arch/PRD.md` §§5–6.\n4106\t\n4107\t## D382 · 2026-11-08 · Financial figures are conditions, not theses; a thesis is a view with a reason\n4108\t\n4109\t- Alex: \"我觉得财务类型不应该是thesis，而更多的是条件。观点应该是独特的view，而不是喊出来的数字，除非有理由 - 这个理由恰恰才是观点\". On the\n4110\t  recommendation to keep a useful company figure no view takes as a condition of the company, he replied \"好\". Record:\n4111\t  `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §§16–17.\n4112\t\n4113\t## D383 · 2026-11-08 · D209 amended again: a material statement no thesis takes is kept unlinked and linked later\n4114\t\n4115\t- Alex chose \"先存着，有观点再挂\": a statement the model judges useful but no thesis takes yet is kept in the company's record, linked when\n4116\t  a related thesis opens; what the closing check rejects is still dropped, including the cash flow he ruled out. Amends D209 beyond D382.\n4117\t  Record: `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §27.\n4118\t\n4119\t## D384 · 2026-11-08 · Clean Step 1 opens only the big matters, by the signed standard as the frozen gold applies it\n4120\t\n4121\t- Alex chose \"按金标：只开大事\": a thesis opens only for the standard's reasons (an obligation the company takes on, its main product's\n4122\t  next node, a policy step, a checkable shared quantity); another company's single project, partnership or product line is evidence.\n4123\t  The reader-based small/fragment line gives way to must_open covered and must_not not opened. Class A (main-driver quantity) stays\n4124\t  out under D382. Record: `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §36.\n4125\t\n4126\t## D385 · 2026-11-08 · Target prices and valuation conclusions are not kept\n4127\t\n4128\t- Alex, 2026-11-08: \"留 EPS 预测，丢目标价\" (LOG §11), then, asked whether it overrides the signed standard's \"孤零零的目标价可选；带推导的\n4129\t  条件估值仍是辅助\" (D243 #4), chose \"目标价和估值结论都不留\": a target price or valuation conclusion anyone draws is not kept; the\n4130\t  estimates it rests on are kept on their own. The frozen standard page and golden-v5 are not edited (D261, D262); the two valuation\n4131\t  entries golden-v5 marks supporting are disclosed as a known conflict with every score (D373). Record: LOG §39.\n4132\t\n4133\t## D386 · 2026-11-08 · Acceptance on fresh articles read by readers against the source\n4134\t\n4135\t- Alex chose \"新文章 + 读者对照原文判\": the golden-v5 test set was used across five configurations on 2026-11-08 and is no longer a clean\n4136\t  held-out set. After the final configuration is frozen (a complete review of the request first), acceptance runs on NVDA articles no\n4137\t  run has used, judged by readers who did not write the change, against the source; dev is repeated for stability. No new gold is\n4138\t  built. Record: LOG §39.\n4139\t- The wrongly opened thesis line for this acceptance is Alex's answer of 2026-11-08 (AskUserQuestion): \"50 篇里最多 2 个\" — at\n4140\t  most 2 wrongly opened theses in 50 fresh articles. The protocol is `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/ACCEPTANCE-PROTOCOL.md`.\n4141\t\n4142\t## D387 · 2026-11-09 · Clean Step 1 adopts c8; the PRD carries its system prompt, closing check and schema\n4143\t\n4144\t- Alex chose \"采用 c8 (Recommended)\": c8 becomes the clean Step 1 configuration, frozen. It runs on DeepSeek Flash at high effort with\n4145\t  a 64,000-token output cap; a format failure or a cut answer is resent once (D380).\n4146\t- It was accepted on 50 fresh, unused articles (2026-09-05 to 09-07, one draw), with every line met under the agreed definitions:\n4147\t  - helpful 132/139;\n4148\t  - wrongly opened theses 1/10;\n4149\t  - search 141/143;\n4150\t  - clear wrong merges 1/57;\n4151\t  - detail retention 22 = 22.\n4152\t\n4153\t  On dev ×3: helpful 214, 211 and 212 of 234. Record: `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §§40–44 and\n4154\t  `ACCEPTANCE-PROTOCOL.md`.\n4155\t- **What was done:**\n4156\t  - PRD §12 carries c8's system prompt, its closing check and its schema;\n4157\t  - a run with no candidate files reads all three from the PRD, and the closing check ends the user message;\n4158\t  - any later change is a new version measured against c8.\n4159\t- On link clutter (about one link in five weak), Alex chose \"先花几分钱查原因 (Recommended)\": the model's own account comes first,\n4160\t  then the council, before any design."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78ccad56-ed8d-4e29-91b7-f576c6d2916d","uuid":"da569ebc-2b71-4fbd-9d47-f75be32be018","timestamp":"2026-11-09T21:06:37.100Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md","content":"## D381 · 2026-11-08 · Clean Step 2: a repeat joins only the group it is most like; three acceptance lines\n\n- Alex, on a record that repeats several groups: \"只放进最像的那组\". On the lines he chose \"三条线\": search at least 90%; clear wrong merges\n  at most 2%; grouped dossiers keep no fewer later details than no merging. Grouping option A (\"A 继续\"): each repeat keeps its own text\n  and source in the group. Record: `clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md` §§9–12; `clean-arch/PRD.md` §§5–6.\n\n## D382 · 2026-11-08 · Financial figures are conditions, not theses; a thesis is a view with a reason\n\n- Alex: \"我觉得财务类型不应该是thesis，而更多的是条件。观点应该是独特的view，而不是喊出来的数字，除非有理由 - 这个理由恰恰才是观点\". On the\n  recommendation to keep a useful company figure no view takes as a condition of the company, he replied \"好\". Record:\n  `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §§16–17.\n\n## D383 · 2026-11-08 · D209 amended again: a material statement no thesis takes is kept unlinked and linked later\n\n- Alex chose \"先存着，有观点再挂\": a statement the model judges useful but no thesis takes yet is kept in the company's record, linked when\n  a related thesis opens; what the closing check rejects is still dropped, including the cash flow he ruled out. Amends D209 beyond D382.\n  Record: `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §27.\n\n## D384 · 2026-11-08 · Clean Step 1 opens only the big matters, by the signed standard as the frozen gold applies it\n\n- Alex chose \"按金标：只开大事\": a thesis opens only for the standard's reasons (an obligation the company takes on, its main product's\n  next node, a policy step, a checkable shared quantity); another company's single project, partnership or product line is evidence.\n  The reader-based small/fragment line gives way to must_open covered and must_not not opened. Class A (main-driver quantity) stays\n  out under D382. Record: `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §36.\n\n## D385 · 2026-11-08 · Target prices and valuation conclusions are not kept\n\n- Alex, 2026-11-08: \"留 EPS 预测，丢目标价\" (LOG §11), then, asked whether it overrides the signed standard's \"孤零零的目标价可选；带推导的\n  条件估值仍是辅助\" (D243 #4), chose \"目标价和估值结论都不留\": a target price or valuation conclusion anyone draws is not kept; the\n  estimates it rests on are kept on their own. The frozen standard page and golden-v5 are not edited (D261, D262); the two valuation\n  entries golden-v5 marks supporting are disclosed as a known conflict with every score (D373). Record: LOG §39.\n\n## D386 · 2026-11-08 · Acceptance on fresh articles read by readers against the source\n\n- Alex chose \"新文章 + 读者对照原文判\": the golden-v5 test set was used across five configurations on 2026-11-08 and is no longer a clean\n  held-out set. After the final configuration is frozen (a complete review of the request first), acceptance runs on NVDA articles no\n  run has used, judged by readers who did not write the change, against the source; dev is repeated for stability. No new gold is\n  built. Record: LOG §39.\n- The wrongly opened thesis line for this acceptance is Alex's answer of 2026-11-08 (AskUserQuestion): \"50 篇里最多 2 个\" — at\n  most 2 wrongly opened theses in 50 fresh articles. The protocol is `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/ACCEPTANCE-PROTOCOL.md`.\n\n## D387 · 2026-11-09 · Clean Step 1 adopts c8; the PRD carries its system prompt, closing check and schema\n\n- Alex chose \"采用 c8 (Recommended)\": c8 becomes the clean Step 1 configuration, frozen. It runs on DeepSeek Flash at high effort with\n  a 64,000-token output cap; a format failure or a cut answer is resent once (D380).\n- It was accepted on 50 fresh, unused articles (2026-09-05 to 09-07, one draw), with every line met under the agreed definitions:\n  - helpful 132/139;\n  - wrongly opened theses 1/10;\n  - search 141/143;\n  - clear wrong merges 1/57;\n  - detail retention 22 = 22.\n\n  On dev ×3: helpful 214, 211 and 212 of 234. Record: `clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md` §§40–44 and\n  `ACCEPTANCE-PROTOCOL.md`.\n- **What was done:**\n  - PRD §12 carries c8's system prompt, its closing check and its schema;\n  - a run with no candidate files reads all three from the PRD, and the closing check ends the user message;\n  - any later change is a new version measured against c8.\n- On link clutter (about one link in five weak), Alex chose \"先花几分钱查原因 (Recommended)\": the model's own account comes first,\n  then the council, before any design.","numLines":60,"startLine":4101,"totalLines":4365}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015p8yZJYHsEGwfUCZv8qk5B","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/claude-independent.jsonl:38:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D7BhY4BGyrKPevgSv8Pxj2","type":"tool_result","content":"2338\t## D168 · 2026-10-25 · Four corrections found by the D167 measurement: one repeat rule, one replace definition, one succession rule, one missing answer\n2339\t\n2340\tFound while measuring the two D167 arms (log rows 44–48); each is general, follows the PRD, and was measured on stored data or by re-sending stored requests before it entered the code.\n2341\t- **A repeat may name the piece shown for the same evidence under another of its theses** (`matching.check`). 06-views step 6: a repeat adds a source and any new thesis link; the writer already resolves a piece to one stored identity for all its theses. The check required the target under the destination thesis and failed an item twice (9 of 17 such refusals over four ledgers were this case). A replacement still stays within its thesis.\n2342\t- **The Jev replace criterion carries the PRD's full definition**: a later version of the same statement or number, changed (the same speaker revises it, an official confirms or denies that claim, the original source revises it); another statement on the same topic is not this (06-views lines 191, 197, 306). Probed against a fresh control, three samples: wrong replacements 10 of 10 removed on the seed and week, 6 of 7 out of sample on August; the right ones that stay are the guidance revisions and a source revising its own figure. A second wording ('the same party') was rejected by its pre-registered rule.\n2343\t- **The writer's replacement rule, generalised from the PRD**: an incoming third-party view or rumour replaces only the same party's earlier piece (subsumes the view-over-view rule of D167). On every stored ledger it refuses 6 written replacements, all a commentator's view over another party's rumour, none right.\n2344\t- **Succession**: the code's rule uses the shared typed-field rule (`identity.conflicts`: 'n/a' matches any basis or unit), so an unstated basis no longer blocks a succession (adds exactly 2 over all ledgers, both right); and the Jev relation question gains the answer 'the same defined quantity or event series for another period', with the direction, the typed fields and the successor check in code, so a succession between differently worded model scopes can be judged (probe: recovers the one such case, changes nothing gold-labelled for the worse).\n2345\t\n2346\t## D169 · 2026-10-25 · Jev is the default matching engine\n2347\t\n2348\t- Asked \"要不要把 Jev 定为默认的匹配引擎\" after the D167/D168 measurement (log rows 44–49), Alex answered: \"要的。你的抽取，trace和当前抽好的db都很宝贵，这些都留下来然后开始做这个阶段的收尾\".\n2349\t- Decided: `poc-v23` (extraction v10, `matching.engine: \"jev\"`, model `jev-1.14.0`, with D168) is the default configuration of the replay (`scripts/replay.py`, which now also requires the ledger and archive it writes). The service's idea route keeps its adopted configuration `poc-v9` (D135/D145) on the sealed ledger and its search index; moving the service to the English library needs a new index and is a separate step. The generative matcher stays a configured alternative (`engine: \"llm\"`); old configurations and stored archives replay unchanged.\n2350\t- The phase's extractions, traces and ledgers are kept (data manifest `docs/research/2026-10-25-english/data-manifest.md`, two backups outside the repository); the close-out is a code review for stale code and bloat, then the documents, then a push.\n2351\t- Not decided here: moving group membership from extraction to matching (D149/D166), which the queued day-batch shape needs; it comes with that task.\n2352\t\n2353\t## D170 · 2026-10-25 · Phase close-out: what the code review removed, fixed and left\n2354\t\n2355\tAlex: \"我们跑一个code review 清理一下过程中stale code 和 bloat 然后准备写好文档push\". Two read-only Codex reviews (product code with its tests; tooling), each finding checked against the code before acting (log row 50). The full measured state is commit `f4ff787`; everything removed here is there.\n2356\t- Removed: the retired research harness `poc/hierarchy_study/` (17 experiments and 15 test files, D146–D162, none adopted; its tests wrote three SQLite files to the repository root through a module-named path, also removed); five finished one-off scripts (`jev_poc.py`, `supersedes_probe.py`, `succession_probe.py`, `key_views.py`, `review_keyed_run.py`; results in the build log and `poc/reports/`); six product functions with no caller (`judging.judge_one`, `matching.same_orientation`, `Universe.canonical_scope`, `testdb.days_with_items`, `store.lineage_of_view`, `store.replaced_by`) and six unused constants.\n2357\t- Fixed: the preflight's matching section now judges Jev records (it had skipped all 250 of the Jev ledger) and fails when every record of a ledger is skipped; the cost reports read the one price table (`model.PRICES`) and refuse an unpriced model; the benchmark stops when its evidence scorer fails; Jev succession eligibility has one rule (`jev_matching.can_succeed`); a test no longer reads the real credentials file, another no longer writes the real service archive; the newsroom category rule has one owner that its test exercises; the replay requires the ledger and archive it writes and defaults to `poc-v23` and the current item window; the compose file uses the adopted idea configuration `poc-v9`.\n2358\t- Left, with the reason: the dependency-cycle pass in `matching.check` (its refusal wording reaches stored retry requests, so merging it would change replayed requests); the two ladder validators (admission of a new ladder and reading of a stored one are different jobs); splitting `matching.check`, `writing._write` and `idea.read_idea` (behaviour-preserving refactors of the core without a measurement round); unused parameters and result fields (signature churn across many call sites); the corpus harvesters' shared fetch code; the test-only `search.fake_embed` in the search module; `scripts/review_english_run.py` counting equal model-written scopes as a breach (D155 makes them a lead, not identity). The reviewers' lead that the API's `poc-v9` default was stale was wrong: it is the adopted idea configuration (D135/D145).\n2359\t\n2360\t## D171 · 2026-10-25 · Review and free proofs for the day-batch acceleration proposal\n2361\t\n2362\t- Alex asked to review the extraction/Jev/per-day acceleration plan deeply and test it against the stored golds and result databases. Scope is review and isolated offline proofs; no product-contract change, runtime adoption or paid comparison follows automatically.\n2363\t- The [review](research/2026-10-25-day-batch-review/review.md) and its scripts reconstruct 960/960 extraction requests from the two saved v23 ledgers. Day-start context differs for 214/480 Jev-arm requests and 168/480 control requests; rechecking the same raw answers with day-start IDs loses 73/797 and 62/744 evidence items. Extraction depends on earlier theses as well as groups. The Jev arm has 86/320 final repeat/replace decisions targeting same-day evidence, and Jev membership alone cannot declare the 26 new groups.\n2364\t- At 32 workers, a three-wave schedule using recorded Jev call durations projects 8.6× on July 27 and 3.2× over 31 nonempty days, before reconciliation/rate-limit overhead. This replaces the unsupported general 10× claim, not the speed target. It is a schedule estimate, not fresh measured latency. Judging already uses six workers per dependency level.\n2365\t- Recommendation awaiting Alex's choice: concurrent request preparation with exact read validation before sequential commits, retaining D149/D159 semantics; re-run requests whose inputs changed. A more aggressive day-start extraction contract must be decided and tested separately. The review defines both boundaries, failure/recovery states, local-ID namespacing, evaluation limits and staged tests.\n2366\t- Verification: 128 product/corpus/diagnostic tests passed; two diagnostic mutants were caught; preflight judged 250 stored Jev records with no skipped records. Existing golds remain unchanged. No batching runtime or fresh model-quality pass is claimed.\n2367\t- A read-only review independently reproduced the 960 request message pairs and schemas, the stale-reference losses and the timing projection; no serious finding against the proposed contract. Runtime implementation and live measurement remain the next, separate steps.\n2368\t\n2369\t## D172 · 2026-10-25 · Build the isolated ordered-concurrency POC\n2370\t\n2371\t- Alex accepted the D171 recommendation: “yes be super careful about the logic ... do not fuck up the order ... flag it immediately”. Implement concurrent preparation with current-prefix validation and one ordered writer. Keep the default serial path and its tests unchanged. Group ownership and thesis/evidence semantics do not move.\n2372\t- Speculative calls may precede earlier commits in the experimental path; accepted handovers and writes may not. Stale prepared requests are recomputed; earlier failure cancels pending work and drains running calls without accepting later results. This scheduling distinction is explicit, not an accidental weakening of D159.\n2373\t- The [first implementation slice](research/2026-10-25-day-batch-review/implementation.md) covers offline coordinator, independent Jev calls and failure/restart verification on temporary storage. No paid call, new spending cap or default activation is authorized by this slice. The current per-item spend check is not safe reservation accounting for concurrent paid calls; resolve it before a live comparison.\n2374\t- Offline slice result: [ordered-concurrency POC](../poc/reports/2026-10-25-concurrency-poc/results.md). Shared request identity prevents conflicting concurrent answers under one archive ID. Bounded Jev submission cancels pending work on failure; the daily cost log includes discarded speculative work. Default replay remains serial. 143 tests pass; five intended mutants fail; 3,595 historical request hashes are unchanged. Final focused review finds no serious issue. Live accounting, fresh controls and speed measurement remain outstanding; no paid ingestion calls were made.\n2375\t\n2376\t## D173 · 2026-10-25 · Isolated provider smoke before a larger concurrency test\n2377\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78ccad56-ed8d-4e29-91b7-f576c6d2916d","uuid":"984ef70d-a453-4234-8d9e-17c34492bbd7","timestamp":"2026-11-09T21:06:37.899Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md","content":"## D168 · 2026-10-25 · Four corrections found by the D167 measurement: one repeat rule, one replace definition, one succession rule, one missing answer\n\nFound while measuring the two D167 arms (log rows 44–48); each is general, follows the PRD, and was measured on stored data or by re-sending stored requests before it entered the code.\n- **A repeat may name the piece shown for the same evidence under another of its theses** (`matching.check`). 06-views step 6: a repeat adds a source and any new thesis link; the writer already resolves a piece to one stored identity for all its theses. The check required the target under the destination thesis and failed an item twice (9 of 17 such refusals over four ledgers were this case). A replacement still stays within its thesis.\n- **The Jev replace criterion carries the PRD's full definition**: a later version of the same statement or number, changed (the same speaker revises it, an official confirms or denies that claim, the original source revises it); another statement on the same topic is not this (06-views lines 191, 197, 306). Probed against a fresh control, three samples: wrong replacements 10 of 10 removed on the seed and week, 6 of 7 out of sample on August; the right ones that stay are the guidance revisions and a source revising its own figure. A second wording ('the same party') was rejected by its pre-registered rule.\n- **The writer's replacement rule, generalised from the PRD**: an incoming third-party view or rumour replaces only the same party's earlier piece (subsumes the view-over-view rule of D167). On every stored ledger it refuses 6 written replacements, all a commentator's view over another party's rumour, none right.\n- **Succession**: the code's rule uses the shared typed-field rule (`identity.conflicts`: 'n/a' matches any basis or unit), so an unstated basis no longer blocks a succession (adds exactly 2 over all ledgers, both right); and the Jev relation question gains the answer 'the same defined quantity or event series for another period', with the direction, the typed fields and the successor check in code, so a succession between differently worded model scopes can be judged (probe: recovers the one such case, changes nothing gold-labelled for the worse).\n\n## D169 · 2026-10-25 · Jev is the default matching engine\n\n- Asked \"要不要把 Jev 定为默认的匹配引擎\" after the D167/D168 measurement (log rows 44–49), Alex answered: \"要的。你的抽取，trace和当前抽好的db都很宝贵，这些都留下来然后开始做这个阶段的收尾\".\n- Decided: `poc-v23` (extraction v10, `matching.engine: \"jev\"`, model `jev-1.14.0`, with D168) is the default configuration of the replay (`scripts/replay.py`, which now also requires the ledger and archive it writes). The service's idea route keeps its adopted configuration `poc-v9` (D135/D145) on the sealed ledger and its search index; moving the service to the English library needs a new index and is a separate step. The generative matcher stays a configured alternative (`engine: \"llm\"`); old configurations and stored archives replay unchanged.\n- The phase's extractions, traces and ledgers are kept (data manifest `docs/research/2026-10-25-english/data-manifest.md`, two backups outside the repository); the close-out is a code review for stale code and bloat, then the documents, then a push.\n- Not decided here: moving group membership from extraction to matching (D149/D166), which the queued day-batch shape needs; it comes with that task.\n\n## D170 · 2026-10-25 · Phase close-out: what the code review removed, fixed and left\n\nAlex: \"我们跑一个code review 清理一下过程中stale code 和 bloat 然后准备写好文档push\". Two read-only Codex reviews (product code with its tests; tooling), each finding checked against the code before acting (log row 50). The full measured state is commit `f4ff787`; everything removed here is there.\n- Removed: the retired research harness `poc/hierarchy_study/` (17 experiments and 15 test files, D146–D162, none adopted; its tests wrote three SQLite files to the repository root through a module-named path, also removed); five finished one-off scripts (`jev_poc.py`, `supersedes_probe.py`, `succession_probe.py`, `key_views.py`, `review_keyed_run.py`; results in the build log and `poc/reports/`); six product functions with no caller (`judging.judge_one`, `matching.same_orientation`, `Universe.canonical_scope`, `testdb.days_with_items`, `store.lineage_of_view`, `store.replaced_by`) and six unused constants.\n- Fixed: the preflight's matching section now judges Jev records (it had skipped all 250 of the Jev ledger) and fails when every record of a ledger is skipped; the cost reports read the one price table (`model.PRICES`) and refuse an unpriced model; the benchmark stops when its evidence scorer fails; Jev succession eligibility has one rule (`jev_matching.can_succeed`); a test no longer reads the real credentials file, another no longer writes the real service archive; the newsroom category rule has one owner that its test exercises; the replay requires the ledger and archive it writes and defaults to `poc-v23` and the current item window; the compose file uses the adopted idea configuration `poc-v9`.\n- Left, with the reason: the dependency-cycle pass in `matching.check` (its refusal wording reaches stored retry requests, so merging it would change replayed requests); the two ladder validators (admission of a new ladder and reading of a stored one are different jobs); splitting `matching.check`, `writing._write` and `idea.read_idea` (behaviour-preserving refactors of the core without a measurement round); unused parameters and result fields (signature churn across many call sites); the corpus harvesters' shared fetch code; the test-only `search.fake_embed` in the search module; `scripts/review_english_run.py` counting equal model-written scopes as a breach (D155 makes them a lead, not identity). The reviewers' lead that the API's `poc-v9` default was stale was wrong: it is the adopted idea configuration (D135/D145).\n\n## D171 · 2026-10-25 · Review and free proofs for the day-batch acceleration proposal\n\n- Alex asked to review the extraction/Jev/per-day acceleration plan deeply and test it against the stored golds and result databases. Scope is review and isolated offline proofs; no product-contract change, runtime adoption or paid comparison follows automatically.\n- The [review](research/2026-10-25-day-batch-review/review.md) and its scripts reconstruct 960/960 extraction requests from the two saved v23 ledgers. Day-start context differs for 214/480 Jev-arm requests and 168/480 control requests; rechecking the same raw answers with day-start IDs loses 73/797 and 62/744 evidence items. Extraction depends on earlier theses as well as groups. The Jev arm has 86/320 final repeat/replace decisions targeting same-day evidence, and Jev membership alone cannot declare the 26 new groups.\n- At 32 workers, a three-wave schedule using recorded Jev call durations projects 8.6× on July 27 and 3.2× over 31 nonempty days, before reconciliation/rate-limit overhead. This replaces the unsupported general 10× claim, not the speed target. It is a schedule estimate, not fresh measured latency. Judging already uses six workers per dependency level.\n- Recommendation awaiting Alex's choice: concurrent request preparation with exact read validation before sequential commits, retaining D149/D159 semantics; re-run requests whose inputs changed. A more aggressive day-start extraction contract must be decided and tested separately. The review defines both boundaries, failure/recovery states, local-ID namespacing, evaluation limits and staged tests.\n- Verification: 128 product/corpus/diagnostic tests passed; two diagnostic mutants were caught; preflight judged 250 stored Jev records with no skipped records. Existing golds remain unchanged. No batching runtime or fresh model-quality pass is claimed.\n- A read-only review independently reproduced the 960 request message pairs and schemas, the stale-reference losses and the timing projection; no serious finding against the proposed contract. Runtime implementation and live measurement remain the next, separate steps.\n\n## D172 · 2026-10-25 · Build the isolated ordered-concurrency POC\n\n- Alex accepted the D171 recommendation: “yes be super careful about the logic ... do not fuck up the order ... flag it immediately”. Implement concurrent preparation with current-prefix validation and one ordered writer. Keep the default serial path and its tests unchanged. Group ownership and thesis/evidence semantics do not move.\n- Speculative calls may precede earlier commits in the experimental path; accepted handovers and writes may not. Stale prepared requests are recomputed; earlier failure cancels pending work and drains running calls without accepting later results. This scheduling distinction is explicit, not an accidental weakening of D159.\n- The [first implementation slice](research/2026-10-25-day-batch-review/implementation.md) covers offline coordinator, independent Jev calls and failure/restart verification on temporary storage. No paid call, new spending cap or default activation is authorized by this slice. The current per-item spend check is not safe reservation accounting for concurrent paid calls; resolve it before a live comparison.\n- Offline slice result: [ordered-concurrency POC](../poc/reports/2026-10-25-concurrency-poc/results.md). Shared request identity prevents conflicting concurrent answers under one archive ID. Bounded Jev submission cancels pending work on failure; the daily cost log includes discarded speculative work. Default replay remains serial. 143 tests pass; five intended mutants fail; 3,595 historical request hashes are unchanged. Final focused review finds no serious issue. Live accounting, fresh controls and speed measurement remain outstanding; no paid ingestion calls were made.\n\n## D173 · 2026-10-25 · Isolated provider smoke before a larger concurrency test\n","numLines":40,"startLine":2338,"totalLines":4365}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01D7BhY4BGyrKPevgSv8Pxj2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
reports/step2-jev-decision-council-2026-11-09/claude-independent.jsonl:40:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018CmcTqdQF4YFPwjfG4kVSv","type":"tool_result","content":"2594\t## D202 · 2026-10-27 · The writer retires nothing on a matching label: a replacement is recorded as a revision and both pieces stay in force\n2595\t\n2596\t- Alex, on the arm1 result and the proposal: \"你接着做，需要非常严谨的看结果和测量\". Census first ([labels](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/replacements-47-labels.json), every pair read): of the run's 47 retirements, 22 are the owner's later value or status of the same claim (right under rule E), 20 retired a statement that should have stayed (10 a contradiction or qualification, among them a denial erasing the delay report, \"subject to final agreements\" erasing the $500bn announcement; 10 a different matter), 5 were restatements mislabelled. Jev's `supersedes` label does not separate them: 17 of 22 right and 12 of 20 wrong carry it; with \"confirms or denies\" split out (`jev_contradicts.py`) 4 wrong ones flip to `contradicts` but so do 3 right ones. No label the matcher gives is safe to retire on.\n2597\t- Decided: `writing` no longer calls `store.replace`. A `replace` decision stores the incoming piece, links it wherever the target is linked, and records the pair in a new `revisions` table (old, new, said day, seq); both pieces stay in force. `replacement_refused` becomes `revision_refused` with the same conditions (a stated day, a tier at least the old piece's, the same party for views and rumours): refused means stored as new without the relation, as before. The late-older-statement rule is unchanged. `replacements` is history: read by every present reader as before, never written again. `store.evidence_in_force` carries `revises` (the predecessor by revision) and judging prints \"It revises eX (both in force)\" so the judge sees the path; the library UI and service do not show revisions yet (open). The Jev criteria are unchanged: the split was measured and not adopted (3 false `contradicts` on right revisions against 4 catches).\n2598\t- The decision word stays `replace` in matching records, schemas and the generative matcher's prompt; its meaning in the writer is \"revises\". Renaming it across prompts, records and scripts is a separate wording slice (D36).\n2599\t- Not decided here: the step 4 analysis call in production (D201's reader of both texts), which would decide `same_as` / `revises` / `opposes` itself; until it exists the matcher's label is recorded as an opinion and nothing is lost to it.\n2600\t- Regression week ([results](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/results.md), `english-v34b-nvda-week-2026-10-27`, $0.23, 98 items, none failed): the whole path runs with the revision rule; `replacements` gains no row. Counts differ from the v18 week (129 against 176 evidence rows, 12 against 9 theses) because extraction is a sampler, not because of the writer; the writer's effect is proved on the decisions themselves. Reading the week's one `replace` decision found a second silent loss in the same code path: an incoming statement said before its target was logged in `late_statements` and linked nowhere (both weeks dropped SemiAnalysis's Kyber delay report this way; the three-month run dropped the SharonAI backstop figure; 1 of 3,216 decisions, each a material statement). Fixed in the same slice: an older statement is stored and linked as one more piece, no revision recorded, the log row kept. Test red on the old writer, green after; suite 209. Observed, not fixed here: 34 of 1,530 stored pieces carry a non-ISO `original_day` (\"2026-07-05 to 2026-07-06\", \"2026-03\"), which is what made the Kyber report \"older\"; an extraction-contract item for the next pass.\n2601\t\n2602\t## D203 · 2026-10-28 · A statement day is an ISO day or unknown; the writer never fills it with the publication day\n2603\t\n2604\t- Found while reading D202's regression week: the extraction answer's `original_day` is a free string (54 of the three-month run's 2,369 raw pieces carry a month, a range or text; 1,445 are empty) and the writer filled every empty day with the item's publication day, against D245's rule that an unknown stays unknown. A range read as \"older than\" the denial it answered dropped SemiAnalysis's Kyber delay report from the week ledger.\n2605\t- Decided, at the owners: `extraction.statement_day` keeps an ISO day and blanks anything else when the answer is sifted (the quote and `event_time` keep the period as written); `writing._store_piece` stores the stated day or '' and the revision path stores the stated day too (the ordering day `new_day` orders the revision only). Readers print the blank; matching's `said_day` still falls back to the publication day for ordering, never for storage. Tests: `test_a_statement_day_is_an_iso_day_or_unknown…` (red on the old sift), `test_an_unknown_statement_day_is_stored_unknown…` (red on the old writer); suite 211; preflight B/C/D unchanged.\n2606\t- The judge's revision note is labelled as what it is: \"Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of eX; both are in force\" (D202's census: 19 of 37 recorded revisions carry a wrong label).\n2607\t- Census of the other silent loss in the same path, read pair by pair ([labels](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/repeats-40-labels.json)): of 40 sampled `repeat` decisions (1,034 in the run), 32 are pickups, 8 merged a different or richer statement into an old piece and lost its content (an estimated 200 of 1,034). The mechanism is D202's: a one-sample label triggers an irreversible write (a repeat adds a source and discards the incoming text). The fix is the argument record's reader side (store every source record, relate `same_as`, collapse same-claim groups for the judge); not built here.\n2608\t- D201 measured further ([results](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/results.md), 2026-10-28 00:00–01:20, $0.85): a fresh 16-case gold written from the complete expansions before any send (action 11/12 and 4/4 held out; nothing wrongly retired 16/16; same-claim 8/12 with one class: a second outlet's own reporting treated as independent, which the contract could not express); arm 2 split claim identity from source independence (the class is gone; two new judgement weaknesses appeared); three draws per disagreeing case under both prompts show the arm-to-arm differences are within one draw's noise, while in 144 draws no must-not-touch piece was ever marked same or revised. Decided: the contract keeps arm 2's split; prompt arms stop here; the variable fields are annotations under D202 and the next build is the argument record (steps 5 and 6), where a wrong relation in one draw is corrected by the next instead of being frozen into the ledger.\n2609\t\n2610\t## D204 · 2026-10-28 · A repeat is stored as its own source record; the merge becomes a labelled relation\n2611\t\n2612\t- Alex: \"补那个洞，跑一周验收，仔细思考很重要\". The hole (D203's census): on a `repeat` decision the writer adds the incoming item as an outlet of the old piece and discards the incoming text; 8 of 40 sampled repeats (20%, about 200 of 1,034 in the run) had merged a different or richer statement, and its content was gone. The mechanism is D202's: a one-draw label (same_statement ≥ 0.5) triggers an irreversible write.\n2613\t- Choice weighed. (A) Store the incoming piece, link it to its own destinations, record the pair in `same_claims`, show both to the judge with the relation labelled as the matcher's opinion. (B) Keep the merge and store the text as a variant of the old piece, hidden from the judge. Under (A) true pickups (about 80%) appear twice in view for their first readings, labelled; under (B) the 20% wrong merges stay hidden from the judge. The standard (rule A: an outlet, not a fact; rule C: support counts sources) is served by the label; losing content is the failure that is not recoverable at read time. Decided: (A).\n2614\t- Decided: `writing` stores every incoming piece once with all its destination links; a `repeat` decision records (target, new, seq) in `same_claims`; the old piece gains no source row and no new link (the new piece carries the outlet and the links; a link written on a one-draw label is a write like any other). `store.evidence_in_force` carries `same_as` (the in-force pieces of its group); the judge's request prints \"Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as eX; the argument counts one claim\". Readers of `evidence`, `evidence_links` and `evidence_sources` keep their shapes; the library UI's per-row outlet count now counts one row's sources (the claim's outlets are its `same_claims` group), an open UI item.\n2615\t- Acceptance: tests red on the old writer (`test_a_repeated_statement_is_stored_as_its_own_record…`, the two D167 tests whose assertions pinned the merge, changed with this decision), a mutant that merges again fails them; the 40 sampled repeat decisions replayed through the new writer at $0 (40/40 stored, relation recorded, the 8 lost contents present); then one week through the whole path (`english-v34c-nvda-week`), counting `same_claims` rows, pairs both in the judge's view, and judgements citing both members of a pair (the double-count exposure the label must hold).\n2616\t- Accepted ([results](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/results.md)): 40 of 40 sampled repeat decisions replayed through the new writer keep the piece, the relation and both pieces in force (the 8 lost contents are present); the week through the whole path ran with none failed at $0.23, wrote 45 same-claim relations and no merge, and the judge's reasons where a pair was in view treat the relays as one source (\"ultimately trace to SemiAnalysis\", \"not independent confirmation\"). The three-month run may resume on `poc-v34` with D202–D204 in the writer; that is Alex's call.\n2617\t\n2618\t## D205 · 2026-10-28 · The judging call's output budget is 16,000 tokens\n2619\t\n2620\t- Found in the three-month resume (`english-v34-nvda-3m-2026-10-28`, 01:32): the judgement of v10 (Data Center revenue, a 24,150-token request) came back empty with `finish_reason` length: all 8,000 completion tokens were reasoning tokens, so the code read a malformed answer and the day halted (resumable; the re-send passed). Census over every archive: 2 of 793 judge answers hit the cap this way (the other in `english-v29-nvda-3m`, a 56k-token request), 13 of 793 used 6,000 or more. The archive books such a call at $0 while it costs its tokens (about $0.006 each).\n2621\t- Alex: \"嗯你加倍吧\". Decided: `rejudging.max_output_tokens` 8,000 → 16,000 in `poc-v34.json`, nothing else changed; a normal judgement costs the same, only a long-reasoning one may spend more. Applied from day 2026-07-22 of the run (days 1–21 ran under 8,000; the archive records the configuration checksum per call).\n2622\t- Also from this run: the Mac slept at about 02:00 and a Jev call failed with URLError during a dark wake; the run's `launch.sh` now wraps the run in `caffeinate -i -s`.\n2623\t\n2624\t\n2625\t## D206 · 2026-10-28 · Every open theme thesis is shown to every item's extraction (extraction v19, `poc-v35`)\n2626\t\n2627\t- Alex: \"你可以改A然后尝试\". The class ($0, `docs/research/2026-10-28-extraction-quality/report.md`): every one of the run's 1,414 extraction requests named the H200 key in its reference list while the H200 thesis (a theme) was never in the shown list; 48 pieces linked only to theme ids the groups block printed and the link check refused; three H200 theses opened for one matter.\n2628\t- Decided: `store.views_for_prompt(themes=True)` lists every open theme thesis after the objects' own, in creation order; `extraction.shown_views` is the one owner of the shown list (the stream and the parallel runner both read it); the groups block prints only member ids the answer may link to. Gated on the prompt version (`THEMES_SHOWN_FROM = 19`): v18 requests are byte-for-byte as before.\n2629\t- Measured: frozen gold (`gold-standard.md`, 26 articles, 26 statements, 11 core; `ab_gold.py`): control (production bytes, 26/26 identities archived) 9/11/7 kept right against the arm's 13/15/14 over three sends, core 6/6/5 → 9/8/8, written-dropped 13/12/13 → 8/7/8, extra flat; two weeks through the whole path (`english-v35-nvda-2w-2026-10-28`, $0.59): one H200 thesis instead of three, 14 pieces and 3 judgements on it, capex themes 7 pieces / 4–5 judgements each against 2 / 2, zero drops for an unshown id (17 in the control weeks). Tests `tests/test_theme_shown.py`.\n2630\t\n2631\t## D207 · 2026-10-28 · Statements are filed by a separate placement call under the one-home rule; an unfiled statement is kept (extraction v20, `poc-v36`)\n2632\t\n2633\t- Alex, on the root cause (\"你还是没有根因的去解决这个问题 … 要么挂不到，要么挂多\"; \"跑那个归档实验\"; \"你看着结果来做\"; \"一样的标准开始测\"). The mechanism ([routing-analysis.md](research/2026-10-28-extraction-quality/routing-analysis.md)): the in-call link decision was a coin flip (36% right on the gold, 46–78 pieces kept from the same bytes) and the gate at once (an unlinked statement was dropped: 60% of all statements, ~15–30% of them material); the link rule routed demand news to the company-wide quantities (six root theses held 66% of all links; v2's pieces 86% double-linked, 26% cited); the thesis list had no driver layer.\n2634\t- Decided, three parts measured one at a time on the frozen gold (three draws each, 8 threads, $0.04–0.05 an arm) and on the first week's 255 in-call pieces: (1) `placement.py`: one call per item with the title line, the statements and the open questions, each with a code-written 'takes' line (key, event type, research-group names; aggregates admit other populations); the sift keeps statements without a link (`keep_unlinked`); the placer's links replace the extraction's, candidate links survive; a statement filed nowhere is stored in `unplaced_statements` with the handover. (2) The rule is the placement prompt: `placement-narrow-v1` — one home, the narrowest question; company-wide figure questions take only their own figures, guidance and management statements. (3) D42's \"evidence is linked at extraction\" becomes: the extraction's links are its first opinion; placement decides. Gated on the prompt version (`PLACEMENT_FROM = 20`); `cfg.placement` is required from v20.\n2635\t- Measured: core statements filed right 6/6/5 → 9/9/9 (with six seeded driver questions shown) and 9/10/9 under the old rule; wrong links 9 → 3; links to the two sinks 63/60/44 → 6/1/5; junk placed 53/48/32 → 39/27/35; every decisive matter statement (the Kyber/Rubin Ultra rack delay cluster, the AI Compute Partnership participants, Goldman's capex estimate) placed; one week through the whole path (`english-v36-nvda-1w-2026-10-28`, $0.22 against $0.22) kept 580 statements the old path dropped. Arms retired by measurement: the extraction's links shown as a hint (junk and sink links returned), group aliases alone (the groups lack the press names). Tests `tests/test_placement.py`, suite 222.\n2636\t- Not built here: re-placement of unplaced statements when a thesis opens later (the cold-start class, 12% of gold cells); the driver layer (D208).\n2637\t\n2638\t## D208 · 2026-10-28 · The thesis tree: root quantities, a seeded driver layer per company, shared macro themes with declared channels (design, not built)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78ccad56-ed8d-4e29-91b7-f576c6d2916d","uuid":"427e8718-7355-4ea7-b22b-5441f17ad725","timestamp":"2026-11-09T21:06:38.629Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md","content":"## D202 · 2026-10-27 · The writer retires nothing on a matching label: a replacement is recorded as a revision and both pieces stay in force\n\n- Alex, on the arm1 result and the proposal: \"你接着做，需要非常严谨的看结果和测量\". Census first ([labels](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/replacements-47-labels.json), every pair read): of the run's 47 retirements, 22